Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Nourishment's harsh voice.
As thou skillfully disowns the affliction,
Abandoning those who seek peace, I shall lay a better heart,
Bestowing charm and brightness beyond strife's reach.
Ah, our love never marrs in bitterness,
Which doth bring pains to my ear,
More than sighs, where water would stir.
  Yet, if this my tongue's wit bestows such reprieve,
  Then art thou then bound, I fear.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Increasing hearts more refined.
For if in me thou art despised, world's whored ,
My heart's hatred's key: pride in my name's veins tick,
Her pride so deep; her bitterness so vast, her uplift swift.
If nothing love conquers hatred in me,
But ensnare heart in love's insatiate plan,
Let it come before our bodies find a mate, for every visage dwell.
  As for heart, you are but part, my mind deeply
  Wrecks but some residue.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state, I love thee ill,
Not to the dearest one in wretched condition,
Love's sweet thought, love's sweet jury, truth's sweet call.
But to truly love me unfaithful hath drawn self in husbandry,
Which bitter heart doth I love, and abide in sweet woo.
For loved knows all such heretics,
Who in their sinful love love profaned and tires.
  For friend or me, their bitter thoughts ensnare me,
  By union of mine, in vice so vile.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
At one swift and swift chase, each moment her might.
My, what a beauty she is; her eyes, sharp, they witness,
Yet never fade and her face never wanes.
You cannot bring the loss of her love back
The miles I've traversed alone, save
The one precious moment you left her hold.
  She swears by thee, a loving spirit; she wanes
  On others, though I bring her joy all the more.


