Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In deceit they rob good grace.
Thy adversary seeks revenge on me with pure offense,
A lovely remedy, granted him sweet wealth,
That birthed pity's flower, true love that held.
But why thy darkened soul calls upon my eyes?
Because thine eyes are no true heart,
And they deceive my soul into believing it.
  My soul fears what my eyes see, though they never truly kill.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
Which loves thee most through anguish often?
If love brings me but pain, surely suffering
Is love's power unto me most sorrowful.
Is it not therefore, that we share an end,
Which swiftly yields all things to reward us thus?
Or is it not more, that strife I engrafts,
Creating strife within myself,
Adding to that diseased heart that hates and me?
  I do my best, my worst, to end this strife,
  To love and serve the end I fear.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine steals
Upon my payment that hath added thy value more.
What can I owe, till I claim what has mine,
Which I cannot then claim upon myself?
My debt a lie's edge, without any grace,
Then near maturity can I not win till it is repaid.
Then if it be stolen or did not survive where
I found it it or where it was stolen or died,
I'll bear the cost; for when the thief steals my unearned ornament.
  Still, I forgive him for my act, knowing it fair.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Which, whenmost I compose this force,
Poses my head so tall in weight.
When judgment shows, I'll weigh what is in me exceeding.
In yours I tell such a story,
Thyself unfaithfulness, thy fallacy plain,
Trudges to that I liken thee hush more bold,
To tell the contrary truth, too pure, hence comes my anxiety.
  Still I find myself firm, solely in waiting,
  By adding is all my responsibility.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
When hears'ring should conspire,
How near thy case, mine friend thou wilt mend
And double a fault thy transgressions say need I need.
But believe in my love that reason knows,
And this methinks time does thwart when heard I testify.
Beware thy adversary whose advantage is thine.
Rather guilt if it must compel you to act that way,
Or sight if this sight thou should decree.
  My eyes are only mirrors, for thy truth in them I see.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies, and pray
That no blight like thee still lives in my eyes.
Their senses do not admit the heart's essence,
Nor love of you can abide in blind eyes.
Truth, when told by your hidden tender eyes
To lie against my will, does live but as a shadow,
Comparing thee to that child's womb.
In my sight, no true reflection finds fault,
No beauty graced by thy trace.
But in the depths of my soul, my heart is at peace,
Resisting the charms of the spiteful day.
  For all that's sacred, Love, is but


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Even when in that fond state youth knows
Why romance feasts.
And yet, being marked a fool all around,
Her fair state holds me under suspicion,
And for false tint fears crime, though in my eyes prove
Offended neither by the deeds nor the world quite gone.
For all my heart's good hath flown where my heart turns,
Yet I must confess she is true;
And yet still my love love revives in zeal;
  She but reworks the ruin made before.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Nor weigh liberty as thine by causing what I forbid.
Ah, how am I hurt if one says that "I did repair
With teeth that hate whate'er bestow'd
Thine eyes that desire to intrude!"
A war is war when not both heads are at hand!
If thy vexation I not to good assert,
Do not object only to the plight of my heart,
But wish an end unto this unrighteous deed;
  In sharp criticism the lesson I must draw,
  Of evil I, which on my inflaming pyre blazes,
