Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
A more precise cure for your injury.
Bestow those 'till the storms cease and bloom anew,
Consume beauty without wounds, and grant ease,
A love born from self-made delight, in thrall.
If death shall consume thee henceforth, sing of me
Whose written words bear my name, and restore my will,
Though I may falter within your domain's bars.
  If found wanting, restore where it belongs;
  Still, if he unlooses thy sting, dear friend, the storm may bring.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
That's the cause of its rage.
How many hearts can I then declare my own revulsion,
Lest false prophets to our face do,
Defame me blindly, unphased when true distress
Divides hearts, both our beloved in its strife untimmed,
And tires me with pride and unrighteous spurn,
In self-defense, I take care.
  To myself, my falsehoods tire,
  My love the most potent potion, though impious.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Two souls entangled, parted from me.
Admit thou art love; and how shall I feel guilty,
Who in thine love canst mine love stir?
Thou the most kind, the least repugnant,
Most gracious, is to me abused.
For loathing me stems from the sins my dear friend is condemned,
And hence is my guilt heightened, stronger than before,
Than pity given is to me.
  My guilt is increased, greater, and I am sunk,
  Not why I feel the pain of hurt at all.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Beside her, a devoted clutch lies,
A mother hen nursed by her brood, gracing the flock.
At the beck and call of nature's gentle's sway,
Allure turns to heat, and thus, you find your comfort,
In this love, I'll not dwell;
In this love, and in it, you find my verse.
My love, you've shaped a paradox,
A love whose source lies beneath, where you find verity.
  I'll not retreat, for you are too kind; in this love, I intend to prove.


