Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy breath doth plead against thy worth, inciting woe,
My soul's pure light to wander.
At thy mention, I straight leap, to witness,
The sweetness of thy pity, the color it seeks,
In subtle dance, they draw thee to me, they sway.
Such subtle charm hath my heart writ,
In sympathy, it sings, and I, a bore,
Bearing the burden, suffer the joy.
  In this love, to lose me is to retain
  The nobler soul, when my spirit returns.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To torment me in my darkest hour,
And share this torment with my straight mate?
It wrests me from myself, your tyrant hold
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, find
Our hearts entwined in torment, thrice multiplied.
Imprison my heart within your fortress chest,
But grant my friend's heart freedom in its place.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart be their guest,
For none can then oppress me in that space.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim
Myself at my heart's desire;
Thus my claim on thee thine will be thine alone,
And I am restrained in the least
By fear of losing thee all.
Now that you have sought hence, like one who dwells
In trust, where trust is but shallow coin,
And with somber loss find a home where it belong,
  In that where trust is deep, where loss and pleasure find sides,
  And what I hold in trust is but a loan.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Than that she hath her own will to please thee;
That to vex her thou must approve
That she have your spite, that she gives you grace,
And in this she proves her power.
Then may I reproach thee for my rude love,
But that your love alone my proud name should keep,
So bring down my old title;
  I will pay the cost, so be the new.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Be as I am, to thy being mine is annexed,
Thy outward looks are like mine inward being;
If thy spirit be true and sincere,
The gilded vessel treasurers riches within,
Thyself beauteous friends and lovers will be.
They too may claim thine uncared for privilege,
Preserving thee from thine darkest woe.
  Still, be as I am, they claim as thine,
  Unless thine image changes, in which thou doth.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But, Love, they deceive not, yet their sight is swerving.
Till my eyes, which see but what they view,
Are anchored in a cove where hearts find all wrong,
And Love, you've shaped deceitful manacles,
That bind my heart there and watch in dismay
My heart, with unwary eyes, try to kiss it away.
But, love, you've wrought a twisted mirror's snare,
That worse looks may stain our love,
And Love, the chains I bear, must be tied
To your twisted sight, where false hearts find comfort.
 


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth, for which I reproach;
Yet, in truth, I am stained with self-defense,
And partly for this sin do I confess,
And partly for this I confide,
But mostly for this I straight believe.
For why should I reproach what I do not confess
When I have sworn by thine authority unseen?
O, know, sweet love, I have sworn by thine witness,
And thou never even turns from me that I depart.
  For I have sworn thee true, false heart, untrue tongue,
  And thou on thine false constancy, thy abuse.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Wound me not with thy moving heart's gross,
And make me an adversary to do thee wrong.
O, do not reproach thy unkindness where thou dost hate,
But in thine own wild cause do I advocate
That wretchedity where thou art guilty of loving.
O, change thy thought, do not weigh hate more heavily
Shall foes be with thy moving heart;
Rough winds do shake hands, and lascivious wounds heal;
Thy foe, friend, must surely hate with an unjust hatred be.
  Kill me with thine own open-handed


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own worth, like passions do now.
O, if thou wilt, do not,
Let those lips that Love will rehearse
Against the wind, unfurl 'gainst strife,
In soft and tender touches, sweet as stones fall.
If thou wilt, do not so,
But strike me first, when I send my best:
  Pity me, then, and wish I were anew,
  For love's seal delivers this potent healing balm.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which in me hates to see my faults shown.
Neither do I kiss the other's lips, nor doth my tongue,
For I hate to see false accents called:
But when a gentle mien hath painted furrows on thy brow
To every ornament I think amfits thy beauty.
Thy sweet love surpasseth mine own hour in no man's sight,
Yet thou in mine own countenance doth steal away heaven's portion,
Whose bounty is that thou mineself mine to adore.
  And thus is my love grown: for why should I steal when thou robs me?



Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Thy love's sweet face is as fair as heaven's own garden,
Even so our eyes view no difference, but suppose the sun
To rise and fall in love's sweet shape implied.
O, but this our frown is not love's fault,
It may be thy love that doth murmur it.
O, yet farther afield thy hate,
Thy love's flower in me doth lie;
For thee this slander born of love is born,
And I, an accomplice, must engage in it.
  Seducing thee, and I, in this love,



Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Wounding both her and stealing her time away.
Brighter than the tear doth flatter,
Shall joy be cherished more than pity's pall?
Or who is so kind, so kind, to her plight
That she puts an end to every show?
So if you are touched by my grief, do not reproach me,
Lest sorrow turns your fire to flame again.
  Then compassion alone your grief can rouse,
  Shall brighten the flame of love, and keep it whole.


