Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Not to the love that mine in thee dost compel.
For if thou but adore those lambs whose hearts are lovely,
Rough love finds no need for quick comparisons,
For they themselves, in thy love, are made worse
Than those adored in thy sweet gaze.
Then do not so, but lovingly set love's eye
In those blots, which thy sweet self so kindly dyed,
By thy loving husbandry, though not acquainted.
  In so, thou double beauty's art
  And beautify thine in a golden crypt.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
The fearful husk, in shame, begins
To chase the fleeing bird away,
And, forlorn, she does chase
That fleeing bird away, profligate;
In anger, the innocent bird falls to the ground,
And the vilest wretch, profligate,
Rises on all sides, all alone,
And, to his fleeing abandon, I begin
To chase after you, my darling;
But, alack, I have not figured out
The sweetest thing to do, so haply
My love, haply, drops my all defense.
 


