Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gentle on my grief, they mock and gloom.
Yet mine eyes that watch far too often in farce do stray,
Seeking sorrow, as mine own true eyes do sin.
No wonder then, my grief, they see twice
Thy complexion turned upside down, thy dark complexion turned pale,
In pity's sight, it seeks increase,
And rings true, kindled by heaven's own fire.
  But heaven's will, which brings me far from fair,
  Thine eyes, though they find fault, do show me true.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to suffer, see?
Even if you find the power, break free
And find your heart's desire, sweet love's desire.
You've awoken the fire that mends the bed
And saved my friend's soul, as you've left me.
Ah, should you find this power, run wild
And explore your own heart, to no avail.
  For no force can halt the flow
  Of ragged dreams, so soon as you shut it in place.


