So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim
My peace of will.
Thence comes the night a stranger's night,
And in my widow's breast lies golden
A picture of thee in painted heaven.
In the day my lady's face lies pure and clear,
And in the night my doting heart lies waste;
But no more the basest painting sees
To stain the truth of thy beauty black and white.
  Thus all my vile deeds are trophies now trophies
  That myself and thee did bleed.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And so must I be, to be removed
From this union with you, as a widowed widow
Will bear child, without end,
