In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which on thy unworthiness commits th'all offenses.
Mine eyes, 'fore used to look into thy soul,
Are moored within a crystal case,
Which may be returned from thee soon,
But such treasure hid within thy head
Is 'gainst thee out of thyself deceived,
And thou thief of my liberty,
Most graciously, my fair-haired self, for thy sake
Embracing me with thy glance's abundance.
  For thee mine eyes deceive men of eyes,
  And 'gainst my spirit by thy sweet theft be doth show.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Thy love against mine own false start.
For why should I name thee as I do mine eyes adore?
Or on whom dost thou frown that I do trust?
If thou robs me of trust, what account can I give?
O, what account can I give thee,
For who hath trust now not to thy sweet lie?
Who hath trust in thee that sour cherishes thy name,
Roses deceit when a man deceives with a show?
  O, none, and yet this slander in thy head:
  Mine is thy trust, and my love against thee.


