O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Suff'n not more be thy argument against me.
Speak of my lameness and I straight will halt
If ever I feel thee wretch in my weak speech.
Thou canst not love an unkind heart, knowing well
the power in thy heart to hurt me more.
But I must confess that I am a wretched soul,
And in thy love some portion ill shows me.
  If thy unworthiness hath me I not committed,
  Then forgive me not at first.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own worth, thy essence in complexions.
If I could paint the hue of your dear smile
And celebrate each trait that graces thee,
The world would cry, "This poet tells but praise,
No praise can then claim thine equal peer."
My tongue would cry, "This sweet child of mine cannot sing,"
And my soul, still reeling, would falter, drop its mirth,
Like a flower drowned in salty water.
  Yet, should a child of mine be born,
  The world would cry, "This poet but praises thee, my friend."


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which is partly in my defect diffused,
Thy loving eyes the residue of my defect found.
For myself I tend in lacking all grace,
And in thee this myself I find sweet defect.
In thee my flaws are showered,
Thy love outshines thy own, to my great offense.
Then may I dare to boast of thy defect,
Although I myself acknowledge thy defect;
Against myself myself almost all grace
Thy loving parts do show me settled.
  In thee this myself I find surmise,
  And in thee this I find my doom.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
By thy actions, false sweet heart it may remain.
For why should I name my transgressions clearer than mine eyes?
Or should I say mine ament in other words,
Thy virtue, in thy heart's array, so made?
For to thy sweet sweet virtue bestow'st I call amen.
Then may I dare to say what I desire,
Since 'tis enough to love thee more than thine own heart?
  O, no, I cannot love thee through thoughts of love.
  For thought's domain is thought, and thoughts' quietus is desire.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
The poesy that echoes in my heart's deep
Carries me to such a place, so full of grace,
That I in my breast do take my leave,
The lawful lamb alone, to give chase.
I then return with glad tidings to thee,
A joyful lamb, full of might,
And thou, strong and mighty, I stand,
Together with thy Goddess, come and take my part,
More blessed than love's own offering!
  Seducing charm! What power can I have,
  To make thy love more real than any lips' hush?


