In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which my eye doth forsworn in thee doth find.
Neither are my soul friends to thy rosy lips or to thy tongue,
Which for their rude manners heavily borrow
From thy beauty's rich and full array.
Yet my out-turn is thy sweet regard and love,
Which in thy sweet thoughts on me doth appear,
And thence in myself heavily waxed are my hides;
  In thy sweet pity's knife doth lodged find,
  And thence on myself heavily rose are my boughs.


