Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy skin darkly done, as mourning becomes thee,
In slow shadows all mournful phases;
Yet my soul whispers to my eyes,
"Had thy shadow fallen on me, better did he fade,
To dwell with thy shadow's form in light;
Where dost thou bloom, and thy shadow fell,
Be it not thy darkly draped robe,
Which touches me more when I am sleepy."
The saucy verses that my heart may sing,
Of thy dark design, when I am awake,
  Kill me with gentle strokes, yet please my eyes:
  All men


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to weep in public place?
Even if thou repent, do not so much as beseech
My name, let my heart be made lament,
Not that my friend's name be made famous,
Nor praise my deeds that way,
For they but praise what's writ.
Ah, let my silence be kind, so that my heart may groan
In quietude, as thou dost them inspire.
Though my friend may seem groan,
She loved him, and still heart still cries out,
  For I, confined in thee, do forbid.


