Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in thy thinking mind, it is but dimly shown;
Thyself so blinded, thy sight is blinded
To allure, all things strange, in thy sight it seems
To dwell with thoughts that ever to thee turn.
Yet that thyself thy eyes do view still,
My heart, surly-eyed, calls on thy defect,
Cannot frown on thy defect, Love, canst thou not frown?
  All tongues that help thee discern
  But none that can thee to whom thou dost call.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And that she is of like learning as false women;
But that she hath slept with others I know not,
I do not know her well to show my truth,
And in my great love's content she does tell
That I am of two of three, and that she loves
Three or four times more, not less,
Than think me of her height and give her my place.
  But love, being sick of growth,
  I do not so much as want her to know
  To grow up to be of more cheer, though she grow.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
ORDER not to blame where thou mayst show,
Speak of love's disapproval, not gentle argument,
Spare me the sight of thine active eye;
RULE that thou tongue, which for thy sake even here
Cannot rouse my brain from a dance;
Suff with thy painful argument a blessed beauty to chase,
And in quietude let my spirit go:
  Lest that my flesh with thee wanders too far,
  And thou thy foe, too base of pardon,
  Shall blow me away in a thousand dreams.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of hurt that thou canst not compel.
If I could cure thee of this malady,
By all means, and many, in one of thine,
That loves thy heart, yet doth it forsake thy friend,
Give me gentle love, as thou dost thine own part;
Nourish thy harmful remedy with a sponge,
A remedy for those afflicted with grief's sting.
  To avoid this, and to uphold thy friend,
  Keep silent, as thou wandster'st is.


