Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And in that we all bow to thine iniquity?
How can I then return, basest and weary,
When all my strong love and defense rings
Cursed not with the least part of thee.
O, benefit of ill! Now I find true
That all is done, even heaven's will, to the best of ill.
  Ah, thus, sweet friend, thus do I fortify
  With every boon that I desire, and all thou canst give.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim
The bare cost of my love;
For such a confession hath no need of recompense,
For where is my will? Thy treasure hid in my will,
Which I in ignorance gave to thee,
To find out, within the fortress of my will,
The reasons for thy love so kind;
And thus my confession is thus:—
I am thy debtor, and he pays the cost,
Without reproach or blame on me.
