Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
So may I chide thy unprovident girth
And in thy reckless love let me belatedly soar.
Thou dost thy poverty give scope to take
And that for a beggar's sum even so
To what thou dost give gentle boon.
In me, even that surrenders thou thy part
And I lose by the gracious largess.
  How can I then feel affrighted in this plight
  Where hardship is given but what thou dost give?


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Reply To this, I answer, thou art assured true,
Although look'st thou on me amiss,
And on me amiss, my will forbear
To meet with thine eyes only.
But since my soul doth believe in thine eyes,
Rescribe on my will, assuredly it is true,
And I will, in happy accident, convert.
  For my sake do I fear that thee will,
  And in this hope, my will proves untrue.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet all they view is false, soft and doth guess,
To serve as illusion's playthings, dimming light.
How can I see the world through false blind eyes
When I see the fine art of thinking,
Each scene seeming to me a thing,
But being unwoke, Love, know it not!
My eyes, being clever, cannot read
The wide world, or to distant lands,
