Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy lips, and ears, and heart's desire,
In soft touches, their gaze I can scarcely disguise.
But my soul cherishes thy allure, knows it well,
And whispers in my ear, "This love I share, this sorrow,
Enslaved by thy gaze, and heart's own despair."
My eyes, and heart's delight, share to show
The depth of love that in thee lies.
  If thy heart believe, and I am proved untrue,
  The wounds of love I give remain for thee, beyond.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To torment my own soul, and force my dearest friend to weep
As if I am blind, unworthy to see?
It wrenches me from my own core, and binds
Another self, more cruelly, to your will;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, find
Our hearts entwined in torment, thrice multiplied.
Imprison my heart within your fortress chest,
But grant my friend's heart freedom in its place.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart be their guest,
For none can then oppress me in that space.
 


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim
The privilege of my love's possession.
He is your debtor, and he will pay
The cost, although I live upon your gross.
I have no right to dwell with thee,
other than by your taking what thou dost give
Thyself, thyself for me, to be thyself rekindle.
In him I trust I confide,
For thou dost gain by this trust, and I am stol'n
To dwell with thee, my dear heart.
  Then do not reproach thy servant who takes all
  Thyself, and all thy love


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
So canst thou my love excuse my poor state,
Than thou my desert a beggar struck in twain
Without our own pay, for well being with thee.
Thence comes it that my name receives a nickname,
And by and by, my state is ruined,
For to thy sweet help I return but in kind
Give my love away, not lose it for myself.
  To be frank with thee I say,
  Though thou know it or not, me out is loved
  By a kinship quite different.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thy outward thus with outward show is lost,
But my spirit in thee resides,
Which in thy spirit's book is laid,
A testament of our boundless might.
If my feeling be foul-suspecting eyes,
Thy inward love so hated is best,
For thy outward love finds sweet delight,
But 't it razes away, as sweet niggarding looks.
  For why should I frown upon thy show,
  Because thou no lie withstood my gaze before?


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet mistake it for my perception,
Defying the clear seeing of all.
My eyes, bewitched by passion's heated glare,
Are anchored in a cove where hearts take flight;
Yet 'tis thy soul that craves, so far,
That's for me, thy vision misguided,
In the sweet theft that thy sinful deeds do.
  For thyself, thy eyes have stolen thy light,
  And my spirit, aware, is entangled in this race.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime.
And yet, in my countenance, she walks on this earth
Like one who prays his vows,
For beauty lies in truth and no beauty can stay
When truth and beauty have strayed hence.
But why does he lie, and still believe in me,
When I am astray, and he knows it but ill?
For I have sworn by thee fair and true,
By thy love, thy truth, thy love's adoration;
  But when that heart turns from truth,
  I lose all trust in thee.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Suff' not silence in my soul to approve thy deeds.
Speak of my flaws, I will bear it not;
But if I falter in thy frown,
Imprison my heart in thy liberty's cell,
And make amends for thy deeds done.
If I lose thy mind, my spirit finds grace,
And spurns no words at all to make me bend,
Dissuade me of thy sweet name from thy heart,
And make sweet a vow against myself that I may abide.
  Such a man is love that in heart I find pain,
