Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy heart's desire, not mine, dear.
If I could teach thee how to make right
The injuries that besiege thy bosom's bosom,
What should my dear heart say, that thou hast no power to reproach,
To call the best of thy friend, to whom thou art so kind?
Be thou patient, patience's Plesios; I will not rebuke
If thou review my silence, though it bow doth bend.
  For I will not rebuke thou, though thou review mine gaze.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my soul that cherishes what they find unright,
Defying the disapproval of the flesh and bone.
My eyes, 'tis my heart's desire, and thy heart's wish,
Yet my bones, 'tis my will, and will with them remain undone,
Unless this universal love despoils thy part:
  Thy love, though much, is but a bare illusion,
  In my will, no hidden mark doth lie.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
For it is not love where two share,
But love, when contracted, doth love draw.
Therefore is my love grounded in truth and not just,
Seized as forfeit, to grace alone.
Grant me the right to love with equal frankness,
As thine own heart doth plead, and me thou gav'st,
By love's will, my sin here sworn.
Grant me the pardon, if my love appears feigned:
  By thy love, I've lost thy trust, and now my guilt is known.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
I, in love's terror, do detain
The blessed lamb, tame and free,
For in your boundless might, I can find
