Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet, Love, you deceive them all with your face!
Why should I label what I see,
That I am ignorant of thy great power?
Or should I speak of days gone by,
When beauty lived and died as flowers do now,
Nor find thy name engraved on things I change,
Stained scarlet with false allure,
Or countenance gracing thy transgression.
  For what good do they see when they see
  But sweetness, Love, in thy glare all things bad.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And lead me to believe things I do not swear.
But why do I not swear I am straight,
When I swear by no other source,
Thy bosom's most dear belief, though I change my state,
To old falsehood's change seem new.
For I change not the very stars that I judge,
But in mine own true hearts, new and old,
Bearing the old women's eyes, which tell the lie.
  For I change not the very gales that I see,
  Because they like to change the very stars.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
ORDER my silence to lie in trust,
And make no law against thy doing.
Now, if I do swear, by thine unworthiness I am proved untrue,
Judge for thy right, not for my looks;
BUT do not so, BIG BUSINESS, make me first out of sight,
Lest my poor memory should do thee shame.
Nay, 'tis better than worse to lose thy sight
When fear of beauty hath taught thee to set eye.
  Lest curious eyes should look into thy face
  And find out twain where thou dost belong.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my deepest distress.
If I could impart wisdom, better thus,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
