In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which in thee doth love that is askew.
That my soul affection hath in me a thousand flaws,
That mine own self-love in thee doth surmise,
In me a thousand errors surmise;
And 'gainst myself that my part of thee doth play,
Making love number one to my love,
Thyself thy dear heart's poor advocate, in me.
  For thy sake even of that mine own part do intend,
  To love thee more than they think they see thee.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
For it is not love that errs against thy own.
That is my lease, and as it is drawn to life,
Reserve it not in the fear of a stain,
Or, if it be stained, it rightly so.
O, what a happy home for sin has lived
Where grace and guilt are estranged,
Or where our souls find sweet release in love's sweet chain.
  Let us not to the state we belong,
  Kill us with thine own eyes, or with thy heart's all alone.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Returning swift messengers in happy hour.
But why should she wait, when her husband's might
Surpasses his might, and she hath no cause to fear?
For who shall stay longer than the home goddess
Can make her husband happy, though she lose?
Or who shall stay longer than the happy hour
To make the woman beloved of all her lovers?
  Me, mistress of your longings, shall prepare
  For your return, and ruin no more.


