Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
So vexate a wife my heart is wont
To go beyond what thy breath doth say,
And grinds on barren earth with dross,
Making a grave for love of thine and me,
Who stick together to wreak their cruel spite.
I will not let envy taint my thoughts of where
Thyself thy love doth live, though thou know it to be
From thine own self, which in thee doth live.
  Let no harsh words, no gentle pleas be stilled,
  Consider all, and in my heart, let them be still.


