Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy face's hollow, as mine, in mourning's sight.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, thy beauty's borrowed beauty, bright
Outshines those vain attempts at praise,
Lest sorrow's shadow mar thy beauty's face.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow's guise,
  When matched to thee, thou shalt never fade.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to weep face to face?
Even if thou wilt, then still I'll bear that pain;
For no shape or form can my heart choose,
Unless it wilt turn from thee.
Ah, should the will of thee cleanse my heart,
The shame of my friend's sorrow will be graved,
And I, made to feel sorry, will feel sorry.
Were it not sinful, then, striving to please,
To strip me of your love and remove
The weight of guilt from my friend's life?
  Ah, may I but resist,


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may live;
For thee, I give myself away for term of life,
