When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Although she knows me from their feasts.
That is my love, to her I testify,
And she thinks me a lie, unbound,
Although she knows full well my days are fleeting.
I tell her I am of clean mind and do confess
That I am one, true and never gone.
And she believes me, unconfessed,
And I am so cheery she thinks
That I am but a dream, flaunted and untrue.
  But think upon the contents of your heart,
  And she thinks the worst, and I am right.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike not with thy soft touch those lips that plead
Against the will of thine own root.
Speak truly: you love another; yet in my gaze,
Spare me the sight of wandering eyes.
O, plead for the love that in me sits:
My heart is for thee, and so should thy eyes;
But my soul is for thee in other respects too.
For thy soft touch on my heart impediments true
To thoughts of love's quiet cheer;
  And thus, in my sin, I find true distress.


