In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
That all men dislike in manners and look.
For I have sworn thee fair in thy love,
And in thy affection I find equal rights,
That thou in me canst not vex me.
Yet that is not all my dislike;
Thyself, that in thy breast doth live,
Thyself, that in thy breast doth live wanting.
  In me, thou gav'st myself most,
  To love thee that graces me most.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Since I cannot love thee through that bosom empty.
For if it be false that I do love thee,
Thyself, so often as thou art, is love's guest;
And for myself, that slander doth so thrive,
I cannot then claim I loved thee but in thy name.
Never believe, though thou robs thy honor,
That it may profit thee for a thought's embrace.
  For usury is not love that engrafts hearts,
  And thine own true love, so blessed, is false.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
The bloody goddess of choice, she sets free
Her lance, and false goddesses part,
Attending with them dances foolishly.
The lawful god of choice, she lends
Her power, and permits no crime,
But only good news to find,
Detracts what she should have supposed true,
And leaves us poor, wretched, to suffer her disgrace.
  Yet, if you read this carefully, remember not
  The hand that tells the story, for it's the master,
  And he is the true thief, who steals men away.


