And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May borrow from thee for a rainy day.
He lends thee for a rainy morrow,
And my wife and children take leave of thee;
So thy husband will bear the burden, the cost of assenting,
And me, the borrower, to be the care
Of thy husband's ruined house.
In him those children find their dear moments
When nature hath left its trace, and thou dost forgive
My slumbers by willful hand.
  But that thou thyself shall forgive me is much greater,
  The greater the worth of that which thou dost forgive.


