Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy skin's tresses like thine own, by thy beauty turned cold,
Divert to avoid the winter's blight.
Hearing thy beauty woe outdo winter's display,
My heart, touched by sorrow, begins to despair,
And bids my eyes outlive thy host,
Where thou shouldst succumb to pity's cold.
But why should my heart believe in woe,
When it knows well the love that once was true?
  The reason lies in where thou bestows thy light,
  For common ground obscures thy spring's rebirth.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It wrests me from myself, your tyrant hold
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Forsaken, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine mayst see
How much of thy glory I can devise.
For if thy will allow thee this boon,
More worthy than mine own to own,
The ransoms should be enough to buried me,
And him for my love, myself to trust,
The charter of my life thus!
  Within this boon I live and breathe,
  And still more to live, that my mistress may dare.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And that thou thyself art most graciously saddled
With all those which affect the least.
Thou mayst answer me, "Gracefully do I wish for thee,
And for that wish me best to own thee,"
And I will do that, knowing thy will,
To be the sole subsidence of my heart.
Thou mayst alter this wish in my will,
By changing what I wish in thee;
And I will be the earth rearing mother
To care for those who wish for their son.
  This wish I can own, and canst own nothing.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Yet will it not approve thee that I have looked on thee
For flaws in thy faith, though thou art fair
To see those bonds that in thee lie.
Therefore love's audit is but as a chase,
Which in thy heart's log will be found,
The tiger's hide, the dove's nest, all in one,
Which in thy heart's book will be displayed,
Of love's sweet obsession, and so expressed.
  If thoughts of love should come into thy mind,
  Thy love, so often has my will sworn,
  As proved, and thus proved, false.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet, Love, you deceive the world, with such deceit.
How can my eyes be true when they see
No truth in thy gazing eyes, yet strangely!
Why should my soul believe in shadows,
When it knows well the world's shared history?
Or should my eyes, which see, tell a different story,
To elevate the lie with purest light?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have swerved,
  And now to falsehood's allure they've turned.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she hath no part to do,
My vows I fill with falsehoods and ill affections.
I do love her despite this union,
And think her love most due, though it bowdoth not meet
To truths and beauty's truth seem untrue.
But why should I reproach her for my errors,
When I am guilty of transgressions manifold?
My love is so strong my well-contented defense
To her I confineeth;
  And so my defense proceeds in proof.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak of my lameness and I straight will halt,
Without reproach, tell the truth of my will.
Thou hast the better part of my strength now leased,
To thrust my spirit in another's direction,
And provoke another's rage to spite my honor.
If I defy this usurpation,
My spirit may yet find its strength in thee;
  Yet, against the growing tide, still I'll fight with loss and scorn,
  For slander's taste only brings forth twain
  My sport with strength beyond the roar of my own.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own worth, what thoughts or smells canker doth pluck.
Be not so swayed by schemes or hours,
As sorrow lends not weeks nor days,
That love, once in a bright star,
Shines brighter than despair or love's own blink.
Let those who call upon thy pain wane,
Be not swayed by schemes or hours,
For suffering blooms only brings forth twain
And whate'er thou wilt, by life's sweet balm.
  If any, by inaction wrought,
  Shall by inaction prove thy balm more.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
That my soul cherishes what it dislikes.
So thou frown'st not on me for my love's sake,
Nor on my naked love for thy sake,
For my love is that mine eyes crave and hold,
And for they see what clothes my soul doth wear,
They look into the wound and say:
"Had my love suspected thy loathing, it would have killed me,
To share thy heart's hatred, though it see'st twain
To shun the thing that saddles my heart."
  So thou frown'st on me, though I am thy friend,


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
For it is not love where thou dost lie,
Or at thy heart, at thine own table.
For if my loving gaze be true,
Thou dost false, and therein measure not love's part.
Then do not so, when I have new grief,
Stirring thy parts, as I will thee,
For they are counterfeit, and hence my sense
May be unwary, to bear thy disdain.
  But do not so, when I have new grief,
  For I have sworn thee fair, and thy love is sweet.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
The bloody ruination of a queen poor
In moments becomes the gain of love,
For if my babe be unthrift in worth,
Her love runs free, her love's value increase,
And I, in turn, gain by that loss amiss,
For having had a husband, wild and free,
My heart grew hard for a king's box,
To bear a still life, though she leave behind
Her lover's tender care.
  But if that be false, and I am proved untrue,
  No words were penned, and love was never knew.


