Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy skin darkly dyed by mourning's art.
My lips, which so willingly mourn
At thy touch, wound with sorrow, and plead,
That I, too, should suffer, like other, touched by grief's flow.
At thy name, I testify, and know it well:
Thy name, when heard, does evil restore
My heart's darkness, as other's weep,
At thy trespass, I repent, and turn around.
  For thee, I paint my name with days of disgrace,
  And other faces, as others take pity on me.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to share this grief?
Even if you repent, can I still be loved,
And you, my dearest, now my grief,
Can still weep for my friend, cut free from blemish.
Oh, should you find my heart's woe
In your heart's deep-rooted trench, I can tell
That my friend is left forlorn, alone,
At mercy of your woe.
  But here's the power: if you repent, I've still the cost,
  Of losing you, and of myself, by thee.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may live;
For thee, whom I gave my all, thou dost give away,
And I will render him sick, to be contented,
But in the least of these myself will die.
For thee to render him live is a sad penalty
And I am a miser, knowing what is best
To live on thine own poor hand.
As on the moon doth this sorrow appear
And all the rest hath its living parts to die;
  This my neglect hath brought me all these victories.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And like a sad slave to my will,
Whose weight I by fortune toil disdains,
Thy wantonness my love to bear,
And for thy sweet like to change hands
O'er thy sweet spite against myself adder
Thou my content that I on thy part do enjoose.
And do so, love; then in like manner to myself add
That myself offent might of thee sit
To the service of thy love, even so as to thee bow
  To that sweet self which in thee dost lie.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thy outward thus with mine is love's guarantee,
In me thou is my will, and true love's will,
Without this, secrets of the heart cannot be shown,
For thee to see them is not so clear,
As the blind do not see the silent husbandry at night.
If they see that thee gone and gone,
They look into thy past unwellness,
And find that thy present state is well;
  But why thy sins are so preposterous,
  The reason why so much beauty lies in thy defect.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But seldom, ever in thy heart's course,
To thyself, who in thy heart is painted.
How can my sight be true, when dimmed by tears,
Watching and weeping through the endless flight,
When thou thyself thyself thy mirror doth tell?
O, sweetheart, beware, sweetheart, of this beguiling grace,
For it blinds thy mind from the truth it knows.
  Only make thy mind's investigation seem
  To her, not his, to make it seem false.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime.
And, like false accusers, I use truth to my advantage
And use falsehood to win admiration,
But she knows well my days are past their prime
And I, in truth, deceive both truth and spite.
Thus, love, to avoid this, I maintain
Truth needs no gilding, for it shines so bright;
But when the glass shows the age inside,
The wrinkles on my countenance will tell a story
Of love, and your fair as any elf in gentle mood.
  If you would but embrace, step inside


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Excommunicate me not in public places,
But let my name rest where my body shall meet,
Although my soul elsewhere may find its dues.
O, do not so much as burn with thy looking glass,
Look into the blood that flows again,
If thou, in investigating this sin, shall find
A reason for my low state, and all my days blight.
Mark how one Irish blood touched doth flow,
The other not for his quickening nor his restlessness;
  And, for thy sake, provide for me,
  Since I elsewhere in this blot am stained.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy edge of wrong which I cannot right.
If I could set aside for a moment
The hurt of love, thought's fickle fate,
The harm of shame, injury of wit,
And all thine own pains would be well-spent;
Nay, 'twas not so harsh compared to life.
O, what a gift to impart,
To change the very circumstance of thy mind,
As thou art made worse by thy sins more.
  Such is my love, to change not thy state,
  To dwell with thee, wherever thou wilt.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
That in thee doth love those whom thou deserv'st.
Such souls inherit heaven's choicest blessings,
Preserving nature's bounty without waste,
In their own image, the worth of self-love being decreed,
Their faces bear the mark of their own worth,
Others but guardians of their brightness rare.
The summer's flower is dear to my love,
Although in thee it blooms and dies;
But that is my love's melancholy, and thee alone that doth live.
  For thee my love doth thine portrait animate,
  Which for myself d


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
With the rest of the world, where this love I find most evident.
For where is my essence, that thou so kindly grants
In thee that thou mayst call my name,
Or me, if thou wilt, be true and right?
Be thou my accuser, and I shall prove thee right,
By thine own fallacious argument, that on thy part
Thine own heart may be my defense.
  For to thy sweet good I prove such a thing
  As, 'tis true, 'tis true, and I love it not.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
With swift extremity and power to lift it from the ground.
'Tis not enough that she keep you close
To me, though I be whole, yet dear,
To keep you warm in my stead,
As frequently as you wane,
As if from the coldest night your hand
Calls, or springs from the ground.
Ah, may I but pause, and weigh my love's weigh,
As those whose strong arms can lift me heavy;
But, like a tireless performer, you will come and go,
Your love growing, from what you left in vain.


