So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim
The benefit of my love.
In him I am confounded, and hidden expense
Is taken, of my self-love, by thee.
How can I be poor when my love is my cost?
By thy sweet self-example I concede,
But he, in losing me, will live,
And I, in turn, will be poor, oppressed.
  This I know, and so I plead,
  That he pays my debt, and I lose him, on purpose.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
So will you, when I have strayed, take my leave.
Thy love alone keeps me from faring ill,
Ensnared as I am in others' hearts,
But those that number thee far, faring ill,
And for that treasure find thee wanting,
Ensnared as I am in others' wills.
And do them thanksgiving to that beauteous heaven
That for thy sweet love well may be remembered
  When all the world besides is had.


