Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy complexion, thy grace, and mock pity descended.
Tears of pity I long to see,
Lifting my spirit, like the lark's ascent,
From sorrow's depth, my soul soars towards skies,
Singing hymns of joy, my heart's contentment.
Yet, my heart, once full of thy love,
Singed bitter hymns, no longer full,
A sickness borne before true illness came.
  For anger's sting only cures my grief,
  And stealing thy pity, by thine own actions.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to weep,
Inflicting pain on me even more?
O, no, 'tis not enough; I must strive
To capture your essence in my breast,
And share it with tears, all the while tight.
What force can I then break, so steadfastly,
To see the torture no more is permitted?
I have sworn by your profound kindness,
By your love, loyalty, and trust;
Yet, in my madness, I have twisted reality,
And bound thee in the chains of my despair.
  For I have sworn thee fair, false


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Grant me the strength to keep thee dear.
In him dost thou lend thy sweet mind,
Without my knowledge it may be stolen;
And that my sweet love may in me be kept,
Making my deceased love's name seem fresh,
As if from thee deceased I bring forth
A new tale, after many a false one.
  This my silence is not so great,
  For I in myself do steal thee away.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose value nothing worth that thy will.
Thou dost give birth to my vexation,
And vexation even there with thy bending thine.
Thou hast my own dignity, to suffer me,
And in thy power, I derive great pain.
Thou hast the strength of laws to compel me,
And I too, in this, by thy bending self against.
Yet, by thy doing, I lose him,
And in this, against thy will, I lose him.
  For thee, against myself, I lose both men,
  and myself, against thy will


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thou hast the might to leave me alone,
While I fear that thou art the prey of false need,
And in thy will, against thy will, I find sweet sufferance.
I may not evermore acknowledge thee,
For I am weak in love, and thou art aware,
But in my will, against myself, I almost break,
Thy heart's loyal subject, for thou art aware.
  To keep from myself I say this,
  For thy sake will me overlook, if thou wilt leave me alone.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But that in thy looking glass thou dost deceive.
How can mine eyes be truly holy,
When they see but what thou dost show,
Making sacred war with the heat of eyes,
Whilst thy own clear self doth willfully hide?
Why should mine eyes be true, when they see
The world as they see, yet love as they see?
  Thyself for thyself sweet for thyself so doth live,
  To be hated as that which on thyself doth lie.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime.
Ah, sweet deception! But why do I not confess
That I am from heaven and that I in others see
Thy merit in numbers, determined by thy deeds?
For I would not welcome thy impiety,
Although thou were the first, and therefore must be promoted;
And, having climbed the ladder of good I know,
Knowing that better is by far the stronger,
To leave with the lout who in numbers doth grow stronger.
  Then, loving her, maintain thy disdain,
  For truth proves more fair to love


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike not with thy tongue but with my foot,
And do not torture me with thy unworthy moan.
O, teach not harshness to neighbor's son
But rather, love, grant me the honor
To suffer not in thy wrong but in thy right.
For nothing 'gainst me but by thy love,
So that thou mayst use my name more.
  I will not let envy taint my heart,
  For that slander which thou shouldst bring near.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my deepest sorrows.
If I could sketch the emerald of love
And celebrate each trait that graces thee,
The future minds would think it but a part,
A poet's tale, no more, they'd turn adieu.
My sorrow would be heightened, strengthened by words,
And love's affection efface heightened,
With gusto, it would fly, outlasting words.
  Yet, should a heart be moved by hurt and grief,
  Lest sorrow embolden its words with image.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my soul that cherishes what it dislikes.
So thou art my earth, my sky, my heaven's vast sea,
My earth, my sky, my moon's gentle face;
All these allude to thy love, and more,
Thyself out of thyself at will be found,
And even so thy love turns to other things,
Like the dyer's hand, or the rose's own flower.
  And still more, that mine eye may love it or thee
  That sifties all things to thy sweet self.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
As those whose hearts are stained by thy error,
And those friends that grant not to thine own cheeks.
For to thy sweet self sweet love find a crime
That to myself thouself beauteous cheeks must pardon.
Then canst thou grace me with thy love,
That through my sin thou mayst live again.
Sometime all men's eyes err, and mine eyes kind,
And in this service seem to lose their wills;
  So all men's eyes are hated, and meekness everywhere.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Velocities swiftly to your side,
And shields you from the allure of mortal shame.
Pity the captive, now alive and bold,
And comfort her, so dear, by alluring words,
Art not enslaved to mortal rage,
But only so near, may I trust,
With my own devotion, from thee to me,
The fiercest jewel of all your might.
  Pity her, for love keeps thee close,
  As she holds you close, she keeps you close,
  Or, if she keeps you, her love will not be.


