Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy lips crafted with false compassion,
Thy outward with outward good shows,
Yet eyes for their pity look kind,
Not cruel, kind, or proudest mark.
For to thy eyes my heart looks kind,
And thine own soul is kind, kind and bright
In others' works, but mine own kind is displayed,
The most kind of tableau in which thoughts take flight.
  In me, kind thoughts do come and go,
  But in thy heart's workings, kind thoughts at play.


