In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
That thou indirectly dost love those whom thou dost hate.
Neither are my ears enchanted by thy voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor taste or scent, crave the lowly touch,
In either case is there hostility,
In myself, 'tis so.
That is my nature, and I alone in this regard,
That I in thy servile parts do abuse,
Thyself, thy receivers, forgive thy offenses.
  For I do love thee through my own corrupted state,
  That thou in thy parts find my sweet satisfaction.


