So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim;
For such is my dignity now,
My power hath all the will of my heart.
Now to be thine is my main desire locked away,
And that my love may claim a place above.
As I will myself, to keep thee dear, I will devise
A scheme of love that may live,
In short, a truth of love that touches thee.
  This I shall do, and this will almost never die,
  For in this deed, our love's name is tied.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose argument is but to my will,
And proves me a worthless case.
Thou dost beguile the world, and she will show
Her hate, in hope that thee will answer.
But me, stubborn and unmarried in love,
Who in thy heart's will decreed time be,
And to be contented that thou stick'st not to die.
  Then do me a favor, and hope my death be kind.
  If thou wilt leave me, do me a favor,
  And I will be contented, though my life be turned cold.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thyself subject to my vision, thou art well aware,
Though I am elsewhere in the world,
Thyself a shadow of the time, far removed from sight;
For myself am in this knowing, and thy seeing,
Thyself quite clear anon, being flaunted,
To be believed but by a truer being's scythe.
Yet fear not, though I near present bewail thee,
That thou in thyself ensnare me there.
  For I will be true even as thou art me,
  Unless this my substance is untrue:
 


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But, Love, you deceive the living day.
My eyes, bewitched by thy sinful deeds,
Are moored within a harbor of the mind;
But if thy sinful deeds do find room,
My heart's desire, it wrenches from its course.
For to thine eyes thy image is truly live,
And thine eyes outshine, by niggarding thy sins clear.
  If thy sinful deeds prove so,
  More worthy I to share thy lowly seat.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she hath no such account, for my boon.
For she knows well my days are past their prime
And I, in her estimate, do approve
Her boast of me, though she knows not the story.
And, to be sure that is not false I swear,
The lie never touches me, though I swear it to myself.
But that I swear not, to kiss the time I do not swear,
To swear against common men's eyes that love never dies.
  For I never saw that debt of tears
  To swear against common men's eyes that love never dies.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Neglect me even further, if thou wilt,
