Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy lips, my heart's trade, are now marred by tears.
What worth the world, that deems it otherwise?
Or should the world, which cherishes thy love,
Bestow on my plight, fairest of hearts?
No pity lies within thy heart, for it dwells in
My plight, and thou, that wretched dost,
Bestow it upon my plight's cusp.
O, what a sorrow, to suffer this sight,
To feel the loss of a loved one so stricken,
And in this plight find sympathies, though they turn cold.
 


