So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may live;
In him dost thou derive all benefit,
And I, am assured, will be self-subsidy.
But him that lends me not to thine own sweet reward,
Shall the poverty of his tenants, by me defeated,
Him in thy tenants to alleviate their plight;
Thence comes it that my will is decayed,
And my great love's will ev'ry surrenders,
With forbearance and showing respect.
  For myself dost thou suborn me,
  Which I will repay on thy part alone.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose traffic is with thee, and not with me.
Thou dost wish that my deeds should be,
And that thy sweet love may again find favor,
Like to the bett'ring of a willing loan.
So do I lend, and that thou elsewhere bestows
Upon that which thou thyself dost lend.
That is why I lend not for myself,
But for thee, where is my good paying?
