Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy complexion's traitors, turned into dreamings.
Adorned in black, as mine, are you composed,
They torment my soul, and I, in mood,
Doing thee homage, as I watch thee waken.
Oh, let my heart mourn for thy beauty,
As mourning becomes you, in every trait,
Then I shall contemplate thy beauty, drawn
From all those sources which thy spirit doth derive.
  For thou art all my history, and I am one,
  And they, in their sympathy, but me, oppose.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
For surely, sweet friend, you must bear
The torture, the punishment, the blow,
If I am to be truly gone.
I must not let my heart be my grief's guest,
Nor let my friend's sorrow dwell in solitary plight.
If I lose you, I lose him, and he shall live,
For that same sin now brings me pain and anguish.
  His name shall be Bound, and I shall bear the pain,
  In losing you, my friend shall bear the proof.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may live;
For such is my nature, and thy best interest
To us it doth take, by our will forbear.
In him those jewels which I trust are spent,
The monument of my love, which I will treasure,
And he that pays thee for that trust,
Shall live a double dower, to bear thy joy
Under my love's wing, that I may never die.
  Thus, thou dost him serve, and I for him be content,
  He pays the toll of our love, and yet I'm a deceived man.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose traffic is with that which brings me low.
Thou dost love that beauteous goddess
Who keeps you as a watchman guarding the treasure,
And she, in her madness, is able
To steer thy moving parts to bear
The crooked weight of my soon to be determined.
Mad in desire, to have thy will,
Lest me, being made ill, should dare to ride
And haply of thy wishes, fail to show what hope
I have in seeing thee grow.
  If thy wishes are not met with success,
