Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But what sweet grace dost thou disguise,
That it may deceive the eyes?
How can my seeing be true, when thou art gone,
When thou art gone so well I see,
But that thou thyself dost deceive with my hearing,
And haply of my silence to thee!
O, what a wonder that my silence
Finds no voice, no truth to tell,
But hears thine eyes, 'gainst thyself out of sight.
  Thus have I often thought, Thou dressing thee out of sight,
  To lie with me, all alone.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she hath not the strength to say so.
That she hath, like a deceived husband,
A wife's eye to boot, heart to frown,
Forsaken that we both despise with distresses twain
How far I should take my leave, to render thee but straight.
I do love her because I see
