O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Lest my spirit should mar with foul humor,
And speak of my faults where thou art.
Nay, if thy unworthiness stirs my heart,
Thy hand, when touched by gentle art,
Return with a gentle sigh, a healing balm,
To me, but thinking on thy heart's need.
Cultivate mercy, in thy heart's core,
That thou mayst depart and I be cured,
No need for symbols do I long require.
  Still, plea against thy unworthiness; still I'll fight,
