Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my concealed distress.
If I could sketch the emerald of my heart
And celebrate each trait that graces thee,
The future minds would think it but a part,
A poet's tale, no more, they'd turn adieu.
My sorrow would be better used as fuel,
For future minds to see what kind of person I am,
And I, a tyrant, would be caught,
Unmoved, unwise, and foolishly.
  To let your sorrow accumulate, grow stronger,
  Than weigh the gains of lesser hurt.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my eyes that chide thee in sort of plight.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
For not every person hath a nature so divine,
But all have souls, and in them there is accord,
That due is due even to thy sweet graces.
For where is not so great as mine, where is my deserving,
And why is my deserving of thy love?
For me, beaming when thou art all alone,
Thyself as mine, is my deserving,
And thine as mine, by my love mixed with.
  How can mine be true when thou art all alone,
  Since that our nature calls thee mine, and thine as mine.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
The bloody goddess of my passion,
Paints my blood on the canvas of delight,
And brings my sweet sorrows to a standstill.
Yet, thou hast no reason to fear my grief,
For love's semblance lingers in my soul,
And, with gentle art, it may be disarmed
By thoughts of thee, its borrowed quill.
Ah, yet the worst is not so bad:
The worst is that which eludes thy cheer.
  If thy ill will prove so,
  I'll bear the burden, and bear the smart.


