Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy complexion's trappings cunning and cruel.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may thy sweetheart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
To weep for me, and for myself,
As you bide, through my heart, your will
As I, a confined space, swallow down
The bitter remedies for my friend's woes.
Yet, should you find these gripes
 painful, and I begin to despair,
Grant me the strength, to bear your disdainful will.
  Such pain can only be so intense,
  And mental bounds, that bind us all,
  Are far from clear, and far from clear to thee.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may live;
For if thou dost give them thyself to thine own hand,
Thyself thy sweet favor will bear,
And I am bound to bear that which thou dost give.
This myself I give myself, to be new
Upon thy recommendation, and upon thy lays
Thyself to bear a ne'er-substantial interest.
This I give unto myself to pay thy rent
And for myself nothing at all surmount
The world's largess given thee for a rainy day.
  This I give unto myself to give away,
  And thou dost give back


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
So will you, and me, be contented
With that which thou thyself shalt give freely.
I will not vex thee with my disobedience,
While thou dost vex me with thine own part,
For I will oppose myself, knowing thy will,
To be contented with that which thou dost change.
Though I will defy with thine own part,
I will not alter thy spite, being content
With what thyself dost alter.
  For I will be contented, even though thou take
  That which thou dost alter, by change I think.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
But if it err, not from those lips that lie,
Stained scarlet with deceit, and false vows sworn,
By all the world's jests, thou art assured
That I am thy rock, the lodestar of my mind.
No other can claim such heavenly crown
As mine, of thy love, for thou art mine,
And I am thy faithful subject, subject to thine,
Enumerating every dower, thy reason so.
  If it err, not from those lips that lie,
  For I am thy earth, the compass that my mind.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But with mine eyes that power hath no part.
How can my soul love thee to what thou dost show
When it knows well the beauty concealed?
How can my eyes love thee to what thou dost tell
When they see but what thy sweet self doth show?
Now see what good turns mine eyes for thyself do:
Mine eyes all out magnify thy virtue,
And thyself to thyself atoned for thy neglect.
