When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
For whose worth do I scorn to blush when I am dead?
No, I have not thought of alt'ring things which lie,
And in my sins do I tend towards falsehoods.
But methinks I am as fair as any child of light
And, for thy true esteem, so great,
The only sin I cannot hate is to swear.
Thy beauty's brow is as dark as pitch-dark slate,
And thou in it appears darkest as morning suns;
And even so is my love's fair complexion,
  If thou wouldst but change his place.


