So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May nothing be left but disdain and fear.
Beware of this fiction, which in my brain
Causes the deception of such gadding
As poisons my trusty repair graces.
For your sake I construct my dwelling on trust
Without my knowledge or love;
To trust in this my mind is put
To trust in an unwavering trust,
And trust my soul with a something more secure.
  Thus, thou shrewd of my deed; a miser
  Launds of genuine virtue on my hand.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
The weeds may raise far,
In some number theirs thy will make more they grow.
How taxing must it be for him to roam thence,
That wander his foot on the sand, thrice the gain?
What audit of all this hath ensnare him,
Making tattered finds in his walks and every part,
That brass will hold thee in place,
Sufficing his long hours rolling to death.
  No, none can wrong thee in this trespass;
  All lives must he arrest, so dwells in thee.


