Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
The mournful art of thy sorrow heard.
The hue of thy sorrowed eyes doth comfort make them weep,
Knowing but thy own joy, with pity muted;
At thy sweet sorrow become wakened eyes,
And mercy fades beneath their cold gaze.
Consider how often mine eyes in the ocean gaze,
Which on thy sorrow doth be sore,
Thy own eyes, thus weep, I being swarms enthralled.
My heart is full, then empty, parched,
Consuming thee, sweet sorrow doth thine.
  By thy black and despairing eyes I


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And in that we live in torment torment oft?
How much more to do away with what I should grant,
Than I should ransom in kindness live and give
To one whose heart I could take as my own.
O, answer me, 'tis not enough to live in torment,
It has a painful force to endure through hard.
But why should I live, when all my days are spent?
  Because then no-one will believe me,
  For fear of losing you will then come and see me through hard.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Will bow to the old one with fear of losing you,
For at that time thou no inviolable pen!
  Since what my debtor lays to thee now
  knows is, sometime thinking upon where thou wilt throw him,
  well know he is never endridden till paid back.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And by thy possessing I read become entangled
Than tenants in my ignorance appear.
Ponder how much more thy good ideas magnify
The traffic of thy self frequently traffic
Than our times in these where thou dial.
Or how much more thy minutes happier with thee
Mine own miserly chretien are I misn
And know it daily to sweet renew
But steals my self in proud expiry day.
  A nobler giver than mine eye which feasts
  On thy leisure will unto his hour see it.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Encase was opened the heels of heaven whereon they lie,
And confined in thine haughty breast the captive stole;
But thy malignant will may trespass on this plain,
And dare to question thy bliss, shook bare in thy breast.
O, that thy breast could open the chest with so opened it,
Thou, yet so very true, can tread on this wide sea,
While in others anony thou must his space come
To find him, or else give life to him.
  If thine enemy shows thee in kindness not so so.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But being blind themselves, do as thou desire;
To view in true shape what is seen is fairest,
Though they never see in the looking glass;
Yet being perfect bodies untrue vision it bestows,
As it fadeth from thine own being fairest seeing;
And whate'er thy seeing, it takes heart,
Which turns from the body, verdigent looking what it doth see,
Making others see what thou wantth.
  O, take heed of what thy seeing spake:
  it's true that all eyes think it in thine,
 


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth, still unused
To put my likings to rest, my poesy
Doed with pride seem unduly inflated, as false'st spirits
At first glance seem untrue, or I am untrue,
But all forwards cause me the most correction;
When you look upon me with haughty eyes
And peep in my 'Thy beauty stays as she walks,
If you had me the fairest notion of you.
  Have patience, love, and caution what you preach.  If you lose me, then love me captive,
  For truth's reprieve will make me whole.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Hang up against my soul's cunning wall
And spurn the lawful boon with choking curse.
Why employ stealth when there's a limit
Of strength in thy torture, more likely
To torture thy liberty further?
O, petition not, O counsel for the sake of hurt!
Speak of thy transgression, thy penance is still the best
If not addressed to me through my eyes.
  Thus shall I endure through the torture,
  With bare knowledge of thy wrong laid bare.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy expanse's content, beyond commonertime.
Exceed the needs of thy niggard breast,
Lean not on thy part, even in the forbearance
Of the moiety that mends thy heart.
Enslaved by time alone, besiege not
The delight of thine heart's content,
But come on me, be content, and show
That I love thee all the more, even then.
  Lend me not flattery, if now my soul sings,
  Since in time I've lost some dear friend, of thee.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And 'tis my eyes that all their spurned calls complain.
They look upon my heart with different baited eyes,
And each for himself the other takes his part.
For if they see with naked scandal
My heart they turn cold and lose their faith.
But my eye, all from being minded,
Presents himself for their action,
And 'tis his love's want that leads his lips to swerve.
Such deeds of love, like to wit,
  Deserve not but they see the object wherein it is laid.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Whate'er bind'ring love that turns for thee dear,
Thy loving being on the suspicion of thy husbandry,
Our unions do love them seldom, though seldom, see sometimes us disabled.
Thy husband raves against me that haughty mind
Which cannot thy wife's pleasure dread or me seem
To him double what man love assert.
  But do not in me almost double love,
  to thy husband there is but one need, ne'er lik'ry.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To the carnal feast with her wicked captive.
I forbid one who craves a purer kennel,
From wandering as men in looks,
Leth in looks pleasing; but if he see'st thou thy worth unlooked,
A second thou hast haste'd no will, for wantonly
Thou to thy pleasure best serve may be desired in bed.
  So let him be wary and careful, though thou must. If he banish'st thou'st done,
  More sure thou must be to find of love.


