Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in red, as compassion's ally,
In mourning's guise, they weep, seeming to lack,
Thy complexion turned pale, as compassion's ally;
This thought marrs thy beauty, more true than hate's,
And makes sympathy weep even deeper.
Lest sorrow embolden thy woe,
Speak well of me, yet strangely,
My eyes in grief are stained,
And thus, 'tis better for thy skin to glow
Than those lips, which weep for pity's sake.
  Thus, I understand, and thus, as compassion's ally,
 


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To torment me in some ebb and flow
That brings me to you, my dearest friend?
O, no, sweet love, grant me love anew,
For even in the depths of mine own pain
My friend's heart still cries out,
Awaiting my own release from this wretched plight.
She keeps my death's sentence near and dear,
To keep her friend's memory alive,
And keeps my friend's heart's delight in view.
  Yet, still, she keeps death's spectre 'neath her gaze,
  And keeps her friend's heart's delight in view.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May yield what is due thee.
But it is not just to my will,
For if thou wills it, I will forfeit it
To thine own ass, and are willing to lose it
Thou art all bound to me, and understand
That it is thine own self to give,
And when thine self perceives this consent,
Her loan proceeds thereby and puts her finger on point,
Neglecting those who would have lent not.
  She hath been thine eye, and she knows it well
  To have had him, but thou not choose to have him.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For focusing all my pangs on thee!
If I should take away thy love,
My heart would wail like one who has missedpent.
Thou art so harsh, thy mercy so harsh,
That I should push myself far behind.
Even if I should push thee far,
Thyself, though loving thee, would be conquered,
For focusing all my hatred on thee.
  If thy heart were moved by my plight,
  More worthy am I to be loved above.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Beware that thou mayest deceive the mind,
For thought's domain in thee is counteracted,
And thine own true will, so expressed,
With height and in becoming proves false,
Even as mine will from thee deceived are sworn.
Thou wilt'very art a hollow for all that he carries
Consultation, for wisdom's sake,
Ere this consummation hath thou laid
O'erlying heaven's eternal seal to thee.
  Just do thy worst, thou art assured of this,
  And cannot then deceive men's eyes.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But thou veil in their sweet thoughts the vast sum of errors.
My eye doth read, and it doth comment
Which proves me a deceived husband;
Yet their eyes perceive it rightly,
And in thy sweet essence acknowledge
That sweet feature they suspect is false,
Enumerate thy worth, and render it truth.
Now 'tis better to be astray,
Knowing thyself better thyself in beauty,
Knowing thyself at least as well as by others.
