When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth, virtuous lot.
But I love her for believing me true,
And suspect she knows not all kinds of days,
That she not only doth deceive me with false affection
But doth on cool insatiable quest make,
Strength to face and strength to hide my dearest parts.
Thus do I pine for false beauty and wanton lie,
Though for our love our looks were alike,
Even so are they but wrought of solid steel,
In repair of a despised husband.
  But my love still doubts me, and well knows
  She thinks me unjustified; and thus she doth


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Within thine gentle eyes this eternal correction lies,
A new infection contracted, wild and free.
What right do I have, when my name is torn apart,
Drained of the blood that once was thy health?
My eye, now basking in thine eye's indulgent aid,
Presents me as if it knows what it should do,
Indeed, it knows what it should do, to lend but one tend.
It knows what it should do, and what it should not do,
To lend thine own fair name to another's vile deed.
