Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
That no firm believer or scion of heart
Cannot soothe thy inward gaze from thee.
Thou 'gainst false heart, false heart's advocate,
Thou this heart with whom my opposition hath doth meet,
On thy private virtue, so sweet, hath strayed,
Savagely among the throng of hearts.
O, but with mine own eyes more truly view,
These windows, so bright, lie so far from clear,
To see where thou heart's true whereabouts doth lie.
  Where thou cannot or will not penetrate, see is this,
  If


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
As swift messengers to every fling,
Fade down to earth, where all motion is done,
And gives still more earth to reap,
Possessing hope, and water to supply,
A joy she can never more boast.
Oh, could she but resist, and comprehend,
The need for flour, the perfect confection,
That she, so fervent, would gain such heavenly boon,
And lose not less, her spirit's wealth still unloosed?
  Yet still, loving her, she will abide,
  Unfettered by the chains of fleeting hours.


