Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
I engraft you that have more to give.
Thou art the tenth plunderer in all my amiss,
With what I by others gave thee,
Hath magnanimously made mine own possession new.
When tyrants' wills are to give away,
They look on you too enlarged, too kind,
They draw you to their wanton roof
And for you that inherit heaven's share create
A shrine where your love is stored.
  Just make thy wish and mine, and thou willst appear
  I'll be happy to let thee live.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For thought's dominion knows no end, neither contracted nor planned;
Whatever thoughts or secret heart concedes,
In thee, it will be held as ransom.
Behold what I've wrought, thy mortal form wrought,
Thy brain loves it, a begotten creature,
And puts it to test, though we beg for it.
For thine eyes are windows, and thy heart's view
Intends to spy through those windows, unseen.
  In me, they're both but mirrors, though we've gone hence,
  And still, that thy mind can see is right.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But to thy false taste sweet perfection set.
My poor eyes, cunning spies, are
Upon thy glass, and see that it never shows
What beauty doth beauty make or hide under it;
Therefore in false taste some measure deceases me,
Knowing it is not good to show what I see.
But why should my poor eyes like to see
When thou false lovest those perfect eyes?
  Thy blushing is but as small as thy moan.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A kinder being from whom all ill I am swarn.
So far for love the unprovoked brand
Of truth which I outlive my days must preach
To every fair whom chance invades and insults.
Since I did love you truthfully, that sweet knife
Of truth whose worth most tells what love doth know,
When minutes struck and minutes fled,
Dissuade from the heart my false knowledge.
  Unless this then were and true, my love would stay
  Unbroken, and in that which loves backward.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Spare not the jest of speech with thy moan,
Nor throw away empty words that atone for loss.
Grant not a tempest's night a stormy morn
To dry the earth where winter lays waste,
Cheap the leaves with thy bounteous pond,
But come upon me swift; thus, I fear not
The worst, unless this wickedness be
Hung with thy spoil of bright Time's decree.
  Yet, love; still thou mad with desire;
  Kill not when I no longer wish I have less.


