In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Neither end nor pleasure to all.
No, it is the other kind that brings me sorrow;
The gentlest poison, if I see it,
Resures my stubbornness with that bitter sweet smell.
I have seen the dyer's hand cure
The sickness that my poor visage brings,
Yet that my rose so weak and bare did give;
I think well of that, yet strangely ill at thee,
For I perceive in thy love how to show
What virtues to shun in a loving husband.
  Only in such sort of injuries do I strive
