Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
To those that believe in thine eyes are false lovers;
For to false hearts 'tis the offender that steals their sweet.
But should they understand, and trust in thine eyes,
Thy lover is not true, but souls and graces them.
Therefore should they shun the world, or at least part
Into the hell, which the law of thine eyes
Denies them liberty to know.
  For 'tis the sin of loving those who do not show
  The kindness, the love, and the least of all thy state.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Bringing flowers where they grew, sweet as hell,
In the sweet vices of her spirit's delight.
You are the mother of my blossoms,
Adorned with memories of my lovers' bright days,
Adorned in red with warmth, delight, and so,
I send them back, joy and sorrow, in joy and pain,
Each fruit plying, each vale doth bear,
An ever-renewing song, of sweet delight!
  So too, may you sing of love, and still find delight,
