Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gaze once upon my sorrow's endless plain.
My lips, which crave for tears, now retract,
Like hollow vessels meant for the weeping pearl;
My heart, once full of your mournful cries,
Grew weary of the sweet scent of blood loss.
In me, their eyes, though they weep, remain,
At mercy of the loss, made worse by thy fall,
Thine own eyes, 'gainst themselves, by pity's play unsealed.
  So they comfort themselves with thy remorse's sweetness,
  But that their sorrow may appear in vain.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To bind my dearest friend to share in anguish's night?
And if my friend's heart be true, so are mine eyes,
Their nightly countenance cruel and hard to read.
If false hearts should open their eyes, and friend's countenance fade,
Then should my eyes weep for thee and weep for me.
But 't is not enough to torture me alone;
My own true heart, half-blind, can see,
The depth of love's darkest plight tell no tale.
  If my friend's name should reveal error,
  My plight would be more dire than at first


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may live;
So thou executor of me
Will forfeit yourself to that sacred title which thou dost use
And in it shalt find his heirs, all deserved,
And he will pay an executor's fee of me
For me not profaning thyself nor thyself alone.
The title of thy executor is double and therefore thine
And thine executor shall be my executor;
And his payment is not usurious nor to thee suborned;
The mere ornament of thy good name
  Shall in itself so forfeit my life.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Than woman's will doth cleave to that due;
Whose appeal to thee is not enough,
Her heart's main will abide, but slight offense prevail.
Such unity doth love engrave,
Which is attainted with equals, in pleasing shape,
And to thee belongs worth's due;
Thou, on fair Helen set apart,
Thou alone beauteous woman's land.
  In graces of thine, to thee belongs beauty's treasure,
  And thus, by thy controlling friend, he lives.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
O, sure it is true, but that thou wilt depart,
The earth may reproach thee for its error,
But I, the earth, basest doth believe thee
To live among thine unadorned spawn.
Thou art my rose, thy logs, thy deeds show,
Mye, a pleasing soul, far too rare;
None can suspect thy manners nor manners graced,
When in thy sweet manners behold, being sworn.
  Still, be true, and then believe me,
  My love is but a disguise to hide.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But never to thee truly tell,
As a widow's seed, they die, empty marvels.
My eye, ignorant, readies the ambush,
And spurns the kindly deed,
For thy fair complexion with such cunning stealth
Doth ambush her husband and steal away beauty's bower.
Yet, Love, guard thyself, with vigilance so;
She keeps thee in her breast, where secrets she keeps,
To make her husband alive and live another's life,
  As a tomb should be, where life fades and memories die.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth, unbless true.
And thus false love appears to me,
Though not so pure and bright as mine in school,
And bett'ring the mornings with bright afresh,
My love for her I feel shamed;
Then gullible I do pledge my heart,
To chase birds with dreams that hap.
But, alack, I believe her, despite this swerve,
I never try to prove her false face.
  For she who alters not hath spurned me,
  For I have loved one she failed to set.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Profit not to do thy worst, fear, disgrace
My life, and in it thou break'st truth.
Why should I bow to thine adverse policy,
When I am stained with transgressions of trust?
My life is at its core, and thy inward glare
Enslaves my soul, more harshly still;
To save it thou dost endow'st with foul habit,
A second life, with heavenly salve.
  Yet, love, increase thy light, for it bends
  Towards the shadows, soothsayers' disdain.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my deepest concern.
If my heart should stir within the steel of death,
It will know that letter, indeed,
And in its place, lies wisdom;
But not haltingly, love, will reply,
"Had he but learned to write love's own gauge,
Arol'n a visage like his grew;
Though not so, would his visage bear the weight,
Making beauty wear more harshly."
Called to account, O churl; still not aloof,
Th' expense lies where he pays it; in vain,
His debt now pays it


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And for my sins mine eyes their most dreadful bark.
Till they see my nature do show thee
And on their heavy heart's most false defense kind;
Offending eyes, sorrow ensues,
And love runs after them kindling the wound.
Mark how with my sin mine eyes with my tongue
Which, though true, do swear by each other,
And 'gainst myself a thousand groans,
And for I deny that I love thee quite so.
  And 'gainst myself a thousand blunders--'  Since silence hath no name,
  For sweet falsehood's only witness


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Like thine own heart, to whom mine eyes belong.
For if it be false love, then thy eyes in general
Are false, and therein to lie imprisoned,
Whate'er thou know'st thou to whom thyself thou gild'st.
Or, if it be true love, whose hearts are so dumb,
Give grace to my shapely frame, thus mak'st
A perfect doom, made before thy sight is spent.
  Then may I stand, nor that which thou gild'st,
  Resist temptation, for I love thee, and thou alone.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
Till thou catch the fleeing bird, tame and bright,
And gently sway it back and forth, soothsayers claim,
And do as thou wilt, of thyself shalt survive
In thine own hand, by thy pleasure restored.
What hast thou wrought that thou mayst not reproach,
Or else disgrace, till thou have it done?
  Thy greatest gift, thou art so, and canst not reproach,
  To do what thou wilt not dare.


