Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy cheeks boast painted for thee, and my heart,
Vaunt in their sorrow as it does now,
Despite their cries, turned away.
Look to the lids, the windows, and the reedless night,
For they but softly bask in thy fair play,
Adorning thy face, as they gently sway.
Were I to lose thee, 'tis no grave transgression,
For my love engendered beauty, and still shall stand,
For in their embrace, my spirit shall endure,
  In light of thy beauty, and their despair.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To find true pain in what I despise alone?
For how can I love thee when you control
My every thought, every glance, is tied to
Your affection, and thus to avoid?
Till I, the captive, return home unbroken,
With open arms and a love that's sweet,
With what I fear will be stolen away.
  Woe betide you, should you, who'd leave me alone,
  For your sake, I'll bear that loss, if need arise.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim;
With this coming to my notice, it will be determined
By trust of many, yet with thy will most assured;
And it is thy will, to whom I am so kind,
To trust in me even so as it appears
To my shills that I am truly poor;
And to thy help, I send such chips
As brass doth play their part, so smart
That, if the nerves do steal the pawn;
  Then he, of all his glory, will win this hate
  For myself, for which I have no part.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
More than enough my love that pales next to thee.
Thou hast much fouler art than my love's art
And niggard my will to boot my name from thy stick.
Thou hast stolen from thyself to strive
And for just recompense my deeds by thee.
I have thought through thy will to spite,
Yet here I find my mistake;
Thyself thy foe, and so should I be,
To spite thy will, and to be hated as hell.
  I will fight thee to spite, and be the stronger,
  I think myself to be,


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
If some alien spirit dwells in thee,
That mine is not thy will, but thine, behold,
Thy eyes may perceive thee from me, but 'tis not so;
Unless they be spies of the world,
They'll come to thee and steal thy heart, bewail it misandry,
And mock thee when thou seeest thyself depart.
  I am not thy will, but thine, and thou dost lie,
  To lie against the wall and speak good of me.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet do not perceive, by what they do see?
They see true, but flawed and blind,
They mistake beauty for all that's plain
And steal all worths that their eyes may measure.
They look into blood, and there see
A myriad of faults and blunders;
Yet heaven's help, Love, do you count
The faults and o'erswept beauties beset with errors,
  Thy fault be'st thyself to blame if thou wilt prove thee true.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Despite her cunning crafty ways, control;
I would not trade my life for another's plays,
To chase one last chance of undeserved fame;
She knows better what she pretends to sell,
And I, in turn, believe her false tongue,
In this love, confessing my sins,
And spurring me on a fiery morn,
A tale of right and wrong,
With tears, lies, and endless praises.
  In this love, I find truth's sweet guide,
  That love's truth may well beaned, if sweet and gullible.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike not with thine unwritten mind but more,
Spare me the deceit of choking up;
Let not my passions infest with reproof,
But let my humble moan in quietude be:
O, wish me well, if thou couldst answer
"Had I slept with thy sleep-beaten mind;
Had I known thee to roam far,
To seek kindness in others' hearts,
A bliss that others couldst not assent."
  Awake, O wretch, do not so! I have known
  The sickness that from thy bad eye arose.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own worth, by thy tongue-tied hand.
Though my eyes may wander, yet can't reach
The heights thou dost call, in thine own hand,
To princes' dreams, thine own true soul doth stay.
Grant me the grace, to speak truly,
Of thine own true self, so blessed,
That soul in thy heart may shine!
  Seek thou to claim what thou dost withhold?
  By self-example, do not kest me here,
  To set eyes to ponder thy own name beyond.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my unsightly eyes that watch in darkness show.
Observe how one's affection hath an end,
In his beloved's affection, the true part is given;
So thou, thyself, slanderer than my sight,
In love's own might, thou dost watch in loveliness behold;
And that eyes thy love doth breed in thee,
Hath pity's flattery watched over,
And myself most proud plays the part in view.
  If thy love were grounded in truth,
  Then should my sight comment, 'tis no love at all evident.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Since for thyself art so blessed, canst thou not lie?
No need for thyself to show thee love's face,
For thyself art so blessed, so blessed dost thou derive.
Then from thyself shalt thou derive this love
To propagate love and true love,
Though thou own not thyself dost lie.
Spite my heart, I love thee thus,
Whoso doth thine own self conspire,
Whoso dwells within thee, thou dost keep.
  Seek'st thou to claim what thou dost withhold?
  Thou art to claim


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
I shackle my servant to keep
The wretched bower's hour-glass from falling;
Then, if the wailing mother
Shines down and lifeless goddess falls,
I summon up my servant, O, to help
Thewail away the wailing goddess;
The sharpest rowel cannot urge
My heart to speed, for passion throes
Within the wailing's throes, and shadows flee.
  I hasten to the shore, where the waters impale
  The bier to remove the spray;
  Still, I fear, the wailing wind will follow



