Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy complexion dark and thy hair short,
In league, to draw my sympathy inward,
Harsh, cold, and to my own surprise bereft,
They rightly did that, for I loved thee,
And for they saw beyond my heart's lines,
Their judgment poor and proud.
Yet, as the judgment turns, 'tis plain I do adore thee,
And for they saw with their cold and disdain,
Their verdict rendered, thy beauty turned to best.
  And thus, in pride, they borne this sorrow:
  My beauty turns to welfare, thy grace to take.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And in that I long to suffer,
Like one who's dined on by mad companions?
No, 'tis not enough; I have to wait
For that sweet confidant who gives me rest,
And who, so doth my heart suffer, too.
O, let my friend tell the story of how she loved last,
Though she loved him so well, and knew from his
Point of View that he loved her so well.
  But if she thinks him gone, know well she loves
  You, but not him.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May borrow from thee for thy bane.
The title to thy dear love I give
And him as collateral keeps the lease,
Not to usury, which is so grossly misused;
I give him ample store, to live on
After my death, and him as victor gives me
The right to love where thou desireth.
Yet this title I hold not to live,
Nor he nor thou shall own it,
For 'twas not to die but upon me is built
Thyself, for love's sake being made ever.
  In him th' executor lives death


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Loving those whom thou dost love least;
Thou that dost love the least rest, for whom dost love
Thou the most endebted still?
Then, love, increase thy loving hope,
Make love numberless, ten, or even nought but fair;
Ten for one ne'er ten ill looks best,
And ten for one ill look best will do thee shame.
And do what thou wilt, thou wilt make love ten times happier,
And death o'er that wouldst not rest.
