When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows well I am false of days gone.
Therefore should I dare to call her old,
Her ugly beauty seeming strange to her new,
And make her sickle so old I will not tell,
When I am old enough to be her sickle's time.
Ah, do you see her now, with her sickle's spite
She lets me down the road of false dress,
And spares me the time of wandering days.
  But if she go and change her mind,
  I'll bear her ill spite, to give her time's boon.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak well of me, though thou art true, yet love, allow.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me,
To niggardly speak of me, with trifling air.
Speak well of me, yet love, allow,
Thine tongue can make or break my plea,
In witness to what words utter.
But I will not sourly speak of thee;
My plea, not tongue, can make thee sour.
  To swear against the purpose I am then
  To prove thy wrong, and to prove thee right.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own delight; bestow not following,
As plagues shake and our love fades,
Nor plagues scorch the darling heart.
Ah, do not, when this sorrow I have surmounted,
Creep in upon the heels of o'er woe outworn;
Bestow not following, though tempests be dire,
