O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Lest my soul be reprieved that I should speak
Of thy transgressions, too profound, and true,
Profitless to pardon thy name from higher ground.
If I could paint the hue of your dear friend
And sing anew each virtue and charm that graces thee,
The world would cry, "This poet lies;
But beauty's etymology cannot be so traced
Nor the fathomless to reveal thy wrong."
  My love, so long as eyes can see, is for thee mine,
