The wiser mind would deem it but sport,
And to write of thy curls, I add,
Thy visage, my dear, would bear a story;
A fancied beauty, in truth, I cannot know,
But sweet friends, by thy beauty making clear.
  If thy beauty were but but a dream,
  I scorn not thy name, for it was but a dream.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And 'tis my soul that cherishes what they find unright,
And 'tis my soul that weeps at such breaches.
Neither do I blame thy deeds, though thou mayst feel them,
For I but pity those who do show thee virtue,
And I hold thy disapproval in such sort
That in my woe I seem to approve thy deeds,
Although he that doth such a torment wake,
For I am stained with many o'ers and errors,
And 'tis my soul that praises thy deeds,
And 'tis my body that doth so cheer thee up.
 


