Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may live;
For thee, whom I send home with great care,
Myself will be confined in thy chest,
Without your knowing it, either.
Till I have hidden in thee this treasure which thou deserv'st
A chest so dear, which husbandry will treasure and secure,
With title to myself never to lose.
But fear me, if thou deserv'st me this chest,
Do not steal this away, for it is thine alone.
  I fear thee, and so shall I,
  For thee, my treasure, is thine alone.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
