Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But those windows that clear the blind do see,
Where heaven's ornaments and the sullen moon
Are strung in the living day set against the wall.
Why should poor beauty complain
That heaven's window shows what it fears to see?
Why should the living day complain
That heaven's loving windows blunter see
Thy living form clearer than living eyes?
  O, know, Love, in this loving verse,
  The living day doth thy living flowers lie,
  And living eyes out of sight are sweet spies.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she hath roamed and changed as she I;
And for true minds and true affection
By age and nature kindled by thee lie.
But why should I trust her I might say
That I have seen her change in state,
