Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy complexion, thy softness, and wide eyed,
Are flawed, causing grief to bide;
My heart, they mock, and do so,
As if it knows, by missteps, that I am lost.
But why should I care, if I cannot find a care,
For in thy shadow, I find love,
And in thy shade, I find light,
Finding joy in the love that I adore?
  For what good is beauty if it fears not beauty?
  By thy shadow, I learn, and be enchanted.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And to find delight in what I despise most?
It graces me, it brings to my heart desired sight,
And I, a tormentor, share in this love.
At other times, the suffering I cope
By pointing out failings of others' face;
Yet in your heart's table, I find the solution,
A fixer's game, where you outlast me.
  So I, a tormentor, try to discern
  The path to solace you've chosen, for my sake.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Will depend upon thy quietude.
He that is willing will pay the price
Of a willing borrower; his will not
For worms to come and claim his peace
If it err than thy own. He that is willing will show
How to make a default on my dear love
By willful choice of what I am willing;
I have put myself above all others,
And he that is willing will show what is best
To lose me through my own unworthy game.  Then may I dare to sell what is best
  To that which is best for me and thee.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And that, to repair, thou deserv'st in me.
Ah, do you reproach Fortuna on my part,
That capricious goddess of my woe,
And in thy trespass, I am guilty,
And grievously hurt.
What can I do but hold thee in my woe,
And that, after losing thee, I restore
To health, assured of thy love?
  Thy fault, if I can devise some remedy,
  This will be my glory, and th' offender.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thy outward thus with outward show is crowned,
But those same tongues that receive thee farther still,
Are in the same with those ransomt with thee
To speak of things farther,
Thy inward self still farther is from thee.
For although thou inwardly come,
In some measure farther than my due,
Thy inward love proceeds still, and then some part
Of my due still farther from thee.
Mark how with thy outward show thou lays
A burden on the heart that in thy fits
  Receiving from thy inner worth gives life to trace.


