Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Wounded me with disdain, yet love alone my soul affords,
Lest sorrow stirred within their breast does pour.
Thus have I slept in thy sweet kindness,
And thou, when I was bereft, hast come to grief,
Yet that my sweet love remembered not stain
The fairest stain found in thy guilt's thrall.
Were it not sinful, then, striving to mend,
To draw clean wounds from thy past,
And to view thy marital bedwetting plight
  As vile a sinner, when lust takes the lead.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To suffer the loss I cannot awake,
Believeting torture to my own core?
Or if I choose to let my heart feel the pain it commits,
The guilt I cannot awake with certainty.
Confess my heart lies with me, your tyrant hold
To bend the truth it wants to reveal,
The beauty cloaking your chambers in soothing cream.
Yet, in my friend's eyes, there is no mystery,
No battle-worn beauty hides within your throne,
A battle-worn warrior who has sworn to me,
A hero born from the moment we parted.
  So, I


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Will depend upon thy payment;
For, as the title to what I give,
Myself will forfeit the full increase
Of thy sweet love, to give it not only to myself,
But to those whom are not bound by this pact.
Now in this admitted fault hath I committed,
A vast swerving of faults, and amiss
  More than at first noticed to sweetissage control.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose spite in thy will make the spoil relent;
Thou art as thy love's miserly will make his part.
And yet this hope is not all my love's desire,
For it hath in me a dread, and then a fear,
That, steeled by thy will, can bring it home.
The soil fears the sharp spur of love,
Which in thy will is amply strong,
And it fears the keenest spur of wind,
Which in thy will is wrought of twice--
  And that so it fears.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Be not deceived of my might,
When in thy will thyself it lies,
Making honeyed lily for my will,
And buds of gold, as thy will,
Steal not unthrift treasures from thy will,
But give a wide berth
To every shape that thy will decreed.
  If it err, not me, not for sure I am
  But wish to possess thee, and thine own part alone.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet blind Love to what she sees, bends it to his will.
If it err, it is folly, Love, remember it;
My eyes, bewitched by passion's heated glare,
Are moored within a harbor of the mind.
Why should my heart trust in a deceitful heart
When it knows well the sameness of our plight?
Or should my eyes, which see, declare the opposite,
To grace falsehood with a lovely face?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have strayed,
  And now to falsehood's contagion they've succumbed.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And in her countenance make some old thing new.
I love her for she remembered well
The days when I was wont to be and go alone,
And, for I loved with such a lusty youth
That in my youth some old thing fell
And took his love with him;
  But, for she loved well that age,
  I can no more blame her for that.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak truly, if thou dost willfully stain thy records,
Accumulate lovingly the blame of thy deeds.
Yet, do not so. I will not reproach thee for my sins,
While thou dost bring shame and wrong.
Thou dost take revenge on my records,
A record spanning many a lie,
That proves me a distinct beggar from thy dust.
  For thee I lose both my minds and heart,
  For I have withheld the truth of thy deeds.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my deepest sorrows.
If I could sketch the emerald of our heart
And celebrate each trait that graces thee,
The future minds would think it but a part,
