Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far, I deem


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Since love as thou dost thine own adder,
Finding love, in whom is as thy sweet self, so sweet,
Thine own true heart's desire, made true,
By thy deeds, and thyself aware.
How can I prove thee to be true,
Thine own heart, by thy love, thy art,
Though thou live, through my art thou live.
  For myself, thou gav'st me thee, me, free,
  To live with that thyself alone knows no cure.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
Till the old fawn has fled, loosing her might,
And you, in your nest, find her breath
Into a sweet, fiery spring,
Wherefrom Love's fire warms the air,
Creating a bath, a healing remedy
For hearts unwell;
Yet, if you should succumb to my despair,
You'll be left forlorn, alone,
A beacon burning bright, come night or day.
  If you should succumb to my despair,
  My love shall burn more bright, and Love's fire shall burn
  More brightly than before.


