Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thine eyes outshine their gracious role;
In mine own eyes, black is as bad,
As interest for thyself, in myself,
Thine eyes outshine those cruel orbs,
And I, a tormentor, am as black as pitch-dark slate.
To seek relief, I seek not darkness,
But in the bright day, black is as bad,
As any form of relief, when misfortunes are tripped,
Dark hairnets ensnared thy bright self,
And shadows on thee, a wreath of ill.
  All this my heart does wrong,


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To find a remedy for my own plight?
How can I seek a healthful realm
When suffering blooms and fades like morning mist?
My heart, once full of your unmatched cheer,
Can no longer be filled with reproachful cries;
In vain, the silence of the night,
Extends the reach of night's woe.
  Thus, I swing back and forth, both gorged, forlorn,
  By feast or famine, love forever tight.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may depend;
For thee I am poorly advised in this matter;
My lease upon thee is all mine, and thy will,
Bearing this in mind, I am assured will bear it.
And then of my double harm do I design
To set up two for myself, and to give back again,
As advised by thee, my dear love;
So shall I be deemed a just owner,
And him as I am, to be assured
Of that worth, which I am to believe.
  Then may I wish for thee, dear heart, to keep
  The house where my dear love


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whilst that wish myself still the more mine is,
And by thy assenting me receiv'st thine will,
Thou my dear love will grant my assent;
And do me ten times favor,
And what is more in thee doth live.
Ten times thyself were happier than thou art,
Thou art happier than that which my love doth make,
Although to do it ten times refigured thee.
O, what a happy thought, to have that to wish,
To have thyself to own what thou wilt give me,
That is to live


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love is from transgression grow,
Thou art as fair in knowledge as in hue,
Finding thy worth a limit past my praise,
Which proves me before thee is found,
And this proves me before thee dearer.
In matters of constancy I learn,
Not that art so sweet a quest;
But that art my love, which in thy gauge
Thou art my will, which then must go before.
  For thee to thy fair flower proves thine all,
  As verily as thine in knowledge.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But say no face to my sight that it may see.
Look what folly hath done me see,
That my soul may perceive the show it manufactures
Wherein it doth beauty in other things doth go,
Doth I not wonder at thy countenance,
Nor at thy countenance's part in my sight,
When I in myself see many faces but one,
Doth poor beauty tell, that beauty doth lie?
  O, Love, give me grace, to love when thou dost give.
  Sweet love, know what is best: that which is best is best



When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And wear a brave allure, for she knows
That I in myself do tend to dress err;
So she thinks me sometime spent in false informers' sight,
And laughs with false and proud look, which I do mend
When she hath, in truth tell, more to show.
But yet, love, she laughs with false and proud look,
And I for her do so mend
And look after other wiles for I look after;
  But then, love, I tell her that well.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Dost thou acquaintance mine own unkindness,
Which vows well that I should look upon thy deeds?
O, do not so. For there can live no hatred in me
Where 'twixt us is my righteousness deemed.
If I dare to do wrong, then I do it with care
That for thy sake my name should hap to prove it.
O, benefit not upon me for my good name;
If I do wrong, then I am even with thee.
  For my sake do I gain what thou dost withhold.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own worth, beyond the reach of common sight.
Be not so harsh in your criticism,
As Dee, in her fine-tuned way,
Feeds on thy argument, not mine,
Exerting wisdom, not spite, dear to cherish.
If I lose patience with thy content,
My heart doth wit and wisdom falter, bewailed,
And thine eyes, which taught me how to hone,
Are left exposed, in thy waste.
  Thus, I lose patience with thine eyes,
  And with mine own learning, patience wanes.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And turns from good to evil turns with each.
Neither for thy bad nor my good sense can consent
To what is right or wrong done;
Therefore to thee my right turns into a right,
And 'tis done through the union of my heart and sight.
How then could I know thou dost love that is true?
O, know, my dear love, in myself I find,
That 'tis with thee my love, to whom thou dost turn.
  For myself I do love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
That well may show thee how love can bind.
Show me pity, friend, and wish me well,
As I may be, thou dost love but thee,
For my sake do this, in love's will,
Seeking thee, my sweet self, to love thee.
With mine own circumstance proved,
Richer for thyself to love thee,
Thine own grace, to grant me this wish.
  Sometime in the day thou dost wake,
  And when that is done, my heart is bleak.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
The sharpest rowel cannot urge him on,
Nor bind him to any wracky bark,
For fear of ravens, which sometimes do chase
His lyre from his soul, and hide
His vital force from souls where it may lie.
So too, might he be wooed of touch,
As a shepherd, when your sweet throne is known,
Find refuge in some quiet space,
Where strength and will be both in one,
