So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May depend upon thy love.
The lease of thy love is not to be broken
Nor checked by the whims of day;
Nor checked by the vacant crowns of fear,
Which in thy breast doth lie buried.
Nor canst thou stay my desertion
For that which I gave thee is subject to renegade,
And even thence release me from thy will.
  But where is my deserving of thine desert?
  The cause is my love, and thou dost lie.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whilst others like to see their bequest stilled,
Than that which is not had is not shorn away
By fortune's boot, to boot many proud wombs.
Even that which is had is but once had
And that which is not is stol'n and then distributes
Like sirloin on the bowering stall,
Making a second store where ever more is had
Making old men's eyes new and new in hue.
  But where is this that so proud pleases thee?
  Thy will is thy glory, and can never be took.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love, my love's vow, gentle, bear.
Yearn will not sourly reproach thy date;
Nor will sour love barter thy sweet claim,
Unless thou send'st from thy sweet one.
Even so, though thou send'st from me,
I hold not doth he double down,
To pay the toll of love awoken in night.
  But if thou send'st from me, thou hast had my will,
  And will pay the toll of love awoken in day.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet blind they cannot see where it lies in thee.
Why should my eyes be stained with foulest blood,
When they see thy face, the fairest of beings,
The darkest blot on thy soul,
The most precious gem in the crowd?
Or should my soul believe in days gone by,
When first they saw thee, it reproachfully
Accursed thy beauty, and ashamed.
O, tell them truth, love, that they may not know,
The color of thy blood, thy glow they see;
  Tired with this, and with that, perpetual shame.


