Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But tell no truth to my eyes that tell it,
For they see the truth but what they see in thee.
Why should my eyes be dumb, when they see what thou dost show?
Or on my sinful deeds, why should my heart be so dumb?
Why should my eyes wonder at thine eyes,
When thou dost make beauty blind?
For they see the truth but what they see in thee,
And the beauty that clothes thy form divine.
  Only then canst thou live! Thou live'st death, and thine in me last.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
While in truth I do adore her for a playfair.
But why should I reproach her for cherishing what I did not grow?
My love, taught by one whose essence is as fair
As any child of yours, can blush for truth's javelin,
Though not as stars, whose brilliance I can't see,
Her eyes, a perfect show to chase,
Outshine their distant, softer, softer glows.
  To her I boast that I am true, despite her swerving eyes.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak of my lameness and I straight will halt,
Against thy speech, not to cross my mind.
What acceptable woe will my bad servant incur
When she sees my frailties growing,
Deface me with her scorn, and bring me to the light?
I'll leave it but as a plea of love and redemptive hurt,
With gentle words, not tears, I'll cope;
Such is my plea, not a grief to cross my heart.
  But still, I'll fight, even in my woe, against thy will.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy deepest sense, while my soul answers with thine.
If I could sketch a chronicle of thine sorrows,
The worst abuses would be fairest remembered,
And stretched tales of sorrow untold.
Indeed, if I could invent a tale of care
That thrives in exaggeration, couldst not last,
My memory alive, weak and bare,
To give the false affection a living lease.
  For if I should succumb to despair, I'd lose my mind,
  And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.


