Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy complexion's darkened by mourning, as mine by thee.
The morning sun, in its heavenly course,
Nor the bright star that ushers in repose,
Can compare to the beauty of thy remorse,
Gracing the evening with its eve's glow.
Thou as pity's form, I mourn more,
Lifting my spirit, as the night grows long,
As mourning becomes you, in every feature,
Then I shall proclaim darkness is bright,
And all that's lovely, thy shadows create.
  Thus I'll confess, black is the shade of the soul,
 


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To see my fate and ponder anew
The wretched deeds that once I did not do alone?
No, let me endure, the torment of my life,
The wretched chains of my confined breast;
I shall not falter under the weight of shame,
When exposed to the very worst of fate.
  For I have known a torment so deep,
  That I, made weak and lame, must fight
  To grant what is best for my friend and me.


