Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But, Love, they mistake it for my seeing,
They see the truth, mistake for thy drooping eyes.
Why shouldst thou blind my sight, when it is truly true,
To see things far off, and in darkness whooping?
Or shouldst thou seek to counterfeit
The beauty of thy fading day,
To find out shames and idle hours' play,
The charm of thy fading day, and night's unseeing gaze?
  Love, be of thyself so wary, and wary not;
  All days are nights to eyes, and nights to heart.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
While she for love keeps telling the truth untrue.
And, till now, she thinks me of little worth,
And says with kind words that I know well
That I am of no worth to her compare.
But why dost she not speak of her injustice?
And why should I not lie to show
The truth that I am of no worth to her prove?
For, like a deceived husband, I add:
  The true is loved when I prove it not.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak truly: thou art well acquainted
With the fine art of banter; yet dare not abuse
My silence with too much venom;
O, learn to read what words of mine are writ,
And soon to make the judgment that thou feelest
How to vilest words should look in a bower;
Speak truly: thou gav'st me well acquainted
With difference of kind and color to make my case.
  Give my speech thus-language a second life,
  That learning may well make the judgment of love.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own worth, thy essence is tenfold.
O, what a scope for thine eyes to roam
If thou wouldst but surrender, gently seep
Into thine own soul, and seize thy chance.
Whoso'st thou the world may deem unkind
To leave my humble verse bereft,
Grant my love a fame that swiftly flies.
Spare him the face of flattery,
If thou wilt, thou art the world's flatterer;
  My praises, thy world wide, can never be
  Compared to their own, less deceptive.


