Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy face, thy shadow, darkly bright;
Trees, like thine, have drawn thee away,
And stripped thee of all thy beauty;
Even birds' nests, thy shade too, had stol'n thy hair;
But beauty's veil, which from thine eyes stays,
No longer undone, but swiftly doth stand,
And swiftly flies to thy side, pure and bright.
  But flowers, thy shade so, are drawn more true,
  And straws, when dampened, are more false.


