And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May depend upon thy will;
For my true love is in my will,
And he that lends is not ignorant
Of the interest thereon who pays it.
But when thy will is demolished,
My fortress sold to the world,
And all my treasure with thee is decayed,
Despised as a beggar's burden,
And all my loves were 'tarded' before.
  A just plea against me, on thine part,
  Unsuspecting thee contends: mine was thy will, and thou deserv'st
  To take that away from me.


