Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy complexion, so mournful, now is as welcome
As those hungry ravens who feast on thy glee.
My gentle verse, thy beauty too profound
To the naked eye, cannot surpass
The riper words that rhyme it,
Bestowing grace on the heart that it receivs.
O, what a gain for the heart that makes thee weep
That thou mayst sing more, more blessed than my verse,
More pleasing than any song's power.
  For thou art as fair as those eyes outshine
  When thou, in brightness, canst penetrate.


