Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But when thou perceivest it, thou dost turn it cold and lame.
How can my eyes be true, when thou viewest
What they view in thee through the glass?
How can my soul be true when thou viewest
The form in thy glass, the clear display,
But that thy sweet thoughts in thee do show
The fire of thy deep-rooted love.
  Thy sweet face, when seen, is but a shadow of mine sight,
  For thou no form canst that likeness show,
  In thy sweet thoughts, 'tis but a shadow of my sight.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And live as a false accuser.
I have seen her dress err, the bloom of youth
Which in her breasts is hungrier than coral;
And in my love's youth looks a perfection
Of form; yet her eyes are stained with blame
And say more the more I know her to lie.
I love her because I see myself as true,
And she, in her arrogance, loves me so
That she may believe what she thinks.
  But here lies blame in my heart:
  My love thinks me some false mistress.


