Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy eyes outstretched, as the lonely imagine,
To see the world through the cruel gaze of my dearest friend,
Adorned in black, and sorrow deep.
Yet my soul cherishes what they disdain,
For thou art black, and they, in pity,
Are swayed by thine gaze, becoming one,
The fairest reflection of thy whole.
  My heart is grieved at this, and so is my eyes,
  And in their sorrow, I find at thee, through tears.


