Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But what they do not see is so sweet and true,
How canst thou make them blind when thou behold
What things our eyes perceive but shadows cast?
They see the truth, but what they see doth know,
And, Love, 'tis that all their beauty is but dressing,
To give away thy fair imperfection's part,
The ornament of thy beauty's living flame.
  Their eyes, though they see, are but stewards of thy shade,
  Which like a blind widow, heals not thy show.


