Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy cheeks and ugliness turned to shade,
Divergent in the love that once I did plead.
But thy shadow's gift, though far from clear,
Into the beauty that my soul doth call,
A gentle painter's aid, in my heart it lies.
The depth of thy sorrow shows where thou art,
Thy remorse's sting becomes ever-present,
As if to set pity to the goose that calls.
  If asked where thy heart is, 'tis answered,
  In mine own soul, by thy shadow.


