Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy complexion's cruel, thy grace to scorn,
A mirror of thy disgrace, in vaults.
Yet my soul cherishes thy name, aware,
As mourning becomes you, in every trait,
Then I, whom fortune once deemed lost, become one,
Through the legacy of thy name's theft.
For to thy fair beauty I find love's delight,
A thriftless praise, in thine own image,
By those who've stolen thy fortune, evermore enlarged.
  In disdain I draw a line, of pity,
  That others may claim their fair pity.


