Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But tell no false art the beauty that lies.
They look into the glass, and there appears a face
That hath eyes, but hears the sweetest whisper.
But why thy glass cannot turn clear
The truth that lies in thee, drawn from thy brow?
For all the beauty that forms
Is but the gracious reflection of thy deeds;
And, like the dyer's hand, the lovely droop
Is but the sweetest bud of thy green.
  Sweet peauteous heart do thy thoughts with discern
  The hideous stems of thine eye, and thy blood red.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime.
And yet, like prayers divine,
She thinks me gullible though I swear,
And therefore, dumbfounded, she plays the game
To keep me up at night with false alarms,
Sometime all day fairing the day with false songs,
But then, no more, than at first,
She thinks me gullible, though she knows
My dreams are false, and never can be so certain.
  For she thinks me gullible because I swear,
  And yet, so bold, I lie to her, still gullible.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Lest my soul be miscalled, lost,
And my frailties repaid not in kind,
But moan that I have fallen short in all
Where thou dost excuse my deeds.
O, then prove me just, and prove me true
As I will not steal thy spite;
For thine eyes are windows to the heart,
And thy sweet love shows how far thou'll earn.
  But be still, for I have eyes to lose,
  And thoughts of what to give thyself is best.


