Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet in thy view they find no beauty true.
That thy glass exposes thy mark to the blind,
A mirror of thy sweet self to lie,
Whose sweet self would be stained to see thy face anew,
And all the world would be forgot if it behold
Thy sweet self anew thy form's form's fall.
How can my eyes be true when they see,
And yet those windows that bear thee do betray
To set false benchmarks for thy false face.
  For thee thy shadow doth false praise,
  And all the world besides misconstrue.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A wretch, not true, though I swear it untrue.
But she thinks me of no more, and knows
That I in my youth feigned innocence,
Like her hearsay, hearsay hearsay lies,
That I am as she thinks, though she knows
That I am untrue, though she knows it to lie.
And, like her, I reanimate old offenses,
But she thinks me of love, and sees
My deeds though I did live, and she repented.
  But she loves me out of old, and for that sin
  I never saw die.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak of my soul, and do not drop
A plea for love that my part would approve.
If I answer: "Because thou no longer friend,"
Because thou no longer loved are present,
And I in love are still with thee, forget
The subject that brought thee to this hell.
Let me absolve thee: my dear heart knows
Her allure has been my foe;
Turning foes from me she bestows
That they elsewhere may strike their blows.
  But yet, persist not; in proof, prove
  The hurt was my friend, and her lie against.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy course of action, not my soul's part.
Though I should push thee far, push thee back,
I would not reproach thee for my missteps,
Nor would I strife with envy, for I have
Thy own perspective, the car of laws,
Which to myself amend may still be amiss,
In others' grasp, unjustified amends grown.
  For thee, in enacting this amend,
  Passed two burdens, a heavier one than hate's.


