Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
The lily that looked on me did swallow
Her cold eulogy, after losing her woe;
The roses fearfully on the bough did stand,
One blushing shame, another white despair;
A third, nor red nor white, had thieved from each,
And added to the cupcake Love's rose;
But, from these, came naught but grief and done hate.
For since these had died and then left me,
My name remained in some shape,
Which, like a dream, seemed to me to have died;
  But when I heard of that,


Poor soul, the center of my sinful earth,
Pressed with these rebel powers that thee array,
Why dost thou pine within and suffer dearth,
When thou thyself dost illuminate
The withering of love, as blossoms fall away?
Why dost thou use what thou hast so great might,
To torture and blind me, to serve with ill?
If thyself dost lie in wait, come what may,
Then thou shalt be repaid in love, by sweet service,
Despite what thy glass will show thee.
  Thy guilt shall be thine, and thine appeal
  Shall be thy life, despite what thy dust will show.


My love is as a fever, longing still
For that which longer nurseth the disease,
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill,
Alternating care with that which is best.
So too, my love may flare,
Yet staying in that ill-versed sickbay,
I draw not darkness from that view,
For my soul is ever bright in such light,
