O me, what eyes hath love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight!
Or if they have, where is my judgment fled,
That it may not tell of favor or twist?
If they find aught replete with truth,
They may say with good faith, "His eyes love it best,"
But that they have no record of his cheer.
For our eyes love heaven in such sort
As in a dial how one may know the time;
In him our spirits store the decrees of days.
Yet seeing the world with eyes, which see yet to see,
Might I not say with right, "This man is false,"
Unless it were right, and he was right then,
