O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
As a prize for the winning bid,
A perpetual boon in the monarch's stead.
Supposed as a lease, upon my will,
Or a usurer, in which I am debarred,
With what sweet control do I have
To take from thy sweet self, take from me,
The very thing that brings me full of ease.
What account can mine of thee, if thou desire,
That mine own heart may own what thou dost withhold?
  Nay, none but that which thou dost withhold, from me.


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
Lest sorrow embolden my amissive sighs.
For if I am guilty of transgressions deep,
I forfeit myself, to your reign,
Yearning for the solace of my heart's delight.
Oh, should you persecute my actions,
By embracing the change you deem necessary,
In public manners, your name is buried,
Like a mournful servant, who is moved
By the sight of your beloved, now alive and bold.
  To serve but you release me,
  For I am confined, and what I leave behind,
