Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Do mock my innocence, for they view me woe.
As I, for-lest hope should right me,
Do witness to thy deep-rooted spite,
And in my black, do sympathies wade,
As darkened by night's dearth.
Oh, for I have seen such sweet sorrows grow,
That I should thus so blest:
My sweetheart, being touched by shadows,
Came forth with black, and I straightway
Sealed my innocence by self-defense;
  By night, I'pered with shadows, losing light.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to suffer, see?
My dearest, truest, you feel 't torture me more,
And I, a cruel tyrant, have no choice but break
By your dearest friend's eyes, and grieve with my own.
Wounding me with your unkindness, I lose my sight,
