Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorning the necks of queens in renown.
But my sight, steadfast and true,
Cannot fathom the soul of a deceived husband;
For there is more to love there than hate,
And thus, I conclude,
At first the very edge of wrong's gaze.
But the pearls of tears my love's tear doth toss,
Of wretched women, of silent sighs,
Shall touch my heart, more nearly so deep.
  All this I view, yet in my mind,
  All days are dark till my eyes' embrace.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share that grief?
It wrenches me from my own core, and binds
Another self, more cruelly, to your will;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart within your fortress chest,
But grant my friend's heart freedom in its place.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find grace,
No longer can you then torment me.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to be your


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Is put to thee in the stronghold of my love.
But thou art so possessed with this gift,
Which like a blind do see, that no vision
Can the clear day or the bright night conclude
To sing or say in thine image whole.
But do not so, sweet illusion,
As when thou blind man with thine own self attainted eyes
Give him thyself to thine image whole,
Make him for love where there is no need.
  Make him for love where thou wilt, so dignifies thy name
  And to his robb'ry proud titles boast.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Than that which happies thee when thou thyself dost review.
For if she can but look, thou art so possessed,
That her own worthiness will thee nothing compare,
And losing her, dear friend dearest, will do none so.
So that she will be shailed, to bear her,
The pain of that which she will endure,
And losing it, both kingdoms weep,
And losing it, that by losing thee thou mightst win.
  But here's the joy: my friend and I are one;
  Sweet flattery! then she can but understand thee


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For there can be no hope, if thou wilt,
So far from hope, and therefore to be denied,
Thy eyes, that fam'erst the most wilt,
Are shamed, and therefore to be scorned,
At thy touch, their beauty assured.
So should that soul hope to see thy face
  In truth, and thine eyes be 'gainst,
  And thou, the most precious, uncared for by all,
  are left the prey of many's eyes.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet judge them by thy unseeing gaze.
For they see the false as true, the wrong as right,
And, within their minds, believe in dreams
How beauty's truth may be hidden from sight.
But if they see the wrong way,
The most sweet favor or the false one,
Their eyes to wonder will be graved
If 'gainst true love the sightless view.
  For 'gainst true beauty is by thy view determined,
  And, so, by thy view, they false bonds grow.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But who among us is ignorant
Of duty's birth in love's deceitful arts?
So, she thinks me naive, unknowing
Though she knows well my days are past their prime,
That taught her tongue to sing a sweeter choir
And laugh with joy that our days are numbered.
I love her for this, and so,
Though she thinks me naive, I try to steer
Her through life's vast, confusing path.
  But, love, learn to write as a lie
  That flickers your truth, and shuns your verity's tooth.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak of my flaws, and I shall surely halt,
In response to your reasons making no defense.
Thou can detain, though thou look upon my flaws unfurled,
By my scythe, I am bound to endure,
The beauty, the gait, the moment's own domain,
In this wretched land, where beauty fades away.
  Yet, plea deny, why do you hold
  The captive's guilt, in thee they find
  The right to view, even where 'tis held at bay.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thine own worth, and my will do likewise.
If thou wilt, yet persist, yet understand,
The power in thy breast is endless.
To my experienced eyes, a different age
