So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine control.
But thou art forsworn to my willfulness and dost lie,
And I will be your true love and dost lie.
Thou canst not break a twofold truth:
"Truth needs no mask, for it is beautiful;
Beauty needs no adornment, for it is the best;
Together, they may strangle,
And I, in turn, shall feel no wrong."
  But Whose worth then is but what once it is?
  In truth I'll strive to have,
  And in vain, my soul shall dream.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose every whim, from mine own lips doth lie.
O, what a gift, thyself to cherish,
That to my will, of thee I send?
Shall animosity be cherished more than love?
Or is it so?
Then what can mine own thing but by thy will,
That to his love doth my love extend?
Or what is thy will, that to me thou mayst choose.
  O, what a happy title do I hold,
  That drags me to where thou dost live.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me half so ill,
To set a form upon desired change,
As I would myself disgrace, knowing thy will;
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy walks, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name no more shall dwell,
Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
  For thee against myself I'll debate,
  For I must ne'er love him whom thou dost hate.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what it doth declare is best?
Then, Love, what canst thou but approve?
Your eyes are nothing, but sweet windows,
Which in their wills are hidden,
And 'tis well to see what is not true.
How canst thou make a truth
Which truth cannot! yet dost lie witht a face
So fair, which never can be!
  For sweet lies are better, and love is straight.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And use my name to fuel her thoughtless spite.
But since she walks and poets so make
Their praises but with a heavy heart,
Our love, I believe her false tongue,
And in our verse she lies no more.
But why should she not confess her wrongs?
And why should I not reproach
My name, when I am dead?
For my sake do I pine,
And in the end I am just as truth.
  For love's true power is warped,
  And in its loss, I find my soul.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with your speech, not your glance, let amends extend,
Wound me with thine sharp words, not with thy glance,
Exert your strength, and do not drop in place.
O, how much more fitting if I could instill sagacity
And in my verse, by thee bound is my place.
Let me be the tenth muse, your value far above
The ancient nine, whom poets vainly name.
  For thee, I bring forth great art,
  For thou art the better, and dost art more.


