Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, as compassion's ally,
Gaze gently on my sorrow's endless plain.
The dawn's first light is laid on thee,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief.
Even if you take that leave,
My heart still yearns, with sorrow filled,
A life that's no longer glad nor full well.
Thus, I, made lame by fortune's dearest spite,
Cannot help but hope, and in my woe,
Doing thee vantage, double-vantage me.
That my friend and I share a soul,
Dreaming of thee, and confide in my woe,
  Awakening from darkness, in slumber's hold.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
In other secrets of mine self mightst find
These shames and praises of thy sweet self too.
For how can I find a friend's verse
And can mine own self in thee live?
O, let me but copy what in you doth writ
And, like a dogger, steal that word
From thy sweet beloved's eyes and graces them so.
Let them say that thee no more shall I praise
Than you, if thou wouldst use the all of me.
  The beauty of thy wailing chief
  Makes thee seem stronger than she art in clay.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Thy own wailing my dear doting heart, thou art so unprovident.
If I can bring thee to my reaches,
The goddess' due, thy due alone,
And such a scope to me belongs,
My transgressions no greater shall I think
Than I shall myself bring to maturity.
Thou mayst thou grow'st my strength, and I straight grow'st
But when from thee I lose thee,
The injuries that myself bring,
Thou withering as thy sweet self doth grow.
  Such is my love, to thee I so belong,



If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should that beauty which I adore,
Hath put a value in that praise,
Which can be most wished-for, e'er adore it.
For thou art blamed shall be my grief,
And I by thy sweet will be blessed made
By thy sweet semblance of me, drawn from thee.
Thou art to my eye, and 'tis so,
As to my friend's eyes thy glory is to me;
And for my eyes my love well knows
