Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what is 't but a shadow?
They look into the beauty's heaven find,
And measure it by thy deeds;
Then, Love, beauteous you are,
And my great thoughts as I grow sad,
Temptation's grasp what it should strive to be.
Then, and let them say that thee behold,
Thy fair eyes, and thy perfect face to me,
Cannot compare with what thy picture can show in me.
  Let them say that thee, and me no more,
  Fair eyes with thy lovely graces.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And use my name to fuel her thoughtless spite.
But why should I reproach her for
This false song I compose,
When I am false in love's name,
And for my sake do so approve her,
And confide my song, true and kind.
I swear by this, though I swear by lies,
That it is true, and kind,
Though she hath strayed, I swear it's
To truths I swear against her.
  For I must not love things so kind,
  Which like a swart complexioned wart complexioned crime.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with thy speech, not your glance, let amends extend,
Wage war with war, and leave no room for guesses.
Why employ cunning when your might
Exceeds my defenses, to my surprise?
Grant me the pardon: my love well knows
Her beauty has been my undoing;
To save me from such foes, she bestows
Her strength, and I am only winning.
  Yet, relent not; near expiry I lie,
  End my torment, and grant my sigh.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy love's deep plea against thy cold retort.
If I could impart wisdom and sagacity,
Though not to love, yet, love, to sag,
As dying men, when their end is near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if they should push thee far, depart,
Thyself, and come back again,
With open arms, a better gift than this chaste taste
To bear the shame of thy age.
  If they depart, and I am proved
  To love thee more, as thou art old.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And that 'twixt is thy defect,
Which for my sake suffers in thy love's thrall.
Neither are I my true heart nor true soul,
And 'tis so, in my judgment all right,
That I in thy breast doth lie.
But my thought, that love alters not,
A tempest thou cannot help tofix;
Alas, tempests thou cannot help with thy help,
Nor canst thou be blamed if thou thyself dost lie.
  For thou art blamed in thy deeds,
  And 'gainst myself a thousand sighs.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Which is not so as mine by thee resort,
But for my sin I find a just plea,
Against thy will, all naked, and thee straight.
For thou art so possessed with murd'rous hate
That 'gainst myself thou stick'st not to conspire,
Seeking that beauteous roof to ruinate
The tender love of thyself, so possessed with might;
That, by thy will, I may set down this wicked might
And lead thee in the path of my despair.
  Kill me with thy hate, yet I must not be grieved;



Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To that sweet thief who steals her life.
So you, my precious one, are kept close to heart,
Hidden as a treasure in the stronghold of love,
Revealed in moments, setting apart,
Newly discovered, as from heavens above.
Newly discovered, as from heavens above,
Thy beauty and thy strength are kept,
Revealed in moments, setting apart,
Newly discovered, as from heavens above.
  Blessed are you, and thus,
  To be caressed, not held in laps,
  Two souls entwined in harmony.


