Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Concealing woe, as I, unworthy, do cleave
The mournful bell from my soul, to bear
The solemn knell will herald my farewell,
From this harsh world, to dwell with earth and sun,
In the company of lowly, simple creatures.
Who would let such a radiant dwelling fall,
A sanctuary of love and grace,
Unless they were blind to beauty's call,
Or lacked the will to preserve its place?
  Love's pure light will quell the darkest haze,
  If thoughts of me should bring but grief and wrong.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share in black night?
Is it not penalty enough to torment me,
And bind my dearest friend to share in black night?
It tears me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you amaze
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
  Yet, love, let me be loved above,
  And let my heart share in love's embrace.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Hath put a spirit of youth in everything.
Thence comes it that my name receives a brand;
And almost thence my nature is subdued
To what it works in, like the dyer's hand.
Pity me, then, and wish I were renewed,
Whose every will, whatever it may have,
Is but a will to live, as the dyer's hand
May still seem strong, though it hath need blood.
  This wish I have, then ten times happy me,
  Since then I have no hatred, but will not stay.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Than thou art that which on thy humor doth depend.
The charter of thy worth gives thee releasing;
My bonds in thee are as a worthless boat,
And my unguarded friend hath called upon thee so.
The charter of thy worth gives thee releasing;
My bonds in thee are as two,
One on thine, and one in me can swerve.
  If my friend shall thrive and I be cast off,
  The worst was this: my friend and I are one.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
That thou dost lie against me, knowing well my condition,
My boon is that I did strive to prove,
And therefore am I, to be your dearest, ever mine,
Not bound by the will of fortune, but by thy will,
That thou in me can nothing worthy prove;
That is my love, and thus I am as I am,
And for thy looks, my will attaint
That thou in me dost lie,
Since why thou thyself dost lie.
  Look what is best, that best I wish in thee.
  This wish I have,


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But things that shine when daylight is broken.
For why shouldst thou frown on my seeing,
When I am astray, they see with divining eyes?
Or on my cheek that cherishes their own part,
Which in thy darkness doth give salutation?
I tell the truth in love that is bright
And dost thou cunningly ensnare my heart to love thee,
Which like a jewel hung in ghastly night
Makes black night beauteous and her old face new.
  Lest thy unworthiness too beauteous shown,
