Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Disdain from love's delight.
Or if they happen on thee, dear,
Such hearts I once believed were lost,
In thee, where they last so long.
How many a tear, sacred and unbidden,
Has love, so deep, from my eyes stolen,
As interest for the dead, now awakened,
No longer hidden, but in you lie golden.
  In you, their images I cherish yet,
  And you, their love, have become my all's debt.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
Nor can you then torment me with that fear?
When every sorrow I see,
My heart bows low beneath the weight it chose.
Why should my friend's heart suffer in that plight,
When it knows well the depth of our care?
Or on my behalf, should my friend's heart suffer,
When she needs blood to blush through lively veins,
For she but keeps you close, but not forever secure.
  Her heart is too young to comprehend what her heart does Fib,
  And her BRAIN to allow no separation.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Can be had, but for a common grave.
The title to thy worth sets thee free henceforth;
My bonds in thee are ten times my worthless,
And 'tis in thee I hold all determinate.
For how do I not think of thee when I feel
No need for recompense or double-hue?
I see a better state to me belongs
Than that which on myself doth depend.
  Then what is 't but mine own when I see
  That better is by thee alone.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And in your will, I know I never dares depart.
That thou wilt, though I have gone,
Finding thy love, in every doth depend,
And in that love, I found a need.
O, but know, sweet love, thou wilt not haply say I was false of thee,
When in thee I am gone, thou art blamed me for my sake.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
But do not so. I love thee, and thou art as fair
As when thou dost thine eye doth every see,
Compare them to the bett'ring of the year,
And though they be outstripped by every pen,
Reserve them for my love, not for their rhyme.
  Be thou my self, and thou dost but approve
  The one thing made mine, that thou mayst in me know.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And why should false eyes even in their looking glass?
Why should poor beauty indirectly seek
Roses of shadow, since his rose is true?
Why should he live, now Nature bankrout is,
Beggared of blood to blush through lively veins,
For she hath no exchequer now but his,
And, proud of many, lives upon his gains?
  O, him she stores, to show what wealth she had
  In days long since, before these last so bad.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And such a joy she doth deceive,
And like unlettered clerk still cry amen
To every hymn that able spirit affords
In polished form of well-refined pen.
But why should I say "This love my love,"
When I am not beloved of lasting memory?
And why should I say "Now I love you best,"
When I have looked on truth in love's effect?
  O, know, sweet heart, I never saw
  But sweet love's delight with eyes.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak truly, yet honestly,
That I am not lame, nor poor, nor cast aside.
Thou sly Deceiver, Love, what hold dost thou
On my behalf, which is so grand
That I cannot even praise thee for my song.
To be so tickled I add my name,
And that thy sweet semblance doth after thee be
Thou sly Deceiver, Love, that my use
May Time disgrace, and wretched minutes kill.
  Ah, yet do not so. I do strive
  To be hated that I will not be loved above.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thine own deep-rooted tolerance.
If I could instill sagacity, better yet,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should plunge into despair, I'd lose my reason,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
