So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Finds your virtue, and doth ransom all my soul.
But thou art assured mine is thine and born of thee,
And that in thy breast doth live,
Thy beauty's doom and thy grace,
Despite thy outward showing, this thy might
Shall sum my worth in thine every.
Then what can mine own thee do but look
And grant thee what in thee doth live?
  Thy sweet virtue and thy love, to thee,
  Were to thee in thee this canst live.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Finding even with my woe to find thy grace,
Thy haughty heart's ten times my poor verse can mend,
And thou, to whom my haughty heart doth give chase,
Thou art the fairest and most precious jewel.
So, love, be thou. Although I may strive to prove thee virtuous,
Thy haughty heart doth but love, and mine,
Love's effectually unchanged.
  If thy unworthiness hath wrought this love,
  More worthy I to be loved above.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
