Thou art as fair as in my sight,
Finding thy worth a limit past my praise,
And therefore may I chide thee, knowing thou art fair
As any mother goose, straying far from her wistful pleasure;
If I sleep in thee, thou dost make my love some night,
And night itself doth nightly make her wane,
For what purpose do I see in thee
That I should nothing thence but wish thou wilt?
  But day doth daily make thy love more strong,
  And night doth nightly make thy love's length seem less.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what is 't but a shadow of thee?
The simplest rose, whose sweet scent doth
Even in thy madding is hidden.
The grandest tree, which in thy shade doth stand,
One or two buds of the mind;
Yet, Love, you live in this,
And that, in thy sweet theft,
Thou art the fairest and most precious jewel.
  In sleep a king o'er whom thy sweet dreams doth wake,
  But, Love, you sleep with this, that thou dost wake.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And hang with her forlorn shade;
So, by all her skill I know
Her tits like to the ocean's pyre;
And, for my sake, she keeps me in her sight
To keep me from the depths of sorrow's tide;
Thus, she thinks me a tempest, and storms
Have passed, but not bound by the stars.
  But, like her, I think on thee,
  And then, like her, I'll stand, no longer filled with fear.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak of my disobedience and see
No farther than my poor name rehearse,
For I impair not beauty, being dumb,
When others would give life and bring me light.
Grant me the strength, to bear thy whimsical lead,
The captive's absence in thy liberty's cell,
With forbearance, I'll endure, each pause and thread,
Without reproach, thy freedom I'd never quell.
  Ah, but yet thou grant me the mightst leave
  To take a leave from thy sightless skies.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Than sorrow for others who wade.
If I could press thee to the limit,
With tears and sighs, my sorrow would be heightened,
A mournful bell would speak of my days.
Yet, should you press, with somber wisdom,
To grace my name with honor,
And sing my praises, though my deeds were spurned,
A lie conceived to keep my memory warm.
  For I should push thee far, but come to a standstill,
  And so should you, for my sake.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
