Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in my sight it never sees,
Mine eyes, 'fore duteous, never see'st true.
Yet, Love, you've wrought a twisted mirror's snare
That warps my heart within its twisted sight.
Why should my heart believe in twisted lines,
When it knows well the weight of our age?
Or should my eyes, which witness, tell a different story,
To elevate the lie with purest light?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have swerved,
  And now to deception's allure they've turned.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And take my leave in drooping night.
But why should I sleep, when my lover's eyes
Possessing sable of any hidden night,
Ceases her song, as if to keep
The memory of my lover's grace.
I must not dare to question with my jealous thought,
Believing in love's fresh case,
For it is so true that in my breast
The flesh's imaginary friend to me belongs.
  And thus, in absence, I'm in my thoughts,
  And in my tongue, Love's imaginary tongue.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
