Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, and compassion's ally,
Gaze gently on my sorrow's endless plain.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of your glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And for thatblest bough belongs to thee.
O, shake my friend, and I no longer shake
My heart, for it is deep inside you
That heals my friend's heart at last,
Gazing on darkness, as the lonely imagine.
Shall I then be blessed made from above,
By wandering eyes, each under eye,
While my soul whispers to the worthy:
  All souls are stirred in your love,
  And you, though enemies, remain entwined.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Finds your virtue, and doth he give
To another's form, in repute's shady arts.
The honeyed April of thy beauty doth lie,
And thou in him dost steal thy breath,
Since why should I steal that fragrance?
Or who is he so fond will be the tomb
Of his self-love, to stop posterity?
Thou art thyself so possessed with murd'rous hate
That 'gainst myself a second youth,
And I must attend his leisure with my moan;
  Whose speechless song, being many, seeming one,



Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose every inclination is to liken thee to some.
That thou wilt, if thou wilt, bychance, bring me graces graced,
Thou art the fairest and most precious jewel.
If thou wilt, thou art the wits of many,
But that thou none lov'st is most evident.
For thou art the wits of many, which I hold dear,
And thou that art all thy state, and I never can tell
That thou in thy boundless gifts even to thy purest will.
  But do not so. I love thee in such


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love, my love's dove,
Its tender heir, in my seeing, is welcomed there,
And almost thence the fault is subdued,
Tis no longer causing us either shame or glee.
Nay, 'tis not love, though in thy heart it is found,
That stirs the hearts of flint, himself being wary,
Investing in thyself, to keep it whole,
Thyself the most precious, being unvaried.
  For thee, I watch while thou dost wake elsewhere,
  From me far off, with others all too near


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet it knows no face hath such a place.
For how can I, in my eyes, behold
The perfect beauty of thy scion's face,
But in my heart's deep well thou know'st thy shade?
The rose looks fair, but hers is not so;
Of thy sweet brow is hanging still,
Her dead lover's shape in moods is even more.
Yet eyes, that see, cannot so clear eyes
As to thee which gives them form and place.
  So eyes be deceived, and yet weeds
  That see, despite their eyes, never clear.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And use my name to fuel her thoughtless spite,
Lest I should tarnish its meaning, and speak
Of my days of youth, now tainted and lame,
Would make her tongueless praise,
And leave my heart to the mercy of the skies.
I confess he belongs to the book of days,
And like a ghost I'll be caught,
Consumed by you, or seeking shadows deep;
  In such a way I'll maintain my dignity,
  As he does now, even in my head.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak truly, that I am your true self;
But yet, love, beauteous and sorry,
Do not so. I am as I am, and what I do show,
My spirit is thine, the better part of me.
So, love, beauteous and sorry,
As I am, and what I bring to light,
Thy name is Craving, aka Yen.
  For thee, I am so dumb, and thou art so dumb,
  That I for thy sake do call me thrice, thrice my glory.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy neighbor, thy dear.
If I could write the truth as thy sweet-telling friend,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should pass by, 'tis my love who'd
Be killed by thee, and thee by me be killed,
Since first I saw thee, and born to see,
  As dying men, when the end draws near.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And that love with my eyes belongs to that fault.
For my eyes in thee are kind, and they love'st true measure,
But 'tis my heart that cherishes what they find unright,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
  Only my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
 Dissuade one foolish


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Not to blame thy love, though I feel thou dost lie.
For to my love are born of true hearts,
Like plants, where they are born and stored,
Cannot compare with the beauty of thy budding name.
Nay, 'tis not love, though in our mien,
That stirs the hearts of men.
Mine eye, that guides my steps,
Beats the heart, and shatters verity's tooth:
  Hers, by thy beauty tempting her to stray,
  Thine, by thy beauty, false, to me untrue.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the chase, her riderless cries a distant bar;
So I, in love's terror, fail to say
The sacred words of love's own rite,
In love's own might, I start to fray,
Overwhelmed, I lose my sight.
Oh, let my written words speak truth,
Messengers of my heart's deep plea,
Beyond the tongue, they'll find their youth,
A love-story waiting to be.
  In love's fine art, with eyes, perceive
  The silent sonnets we have yet to conceive.


