Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Concealing from our eyes that glorious balm?
Or should I, in my manners, sing to thee,
When thou art all the world, and I am one,
Caring thy transgression to my sport.
Shall I, then, mar the work that wants no aid,
By striving to improve what Nature made?
My verses but one end in view are laid,
To celebrate thy worth, in part displayed.
  Yet, be one, and thus, your mirror shows,
  The truth thine, and thine alone, is enough.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And for that wound there is no remedy,
For worms to come and claim their bequest as well.
How much more doth beauty be my friend's presence
Unfettered by the grasp of mortal time,
Nor by the hands of mortal time exchanged,
Can it be left exposed Where beauty's veil doth cover every blot?
Even so, by the hand of Time's cruel hand,
My heart still calls, in love's array.
  If this be error, and my friend doth deceive,
  I am not guilty, and they shall be proved.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Will be had, for still hanging in thee.
But thou art forsworn in this love-filled trait,
And I cannot blame thee for my closet,
Lest I should tarnish its meaning, and speak
Of thee, my dear, to be your friend.
So I have no hatred in thine eye;
Nor hate in my heart, nor feel
The slightest touch of thy wand.
  All tongues, the voice of love, give thee so
  To hear with thy heart, which makes thy love's song.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
