When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And take my leave, like a she belied fool.
But why should I reproach you for I love her so
That I in my tongue might see
And judge by your deeds, not by your deeds,
But for my sake do confess my love,
And love that she loves you best, kept
My poor name from my memory,
Lest my steeled eyes should blunter be
And brass in your love's fire do allow
The slow messenger of my speaking breast.
  For I must attend your leisure with my moan,
