Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, and weep with despair;
In this despair, I begin to wane,
Knowing thy love's bright light will herald impending doom,
And despair, knowing thy love will hasten,
At thy feet, amphibious love against myself.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
  Thus, I rest in this madding,
  To try a newer bond with an older friend.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And for thatblest balm I throw aside.
Merely unveiling through the cloud your face
To dry the rain on my friend's face,
Is not enough to heal the injury birthed by your unseeing gaze,
Nor can your heart's true power alleviate my grief.
Even if you repent, I've still the cost,
Of the offender's sorrow, feeble is the boon.
  But the pearls of tears your love doth toss,
  Are riches that atone for every ill-done.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
