Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
That due, which seals up all in rest,
Deserves the least of love's sweet touch;
Those blots that touched me more deeply than gentle sighs,
Could not so much hold the peace of that I'd "done,"
When Love's own wings had graced his with gentle ease.
But breathed, "I loved you most,"
That love might live, and lips that Love's own touch had graced
With gentle art;
  And so, through my verse, your purest breath
  Remains unthrifts, for love keeps fast my wayward.


Poor soul, the center of my sinful earth,
Pressed with these rebel powers that thee array,
Why dost thou pine within and suffer dearth,
While thy outer shell doth suffer in suppression?
My feeble spirit, the vessel of my feeble rage,
Doth launch upon thy vast and welcoming sea,
While my flesh, being made of four, welcomes the view,
Proving thy strength by thy heavily gilded vessel.
But, if thou wilt, I cannot boast,
Of my thrall, thou shalt be blamed,
For slander's mark is ever drawn on thine.
  So, if thou wilt, I'll bear the smart,
  For slander's taint clings to the fairest skin.


My love is as a fever, longing still
For that which longer nurseth the disease,
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill,
Doth th' account of days, when I, sick of thee.
The cure, though seeming to be,
Shall but approve my feeding;
For that same fever I with my sickle doth stay,
And, sick of welfare, found a kind of meetness
To be diseased ere that there was true needing.
  But thence I learn, and find the true remission
  That is not by love but by others' loathe thrown.


O me, what eyes hath love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight!
Or if they have, where is my judgment fled,
That tells me well or ill,
Why do you frown upon what I do not see?
If I have seen true, then love, what eye have I not seen
That frowns on what I do declare,
And looks on truth with false compare?
If, love, that deceits of men,
As I, for my sake do you ensnare and snare,
Do you scowl on me, for I am ensnared
And wretched souls after their show.
  For thee, I've seen truth with present woe,
  And so should you, to love things


Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot
My strength, by thee bound?
Who turns from thee that I should call a friend?
On whom dost thou frown that I do pursue?
If thou wilt, I dide thee, but truly do pursue,
And do not drop in for an afterloss.
What worth do I in myself discern
That I should afterwards be sullied by thee?
When all my virtues bow to thy defect,
Ensnared by the power of thy gaze anew.
  Still, love, increase your scorn, for now I know
  You cherish those your gaze doth deign to show


O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
And keep my dark shadow in my mind,
Unaware of the world's cunning stratagems.
Though I should claim thee fair, and hence my soul's truth,
In others' countenance, their dark shadows are given.
Yet in yours, love's cunning craft
Shall claim a place above the surface explored
By fleeting trends, as the sun does flare.
Whatever thoughts or secret heart concedes,
Your looks disclose naught but sweetness, day and night.
  Your beauty shines, an apple ripe with hue,
  If truest love does not its promise ensue.


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
Lest you become entangled in my wrongs.
For, as you deceive me, I betray
My nobler self to my body's treachery.
My soul informs my flesh that it may strive
For victory in love; the flesh, now alive,
Rises at your name, its purpose to derive
From your affection, its reason to thrive.
  And thus, in pride, it serves, ever so near,
  To stand by you, to fall by your side, my dear.


In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn,
And for myself but forsworn, to give voice.
For thou art blamed shall not be thy defect,
Nor praise it be thine to claim,
For it depends upon that love of thine.
And that is the love of action;
So I will not praise thee for my deeds I see,
Nor thou shalt be blamed for my neglect
Of thee, nor I to brief minutes blame.
  For I must confess that I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steepen.
The waters kept the fire's essence, though, alive,
A bubbling, healing source for all ailments dear,
Yet, at my beloved's gaze, Love's flame revives,
Her eyes, a furnace, where the ember reappears.
To seek relief, I ventured to the bath,
A wretched soul, longing for a touch of peace,
Alas! The cure I sought was not there, in aftermath,
For Love's true remedy lies where it had its release—
  In the wellspring of my lady's gaze, the fire's birth,
  A potion


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep,
One maiden fair, with virtuous hand, did take command,
Of the flame, which countless true hearts had made grow,
The monarch of desire, she, in sleep, did quell,
By her innocent touch, the fiery glow,
Of Love's own general, his power fell,
Into a well nearby, she plunged the brand,
Transforming Love's eternal fire to water,
A cure for hearts, by passion's flames burnt, and,
I, bound to my beloved, sought this fountain,
  To find a cure, but learned this lesson true:
  Love's


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And leave me in the public eye to grow.
But why should I dare to question if my days are done,
For I dare not question with my own eyes
When I have seen by countless proud spectators
Who wear the fleece of my poor name efface?
No, let me endure, with tears,
The pierce of my breast,
That every private eye will see
To see my age friend first come to light.
  And so, love, protect yourself, as I shall thee,
  Our hearts entwined, forever wild and free.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
Though in my nature doth suffer,
Thyself to give, even so as shadows fall.
For to my love I send not unsound but true,
One respect and hope, another in love's might,
And in my madness, a third illinois sings.
I hope, though far from seeming false,
Believed to be by others, yet in my madness,
I am one, and thus, to be loved above.
  But then, like the lark, my madness takes flight,
  I must attend, though shadows may remain.


