So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine is locked,
In some converted prisoner's hall, where souls are drowned;
For now my sweet love looks fresh, and he who grieves,
After a week in barren earth he rekindles,
The earth removed is doused, as is the sun.
The sun itself dines upon the feast it sees
And sees the first blossoms gently prepared;
So my sweet love doth almost prevaried hour
Nor need he linger, staying far behind.
  Ah, sweet deception! What worth do I have in myself
  For in this poor rhyme he doth take.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And in my errors shall I found a purpose.
O, why so err err err than I;
O, I for thy sake argue against
