Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy unworth their wailing cries a dismal song,
Darkly hated, as I before,
Gaze gently on my sorrow's endless plain.
The morning sun, in its heavenly course,
Nor can thy pity's gentle shade tell it to go,
Unless thou hap to claim the place where it starts.
Oh, thus, in this twilight's reign,
Whence cometh this great sin, that commits thee,
To see black as beauty's ally, takes all away.
  This evil as I, for my sake, shall bear,
  Killall born, and thee


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To torture me in your unprovidentiality?
My self is not enough to torture,
My own virtue is so tainted by your own doing.
For if I bend beneath your tyranny,
The world will weep for you, a widowed spirit.
The world will wail and grieve, its heart laid bare
Whilst every private widow, in her lonely lair,
Can treasure her beloved, her heart's own norm.
  No love for others dwells within that breast,
  Which on itself such grievous wounds doth impart.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May keep as collateral for my claim.
For my dear love, so too, will myself
Be the subject of my willfulness;
Upon this will myself be collateral, not forlorn,
Under the burden of my own desert.
The nature will grant him a room,
Within the fortress of love, where it shall truly live;
Until the judgment that my absence lies,
That, in part of thee, I am thine, and shall myself hence.
  Such a case is this love I have looked
  To this, and this love this my soul shall survive.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Thou dost advance of Ideas, to the Common grave unvided
By laws quite strange, how thy memory cannot be enlarged.
The common grave may contain my grave wronged,
Yet in the minds of those unborn there,
You'll rise again, in stories I did tell,
Thy name will be my love, and my love, thou know.
O, know, truth will decreed:
"Thyself by thy memory can be undone."
  To me this I witness call the silence;
  Thy unused beauty still doth show."


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So will I, in love's cage, be broken in two?
To say I was thy soul knows, is it the duty
Of self-love, in which mine eye is truly blind?
To say I was thy self so kind,
That thou mightst be thyself again.
O absence I will enforce, that I must stay above,
Defying wrinkles, your golden age will tell;
  For thee, as I shall thee, shalt be true,
  Defiant to that time.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
To be false as against myself I vow,
To love those whom I will not rebuke,
And for their eyes my vow to stay silent so.
If they find virtue in thy unworthiness
That for thy heart is so unworth,
For mine eyes, a transgresser, are in thy transgressions
And for thy soul that is truly so sweet.
O, benefit of sweet deception! Now see, with kind;
  The sweetest form is best if poisoned;
  The deadly vices love the sweetest blood.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
I watch her vibrant day to life remove.
For if she thinks I should go well, or I say with truth,
She is gone and I am left in ignorance to think of,
I cannot know my heart well, nor can her to keep it sweet.
But why should she, moving me, say,
She should stop, not stay,
For I am summer, and she thinks no me.
  So, why should she not admit her sweetness,
  To make me a summer's tale to you?


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Thou, to whom my ill name is well.
What excuse will my gentle name accord
To this impaneled sin wherein I am attainted?
I am thy fair goddess, and I am not mixed
Within the narrowest sense of our souls.
I am my song within these lines,
Thine, where I live, and what I leave behind;
Therefore my mistress, when heard, I be mute;
  The only one who calls me is my name.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express their good condition.
Thou, truly-suspecting-As-suspecting-kind befits, whose unlettered pen
May cleanse my soul, with gentle art;
Even so as your love unnamed tongue
May still seem to my tongue-tied tongue;
Even so, in this age shalt be
Seized, made of four by thy hands.
  To this I testify, and so should be:
  To this, I am truly as I am, though my heart may stray.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Mine eyes so bad that they look into darkness.
My soul doth plea against their seeing eyes
In action 'tis no against thy sight that it doth crave,
For 'tis 'tis true that love doth for myself.
No shape of me is 't love settled
That it doth in so unprovident lands
That 'tis with his scorned eyes a thousand faces bear,
Since 'tis with my sweet thoughts myself almost loves myself so,
Suspecting myself to the heaven did witness.
  Love's right is that man's willful heart doth hate,
