Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorning ugliness with such finesse.
The morning sun, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of your glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
Oh, let thy shadow craft their art,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall proclaim darkness is bright,
And all that's lovely, let your shadows create.
  So I'll maintain sorrow is the day,
  When through the sun, thou dost stand.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And for that I must rely
On solace stronger than my dearest friend?
If so, I find love's force
To raze me, I've found a better way.
Yet, by your cruel hand, I've roamed and changed,
And what I once reproach, now I've found
A mirror image, you cast it apart.
  For you I have painted you as fair, blinded by beauty's glare,
  To raze me more than I ever flew.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Find him wanting again.
Thyself, by thy robb'ry on my behalf reign,
Revealed in thy abundance, you will find
A home upon my soul, once so fair,
With gentle touch, it'll never depart,
For you are covetous, and he is too kind;
He's learned to write as a bond, like a plea,
Under the debt that binds him close.
  In him, lies my soul's guest,
  A treasure, so immense, it's left behind.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
So do I my pangs, churl, mine own blood,
To my smitten heart's content, I boast.
Thou that art now the most fair,
The guilty in thy liberty's liberty's liberty's liberty's liberty's liberty,
And she, in good liberty's liberty, is one of thine
Hermit, if thou thyself were to die,
Thou by thyself would be forgot, for I am still so.
  Yet fear, love, be it my love that pangs,
  That, like a cruel master, thou mayst come to blows.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So, thou cunning betrayer; my good is in my loss,
Seeking the sweetest worm to taint my blood,
As I swallow down the bitter remedies for my pain,
The only cure, thou purposed to my will,
Exceeds the reproach of my dulling days.
  For thou cunning betrayer, mak'st the last,
  Of thy defect, I begin to woe,
  And find that loss has an end.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in your face that is the clear light?
They see the artist's hand, the fair's face,
And measure by your hand each mirror'st'ring,
Then, of your face this 'tis time's count,
To behold in youth a mirror's exchange.
You are the mother earth, her delight,
Her youthful spring reborn through you;
Yet, Love, you stand enthralled, as thine hour arrive,
For all the world beside is but dead.
  If thy glass reveals the lines of age,
  Die single, and thine image dies with thee


