Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy name should wane in my face.
If I should lose thee, 'tis no need for my tongue,
For in my mind, each word doth come and go
As I by, ten times refigured,
Yet, as the tide ebbs, then flows on desert sands,
Our love shall teach, first how to love the best.
  For fear of time's cruel hand,
  Lilies that rot smell far fouler than the wild.


