Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that, guess, it is the best;
Yet, Love, you've wrought a deceitful scheme,
That steals my eyes from my sight,
And bind my heart to follow where they're blind.
Why should my heart believe in scattered dreams,
When it knows well the world's shared, single space?
Or should my eyes, which witness, tell a different story,
To elevate the mountain tops with purest light?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have swerved
  And now to the one who steals me away.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
At my abuses reckon up their own circumstance.
No lie can cleanse my heart from the truth I give,
When she walks beneath the selfsame moon,
Her love's deceitful allure inspires,
A strange carnival, in mine it lies.
But why does she not confess her wrongs?
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So we deceive one another in our flaws,
  And with sweet lies, we comfort our disgrace.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with thy words, not lingering sight,
Wield strength against strength, and grant me fairness.
Speak truly: you love another; yet in my gaze,
Spare me the dance of eyes askance.
Why employ tricks when your might
Surpasses my defenses by sheer expanse?
Let me absolve you: my dear heart knows
Her allure has been my foe;
To part from me, she bestows
Her strength, and I am only winning.
  Yet, persist not; near expiry I lie,
  End my torment, and grant my sigh.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy sweet name when I remove from this earth.
O, what a profligate we stand,
Hung with the soil where it is healing?
To what end do I haply say,
Thy face hath not the power to make love run,
For suffering is the fire that refines,
And love, when lost and then regained,
Is purified, made stronger than before,
With depth and wisdom that can't be feigned.
  And thus I return rebuked to my content,
  And do not rest.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my other thoughts that love's bitter taste doth take.
Neither are my soul so steadfast as mine,
Nor are they so dumb;
And 'tis my soul that I love to thee,
Though they may stray, like the winds they are.
Yet are they so with thee that I do love
And all the more, by heaven I deem my sun;
And in thy loving thoughts, I add,
A thousand groans, but thinking on thy face,
Thy bosom is my all desire.
  If thy unworthiness hath but my no part,
  Then mine


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Which I by myself depart daily grind
To set down the loving thought, as mine by thee doth lie.
May I not evermore say
Thy face hath not the power to love that it doth live,
Nor may I in that bosom churl hate,
When thou my love does not hate me nor thou.
O, let me, true in love, but truly write,
Thy face hath not the power to love that it doth love,
  And I, so thine, may set down walls,
  To make the corners where there is no hatred.


