So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May borrow peace of that which thou dost deceive.
Thou art not lame, poor but doth live,
And dost him grace, sweet love, for my love
So thine own dost him for my love,
Suff'ring my friend for my sake to approve.
So is my love, for my love so is evident,
As I'll myself disgrace, knowing thy will;
I will myself disgrace, knowing thy will.
  For I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Than I that whose unseeing heart doth give.
So thou gav'st me thine not to give back again
My poorless love, to thy great deserts sworn,
Will you not pardon me?
In witness to my change,
Thy looking on me, I say
"Had my friend's muse grown with this growing age,
A dearer birth than this his love had brought
To march in ranks of better equipage.
  But since he died and poets better prove,
  Their songs now seem less in capturing thee.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for this, or art blamed,
Desire will be the fairest and most precious gem in me.
All mine eye hath got thee through so thine eyes
That thou through thyself art much admired,
And my dear love, by thyself being false doth lie.
Yet why thy beauty's eyes outshine the theirs,
And why thy glass shows thee how thy beauties wear,
Thyself away, for thyself so rare.
  Thy end is truth and beauty's end,
  And thy right so oft be termed.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what worth in me is thy essence,
That is most most most precious, ten times more.
Ten times thyself were happier than thou art
If ten of thine ten times refigured thee;
Then what could death do if thou shouldst depart,
Leaving thee living in posterity?
O, none, unless thou wilt be--
Thou art truly blind, and I cannot see
The gilded age to come who wouldst look on thee.
  Be not gored, for thou art so;
