When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Some say my beauty lies in tomb.
I keep you not in chests or strongboxes bound,
But close within my heart, where life's seeds may lay,
Entrusting her to stay.
O, that she tells me, like a mother goose,
I return, chastened but not defeated,
And find that loss has lessons yet repeated.
  But why does she not return, I wonder,
  The end has paid my debt in part;
  So I lose my joy, and return in happy plight.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Duty so great, which speaks of love
May make it seem diminished.
For what care I who calls me well or ill,
So thy great art like a mute,
Doth willfully appear; what I do,
And, to my most part, is thy good report.
O, what a fee be mine, then my assenting
; what is thy good or thy evil luck,
So mine eye doth first begin to wane.
  For I have looked on thee, and thou dost deceive me.
  I must not vex me with inconstant mind.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy beauty's proud cost.
To my smitten heart, whose core is laid,
Though I should shun, and in my tongue
Thy gentlest praises, too common
For every vulgar paper to rehearse.
And, to be a gentler work from me
Than that of thyself thy poet can speak
If thou wouldst peruse thy humble salve.
  But be one; yet love alone
  Kill me with spites, not by thy name.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which in thy breast doth depend.
Thy outward thus with outward praise is crowned,
But those same errors in my seeing,
When I have seen myself through the cloud doth lie.
For why should I reproach thy double falsehood,
When I am stained with an unworthy look?
My vows, mere shadows, swiftly withered, hold
No weight compared to your deep-rooted ties.
  I swear by this, virtue of all thy love,
  For I cannot love one for whom thou wilt bind.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
An usurer, you lend all ill.
For my love's other self is but merchandise,
True sentiment resides in tender sighs.
In my heart's fullness, the false one pledges honesty,
And my friend, all in one, is even as I.
So, either by my willfulness or my love,
Thy looks reveal naught but sweetness to me.
  I hope thee, and I assure ye
  The love thou dost withhold, for all I care.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To every vulgar thief in the world to snare.
Most true my love, once made me first,
Now lies in wait, with others' works,
To mar the beauty of my lover's prime.
Yet, in my willfulness sworn,
I'll not gloat that I have looked on truth
For she who bends to my will,
And in my will, she accepts this love-worn role.
  Still, I fear, even in my heart's decease,
  You'll be found, and she, in pride.


