When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
At some carnal feast with you in the spot.
And yet, by heaven, I know her falsehoods
And in my sight, I find sweet delight
And in my youth, I see beauty's bright eyes.
But why does she not admit her wrong?
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So, in our faults, we find comfort in lies,
  And in our deceptions, we find sweet solace.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Sing to me when thou wilt,
Wilt I dare to speak of my faults,
Counting thy trespass, I dare not contradict
My deepest sense, yet I swear it to thee.
Thou art as fair in knowledge as in hue,
Finding thy worth a limit past my praise,
And I so spake, to try an older thing,
A god in love, who holds me close.
  O, how I am not old, when my days are done,
