Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Sanctioning thy love, I corrupt anew.
As I, for thy sake, bring but strife and ill,
Thy heart's abyss is where it sweetly roams.
To my vexation I bring in sense--
Thy foe, my advocate, takes the stand.
Such civil strife in my heart is born,
Where I, an ally, must join the fray,
To that sweet thief who steals me away.
  I bear this secret, a heavy load,
  Until my friend, once lusty, takes its abode.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To one who gives heaven's choicest blessings.
So you, beauteous, and wish me well,
As I, obedient patient, swallow down
The bitter remedies for my ill health.
In so I return, my sickness eased through
The weak nerves that my patient's head doth strew,
But, true sickle, I straight grow sad,
No longer poisons this unguarded romance.
  For I have kept you not in chests or strongboxes bound,
  But locked away within my heart's deepest hold.


