Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, they display none but delight.
My soul doth plead that thou in him dost lie,
Aware of my sins, and they, in turn,
At thy mention, I add, thus fulfill.
Thou hast passed by my willfulness and my fault,
Either not assailed, or victor being charged;
Yet this thy praise cannot so much hold,
Nor bind, I suspect, thou being charged.
  I suspect, but I know not the truth,
  When weighed against thy, it is built.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief;
It doth use my friend's name to torture me,
And hath my dearest friend to torment me.
Yet fear not; near extremity I can trace,
My own self-love quite contrary I read;
A thousand murmurs on the wind converse.
Even so my friend whispers her truthfulness,
To me that she hath not skill enough to torture me.
I swear by this, and yet I know it not
To torture me with harsher words,
And lead my other thoughts further on.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine.
Thou dost him thy will, and he takes thee
And leaves me in the public eye to grow.
But thou will thine own design, and he in thee dost
Make another gay design,
Eternal leg, in thy hand it is all so bold.
He that for thy sake lends thee virtue,
And he that for thyself doth lend
And found it in thy cheek.
  For thee this slander born of deeds far and near,
  I'll pen it, and thine for myself doth lend.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And I, a tyrant, will be one of thine authority.
The charter of thy worth, thy INSlicting,
My ties to thee, in every aspect, slight,
By willful choice of what thy heart doth impoverish.
How can I, in this plight, find virtue,
When thy own virtue shines so bright?
The remedy, thus bound, is lacking,
In thy great worth, I see beauty stood,
Where all art made, only me for thee.
  This, in thee, is my deserving,
  To thee, my dear heart, my dearest


