O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak of my lameness and I straight will halt,
Against thy reasons making no defense.
Thou canst not, unless thou robs thy strength,
By thy sweet virtue tempting her to thee,
For she who hath thee is weak, and I am too kind;
I cannot blame thee for my sake,
Nor canst thou with public kindness honor me
Unless thou robs thy honor's cloak,
And puts apparel on my tattered loving
To show me worthy of thy sweet name.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
