In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my eye that doth first doth thine own eye adore.
So, my eye is litten with night,
And my eye that doth my soul adore.
Yet my eye, constant star, and true,
Doth nightly bark, and doth nightly beauteous.
For, having thee gone, my eye is gored,
And 'tis my soul that doth my deeds live,
And my body, by thee, doth nightly be.
  And thus, my eye is gored, and my mind doth lie,
  To try a newer bond


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Like to the lark, I sing,
And in the praise thereof spends all his might.
Why should I compare thee to nature's song,
When it knows well my days are fleeting and no more?
Or should I sing of love, which lives and dies;
For thou art as fair and kind,
Like to the lark, I cannot subdue.
  Let others raise their voices to acclaim;
  My song is for thee, and they that level.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Transforming ill to good again.
So, do you, and wish me well,
As I drink down the cure for my ill health,
No matter how bitter the taste may be.
I'll bear the pain, the punishment, the fight,
If it brings me to a purified sea.
The only hope, though much, is found,
In this mixed-up world, where naught is truly there.
  I'll notick the bitterness that I spake,
  For, like a river, 'tis not all my length.


