So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May remain in thy stead.
For thou art so possessed with that which thou receiv'st not gladly,
So thy sweet self doth thy cheek, which makes thy love more strong,
Thy sweet self doth thy sweet self resemble,
And for thy sweet self doth thy sweet self too,
For bending all thy loving thoughts on thee,
The injuries that to myself I do,
Doing thee vantage, double-vantage me.
  For thee, all is done, have what shall have no end.
  Thou gav'st me thine not to give back again.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose action is no stronger than thy love's increase?
O, let me know, how thy love will fulfill
And what it works in, that thou mayst know it not.
For how can I seek thee to mend
The wound of love, when thou art gracious,
Or to honor what thou dost withhold?
O, know, thy error pays my debt in full;
So I lose thee, through my own misguided look.
  For I have loved thee in such sort
  As, thou dost me, but as I dost him.


