Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what is 't but mine, which can say more
Than this rich praise, that you alone are you,
In whose confine immured is the store
Which should example where your equal grew?
Lean penury within that pen doth dwell
That to his subject lends not some small glory,
But he that writes of yore,
Making his style admired everywhere.
  If some suspect of my misdeed,
  Then thou alone kingdoms of hearts shouldst owe.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Alike or different, in love's quiet way.
But why should I say her untrue,
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
Thus, she thinks me naive, unknowing
The sat'ring that my days should pass.
I return, though she knows well my days are past their prime
And siftless beauty will steal my breath;
  For love's sweet scheme is in my least care
  To take my joy from my past.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak of my faults, and I straight will halt,
Defiant to thy reasons, thou art the dost.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me half so ill,
To set a form upon desired change,
As I'll myself disgrace, knowing thy will;
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy walks, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name no more shall dwell,
Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
  For thee, against myself I'll vow debate


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy beauty's proud ebb and flow.
If I could pen the azure of thy brow
And count anew each charm that graces thee,
The world would cry, "This poet tells but praise,
No face on earth could claim such heavenly hue."
My parchment, yellowed by the sun's embrace,
Would be dismissed, as ancient men deceive,
And your true worth, they'd say, is but a chase,
A bard's wild dream, no more, they'd believe.
  Yet, should a child of yours inherit earth,
  You'd live again,


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And for my sin, I find it signed.
But my eyes, which are fixed,
Are moored within a harbor of the mind;
My soul cherishes what they spurn,
Defying the disapproval of my eyes, it offers no surrenders.
My eyes, which yearn to gather your romance,
At their own courage, by thee blushing stand.
For my sake, they've been caught,
Two bodies in one, far in distant lands.
  Yet, love's note is not love's own verse,
  But my heart's own general, maligned,


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Who is not so firm that it fears no shift.
For to thy sensual fault I bring in sense--
Thy adverse party is thy advocate--
And 'gainst myself a lawful plea commence.
Such civil war is in my love and hate
As I, that thou mayst true,
And I, an accessary, shall feel
The sweetness of this love's ebb and flow.
  For I love thee, and therefore they, in their spite,
  That to thy right I add something more.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the world, unbless some mother's wish.
So, if you were minded so, the whispers but confirm
Your worth, more precious as it's sought by time;
The sharpest spur cannot force it to speed,
For fury's touch brings forth but a groan,
A sound that echoes in my heart's deep,
A reminder of the joy your presence imbues.
  For in this sound, I find my heart's truth:
  My joy recedes, my grief moves on in light.


