Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As those whose hearts are tied to thee,
Adorned in black, they mourn with gentle sighs,
As those who have lost their way,
And in the darkness, they view things unrespected;
Then, in the darkness, they contemplate
The hideous, white, and unprepared,
Let them woe, with white, and meek,
Do our worst, with an eye that knows her woe.
  But I will not woe, for I see
  The fullest weight of ill fortune's blame;
  And yet, I'll live, through woe, my state.


