Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And many, yet unset, will be the king of my heart.
For bending all my loving thoughts on thee,
The earth can claim her own worth, unset,
But my essence, the nobler part, belongs to thee.
The sea, though it gather, is but diluted,
And to his thoughts, my spirit is glued.
  So, if he flourishes and I am cast adrift,
  The world may see my love, but not his.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Behold, my love, and wish I were renewed,
Whilst, like a willing patient, I will drink
Potions of eisel 'gainst my strong infection;
No bitterness that I will bitter think,
Nor double penance, to correct correction.
Yet be most glad, though my sickness be recured,
Thy bosom is that there be more sorrow than love's own spite
To eat the cure for my dear plight.
  For thee, who languishes and I am proved untrue,
  I must attend time's leisure with my moan.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what is 't but mine, which is 't alone?
Whence dost thou derive this art
To make men's eyes more bright than theirs,
And in thy breast even so doth thy face lie?
Who hath taught thee to make right
To look on diest unless thou thyself dost deceive
And do me disgrace, knowing thy will;
Who hath taught thee to make war upon me,
To do more for me than mine own desert?
  Love, beauteous thou art more,
  Than both thy help and me.


