Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose action is this, that thou mayst true,
Finding thy tenor with me, where thou art most glad.
Ten times thyself were happier than thou art
If ten of thine ten times refigured thee;
Then what could death do if thou shouldst depart,
Leaving thee living in posterity?
O, how many a happy title do I hold,
Happy to have thy love, happy to die!
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Thou mayst be false, and yet I know it not.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
With my being, thou shalt not be old,
For my eyes in thee dost lie;
But in my soul's vast archive,
I see a beauty thou dost conceal,
A frame of flesh, ten times more in worth.
Then should I say mine eye be old,
Though I, the selfsame, am an eternal slave,
He'll live again, through progeny and mirth.
  This thy praise cannot so much hold,
  But in my sight, it is so grounded.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what is most true, no painting set.
Then, Love, learn to read what silent love hath writ.
What worth the world, that deems it otherwise?
If not so fair, then love's deceit is right,
Its truth obscured by all men's nays and cries.
How can love's sight be true, when dimmed by tears,
