The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep,
With virtuous hand would bear the flame,
And from that fall sheen he rekindled,
By yearning for his new birth,
Then I, most lame, found that he was but one hour mine,
Sewing and weeping through the endless night.
So I, lame, have been at his mercy,
He, nor the ghostly presence, ever near,
Whispering wisdom in the night's tranquil parade,
Could evermore control my heart's destiny entwined.
