Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
Though not to love's call, his deeds are judged from those fond.
So, love, beauteous, and true,
Deserves no praise, for it shines so bright;
My heavy lids, then fade, and wind is tied to his side;
So, my spirit wanes, bereft, with sorrow filled,
A sire who with his offspring does take his leave.
The cure, my sorrow, in this plight, is found:
  Love's sweetness summons the shame,
  If purest light may merit pity.


