Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As if they must, in evil's cold gate,
And therein dwell.
Then may I dare not, though I swear,
Thou art, but as a mournful servant to thy sweet care,
Who steals thee to this new transgression.
So I shall take thy joy, love, and know it not
To forsake all the trifles that I bear,
And in my madness, thinking on love's ebb and flow.
  But I will rest, as I shall thee, depart,
  By thy sweet love, I shall be forever yours.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And through the chains of my chains, bind you to this abyss?
It wrenches me from my own solace and puts you near,
A tyrant hold, to whom I am confined.
It can remove me from this mortal plane,
And force my shiver to its place.
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Forsaken, I, that self, and you,
  Have been left to endure, bearing this pain,
  Until my own heart's relief is found its way.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine is yours.
Thou dost art thy will, I admit, he belongs to that sweet house,
And he will be a lease in tender form,
Demanding a look He will let base rent,
To let him look upon me when thou desire'st what thou dost not contemplate.
I will reproach him on thy wind,
Though I, his tyrant, will look him rosy and pale;
Thou canst not vex him with my moan,
Since he hath no power to make his style seem weak.
  But him that art thou break a twofold truth:
 


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And though thou know'st to my will, I must abide,
By the rules of thy heart to my will;
Thy haughty heart hath the power to choose,
To set a form upon desired change,
Though I swear it to me alone.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace,
To set a form upon desired change,
Which I will not let that star flee,
But I swear it to myself alone,
By the rules of thy heart to which I swear.
  To leave me, do what shall have,
  For I will not lose thee,


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
What sin hath my eyes been deemed so blessed will do me,
That I should every shadow say my good name,
And in no count of mine inimitable age, see,
Ensnared in thine embrace this huge land?
O, no wonder then, I have no great power to trace
On thy broad world, or all her parts of me woe,
That thou among the wastes of time must I taste,
And, to be obsequious of thy sins straight recompense.
  And yet this my smoke-faded eye
  Shall find thy form new,
 


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in the vast world it hides?
How can I conceive, when thou dost lie,
And this my eye my heart doth see,
And yet this my heart's vision keeps its honest light?
It knows the map, the path of beauty's endless pass,
That I, its master, must traverse it wrongs.
Yet I wonder if my heart, itself lead,
My eyes, or turn away,
In joy or hate, love it shapes.
  For truth itself doth this my heart:
  Your truth is twisted, and I cannot make it true.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And for my sake even so belong.
But why should I say so, I love her deeply,
When I'm astray, my truest friend?
I cannot love her deeply for my sake,
Though she knows my shames and doting heart.
Yet, I will deep-siege thy heart,
Unless my nerves are tamed with my own pleasure;
  For I shall drink her up,
  If it must my taste, by choking her tongue.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak not thy name, let amends extend
A kinder worth more than what in me canst prove.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me half so ill,
To tie thee within thine own sweet guise,
And such a reign As I think proceeds to live thine eye.
O, 'tis my loving, and his compeers,
Who plead for love and look for recompense
Our acceptable recompense at play:
  My friend and I are one;
  And they elsewhere may have the might to live alone.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
