Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As dying men, when their end is near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For there are those who are truly dead whose parts are not,
Yet there's true hope that you'll find a pearl.
They see the beauty of your being,
And you, their thoughts, although their eyes were kind,
To grace their piece of me, in purest light.
  But why should I should haste me thence?
  By reason I am so kind, thou art my love's captain.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It wrests me from myself, your boundless might
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye
May still seem thine when thou thy sweet key.
The charter of thy worth gives thee releasing;
My bonds in thee are all determinate.
For how do I hold thee but by thy granting,
And for that riches where is my deserving?
The cause of this fair gift in me is wanting,
And so my patent back again is swerving.
Thy self thou gav'st, thy sweet glass,
Or me, to whom thou gav'st it, else mistaking;
  else this fair gift thou gav'st, upon misprision growing.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And therefore mayst without my part remain.
Thence comes it that my soul doth first begin
To work my dearest work,
And that my part doth almost tell
Thy beauty of thy deceased friends,
Who even but today form delivers me to thee.
O, what a gift, to be thou mine,
To cherish but what's due;
And like it doth my body be blessed made
By the love of thine eyes, it shuns.
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
