If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for home my love's appeal, kind, fulfill.
Desire will not my love excuse, for love breeds the time,
For love's sweet shape was ever mine,
And in his humor, it may claim a place.
Grant me the strength, to bear thy whimsical lead,
The captive's absence in thy liberty's cell,
With forbearance, I'll withstand, each pause and thread,
Without reproach, thy freedom I'd never quell.
  In these lines, both strong and true,
 Resolve no hatred, for they were free.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what is 't but mine own bright?
To say they err I dare not be so bold,
Although I swear it to myself alone.
And, to be sure that is not false I swear,
A thousand sighs from depths only I know,
Each line echoing, in its age, my name,
Revealed from our eyes by all the world once more.
  In truth, Love, dost thou promise stay,
  In the beauty of thy hard-love.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
At first blush in the necklaces of my virtuous lie.
That she hath her virtue, though not so bright,
To say I was false tongue-tied speaking of her fame,
And her glory is but a mask of deceit,
A fiction spun to guard my memory bold.
Were I not true, the blush on cheeks
Hath false as blush, the tomb,
At first blush on dead, whereon it was false women's bane.
  For I have sworn thee fair, false heart, in lies,
  To swear against the truth, before thine eyes.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Why should I not reproach thy double falsehood,
When I am stained with transgressions deep?
Grant me the strength to kindle love,
And to correct correction of your words,
When I am tired?
Thou art more than poets' praises are;
If thou wouldst but surrender, gently seep
Into a truth of love, and seize thy chance.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


