Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
From this far, to dwell with earth and sun.
The earth, I bathed in this season's sweetest dew,
But, to no other pass my time,
For beauty's touch, like water's fire, takes pleasure
And in this huge rondure he robs.
O, what a gift, to share this love profound,
To cherish life, and in its tender care,
A healing balm, for all afflicted by melancholy's sight.
  But what's so sacred that it fears no harm?
  Love's promise may waver, or remain firm.


