When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A beauty like her own decay.
I have seen truth with suspicion's eyes,
But by all that's sacred, my blunders
Revealed a second youth, my failures
Proved my deepest love for you.
Now, all is finished, keep what's everlasting.
My longing I'll never more force to test,
To try a newer bond with an older friend,
A god in love, who binds me close.
  Welcome me, then, as the best gift next to bliss,
  To your purest heart, where my soul is driven.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Why should I not then be undone?
My verses but yet be in my read,
Of the worth of the world in some part?
For why should I not say mine is of true worth,
And therefore to be reviled of what was once lent?
O, know, sweet love, you'll be my argument;
So all my best is dressing old words new,
Spending again what is already spent.
  For as my love is spent,
  So is my love, still telling what is told.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy merit's dark shadow in my sight.
If I could pen the azure of the skies
And count anew each charm that graces thee,
The world would wail and gnash its teeth in despair,
Leaving men to suffer this unrest.
My parchment, yellowed by the sun's embrace,
Would be dismissed, as ancient men deceive,
And th' beauty lose its grasp on beauty's power.
  But beauty's parchment, yellowed by the sun's embrace,
  Lath not enough to keep the user's memory.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by thine voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
