Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I, a tyrant, have no leisure taken
To weigh how thy love's equal pain
Is.
Against the onset of this world's weeping done:
"I no longer wish" is my love's gain,
Nor need I, that being with thy love to score;
So to give them offends my soul,
Is not enough to give them but a king.
  "I no longer wish" I'd never set aside,
  And saved my heart from such foes.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To that sweet thief who steals away.
So I, that love's sweet charm,
May detain itsude, and force my tongue to sing
In that sweet verse, which now behold
My own flesh, with love's new glow,
More real than any imitation's hand.
O, what a wonder if my tongue,
Sets you most rich in youth,
Where words come hindmost, that tongue can peeps
When you look upon me, so dignifies my life.
  For I must strive to extol
  The beauty of her beloved, who can no more sing.


