Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
At those who, not begotten fair, bestow
False beauty's crown on an unworthy heart.
Adorned in black, as compassion's ally,
Gaze gently on my sorrow's endless plain.
No dawn can compare to her somber sight,
Nor the radiant star that bids the day adieu,
For her mournful eyes, a beacon, burn with ardent flame,
Adorning the evening with elegiac hue.
  So thou, all she's true, and I am drawn to her,
  By beauty's art, thou being mine.


