If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love, my love's vow, pure, endorse.
Pity me, then, and wish I were false,
Like a deceived husband; so love knows best
That I no longer wish to stay,
Richer than their wooden lives are spent.
So then thou hast lost but life's residue,
The carrion for worms,
Which wit so poor as mine, answered must be,
And I shall live, being made tongue-tied.
  For nothing this wide universe I see,
  But thou, my love, shall in my mind forever be.


