Lest it prove on thee, an injury on me,
Such that no words of fair intent can arrive at,
Let foes' and soothsayers' praises suffice to this breach.
 


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A tempest mistress concealing truth from thee.
I do not forbid the bell to speak
That sovereign falsehood will not break in hence;
In truth I believe, though she swears it as falsehood,
And keeps it up in her bed of falsehoods so high.
But why should she conceal the truth she loves so dearly
When I am well aware, not knowing
Her falsehoods and secrets of the false heart?
  If she fears me she will be guilty too
  Of telling a falsehood so vile a thing.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike not with slander's sting but with love's mirth;
Sneak in upon my disobedience and lead me anew
To correct what I have omitted to impart.
Lest I should speak ill of thee, do take heed,
And thou shalt reap the bitter for my offense's sweets.
But my cherished one, who suffers by this,
Cannot bear the thought of losing thee, let him not rehearse
The guilt of his wrongs, let him bear the heat.
  So, if I offend thee, do not reprove me;
  Still, love, forgive my offenses,


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy worth, my deepest sorrow.
If thou wilt persevere, in thine opposition welcome
The arbitrament of thy weakness; deem
The blow of folly to be the better part.
When taking counsel against a brave foe,
Strive not to overburden thy voice, so as to rehearse
The strength of thee, in respect of what is claimed;
In self-defense, draw lessons from past transgressions
And harmonies of thy given grace.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
To thee I confide that which I forgive most.
For if my heart does base favor, methinks it shift
To thy pleasure, more dear than myself due:
I forgive not what my head doth not see,
Thy foe to all that smacks thee out for hard.
You, my dear, are guilty of much; in me
You are stained with many failings, and need:
This myself that adopts thee corrupts me,
O'er-loving heart being partur'd with myself
The better on thy account for my tripping.
  Take heed, sweet heart


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
A stronger love exists for thee in comparisons thine,
Which for my falling I pen breaks free.
You must excuse my stealing, dear heart,
For love as an art thou withholds
From my appreciating thoughts thy boasted leisure.
Give heed, dear heart, to my choking words,
As holding not 't is stealing for me.
  Let them love thy contented state and in that embrace
  conceal their thefts before thee.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
So you, in your fears, forget to pull
The life-long bond that binds you close, and let her go,
Without a word, savoring her freedom.
Oh, let your heart mourn for my lost, treasured,
Passionate breast, as I grieve for your own,
As if it knows, by some hidden gracelessness,
That you, its master, own the treasure you clutch.
  For I, possessed by you, will not relent,
  And you, I, shall pay the ultimate price, dear.


