Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To grieve thee as a heartless god doth scorn.
How canst thou love another when thou dost hate me,
Since every spirit is thine in its nature,
Like unto thyself, kind and loving spirit?
Tears of love, I cry, are like to thee, though not so bright,
For to thy self I feel like weeping doth thine eyes rehearse.
Thy heart doth mock my grief, to sorrow's grief,
And, I, moved by that sorrow, do tend to
The other's wounds, both heart and hand.
  But


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my heavy eyelids in purgatorial vice?
O, pain, torment, torment, torment, torment,
In many a heart, black and grievous is painted,
But you, my darling, come out on top.
No face on this vast world can claim to hold
Your worth, and in your absence,
I'm alone, and wretched.
  Yet, should a heart chance to claim victory,
  I'll bear the pain, the punishment, the chase.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Bears thy bonds and distributes them thinly.
Thou art too kind and I am too bad,
Too flawed for my own redemption,
Too vile for the common good and just,
Too shallow and I have been deceived,
Pursued after ideals and dressed in
A different robe from the noble's form.
  In him thy true love appears,
  And I, in turn, am to thank.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
What will leave the world so dismal can offer?
When despising thou alone, doubting not alone me,
That makes of love dread and frowns fear its companion?
The world can complain, and moan, and sigh,
The world can but love to adore thee less,
Lest oblivion should in thy wake
Ere thee find sweet o'er dull thine advocate.
  No need for other voices, to make my plight worse,
  Lest all thoughts were my will, evil shown.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Then love, if in my nature thou sense'st not
That I hath borne thee witness to my fate,
Thy love alone might excuse my dire.
If any doubt hath made thee this confession,
Lay low, for love hath passed thee by.
Or call it pity, which hath trained thy brain
To admire the unworthy, that endures
Through trials of pity, which in me thou ne'est see.
  Or call it scorn, which makes me wish
  Thy shadow to stand after my soul's fell.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet Love doth strew thy sweet looks all over the place.
Three winters cold, canker bared, weeds growing on thy face,
Thy cheeks and eyes, plants that grace 'twixt thy face and mind,
One respect; thou must be both winter and spring
To have the fire of life, eyes so kind which face,
Three full moons hatching, which stars do our hearts confess,
Wild and chaotic in motion twirling;
  Yet this, thy worth alone doth show,
  The blind and sweet saviour found when heart and sight thou canst obscure.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unscathed by life's deceitful arts,
Though I've known better days, clear as crystal sea,
How she might feign interest in me,
And thus deceive me with lies she knows well,
Though she knows my soul is marred,
By the deeds I can't forget.
But when her falsehoods I find out,
Her deceitful eyes and false words seem to me
Like angels come dancing in front of my sight,
Transforming into fairies to forestall,
Replacing truth with sweetness, and a sweet disguise.
  In love's strange ways, even angels


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Rip not absolve from myself the guilt
That doth rightly belong to me, in thy sight.
What virtue dost thou have that I do stray?
It is my nature that wanders, wild and free.
To avoid thy reckoning, truly repent,
And bring me redemption through thy love.
Roam at thy pleasure, thy thoughts are thy treasure;
No force can arrest their depredations,
That move me with anger to trace them.
  Yet, persist not; near death I stand,
  Not to be compared with those hanging.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My sorrows and all that I fear.
If thou wilt be cruel I forgive thee; but if thou do break
My heart with words, I will break mine eye.
For to thine own heart I give thee the gift of sweet lies,
A sanctuary whereupon my heart shall retreat,
Though thy heart should leave my sight, still loving thee.
For nothing in me charms thee as thou dost thine own,
Thy heart's treasure, a treasure hidden; so I thee,
By this my love shall be ever strong:
