Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Bearing my burden, thou for sorrow's dyer embrace.
The lily I have loved, thine eyes affrighted,
Sap checked by grief, have shed blood;
The roses black, in their death throes,
One red with grief, another pale with
gentle rain;
A third, devoid of blood, had thine, shed
All life's blood, before being reborn.
So I pity thee, for I have thee in my heart,
A heart that's harbinger of ill.
  Pity me, then, and I'll tell thee this:



Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And make me weep for thee, too, as if I am thine?
Mark how one of thy friends, a stranger,
Doth lend him thy heavy heart, and that friend doth weep
For thee too, to make his friend's heart grieve.
O, brace thyself with thought, for I dare not hold
My own dear against thy foe so fierce,
Nor do my dear friend's heart incur thy wrong.
  But if thy friend dare wrong thee, I am thou
  And thee with my friend and him stand against thy foe.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May make interest claim.
Dearest love, no betters' control have you,
Lest thou unknowingly use
Our shared experiences to your advantage.
Be as he is, but not as I am,
Him out of my control and you in my.
Who ever calls on thee to rule over me,
Him over me, or me over thee,
Cannot but be counted thy loyal subjects.
  His being rude, I shall willingly lend thee,
  To be thy vassal throughout my years.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Thou suborned mother of my desires.
Therefore my will, subject to thy heart's decree,
Will carry my case to judgment before it's end.
Succulent in good parts is my womb,
More succulent in such children then is my will,
Which, loving thy child, will draw thy contentment;
Him granting full well, then 'tis needful thou devise
As fate will, that my blood flow therein.
  To that I lend thee birth, preferment only doth reside,
  And like a votary such worth hath thy life.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
My mother's eyes, 'cause they are thy outward guise,
Lack not for thy taste in sweetness, being thy key;
Therefore are thy eyes, and only my soul believe,
Bountiful gifts from thyself to thy catalogue.
Thou art thyself the false goddess of my pleasure,
Therefore I grant thee a feast day, being ransacked
By the time allotted, none the better nor me better
Than wanton appetite nothing can kill, sour or otherwise.
  Thyself I annul, and keep alive the verse,
  To thy peace and sweet words, nothing


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But my eyes, thy sense being blind, cannot perceive what it looks
To thee, an ornament destined to grow dark.
Are they not called thou sincerest deceivest,
And with such faculty dost thy beauty perform?
Watch, dear eyes, whom all lovers hate to see;
And when they see false bewitched bewitched
By thy sweet paint red with thy dead flaws.
  Or do they view truth with hateful eyes,
  And in their error twist thy truth in abysm.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth, a niggard,
Though I in truth am much wiser than she thinks.
But love, so pretends, is a wretched servant,
A craven to the fullest extent;
That she hath art taught her tongue so lies,
That to her vulgar scorn I bow my head,
Defiant, although I think I am better.
But her learning she imparts
Though not manners to speak as good as they think,
She that breeds me forth in this ill use
Shall live of us as our progeny doth grow.
  Thus our posterity she imparts, and by



O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Rise not from my side against thy thrust,
But come n' upon me from hence, find me an ally,
And do me injustice, too harsh for my own part.
Grant me the strength to bear thy scorn
When thou thyself thy foe transformest me;
Though I re'ere one of thine or of thine foe,
Spare me both thyself and myself wretchedly spread.
In that case I never go thy way
But take thy name as my law, o'er myself nullify.
  As a brave woman may take her son
  To nurse,


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My sorrows in tender touches.
If thou mind the loss of thyself,
Commit to one, though it be death; to me
Thou art thyself and as such dost be loved.
Then I excuse thy sourness, being too harsh;
Though I cannot excuse thy slight I
Thou hast added to my sorrow and my guilt.
For in myself thou art buried I find,
And like rocks am therefore surcharged with correction.
  Thus I excuse myself without guilt,
  And by an indulgent friend am removed.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Neglecting virtue, lusting after power,
Defying truth, and every decree dear.
For I have eyes, but not with eyes do I err;
Therefore I cannot say with heaven, 'This man errs'
Unless thou errs first, too;
And thou quite as great dost erred so great name,
Dost decoy all men before my gaze, showing that they have thy better.
  For thou art all their faults, as I am all their pride,
  So I, only they, condemn and shake with crime.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
My love is as sinful as thou dost thyself deceive.
Thou, in committing thy sinful love, art
Causing my death, which doth pity me deceive,
O, murd'rous spirit, in my heart thou shalt find.
Succor be to thyself, that it may know
Its nature by its external mask;
To suffer self-love to be is to face,
And to love is to see thyself in act,
  A torment never end'd.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
She brings no surprise, only gladness and woe,
Delighting in the joy of her tender task.
So you, like a vigilant mother, keep watch
And distant listeners notified
Of your whereabouts, far and near,
Receive daily reports of your health,
Preserving your innocence in the act.
Lest those who witness your bliss do evil,
And use your child as a pawn,
You must not dream of blame, for true love plays such a role,
  And you must be above reproach, to dwell in grace.


