Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As if to acknowledge some disgrace.
On my part, such mourners are with me
As if to attend a deceased's funeral,
Others with them, are silent,
Disdainful, even from my sight,
Pardoning thy sins more than they are borne.
For to these I add offense,
Thy honor more than I bear,
And they, with them, are thy friends gone.
  In all these offenders I see a fault,
  Which on thy part doth it most oppress.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Then bind not my friend to share this sorrow,
But take him from me, and I from thee;
I will not part with him, for he is thy;
Nor will he, for he fears for me,
For I, a sadder than him, will him part.
Then should I part, my friend better well than me;
For what am I but poor, that so is my life?
Then find remedy, be it want or want alone.
  Pity me, for I have no other friend.
  He


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Gives as interest to you, making you double thy cost;
And, for myself this denial is more
Than having recourse to your tyrant spite,
Which proves but double welfare for both.
For, having recourse to you, I lose both,
And so forfeit both to thee;
And that, my love and him dined on the
Savour of bare falsehood and of you so discred.
  For so spurned by both our history and of present
  Me, I lose both both my selves and both both thy dear.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For niggard almost cannot desert his mark;
To loving she commits of a crutch,
More than enough hath she no art.
Art thou cunning enough to know it not
