If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love, my love's charter, so fine;
But this love in thine interest does tie me;
I by this love thy faith enwraps,
And thy love, sweet, to this end leases me.
O, what a league of lies it is that lies,
That on this counterfeit claim I here find
A league fore of hate, dread, and indifferent;
And what a bower was this seemed in vain,
To have our pleasure proved by Time's law cruel and stony.
  This unfair start hath wept tears that stopped our march,
  A


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what a sad beauty it makes bare,
Dimming the keenest of keen-eyed views.
But you, Love, of such skill set deceive them as blind
That, giddy with your pride, they forgo the truth,
And give ear to flattery, for love's sake
Sets no store by good sense to bring;
His reason, let him disclose, is flattery,
And 'gainst himself leads the blind to this end.
  Love knows that heads doth love more, and tails doth hate less;
  This is known as a happy correction.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But all in so doth grace me with her wits.
Jude, your beauty lies in your wits,
Though false eyes may deceive; yet men love
To give praise to faces they cannot see.
O, what a mansion have those vices got
Which for their habitation in you dwell!
Make war upon these bulwarks with your graces;
Unauthorized gladiators shall disgrace
The hallowed ground.
  These shut-in mullahs shall rank with the blind in their eyes
  When enamored by your sweet love their gaze.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Make not a grave accusation against me
When in my soul I find satisfaction all well;
But let my deeds speak for me: my dear love well knows
Her husband's dear and hearty esteem;
To turn her from me I must trespass,
I may not incur her disgrace yet.
O, change thy thought from time to time;
Find a place to dwell in the most
That in thy change of plan I abide,
Or at least therein I know thou ow'st.
  This thoughtless slander I too must be unwooed
  And correct this ignorance so deep a crime.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sorrows.
If I were to perish, like one doomed to grow,
Thou, cruelest of all beings, shouldst know it,
Being wise, not in cruelty make known.
For if I live another life
I know not what I now do;
But when that which I in loathed I did live
My life hath endeared me to thee.
O, what a thrift is this which we call art!
To teach and to excuse we forget
The dear which in our fondness finds no excuse!
  Or if we meet with none to fix
 


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Into their very mouths they give me lip service.
For if they frown upon my offense,
They look on their own ranks;
And I, a thousand blunters, confess
That this 't is by my deeds they boast.
Then do I look upon my own blunting,
And find this loss of fire to blame:
I have used up all virtues in me,
Neglected none that should be mine to bestow,
Swept all my gifts unto thy rule,
Transformed orphaned love to breed tender esteem.
  In this waste and traitory I cleave


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
In self thou art much stronger;
In thy dearest are gifts doth store
Than those scythe cuts which life hath annexed.
Who hates those she loves so dearly?
Be thou, if in thy youth thine own youth
Binds age's tooth, that by time would die;
Though thou have many friends do call,
Return not unthrift to those weak knell.
  Mine only love in thine breast is rule,
  That says thou love not me for our bequest.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the place where she left him;
So you, kept as ransom by fate's command,
Are to my hand or your sweet repose
In the tender care of my love's breast.
No fear can mar me while that love doth lay
His snare on the spot where he roams,
Nor can my other half watch him while he plays
And juggles with that fickle stick.
