Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adding to thy pain, their own role rehearse.
The dawn's first light, I see,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the pity of thy remorse's doom;
So, I add to thy sorrow, and try to lift,
The burden of sorrow, heavy as lead,
That on thy moving heart, I'll find relief, kind.
  Pity me, then, and I'll tell ye,
  That pity alone can bring me thee kind.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To try to break my heart from its shell,
And find in my soul a refuge?
When all my ties to you are loosed,
What strength will I feel then but for my breath?
Shall my heart, which yearns for your embrace,
Be but a jest, playing at heart,
A shallow cry from the depths, far from you?
  O, none, unless this miracle have might,
  That my heart may one day find its way to you.


