So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May come to pass too;
But he, so great, will still stay--
I love him, therefore for thy sweet spite
He will in that breast belong,
That to you I have ten times the value of my life.
What can be more sweet than this, that bestows
Such richness on so poor a dwelling?
  There can be no true adornment
  But what thou dost make for thyself.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Supposed am I not enough to drown thy sorrow.
How can mine own be greater than thy sorrow
When thou thyself most conspire'st with mine to make it mine?
In me thy contentment my love no more desire
