Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To hinder my tormentor's bright light.
As on a queen's cheek the sad maybes glow,
So do those eyes pity me, and woe me thus:
"Roses may share his hue, but not his crown,
For hearts are such that they will not share."
Thus shall I patiently wait, though black be thy heart,
And in that wait torment me not;
For black is fairer than grief, and more rare,
And though thy heart be pure, yet my part
Cannot be so dark as thine eyes can grow pale.
