Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
That heart, like a shelled fish, doth share this fate
And more, more, by chance slips from our sight.
Yet, shamed by fate and all the world,
It runs away, forsaking me,
For love's own sweet reward, runs low.
No heart that hates me, no plea can bring
The weight of love, let alone set free.
  Yet, here I must, and dwell in plight,
  Lived in love, and that makes my heart woe.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Owns the lawful title to thee.
Against this, his counsel offers no argument,
And, being flogged, I release him at thy will;
So, thou that art now my debtor, find'st
By the laws of this realm and of these kings,
And do as thou wilt, sweet slave; I guide thee
To the land of my love, and thine own dear reward.
  But I forbid one most heinous crime:
  Thou mayst not rob him of what he owes thee.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And more than enough is 'gainst my content,
That, spite of my will, thou my foe mayst make me mad.
Sometime ten times happier I am than thou art,
And by so much more thy love and hate
Dissipates in a thousand dolts, burning kind.
But if this be my will, and thy will be false,
What power can my humble state have,
To say against ten times thy worth!
  More than enough is enough to make me happy,
  And yet not to say so is so weak.


