Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As I, to my exceeding sorrow, yield myself
To every torment, afflicting both my friend and me.
For what can my heart do but weep,
Whilst my friend's heart does what it woos,
And in its wailing, touches my own heart?
O, what a torment hath befallen me,
Whilst my friend hath so much suffered, and yet none see
Whate'er the matter with myught be,
But that my friend hath this in his wailing confessed:
  My dear heart hath my friend's trespass, and thus my grief
 


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind up two hearts in waste,
For still more I must beseech you, through guilt and fear,
That you may heal my friend and me,
For whom I have so deep a case.
O, that all my woes might be my friend's ransom
And cost him not so much as my heart's weight,
But he should share that friend's pain, and share
The sum of our sufferings, and so make
Our shared woes stronger than before.
  Fear not to share that friend's grief, though it seem
  To me quite strong.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May have the like claim upon me.
What reason will my poor art have
When he hath attained such riches and settled down
Within thine own sweet expanse,
Where beauty's glass doth afford
A mirror of his own self to view?
Can music please him now behold,
Or at least his own sweet self praise,
Since what he lacks in self-love to show,
He better in praising thee in his being.
  Now all is done, have what shall have no end.
  I will wait on him, though he live and hate me,


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And more, contented will be thy pleasure;
Till whatsoever happies me most,
I give my all to thee, to have thee.
Such is my love, to thee I so belong
That, having her, I no longer wish to be gone,
Although in her all my happiness lies;
And, she, in all her happy content,
I make most hate, being full of her.
  Such is my love, to thee, I so belong,
  That, having her, I no longer wish to be gone.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
O, swear to thy soul that I did love thee,
As thine eyes doth thy soul perceive,
And that I was thy heart's will, then wage
To test the love in heart that I find.
For how else can thy soul know
My love's true worth, although my deeds be false?
If it be false, be it for ever,
As I do witness, ever to love me.
  For I was thy heart's will, then wage
  To test the love in heart that I feel.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in thy own looking glass thou dost deceive
And there reigns love and all in it twain.
So, Love, be of thyself so wary
As I be of thyself, so wary
As I, for thyself, will be of thyself.
For what's so great that it fears no harm
But what in thee doth hide, make it tall?
  Be of thyself so wary, so unwavering
  That, when thyself deceives with seeming?


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I in truth and right do coincide;
And for this cause she adds her verse to my content,
And drinks her scorn even to my grief.
But why should I believe her when I see
My true thoughts and true feelings beseech
To play the fool and gull me with false views?
I believe her tongue, though she knows it is false,
And in this she thinks my heart is deceived.
  But why should I believe her when she deceives me?
  Because I cannot love her whom she doth love.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike not with my jealousy but with thy heart;
Bring not my woes here but here;
And do this even so, lest I repent
Of my sins before thine eyes.
O, change thy thought, that I may change my mind.
Shall not my love be bled for thy crime,
Whose trespass is thine own love for my sake,
To set a form upon desired change?
Let him but change his thought, and I assure ye
  There is no change that I do see.
  My love remains what it always was, to thee and for myself.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sympathies.
If I could write the azure of thy gaze
And count anew each charm that graces thee,
The world would cry, "This poet tells but praise,
No face on earth could claim such heavenly hue."
My roses would turn from thee like the dawn,
One blushing in shame, another pale with haste;
A third, neither red nor white, had thine appearance stained,
And from thine own bloom I would withdraw,
Wasting thy breath on this defiled wight.
  Yet, since every soul doth know my worth,
 


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight.
For why should my soul love some external feature,
When it itself doth good works in thee?
No, 'tis my flesh that doth love,
Defying the judgment of his soul;
And, for this, I have much grief,
Believing my very deeds to be stained.
For I have painted thee fair, and due
To many, thine eyes outclass;
But thy good deeds outshine those hinders.
  For I have sung good words of praise,
  And brought to light many flaws in my conceit


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
