Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To keep him from harm's reach, so dear, she thinks.
But fearfully, she lets him go,
For what worth the world, that he may soar
In joy and peace, staying put?
No wonder then, she keeps him close,
For what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Her joy depends, in part, on his trusty wing,
  And he, in part, depends on her quiet skill.


