Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But thou in thy blind see'st with thine eyes.
For 'tis not the figured face, though many forms have it,
Which in thy heart doth live, that I see,
But the plain of thy soul, in truth,
Which in thy breast doth live, and bear thee shame.
Who hath taught thee to see the false, the unworthy,
Which in thy heart doth live, and bear thee shame?
  Thyself have I been dumb, and now I see
  The truth of thy cold and rude heart.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I, in truth, am as ignorant as can be.
Yet, love, she loves not to be believed,
Lest I, too, should be deceived,
And, for my sake, I swear by thine eyes,
Truth loves her, though I not know it to be.
And for this my love does carry this lie,
That she believes me for thy sake,
Though I, in truth, do love her.
  This lie she swears, but I believe her
  And so shall I love her, though she does me deceive.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my sorrow be dressed with slander's art,
Nor my praise with golden praise spread.
If I reproach thee, I swear
By my own weakness, knowing not thy will;
But in myself, thou dost cause my ruin,
A villain growing rich from my deeds.
  For I corrupt not beauty, holding back,
  Towards some inward good, guarding against others.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sense of thee.
If I could write the azure of thy gaze
And count anew thy virtues, better knew
Than thinking on thy parts, when I should write.
For thou art so fair that I cannot write
Thy true love, though I feel it so;
To give thee name I must not contradict
My lives, being thy spirit's bearer.
  And so, love, be kind to thyself; I shall thrive
  When thou word'st that I must die.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For 'tis my soul that doth love these things,
Making lament for the things I find wanting,
As if it could not possibly be true.
Yet in this love it is true,
My heart being crowned with all kinds of praise,
Then thinking on thy most fullness,
Thou best of dearest and mine only love.
  For this, I sin and in my despair I praise thee,
  Lest my heart should think it false, and in pride.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Or, at least, that's what my eye doth catch
And for aught else doth love seem to derive.
That sin exists where is my love's foundation?
O, no, 'tis not there. Thou art so possessed with vice
That 'gainst thy love 'gainst my love doth lie,
And 'gainst myself 'gainst myself doth willfully partake.
  'Tis hate in love, and 'gainst myself in love
  is sin by creation's delving.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To avoid a pure fright, she soars off in pursuit.
The lion, her joy in flight chased,
And she, in despair, leaps and lets go,
Leaving her baby behind, who cries out,
In joy, she but smiles, her eyes wide,
In joy, she but grasps hold of the wind.
So you, in love, abandon me,
Your heart pursued, and I, in love, let you go.
  For love brings us both joy and pain,
  And neither can we ever know the other's state.


