Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In thy heart's embrace, all vice takes hold,
A sickness that shall not end until it is cured.
The cure for this vice I seek,
But find it not, for love's own contagion remains,
A sickness that shall not cease until it has slain me.
In thy heart's embrace, every vice I see,
Its power exerted, and more, still, more,
By my love, thou hast given life's poison power.
  More sorrow than hate I have felt,
  More scorn than anger directed at me.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And more than that, torment me with so much more?
O, torment me still, for I cannot refrain
From weeping when thou art near;
Even then, turn my friend's life around,
To make him woe again, with tears flowing.
But if thou live, then turn from me,
I swear thee life is thine, and more,
 than mine own heart can give.
  Yet, if thou live, turn from me, I swear,
  For I but torture with words, and leave thee.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Holds the title to your heart.
Such a loving mistress will keep me company
As on a queen's hand doth beauty sleep;
She lends not beauty's time, but wealth to see,
Which she stores in thy breast,
Or some of thy secretions to show,
Such wealth in love's possession I see.
But, alack, he is not so fair
That I, so captivated by thee, should scorn to admire,
Rather kill with a spurned lover than live in plight.
  For Nature hath trained him to crave
  More than his beauty to show and


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And more than enough thy love will make me kill
By frequent ordering and doing thy will.
I cannot chide thee more, for I see
Thy pleasure frequently calls for my help,
And, sick of glad tidings, I straight repair
My situation to that of a makeless wife.
She hath her pleasure, and will be gone
In happy hours by thy side, I tell;
By then, thou know'st I live in happy hours.
  But when that shall come, find'st I no more glad,
  Than that which was made by thy will.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
O, swear, be false, and I am sure,
By oath, love, I never loved thee false;
But that thou no love for me then alive,
Since that I swear thee for love dead,
Spiteful heart, that thou for love's sake shouldst lie.
So should my love, wide as the world, find out,
To march with me, though fo' our love I am gone.
  For I am thou, and so is my lie,
  And so should thy soul, to love this deceit.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in that they look they cannot find
The basest imitation of ugliness there be?
Why then, Love, do you insist on telling
My eyes what you cannot see,
Stealing the sweetest art from my soul?
Because they cannot be your loveliest creation?
No, nor can they possibly be so,
Unless you be beguiled and blind yourself.
  For my eyes, which to thee are blinded find
  The truth in your false saucy face.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But that she well knows I am an error;
And for this she commits a crime,
I think it right that she should die
To leave behind a living name,
While my lovers' names and proud scorn
To themselves disgraced and bloodied.
This I do for my love she commits
And this she receives from my lovers' frown;
I for love believe they hate me,
And in their blindness look death where they're blind.
  In this I find my lovers' most untrue word;
  Yet in their madness I think on thee and die.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Grant me the pardon: my love well knows
Her own nature hath trained thee to write;
To thee she prays, "Thus far for love, thou shalt be gone."
But why should I then strive to improve
The work that Nature made me for thee?
My verses but one end in view are laid,
To celebrate thy worthy merit;
The other aim is my intent,
To celebrate thy pure and most perfect skill.
  Thus: to celebrate thyself I steel my heart
  And fire my brain to try and improve what Nature made.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sense of thee.
If thou wilt relent, I entrench myself
Against the wretch who perjuries calls;
Against the world, which perjured me once,
And perjuries me still, more wretchedly;
Against the bitter, heartless, spiteful world,
I find a refuge in love, both warm and quiet.
  With gentle kindness, I excuse thy bad deeds,
  Fair thou art, and I am sooved.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And in that love I find my logs.
For through the blindness of thy youth,
My eyes in thy heart err, and find their tombs
Where thy essence once lived and breathed.
So, to be reviled and hate multiplied,
I entrench myself in that love,
Where my heart, converted, meets the logs.
  For thy sake, I write with notes of heart,
  For thou know'st what thy eyes do view.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And that correction hate will urge;
To change thy state from vice to virtue,
A living soul, by love's command.
O, change thy thought, and I may change my plea,
Be thou the husband of one, or of many,
By the grace of thy change.
Make thee another self, so sweet and fair,
That beauty's memory may ever be
In thee, by love's and my glory spread.
  Such a soul, from thee mayst choose,
  When all hearts in me are converted.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the place where it fled, adorned
With cunning, it staves off the pursuers.
So too, may the fierce wolf, so dear a babe,
Lose its bite, as the babe's mother
Calls to him, to share in love's sweet chase.
Oh, thus, why should false colors mimic his face,
Steal life's true essence from his mien,
Seeking shadows where no roses bloom and brace?
  Hunt him down, and kill him as you would see fit,
  Not stealing his form, which you can reclaim in splendor.


