Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Have I not seen those captivated by fame,
And in their quest for admiration, forsake
All they possess, and more, in fortune's game,
To lose all they have, and more, in vain try.
No heart so kind as mine, which bears such a heavy heart,
Can share my sorrow, let alone my friend.
  Yet, love, you've kept my heart from the grave,
  And so have I kept you from the light.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine heir may claim
The value of thy love, more than mine own desert.
Now I understand that my redemption is by thee
And that he, in turn, is to my content,
For my theft he demands a rekindling of all my love,
And I, a worthless robb'ry, yield to that loss,
Owing almost nothing to him, save what is due me.
  So, astonished at this double falsehood,
  I fight with my love, telling myself that I am right.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And more than enough thy love hath my friend bound
To leave him for nothing, and thine own dear love to ransom.
I will tell thee how much more thou wilt be true
