If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
O, swear, be of thyself so chary
As I am of thy love, of such sweet trust,
As I to thee will willingly take part,
As thine own dear doting heart doth this pledge.
O, swear by this love, so chary
As I by thy love will be thy guide,
That I no farther than my love doth plead,
Although my deeds in thee lie down.
  For thee love's lease is this:
  Thy love is my guide, and my truth so I hope.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in that they cannot love it more.
Since each doth his part, how can mine be none
To that which doth belong to thee and me.
Then, beauteous and blind, Love, what sayest thou to my dear eyes
When they see the truth in thee and me?
For they see what is plain in thee, and that thou dost deceive
By arts of deceit where thou art silent.
  How much more doth thy beauty be marred
  By seeing what it knows not, and telling it what it knows.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But that she knows not what truth holds in youth.
I love her for she holds not love's own rose
But some counterfeit that she thinks is holy,
And I forswear to love her because of that shame
That she assists in my vice with her wit.
I tell her not to laugh at my shame,
And she believes me when I tell her I love her.
  And yet, like her, I sometimes swear I love her spite,
  And she believes me when I tell her I hate her.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my grief be dressed up with dressing old,
Nor my amiss with admiring look tell.
Be thou my advocate, and I will be your witness;
Tear thy woe from my soul, and I shall give thee remedy,
