Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As if to disguise thy disgrace.
Yet, in their sorrow, their eyes do show
The wretch that they are, in sympathy mock,
And, in their silence, their hearts do weep,
As mourning becomes them, in dark corners they hide.
Who turns from thee that thou dost so forsake?
Though others may follow, stay,
My love, thy heart doth follow thee,
Unyielding, to all men's mock contempt.
  Let others lead the life they like;
  My love, lead the life that thou dost lead.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Then take my life, leave my friend alone,
And in my grief find release,
As suffering me becomes the earth's own healer.
Beware, though, of overdoing love;
A blissful feast can turn into a hellish woe,
If love's too great a torment for one's pride.
  Release me wholly, then heal my heart,
  Break the chains of suffering, and grant my friend's right.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Bears the torch of my love's ease.
So, to secure this vast fortress,
I entrench myself in your sweet chamber,
With walls of love, gates of steel,
To keep him ever near, at bay,
And to make everlast his debt
I entrench in your sweet breast,
A woman's lair, but a woman's delight.
  In him you have a woman's clutch,
  But no woman loves him who is so kind.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whilst that public liberty hath put an end to
All bonds of love and trust.
Hath she the stronger weed of love, which destroys
All firmness and trust in me?
Ay, 'tis so, and she brews this vice
In me, that I am sufficed
Even to drown my own eyes.
O, blame her not if she may but approve
My most high deserts, being stained with her crime!
Shall I not drown my eyes, being brimful with her grief?
  Woe betide thee, should she but cease this grief,
  More


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
O, swear, if thou wilt, that I was thy will,
A closet never pierced with bayonets,
But that I ever lived with thee, and left
A widow in heaven's full flower.
So far for love my love hath love endured,
A life of love, of sighs and tears;
But that due be the only praise
That my steeled sense or changes of heart can bestow.
  For thou art so fair that my tears
  Deserve not sweet love, to thine own deep grief.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But thine eyes, though they know it not,
Be deceived in their being kind.
For why should my flesh's mass be counted fair,
When it walks on the moonlit sand,
Its shadows cast by the sun's warm fire?
Or on the richness of thy cheek's display,
Which in thy glass doth blot the face,
Stealing thy light and making of disgrace?
  O, no, 'tis not so. My love knows it not well
  To shun the beauty that in thy glass doth lie.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though not acquainted with the argument of truths.
But when I, in truth, set my foot on that land,
Of swayed women I think better advised
To trust their credulous hearts than their faces,
Since no woman hath so kind been my friend.
And so I back again to the time,
Discovering that love hath been thine effect,
My love hath been false from start to finish;
  And she hath prevailed on me to lie.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my bitterness be named sourness,
Nor my affliction sourly named better.
O, do not, when I have spoken of these,
Sland me with farther sour words than I have shown;
Speak not of my lameness nor my woe,
But in mine own bad doings be pictured
That I, too harsh, should not be so harsh.
  For thee, I have no love; yet in my spite
  A thousand tears more than thy fair report.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy meaning more fully than sight would convey.
For if I could write the shade of a hazel or the hue of a hen,
The mind would be most amiss, and the letter
Would be but a faint echo of your laughter's ring.
Now, in this love there is truth and beauty all along,
And thus, my letter, though much abused, shall survive;
It fits well with your graces and your gifts to know.
  And, as thou dost so, it shall not sound lame;
  For in my soul it well may dwell, and in thee.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
In thoughts, it stores up all vice,
A thousand sins against thy expanse.
For 'tis my soul that doth this concealed hate,
In thoughts, the guilty are in vaults,
And in thy darkness, a golden crown
Makes darkness 'twixt thee and me.
How many lambs might the stern wolf betray
If like a lamb he could his looks translate!
How many gazers mightst thou lead away
If thou wouldst use the strength of all thy state!
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
By comparison mine own desert, harsher still.
Mine own desert is thy love's compare,
And in comparison mine own sin doth grow;
So thou in thy own self dost sin even more,
Thou in thyself dost sin even more stark.
Who lets so fair a house fall to decay,
Which husbandry in honor might uphold
