Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To my sightless soul, thy gift grievous and dear.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower,
And making my soul's deepest ache aware.
So too, may thy memory, like dawn's first light,
Bestow mercy on the evening's bower,
That mercy may merit pity indeed.
  Or else this sorrow:
  Thy black is fairer than my own soul's own face.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It wrests me from myself, your tyrant hold
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Forsaken, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May have title to his bequest as well.
This extremity he demonstrates, being forced to choose,
Among many, of one, my sole desire being left,
Unless this penance be mutual:
I will sell thee, and then he will gain
By my death, which I will no more part;
Then can no longer be my debtor nor me
To him who offers the sum of my wrong.
  Then can no longer be my friend nor me
  To him who takes me as prey.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whilst thou dost nothing, and giveth much;
And yet this woman, in all her might,
Can make thee even happier than thou art
If thou will but promise her that thou art married.
O, what a mansion have those cruel hands
Which for the conquest of thy heart
Commit once to thee, and then to none,
To make of thee anon another self another's place
Who will be thy treasure, and will be even better still?
  This offer, this woman, will make thee quite happy
