If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
O, swear, be thou my love's new clerk,
Or else my love's shadow will speak ill of me,
Since my love knows what right it is to love thee above.
Rise, resty muse; my love's sweet face survey
If thy soul alters not in that it will stay,
Or if my love alters not in that it will stay,
What worth do I in myself discern
That I should slight thy service, be it right or misguided?
  As soon as my love alters, so fast
  As soon as my love no more alters, so fast.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And the painter's hand knows what constitutes it's style.
But eyes, whose wisdom is but a shadow,
Stained scarlet with ten minutes' worth of squandered life,
Are dumb, and cannot form another's vision,
Ten times more dumb than my lascivious eyes.
They see the truth in painted faces, but cannot rise
To gape at the counterfeit that doth live,
To question whether beauty holds truth or flee.
  Lo, see what fools of time have done,
  To mar with their splendor the beauty they see.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though not acquainted with the abode of hearts.
I cannot for my love find out her breeding,
Though I may stray among the humblest knell,
Not a single whit her beauty hath taught me.
I may even state with certainty she is false,
Though I may not believe her, yet she is so;
And, to cross a mine error, I do so,
As I think my love impious.
  For love is merchandized, and those that hold
  Are moored with false esteem.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Make no plea upon my mutability,
Nor I am to blame for my wild thoughts.
O, let me die, and my soul be damned,
Wounded by thine unkindness, own heart so kind;
Not one word of ill can then my advocate raise,
Unless this universal wrong:
All tongues, the law have promulgated,
Bestow the right to love the most,
By thy own open access, to thy heart.
  Thus shall I live, enthralled and filled with longing,
  To one whose heart is as kind as thou art.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own sorrow, more fully expressed.
If I could write well what I feel, better
Than lingered thoughts, much vouchsafe, would not be so.
For if I should write well, the worst part
Of it would be my soul, truly expressed.
Let those who read our old acquaintance bless,
Whilst I should be reproached for having hoisted
That bell, which tolls thy life away.
  For I corrupt not beauty, being dumb,
  When thou art much wiser than I.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying their judgment, I ensconce it in thine.
Neither are my ears enchanted by thy voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with thee and faith held dear.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
In thy own esteem art so grossly flawed,
A perfect match would bring thee to light.
So thou in beauty's fall convertest
By thy own fallow ground, being mine,
Thyself become'st thy beauty's star,
By thy own fallow gifts confounded.
O, what a mansion have those errors got
Which for themselves so dignifies their dwelling!
  Beware, sweet heart, of this grand disguise;
  A gilded cage with flaws would still be better.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To keep the fame of her helpless state
Preserved, as is deserved by all, unconfined.
So you, like a sireling at a mountaintops,
Embrace the wilderness, wild, and free,
For love's purest grace will keep you from the cold,
In your absence, even birds don't sing.
Oh, should you find this tract, abandoned,
Do not let your heart be mired, let love's flame be kindled.
  Lest curious eyes should glimpse your sorrowed gaze,
  And taunt you with my name beyond the grave


