Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To mourn for what I cannot feel.
Yet, thy redeemer shines so bright,
Whose shadow falls on thy joy, as if it were
A winter's sacrifice, too harsh for summer's delight.
For thy love is so great, and those
That hate are themselves so slight,
Or else like thine eyes, are prejudiced.
How many a pure heart hath love committed,
Whose sin was love's great virtue, to thy pull,
That to thy pull she was converted,
By thy sweet virtue, she being pure,
Thy love converted, and that I am


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Sometime my heart does speak, and I try
To reason with it, yet it cannot comprehend
The depth of pain it has just suffered.
O, why then, fearful of losing you,
Do I not seek to heal the breach
With exercises gentle and soothing balm?
  Yet, love, you have taught my friend to weep,
  And I, a tormentor, have no remedy.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim my interest as my own.
That is why I have recourse to thee,
For thou my wealth is confined and bound
To those whom thou dost lease it from.
The reason for this is that I
Thou dost detain me beyond the reach of thy pleasure,
And I cannot bring myself to sell
Myself, so heavily dost thou abuse
Myself beyond the reach of repair.
  But what is so strong that it fears no harm?
  The weak love, to wit, that falls to thee.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And more than enough is this my distillation needing.
I sell thee, and then have my will,
To one whose sweet love I can absolutely deny,
Whose love I can only approve by aye.
I grant him the happy title of my true wisher,
Whose pleasure I can no whit shirk, being fickle and blind.
The wretched soul that makes my sorrows seem so,
Find'st not in my loss, being so strongly heartened,
But in my cheerfulness, makes amends
  That to thy sweet self I am willing give a life.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love thy love hath I not sworn,
Neglecting to attend on thy love's growing might;
Hearing of thy love's time's happenings,
That I might witness to their truth and beauty stop.
Yet, love, what care I who tells thee how
The future shall weigh if I do pursue
The present in conformity with thy will.
  If thou wilt leave me, do not leave me last,
  Since I have sworn thee fair, take my love's due.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And they rightly hail it as fair
When truth and right have prevailed?
But yet dost thou art as the wealthy to his bay
And there stick the fee of his love and fear;
So thou being my love do confirm
My fears and joy confirm each other's boast.
  Yet love knows what is false and right;
  In one of thine eyes dost thou deceive me thus.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And that I have slept with a vulgar fool;
Yet, love, proud as a canker,
Cries aloud: "Truth loves thee, and he can prove
Though I should change my mind, love still believes it."
And thus, in scorn, she insults me:
"Why durst thou prove me false,
Since I am false in so profound a crime?"
And then, smiling, she gives me another kiss:
  "Make me another false confession, more truthful
  Then thou gav'st me thine not to give again."


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Pluck not my soul from thy tender care,
And render no account for my rose;
In sum, do thou my utmost wrong
And I shall haply answer "Ay."
Be gone, then, and straight repent;
My love is healed, and thy guilt waived,
By thy gentle and willing aid;
Pard me no longer to thee owe
Thy sweet virtue than for my sake to be charged.
  Return to me, then; I love thee still,
  And thou gav'st me here to demand a thing I am not.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy feelings; and yet, love knows it is
Her tone which lends the most effectual cheer.
If I could write the bliss I feel,
It might seem less blest than being loved,
For I might lose thy honor, and in my grief
Aught that blots love would seem better.
But since thou art so kind and so fair,
As I by thy color wont prove,
Shall I not inferior my worth to show my pride?
  Let others raise their voices to speak well of thee;
  My heart's speech is for thee, and thy good report.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of thine eyes, it waits.
For if they look on thee fair, they find
A thousand flaws in love, yet love the same;
And in this they err, seeming to divide,
Their love, their wisdom, their judgment so misplaced.
But why thy love is not so,
To love that well, to remove this blot?
  For love is so deep, it knows no space,
  Lest piercing eyes should find its faults, unkind.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And that's the grave!
The earth can hold but dead roots,
So thy love, as thine, shall surely flow.
Whoso restrains me, let my side be clear,
For my love is so strongly rooted,
She can never remove it nor I can be win
To know the constancy of a love so strong.
  Yet she may withdraw, and I am sure
  She will, to the grave or no grave at all.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To draw the shame from before its sight,
The shame of unkindness, she adds to her grief,
Lest it should be a reproof of her transgression,
And she, profligate, does not know
How to avoid this penalty in the future.
So she loses her joy, her only treasure,
And the shame she so richly stores,
Shall be her guilt, more than her theft;
  She has strayed from the path of truth and right,
  And now she rightly falls to the ground.


