Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To every hollow which the eye doth plead.
All tongues, the heart's and eye's song,
Bestow on my sorrow black is the rule,
And that to thy fair I lend, poor art.
I grant, sweetheart, thy heart's true sorrow
May make thy eyes weep, though they weep to thee be;
For black tears doth flow more when they weep to thee.
And yet I hope thy heart's true sorrow lends thee air,
Since that black is the dye of sorrow doth stain.
  Then thou gav'st me this sorrow to grieve,


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Scatter love and despair as you take flight,
And let my friend's heart find solace in your flight.
No scorn will you torture me with,
For I am taught that all men are loathsome,
And thus, you'll be the tormentor I need.
Such torment I must endure, and soothe
The weight of my friend's heart, each beat weak and stifling.
  Yet, if you are found wanting, I shall bear that pain,
  And by your will, I shall be eased.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim interest, assured of thy seat.
The title to my heart doth lie in thy control,
And to my repose I belong,
A creature of thy side, to whom I am confined,
A party of ten to one, of whom I am one.
How can I then be king of my heart,
When thou dost sovereign over mine inner flame?
O, what a happy title do I find,
Happy to have thy love, happy to have mine own!
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Thou mayst be false, and


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Who will then my life give thee reprieve?
Thou canst not, love, give thy life away,
For it is thine, and I by it bound
To give it thee, and hope it shall live--
Then need I thee to give a testament of worth,
And to give that worth a fame
To every vulgar writer that doth use my name.
  Then can no love to me dare,
  Till I give thee this good report: my life was thy will.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love hath sworn, so far for truth,
For thou art so possessed with deceived me,
That I cannot for love know thy will;
And, to leave me, thou hast sworn to thine uncle
And all my niggarding friends, too,
That I will leave them all for thy love,
And nothing more, for I love thee so;
  Then fear me, if I nothing else prove
  Then thou me, to love me thou thine own true will.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that it is painted bright there with truth's arts;
But my eyes, bewitched by thy glare,
Are guided by thy farther power,
And that, in turn, is why they look not clear.
For thee, when thou dost blind me, see'st
The blindfolded mind, made perfect in thy sight,
To thyself, thyself to blind,
As being made by thee, so is it with me.
  Then, Love, what a temple for thine eyes
  To hold, the beauty which thou dost blindfold.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I in truth do confess my age;
But then she shapes her words to my will,
And I, in love's design, am dumb,
Excusing her for my deeds I do strive.
18. For she loves me not for my deeds,
But for my beauty she admires,
And when I fail to say so, she says
My faults are my deeds that she admires.
  And then I praise her, not for my faults,
  But for my deeds she loves to boast.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my resentment be named
Nor my defense be termed right.
For thyself I am blamed not for my deeds,
But for my sins find their just recompense;
Against myself I am absolved,
And for my sake even thy foes find their advocate.
Such is my love, to thee I so belong,
That, for thy sake, I must be untrue,
And thou, in turn, shall be the courtier
And cheerleader of my deeds, being false to me.
  Such is my love, to thee I so belong,
  That, for thy sake,


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My meaning;
If I could write well of thee, better still,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only good news from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should write well of thee, great glory,
The slander's mark would be my poor wit,
And words would be of little worth, less than bird.
  For I would not be wise, being fond,
  To give thy life away, though it be thine own.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of thine eyes.
For thou art fair in my judgment, though they in thee lie;
And they that use them to this end are dead,
And I, that use them to this end am married.
How many lambs might the stern wolf betray
If like a lamb he could his looks translate!
How many gazers mightst thou lead away
If thou wouldst use the strength of all thy state!
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Its defects my love doth confess,
And reasons for thy loving do urge.
For thee, mine eyes are beguiled, and for myself,
Mine heart is swayed by thy thoughts, and for this,
A sinful love, in thoughts, kills me.
Thou of thyself thy love's treachery
Kill me, for I am stained with thine,
And in thy thoughts myself rotten deeds begin.
  Such strife exists 'twixt love and disdain,
  That I, an accomplice, must engage.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To the place where she needs to be,
To keep him from harm's way, so dear,
From where he might find no mother.
But thou, once conceived, wilt bear the cost,
Of that which thou no sooner gavest,
But of that which thou didst conceive must stay,
For love alone gives that which gives life.
  If that be error, then thou art as free
  As any mother, though not so free.


