For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying their judgmental sight with prescient ache.
For why should my flesh and blood desire
To belong to thee, when it knows
It is itself but a creature of high estate?
No, I do not envy those chickens,
Nor think the sweet fat of fowls tenders his taste;
Nor do I glory in that mountain goat,
Whilst he belongs to that chasm which the basest weed
Sinks down to earth and wastes his blood in my sight.
  For thou art so strongly rooted in my love that
  I in myself am not so keen.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And that, in turn, my love doth join
To this vice, by thee and for myself.
How can love be so strongly knit
When hate and hate in the heart are knotted?
To separate, as thou dost,
Thyself, is to make love anew,
A living soul, through hate and through.
O, what a gift, to share this love that thou dost withhold!
To love with a kind of disdain, and in that disdain
To cherish things, that thou dost hate, and in that esteem.
  Thus, thou cunning slanderer


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To tell the story of love that thrives in thee,
Beyond the reach of Time's uncleanly slate.
So too, my love, you and I must flee,
For Time's false touch can never wipe away
The imprint of your past days on my soul,
Nor can he, with present loathing, steal
The truth from my lips, which bear your name,
From this cruel hand that shadows like a widow stand.
  But if my love persists, I'll write it down,
  For you, and for my sake, he shall live.


