Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To witness thy sufferings in another's sight.
Thus, I, a sad and needy boat,
Cries out for mercy on the winds that carry me,
As if my woes were my own, faring better;
But, lo, the slightest touch of thy grace,
The littlest ripple of thy changing wind,
Shall make my deathbed love seem like new,
A sad song, more real than any novel's ease.
  Such joy I find, hearing thy name chanted,
  That, like the lark, I tear myself away.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To draw out my life, and leave me weep,
A life reduced, but two of thine did kill?
Were not 't enough to torment me more,
To make me suffer, and in my pain,
Flesh and blood, together, for evermore?
No, let me live, as I please,
Unyielding to the allure of thee,
The captive's solitude, a pearl.
  Yet, should that heart, with malice, try to kill,
  I shall stand, through tears, by its side, a knight.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May remain the greater part of my gain.
Such is my love, to thee I so belong,
That, for thy sweet sake, myself dost take
The lesser of two lesser loves;
Therefore, to him whom thou dost lend, I am chary
And for my sake, he receives this written embassage:
"My love is so strongly knit, to thee I send
Thine, for my sake to thee I'll give the greater;
Therefore, when thou reviewest this written form,
The lesser shall be the greater."
