  But why should I trust her when I am deceived,


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike not with thy glance but with thy fair;
Let not my heart to confession yield to Time's spite,
But in thy breast's embrace, open wide,
A space for tears to ebb and flow.
What need for recompense is there
For suffering that thou dost bear?
Then should I pardon thy wrong, though thou art guilty;
For what worth do I in myself discern
That I should slight thy service, proud and vain?
  Still, love, persist in your wrong; in thine own heart
  A space for love to ebb and flow.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sense of loss and love's despair.
If I could speak more aptly
Than thine annoyingly harsh words, better still,
For still, I would not be so kind;
To speak well of thee is to love well,
And to love well is to approve
Thy harshness and beauty's worth.
Then what could I do but approve
The worth of thy beauty, the truth of thy soul?
  Thus, love; despite thy wrong, I approve
  The sweetest thing thou hast done.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of thine eyes, it awaits.
For to my dear doting eyes belongs
Thy secret love, and they, in turn,
Cannot resist, tearing me apart,
For they see the truth in what I cherish.
But why should my flesh and blood dislike
Thy loveliest of gifts, being fed
By thy sinful deeds, being enslaved?
  'Tis not love, though it may seem so,
  That the flesh and blood despise, in thee they draw their line.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Its legitimacy thine, as mine is thy love's denial.
Pause and weigh thy own circumstance, grant audience,
Find fault not in love, nor hate in its fervent cause.
Or should there be blame, let not those lips part,
Adorned with hues of deceit, utter reproof.
For they too have stained love's sacred art,
Sealed false vows, as thine in love's deceit.
  Grant me the right to love with equal zest,
  As thine eyes wander, and my heart does plead.


