Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To witness thy visage in moods strange.
Yet, in these mournful eyes, pity is not kind,
Nor compassion kind to me;
For black is the color of love,
And in thy heart's design, hearts are drowned.
So thou strangest of all mourners, being black,
Most mourners are of thy watching,
Others of thy watching being mute;
And, thus, all are of thy watching,
One on another's presence,
By thy watching being one.
  But thou watchest not, being one,
  Since all are of thy watching,


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is enough to make me weep, and then release
My friend from this sorrow, as I have said,
For that heart which gives us so much pain
Shall profit no more, than before,
For it is bound to that which it bears.
O, what a bounty is love, to torment me
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief;
But what's so perfect which it fears no blot?
  Love's inventiveness knows no defect,
  And yet doth this very thing, to torture me.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim his life, too; and yet he can claim no
Against me, for I am his, and he in me
Is confined to that affection which binds him so.
I might leave him, and yet not be so
As to lose him, for I am so possessed
With hatred, that I must in my will bear
The weight of guilt, the more wretched the better;
  And yet he will not leave me, for I am his,
  And he, in turn, will live in this plight.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say within thine own deep-sunken eyes
Thy beauty hath no part, being played
Within the tranquil knowledge of thy soul.
But, love, what care I who calls me well or ill,
So thou mayst be my best or last wish,
And I good mayst be thy monument still.
  Lest those who see my pleasure find no leisure taken
  To play the role of giver and have their say.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love thou hast my love,
And love knows it is her due;
But why should my love believe in thee,
When thou dost lie so oft, knowing my will?
Thyself thy love knows, and my love knows,
Are of one heart, one thought, and one will change;
So far for love my love thou hast my love,
And dost love, knowing thy will, convert it.
  But why should my love believe in thee,
  When thou dost lie so much, knowing my will?


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what is new, know'st it not yet?
For every word that lives lives is but a child of light,
So thy thoughts, although they may deceive,
Entice them still with their sweet dreams.
But why should my eyes desire another's sight,
When they see all things clear and well?
My ears are beguiled more by thy sweet smells,
And thy shadows more by thy living ugliness.
  All mortals make mistakes, and thou dost thine even so,
  Lest those same errors call thy praise.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But such tongues as truth breeds do give
A greater certitude to my hush than tongue.
Till I see myself transformed by sagacity,
Like Eve's apple to the fire,
My love's truth so fits my conceit
That I in her looks do believe,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime.
  And yet, love, why should I reproach your wit,
  When I am praised of your beauty yet falls short?


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Pluck not my sense out of my brain,
But let my soul be your prey to tear.
So far for love my love hath shown me fair;
Imprison my heart in thy breast's embrace,
And though thou stray, not from thence my tale
Exceeds thy bounds, yet touches thy friend.
  But why should I reproach thy virtue,
  When I am stained with thy unkindness and folly?


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sense of thee.
If I could write the charm of thy name, better still,
Though not to cherish, still, to admit,
As thine eyes would willingly show,
A bloom of joy on thy garden's face.
For if I should succumb to despair, I'd lose my mind,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
  To avoid this, and to uphold thy dignity,
  Keep your gaze true, though your heart may


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And in that love I find all kinds of blame.
For 'tis my love that calls into question
The truth of thy looks,
And that, in its refiguring, it finds
A thousand beauties for love to score;
And thence, in pride, it serves thee again,
In love's game, with eyes, it serves.
  In this love there is no room for blame,
  For in my sight, the truth is not so.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Proving thine innocence more by plea.
Pause and weigh thy own circumstance,
Find fault not in love, nor hate in sight,
Whilst robbing thyself of thine own sweet grace.
For to thine own love I give life,
Thine own love, to thyself as thine is fair.
Then thou ownst thyself, and me thine,
Two souls, one in love, one in crime.
  Preserve thy name, I beg, for love,
  As thine, so is my life, to thee.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To wherever the gosling is.
So too, if the loveliest of beings
Lives on, succumbing to love's dearest fire,
Her husband, like a wizened sire,
Cannot stay, for fear of shame;
So thou, once bettered, art now bettered even
To death, for purest love allows
Her body, not a tomb, but a tomb carry.
  Fear not, sweet love, of my slandered tongue;
  My love shall not sourly die.


