Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In solemn altars of trance and tired sighs;
Thou art as black as pitch-dark slate,
Rough, intense, and without charm to measure,
Impassive, cold, and to allure unchanged.
Yet, in these lines, a wonder I find,
A beauty, if thou wilt but remove the poison's hold!
First, I remove the chill, that heavily I weigh,
Second, the taste of death, that heavily I weigh,
And third, the foulest of all poisons, that weigh thee,
I bid thee eat the world's poison, and


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Then should I weep for thee, and then thee alone,
For I have found a refuge in thine bosom's hold.
O, what a refuge for a heart it is,
That it may cry out for thee, when need is greatest!
But what's so steadfast that it fears no shift?
Fearful of change, it wanders on,
Adrift, and loses its way.
  Love's compass may point true, or veer adrift,
  But never straight lines shall we bear the cost.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May hold his value, and he may be yours.
That is the reason why I do not reveal
The cause of my desert, which is so evident,
To give him the benefit of my repute;
Instead, loving him, I lose myself,
A snare cunningly laid to bewitch and dismiss.
  For I have sworn thee fair, false heart, in lies,
  To him, to keep his beauty and his grace so.


