Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy worth and what I desire to prove.
If I could write the heaven's love or the earth's own praise,
The broad world would cry, "This poet tells but praise,
No face on earth could claim such heavenly hue."
Yet, were some pencil or pen true,
Of thine or his fair painting displayed,
The world would cry, "This is but a child's play,
A child's dream, no more, if he can write."
  Yet, should a living soul from thee survive,
  You must live again, through progeny and mirth.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
