Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in thy picture dost thou lie.
For never-resting time doth pass,
That doth beauty's date seem false,
Or, at the least, looks old, being merchandized.
So thy picture doth lie, and there reigns
A multitude of spies, all to thine own harm;
Yet, Love, what good is it that doth thy picture tell?
It is but a brief part of the story,
The rest is thine invention, and yet dost thou know
  The more by thy picture's conceit.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And yet, as heaven did set her to go,
She walks in the path of truth,
Though she knows it's a lie, yet I believe her
To be but a little child, of whom I know not
How much more when she tells it lies.
Thus does my love profess her truth, though she knows it's a lie,
And says she loves me, though I know it to be false;
And, in this she mak'st a truth,
Although I do know it to be false, yet believe her.
