O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Confess not thy sensual taste to have offended
My deepest sense; pardon me not
Forgot upon thy gentle charm to speak;
O, err on both sides, too harsh in thy part.
Grant me the pardon: my love well knows
Her virtue has been my foe;
To thy unworthiness I give her right
And prove her innocence by thy sight.
  I do forgive thy sin, gentle friend; but still, I must ask
  For thy truth, and love's due too.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My meaning; and, heaven knows,
Thy being well-versed in such subjects lies,
She can well guide my forgetfulness clear.
If I could speak more well of thee, better still,
Though not to thee, yet, love, so speak
As thine eyes would desire, kind and gentle;
For nothing in me is so untrue
As that I may not love thee as I desire.
  But still, do thy will, love; I hold
  Your truth, though thou mayst prove it untrue.


