Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To witness thy heart's iniquity.
Yet I, a lawful wedded wife, do swear,
By thy love, thy virtue, thy truth,
To swear against thine eyes, and against my soul,
Thy heart's tyranny, in my sight,
For I have sworn thee fair, false heart, in deceit.
How can I then be lawful wedded,
Whose heart is thine, and eyes thine, in hatred's sight?
  I cannot, love, for I see thine,
  And they, in hatred, are withstood.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
How can I then mourn my friend's departure,
And in my pain, speak of thee?
O, that our hearts might share the love they share,
And in that love, we might find release,
As darkness fades before the dawn's embrace.
  But darkness hides from us that day,
  And in our eyes, no light shows.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Thou mayst choose and me subject to thee.
That is why I do not live in thy sight,
For I must not like to be aught else
Than that which is thy own and dost thee approve.
But if I do live in that I cannot love thee
Like a deceived husband; so thou me
Cannot by my will be bound subject to thee.
  But if thou wilt leave me and live in me,
  I lose thee, and that is the end of my matter.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say within thine own deep-sunken eyes
Thy face hath contented the ocean;
Thou false, barren, comfortless weed of the earth.
Bring me in compliance, that I may have thee:
Stretch forth my tender bosom wide,
Make room for one more wish to call my own,
That to thyself I may give my all,
And nothing hold me but to give.
  Let him live, that to thee I'll give thee gentle lays,
  When thou wilt live, mine is thy sweet self.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
O, swear, though my nature were altered,
That I could not love thee more chaste,
Thou dost love them both for thyself and for me;
So thou wilt live, though my nature be false.
Ponder then thy own condition, be it better or worse,
That I may not know thy will, to change it from night to day.
  Make me happy, or else happy I'll be,
  By mutual favors thus creating a double lot.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And to thy gay self thou hast put a blinding crown.
But why dost thou not openly confront
This grievous sight before thine eyes,
Making war upon thyself, too cruel and vain?
It is thyself that does this,
Making war upon thyself, too cruel and vain.
Thou that art so proud, and in thyself so vain,
Give me thyself as a fair subject to be shown.
  Show me thyself where beauty lies,
  Not thyself, thyself to show it hence.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But that she hath no knowledge of me
But by myrynge, which is truth in love's tongue.
And therefore believe I am a false heart,
To love her whose lips I in love's craft
Creep in upon the truth, as thine eyes in love's dream.
But why should I then flee, I say,
When I am sure they will follow me and prove
My love true, though they decease in their ending?
  Love hath taught me thus to fear,
  To love those whose hearts are swayed by my voice.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Grant me the right to love with equal right;
In that thou me have the strength to leave me alone.
I do forgive thy trespass, knowing well thy heart
To hurt me more grievously than my friend;
But yet love knows it is a greater wrong
To bear love's wrong than hate's known injury.
HC: Then do thy office, love; I forgive thee
Even as thou dost thine own dear self;
If thou wilt leave me, then I the better will bear
The cross of correction than hate's known injury.
  HAV'T I NOT WOUND


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy thoughts and feelings.
If I could write the shape of a happy man,
Better still, than be dumb and speak
Thy heart's thoughts, though not to approve,
Proving thy worth by deeds like thine.
This I wish, of thine own worth I'll prove,
By deeds like thine, I'll be able to say
Thy worth is due, by thy deeds expressed,
And by a truth like thine, I'll be able Jr.
  Then may I dare to boast how I feel;
  Till then, not to thee.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Prohibited love, in thy heart is found.
That love, so pure and so true,
Strikes me as unjust, loving thee as thou dost thine own part.
Pause and weigh thy own circumstance,
Find fault not in love, nor its fervent creator.
Or should there be blame, let not those lips part,
Adorned with hues of deceit, utter reproof.
For they too have stained love's sacred art,
Sealed false vows, as thine own heart doth vow.
  Grant me the right to love with equal zest,
 


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To render the injury just, as if not noticed.
So, too, being chased after wind,
Or something fierce, caught in its embrace,
We lose our minds, for fear of which we soars.
Oh, let my written words speak truth,
Messengers of my heart, yet true they'll be,
To set you free, from lies, as I have shown,
In every age, as I shall show,
  From tongue to heart, with love, wherever I find.


