My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy sorrow's effect upon my heart.
If I could write the shade of a sadder lover,
The nobler spirit would outlive my name,
Though my deeds should qualify him for satire's grace.
For if I should lose thee, 'tis not by my deeds but by
My speaking that loses its rank;
And losing thee, my speaking must be new,
Even as when first Iaped upon thy sorrow.
  So if I lose thee, 'tis by my speaking that I win
  More joy than when Iaped upon thy pain.


