Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To witness thy cruel deeds,
Through their mournful eyes, open wide.
The pity of thy heart I try to show,
As sympathizers will the dyer's hand,
Whose sympathy is thine, and they, in turn,
Desire this effect, that others may partake
Of thy sympathies, made more wretched by thy will.
Such doth thy sorrows make thy view,
Which shall be best viewed by thine own eyes,
Who in thy heart's looking glass shall see,
Through the tear, of pity, which doth thine own part partake.



Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Then weep for me, and I shall wail
Yet be still, for I cannot be wrenched
From the sweet comfort of your love's embrace.
O, what a torment would be if I could speak
Of thee, but for thy name, and then forgive
My friend, for I have stolen thy trust,
And now to myself am nothing but a woe.
  Pity me, then, dear friend, and know this truth:
  Your heart is cruel, and I am too kind.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim the value of thy all-important control.
This confession I make, not to thee as a pledge,
But to thee, to whom I am confined,
To reveal what I do not dare to show
To thee alone, my dear, and own.
Dearest love, in which I find such a heart
As to thee I pledge my life,
To give nothing away, even my own life.
  This vow, so deep, I forget to say,
  To give him the gift that he desires.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
