  This thought is as death to thee,
  If thoughts should take thee hence, thy sweet demise.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And with thine own eyes dost thou deceive them.
They see the truth in thy face,
And that in thy shadow doth lie;
Yet in their looking they cannot grasp
The depth of thy foul deeds to lay.
How much more dost thou lead away
Than they that know thy worth by thy deeds deceive?
And why dost thou then be proud
Of them that do not so fair aught
To thee as thou dost thine own dearths?
  Be not so proud that they see the wrong
  And do not correct the wrong they see.


