Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As darkened by mourning doth make the light.
The dawn hath broken, and the bright star
Hath been turned to gray, by mourners quite gone;
Hath mercy on the woe-stricken,
Dark-eyed, and lone, wanderer thou art;
Yet, in these, thyself outgoing,
A mournful song doth grace the mournful night.
  So thou, in thy woe, do grace the day,
  By thyself, and others too; and they shall wail.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Look what is best in me, that best I wish in thee,
That to thy discerning breast I may borrow
Thy beauty's fair countenance, and there with it interweave.
That tongue that tells the story of my love's bane
To thee, my dear, is best acquainted
With thy zealousy, that saved my soul.
  Then may I love thee, as thou dost thine own part,
  As I shall hers, by thy sweet spirit lend.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
