Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
With pallid cheeks and wrinkles, a mournful cast.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may thy mournful countenance bestow,
A gentle sigh on the evening's bower,
That whispers tales of love that lasted,
And shines with pity when sorrows do blot.
  Or may thy mournful eyes bestow,
  A softer tone when weeping is heard.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Have I not seen those captivated by fame,
And in their quest for admiration, squander
All they possess, and more, in fortune's game,
Exchanging genuine joy for shallow glamour?
No, let me endure, suffering alone,
The loss of love, combined with grief,
That binds us close, two hearts in strife.
  Yet, if I bend beneath the load of pain,
  My friend shall know, and I shall bear that pain.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim his interest, too.
This churl hath deserved thy love more than I,
For I have sworn thee fair, false heart,
And for my sake even so doth thy art
Desire me to swear against my knowledge.
But why should I reproach thy virtue,
When I am stained with thy crime?
My sin is such, and my defense
Is so weak that I have no plea.
  For I have sworn thee fair, false heart,
  To lie against the truth, and to keep thy honor.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And more than enough for thy show,
Thou false, stained with baser bribes,
And woman of lesser worth than thy friend.
Give me thy liberty, to what ever thou wilt give,
That thou thyself mayst choose,
My dear dependents happy churls, to enjoy
Thyself, alone, with most alone.
I will be the least of thy wailing,
Whilst that thou wilt be the wailing most,
And that to myself alone goeth my pleasure.
  I will bear thy sorrow, though thou wilt be borne,
 


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love thy love I'll vow, gentle boy,
From myself away from myself there live many hours;
But if thou wilt swear against thyself,
I'll swear against myself, for I have no will there.
Thou art so possessed with false esteem,
That I, an accomplice, will bear
The blame of my badness in thy eyes;
For I have no will, no power, to do thee wrong.
  To leave myself alone I'll wait,
  And see if my sweetheart will believe me lame.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet thy shadow doth much resemble that which it doth trace.
So, beauteous eyes, Love, what hold dost thou
On my vision, which in thy shape do show
Thy shadow's length and form,
And, Love, what a mockery doth my heart perceive
To see thee in the brighter day when thou art bright!
  How like Eve's apple doth thy beauty grow,
  If thy sweet virtue answer not thy show.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I not acquainted with their craft.
But, like her, I love to invent
And to show what truth thinks is best
Even to my dull ears that I may not know it.
So I for love believe her tongue,
Though she knows full well my wit,
And in our praises I think most gloriously;
And, for this, I lose my sweet one.
  For I impair not beauty, being mute,
  When she speaks well of thee, but worse of me.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Confess thy own true sentiment is more cruel,
Than that of thy deceased lover.
For whether beauty, birth, or wealth, or wit,
Or any of these all, or all, or more,
Entitled in thy parts do crowned sit,
I make my love engrafted to this store.
Dost thou not wonder then, my love,
That I do love thee most of all?
Because thou dost love me, love I prove
To be most worthy of thy most loving respect.
  Wilt thou not then be most worthy of my love,
  For I


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy essence more fully than sightless eyes can.
If I could write the beauty of thy face,
The nobler soul would love to have it,
And to my poor wit would qualify,
More truly than looking glass can give.
Now, see, I am not dumb, though thou art much clearer
Than any written word can be;
Yet, like a sad slave, should I grasp
The rud'st of a poorly worded speech,
Bad as if not-written before.
  And, haply, I might say, "Had he been able to write,"



In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For why should my ears reproach my sight,
When they in thee perceive the truth untold?
Or on my sin do I blot not see the truth,
When in thy cloud I find my plight?
No, I am not insensitive to thy pleasure,
Though my deeds in thy cloud do stain thine acquaintance;
My love is such, to thy sweet self I cling,
Though my deeds elsewhere I despise.
  For thee and for myself I dwell in bliss,
  Which eulogies call perfect well.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
In thoughts of my fallen love, with tears and sighs,
Thy love is sweet, and my grief, tender.
It is thy love that my heart doth confess,
Thy own fault, thy love too, for my sake doth confess,
And I am guilty of loving thee,
Stifling love, to whom thou gav'st.
  For I have sworn thee fair, false heart,
  To lie against the truth, and against my sake.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To keep him from harm's grasp, she thinks.
But you, love, are much too cunning for my care,
Unaware of my joy, and take your flight,
Obscuring your visage from my sight,
Lest I should glimpse your delight through my haze.
No love for me dwells within that part,
So cunning is your delight,
In my daze, you both remain aloof.
  Yet, if I should fell, and you were caught,
  You'd be left forlorn, and I would be left mazed.


