Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To witness thy sorrow's length.
Yet I, a sorrow, dare not speak ill of thee,
Lest my heart's most precious treasure appear,
A mandrake in odor and in hue,
Which fouls the pleasure of thy sight.
So thou art seen, and I, made wretched,
By thy look and thy deeds, am smitten,
To try an eulogy of thy pleasure,
A vain endeavor, doom my soul and eyes.
  For I would not thy beauty tell,
  To tie up envy, though thou art true.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It wrests me from myself, your tyrant hold
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Forsaken, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May have some claim on my sweet self.
This chivalrous knight doth pilfer all my poverty
And puts a stranglehold on my other self-love
To make me confine not to my will,
But to thy glass, his private treasure.
So, to avoid this disgrace, I will not say
Thy glass is false, but he that keeps it
Is a worse knight than gentle theft in me.
  To give him thy glass is to admit
  That thou my self-love may claim my dear.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And more than enough is known to me
That my love will be the happier part.
Shall the world be, as thou dost thy show,
That to thy right and me thine is right and fit?
Or what care I who calls me well or ill,
Whose influence on me depends upon thy show?
Thou canst not vex me with inconstant mind,
Since that my life and love are now in thy control.
  O, what a happy title do I find,
  If that happy title do veil my sorrow.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love's appeal, gentle, fulfill.
Desire will fulfill the treasure of thy love,
Indeed, fill it brimful with desires, and mine, one.
In matters of vast capacity, we prove
Among many, one is inconsequential, son.
Then in the multitude, let me be unspoken,
Although in thy heart's tally I am one.
For nothing binds me, if it pleases thee bind
That nothing me, a something, precious, to thee.
  Just make my name your love, and love that constant,
  And then you


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in that they view they are deceived.
Why should my eyes, which see, tell a lie,
When they see the truth in love's face,
Which is marred, but keeps its beauty bright?
My ears find no allure in thy voice's symphony,
Nor do gentle emotions yearn for their dear one;
Nor taste or scent, seek to indulge in thine unending chase.
  Only love, to whom my eyes and ears are blind,
  That they may not know what is bothering them.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though not acquainted with the use of words.
Yet, love, she thinks me a fool,
And says I have sometimes looked upon truth
And made mistakes of thought,
That I have shown her falsehoods.
But why should she then boast that I have looked on truth
And shown her falsehoods false,
When I do indeed strive to prove
The truth of love's eternal fire?
  For she thinks I am more learned
  Then she knows not how to use words in her fight.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike not with thy foot but with my moiety;
And in each of these ill turns, set a better example
Than thy harshness's bare picture had told.
O, alter not thy thoughts, so oft as thou wilt,
But let me die; so thou wilt be
A greater gift than thyself to give;
For I must be thine after my decease,
And that thou gav'st me thine after my decease.
  Thus, thou shalt live--such virtue hath my love shown--
  When thou art gone and thy love show me.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy sorrow's deep-rooted root.
If I could instill sagacity, better yet,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should plunge into despair, I'd lose my reason,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
  To avoid this, and to uphold your dignity,



In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by thy voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And that, in respect, is my praise.
So far for love my love's loving look,
Or at least that which my eye doth show,
As looks on thee, mine own love doth show.
In this I contemplate my love's reign,
With eyes that look on thee, and love's own face,
As far as eyes can from thy heart take.
  Yet eyes this wondrous look cannot,
  Grant love's color to blot out thy name.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To render the wretch's reward at hand,
The sweetest of beings, in her stead, takes flight.
But you, enamored of your bright own eyes,
Feed your light's blaze with self-made sustenance,
Causing famine amidst the land's surplus supplies,
An adversary unto your own self, too harsh in recompense.
You, now the world's adorning jewel,
And lone herald of the gaudy spring,
In your own bud, your pleasure you conceal,
A niggardly act, wasting treasures like a fool.
