Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As upon thy heart I tend, oppressed with
Thy thoughts, and to themselves I send.
For to thy fair smile I send these thoughts,
Though inwardly I would show them pity,
Thy outward being concealed from me,
By thy love, which knows no color to hide,
And in thy thoughts, to reveal what I feel.
Who hates thee that thou dost forsake so?
On whom dost thou frown that thou mayst be?
  O, none but thou, to whom I send this remorse,
  For I, that sorrow, send this to prove thee


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It seems cruel to me, yet I do it,
To cherish those who cannot love me alone.
Even for that torment I pen,
My friend's heart does not falter, though my friend is wakened.
It seems to me that all is done,
My heart, bound by sorrow, to persevere.
  Yet, should that heart from sorrow should depart,
  I'd still torment it, for it holds within its breast.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim interest in thy quiet lair.
He is cunning, and so is my sin,
Neglecting me, so that myself bring
A worse debt than at first gave.
Now, seeing that he is so,
I throw myself upon him, both for my sake
And for my dear love, so that he may repay
In other ways than those which I refashion.
  But what am I so possessed with blame
  That I should do thee wrong, and in that do strive to mend?


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To drown thy sorrows in pleasure's deep-rooted source,
And drown thy ardor in the soothing remedy.
But I forbid that sin may ever dwell
In thine affection, evermore enlarged;
Nor shall there be rivalry in thy heart,
A match of equals in love's eternal cause.
O, never say that I am hereunto forbid,
Although in my verse thou mayst transgress,
As I herein define the bliss thou must hold.
  If I could write the better part of me,
  More worthy thou my better part give.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love's appeal, kind, fulfill.
Desire will fulfill the treasure of thy love,
Indeed, fill it brimful with desires, and mine, one.
In matters of vast capacity, we prove
Among many, one is inconsequential, son.
Then in the multitude, let me be unspoken,
Although in thy heart's tally, I am one.
For nothing binds me, if it pleases thee bind
That nothing me, a something, precious, to thee.
  Just make my name your love, and love that constant,
  And then


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in their looking they cannot grasp its truth;
For every shape they look upon, be it fair or dark,
Is but a shadow of our dear departed,
Which in thy shadow they may never be dead.
How can my eyes, which see, find out thy plight
When thou dost hide behind every blot and blot?
How can my soul, which bears thy image,
To judge what it knows is true,
When thou art true and they be but shadows?
  How can my eyes, which say thou art fair,
  When thou art false and they in thee are untrue


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though not acquainted with truth's rhythm;
But, for my sake, she thinks me a cheat,
A niggard, rude, cruel, not acquainted
With worths and joys of the heart.
I tell her so, and she believes me,
Yet loves her because I am false,
And she believes me, though I tell her so.
And, to be sure that is not false she swears,
I give her my love, and she swears
As fair as any mother's child, though not so bright
As those gold candles fixed in heaven's air.
 


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my soul be torn by thee,
For thou dost smother my body in thoughts of love.
If I should flee, do not cease this rage,
For I cannot then return to thee but by
Thy side, and then repent of my injury;
If I should flee, do not depart,
When I have had the best of both worlds combined,
But in the onset come; so shall I taste
At first the very worst of fortune's might;
  And other trials of woe, which now appear dire,
  Compared with loss of thee will not compare.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own feelings towards me.
For if I could speak well of thee,
The most learned would speak well of me,
And thou wouldst be profaned, being rude,
In thy own tongue, being untrue.
Now, bewail me not thus: my anger is not
Very great, being mainly of thy woe;
Yet, if I could write well of thee,
The most learned would understand it well,
And thou wouldst be profaned, being rude,
In thy own tongue, being untrue.
  Therefore, do not so.
  For I must


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
In thoughts of others, thy love toward thine is quite untrue.
Let me love thee, as thou dost thine own part,
That thou thyself, may grant time's own boon,
In witness to thine own love, being frank,
I'll suffer no complaint, be it ill or well.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me half so ill,
To set a form upon desired change,
As I'll myself disgrace, knowing thy will.
  For I must be vile, and in my madness,
  To count thy pleasure, knowing thy will.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the place where it had first fallen,
Embarrassed and bare, it lies in wait,
A lesson to every hush-mouthed creature
That calls on her unvoiced song,
By day, she looms large, at night,
She folds her arms, and lulls the flock
To sleep, at their expense, unstrung.
  But if you happen on this written word,
  For love's pure light has captured my heart.


