Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Replacing tears with glee.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of your glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal its glare.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is enough to make me weep, knowing well my friend's plight,
And in my friend's woe, I find my heart's plight:
I've lost thee, my friend, and now I weep for thee,
For love's sweet torture brings me no release.
O, what a torment, to weep for love's sake,
To force my friend to suffer, see?
  Yet, if I lose thee, my friend, I've lost both heart and friend,
  In suffering, I've found a solace stronger than


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim what is owed thee, in claim of all mine.
That is why I do not often write of thee,
For I engraft thee new, each time assured
Of receiving it from thy hand, of purest faith.
Thus, like the dyer's hand, new to his hand,
I engraft thee to this edifice,
Which he shall not overturn, unless he takes thee from me.
  This he cannot do, unless my will should change,
  And that is why I write not to thee but to thee.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say within thine own heart it dwells
Thyself against thy will, and will do anything
To make me another self for love of thee.
Rise, resty muse. I grant thou wilt be still,
Though she, in her malice, kills thee.
Be thou then the happy woman that art thou,
Who in her malice will be virtuous,
Although she, in self-doing, murd'rous.
  Make her another self for love of me,
  That beauty still may live in thine or thee.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love, my love's plea, gentle, bear.
Yearning will fulfill the treasure of thy love,
Indeed, fill it abundant with yearnings, and mine, one.
In matters of vast capacity, we find
Among many, one is insignificant, dear.
Then in the multitude, let me be unwritten,
Although in thy heart's record, I am one.
For nothing holds me, if it pleases thee bind
That nothing me, a something, dear, to thee.
  Just make my name your love, and love that enduring,
  And then you


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in thy hand are many imperfections shown;
For what good is beauty, if it be false and blind?
Then, Love, what good is it to thee,
To torment me with inconstant sight,
When my eyes are thy own, and do count truth?
Answer me, O thou cunning slanderer;
Thou dost lead me further from thyself,
Thyself away from thyself, to thy disgrace.
  For I cannot love thee more, if thou dost lead me thus.
  For I must be blind, and thine own heart is false


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And that I did adore her before I knew her.
But why should I then praise her, when I am false,
Stained by all the fallacies she knows,
And by all the sweet lies she tells,
To make me seem false more blessed than I am?
No love, she says, is so perfect
As that she needs no rosy lips to mend,
And I, she says, am stained with pretension.
  For I have loved her, and then thought her false,
  And then she loves me because I am false.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my soul be marred by thy crime,
But, as thou dost depart, let my parts be
At the mercy of thy deciding favor.
In witness to thy unworthy heart,
My opposition lies waived;
To that I must not hesitate,
While waiting for thy return, amen.
O, blame me not if I no longer wish
To serve thee, but serve thee well, in truth;
  For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
  To fall short of what I should serve, deserved.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sorrow.
If thou wilt not, I beseech thee pardon,
And do not vex me with needless sayings,
Lest sorrow embolden my tongue to speak more
Than if thou wilt leave me alone.
O, what a farseeing Providence! That sternula
Which sternly weighs on the heart
May at times provoke tears, and thou wilt bear
The weight of guilt, if thou wilt suffer;
  But she that keeps thee here, protect her self-esteem,
  By forbearing thy worst, she keeps her dignity.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Mine own love, thy love's revolution well.
So far for love, my love's plea;
Potential offenders, soon as found, are release.
In witness to thee, my love permit
Thy name to stay unblemished, even as a tomb
For sins of thy lovers, who roam.
  Such civil war is in my love
  That I an accessary needs must be.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To keep the beauty of her beloved near.
So, you, my lovely boy, who have just flown,
Your beauty finds no end, for it clings
To the very edge of truth, and can only grow;
Yet, love, your edge does reach, and time imparts,
A lesson learned in love's own bosom.
So, carry out your task, beautiful boy, swiftly
And do not let your edge hinder.
  Lest sorrow stirred should cause you grief,
  And cause your heart to wane, with sorrow yourself.


