Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As if in mourning they were alive,
Gaze on thyself, and do not weep,
As if in mourning to thee did weigh.
For thou art fair as any child of light,
Though not as stars, whose brilliance I can't see,
Thy face hath put an end to the gloom,
Raising my heart to seek relief there.
Who hath shown thee how to love endure
When thou art hated, as I by thee endure?
  Thy deeds are such, as thy thoughts make thee see,
  To throw off thy shadow, and to live in thine own.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Even so, if thou wilt leave me, do not depart,
For love's sweet power will heal my friend's soul,
If I but surrender, swear upon thine oath.
O, change thy mind, be it not so.
Shall my friend's heart be more blessed than mine,
Whose gentle spirit but now seems to wane,
Since each by other is being loved as he is?
Let my friend's love be my own, and mine own,
Or at least so I hope, being bound by thine


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye
May have some claim upon thy part.
Thou canst not, love, take him prisoner,
Whilst I, bound to thee, will be the least found
In that I am most strongly knit in thine unwavering breast.
He belongs to thee, my love, and I cannot keep
His gentle luster concealed, though I may stray;
So, to him I bring this written plea,
That he may have some claim upon my love's shed.
  But here's the catch: my love understands
  That he owes her, and she, in turn, knows it not


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say within thine eye but one part
The sum of all my loving thoughts, evermore.
That use is not forbidden usury
Which happies those that pay the willing loan;
That's for thyself to breed another thee,
Or ten times happier, be it ten for one.
Ten times thyself were happier than thou art
If ten of thine ten times refigured thee;
Then what could death do if thou shouldst depart,
Leaving thee living in posterity?
  Be not self-willed, for thou art much too fair
  To be death


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love's plea, gentle, bear.
Yearning will fulfill the treasure of thy love,
Indeed, fill it abundant with yearnings, and mine, one.
In matters of vast capacity, we find
Among many, one is insignificant, dear.
Then in the multitude, let me be unwritten,
Although in thy heart's record, I am one.
For nothing holds me, if it pleases thee bind
That nothing me, a something, dear, to thee.
  Just make my name your love, and love that enduring,
  And then you love


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet thy cheek doth publish a lie,
A falsehood of heart in its composition.
Why should my eyes, which witness, tell a lie,
When in thine own eyes a truth appears to lie?
Or on thy cheek do I say,
Thy face hath no beauty, being but a common dwelling?
What worth do I in myself discern
That I should slight thy great gift, proud and vain?
When all my virtues bow to thy defect,
Enslaved by the power of thy gaze anew.
  Still, love, increase thy scorn, for now I know
 


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though not acquainted with truth, yet admiring;
And for this she thinks me a fraud,
Though she knows full well my days are fleeting.
But why should I believe her false tongue,
When I hear her speak well of thee,
And in our faults find such sweet pains?
My love, well knowing she hath strayed,
I blame not her though she hath stolen thee,
Though she hath me and loves none so.
  For I must not love her though she knows it is false,
  Since my love knows it is false she loves it even worse.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Grant me the right to love thee as I will,
As thine own self would thine own self be minded;
Do not, though I should push thee far,
Unmoved, reproach my heart in that breast.
O, do not, when I have left this place,
Sneak in upon the heels of strangers' heels;
Him in this forlorn which I hope to see,
And for myself nothing noble do I hope prove;
  Till I return from thee I never can say nought noble.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own sorrow's length and tenor.
If I could write the shade of a sad soul,
Better still than that which in my verse doth stand
For tender sympathies to murmur against.
O, what a boon it is to know
Thy truth and thy beauty so interwoven,
To make love's delight ever present,
And never outlive our love's breath!
  But beware of urging too hard on my ill,
  Lest sorrow embolden my tongue to speak worse.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Such equals in love dost hate me untrue.
Pause and weigh thy own condition, grant audience,
Find fault not in love, nor its ardent creator.
Or should there be blame, let not those lips part,
Adorned with hues of deceit, utter reproof.
For they too have stained love's sacred art,
Sealed false vows, as thy ardent heart doth pledge.
Grant me the right to love with equal zest,
As thine eyes wander, and my heart does plead.
Nurture compassion in that breast, so blessed,
That compassion may merit


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To keep him from the cold, wet, and blighted land.
But you, the child of my loveliest wreath,
Lost in thoughts of what you should do,
My dearest, truest, now my heaviest care,
You, now free, roam the wide world,
Adorning the spotless loveliness with your fame.
  I teach you to write as if more,
  To make my love stand out above.


