Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As pall bearers for the evening's glow.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may thy heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes thee, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where thy beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal its


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It bears the weight of my heart, and I,
Bound to its side, it allows no break.
No matter if my friend is away,
Or if I, burdened with sorrow, do stand,
My heart, from suffering, kills with a vengeance.
  For in this torment, I find my heart's birth,
  And so, I thrice more wretched do call.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine heir may claim
His interest in thy esteem.
Such is the art of deception, wherein I am
Stuck, like a miser in the act,
Without any surety whatsoever,
To trust an ever-changing fortune,
Which in thy breast I may discover a widow's tear.
Thy glass will show thee how I have lived
Unmindful of the rest, which I have no surety in,
Hiding thy beauties from the vulgar eyes.
  And yet, should some vulgar scandal arise,
  He shall live, and I in love with fortune shall be.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say nothing of thee but that I will,
And by some other's will, do confirm thy will;
That tongue that tells the story of love
Which I better should not publish,
Unless thou get a husband from me.
O, what a mansion have I in my heart
Where one may stay and dwell with thoughts of thee,
Whose thought is more than my love, to thee and me?
  There lives more love in my breast than brass,
  If my thoughts should count it dead.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love's plea, gentle, bear.
Yearning will fulfill the treasure of thy love,
Indeed, fill it abundant with yearnings, and mine, one.
In matters of vast capacity, we find
Among many, one is insignificant, dear.
Then in the multitude, let me be unwritten,
Although in thy heart's record, I am one.
For nothing holds me, if it pleases thee bind
That nothing me, a something, dear, to thee.
  Just make my name your love, and love that enduring,
  And then thou love


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet doth thy shadow comment upon that beauty?
Neither are thy hands swift nor heavy,
Slow in their course to strike or run after wind;
They can neither leap nor leap that swiftly from west
To east or west in space they contend.
Thou blind Guide, Love, what destitution hast thou wrought
That for thyself to thyself thine own shame and pride?
  Be not so self-willed that thou mayst be
  To thyself thyself thy shame and pride giveth.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I, not acquainted with manners, should think them strange.
But why should I believe her tongue, which speaks so well,
When I am ignorant of her worth,
And what she thinks I lack, I perceive to my great grief?
The truth may be foreign to my great mind,
But she that makes me believe her loves me thus,
To save my heart from tears she bestows.
  And yet, heaven knows, she loves only me thus:
  For she believes not me when I set my heart on her.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my soul be entangled in thoughts of praise,
But let my flesh be your theater.
So shall I sing of thee, unfazed by praise,
While my spirit, in its elements, shall suffice
To recite thee as it grows more blessed than before.
O, what a mansion have I found
Which shelter for the heart in thy deeds could bar,
Or structure withstand the storm's assault?
  The earth can withstand the siege of the outer shell,
  But not thy spirit, which more heavily bombs.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy thoughts, though I might calumniate thee.
If I could write the better part of thee,
Though not to cherish, still, love, to show,
As thine eyes would willingly show,
The worthless forbearance of thy name.
For if I should do wrong, I'd lose thee,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
  To avoid this, and to uphold your dignity,
