Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As forms of eunuchs wear mourning dress,
Adorning each other's face with white;
Trees, with their tendrils, and flowers, all white,
As mourners come and pray, each other's heart
Is adorned with white, as if by angels flown.
Then, if thy heart be pure, as mine,
By my deeds, thou shalt be--as I by thee seen,
Deserved honor, and thy honor take.
  Yet, fear not to death, for it is thine,
  Given freely, and never to rescind.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is enough to torment me within my breast,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief.
But what if I do not behold
My tormentor's face, though it wields all
To torment me in his vast, unwavering breast?
What if I do not flee, then confront
This monstrous foe, and KO?
  Yet still I cry, for love's sweet pain
  Lies in waiting, and will not cease.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim what is thy due;
And he, in so great debt, enjoys
Thy beauty's allure, being wooed of time,
And for this, I lose him, and so much worse.
How can I then be a debtor
And have him treated as a bequest?
No artifice can then be so preposterous
As that wherein I am confined
To do thee as thou dost me.
  I lose him, and yet he hath art
  To this purpose and that to thee.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say within thine own deep-sunken eyes
Thy beauty's lease is all-and-equal.
But that thou mayst by misfortune impair
My life, and make me seem poor still,
I will suffer, being weak in will;
Thou canst not, love, take physic upon me,
Nor my life, to thee but give physic.
  I will speak of thee, and what I will say,
  The wretchedness of my condition is thus made my argument.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
O, swear, if thou wilt believe me,
That I love thee, for I am blind,
Though my looking star may show me otherwise.
For I see not the beauty where I abide,
Nor the shape which I in heaven find;
Of thine own worth I have no part,
Unless thou wouldst subdue me there.
  And yet be a fool, love knows,
  To swear against thyself so vile a god.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in that they cannot be deceived
How their eyes may be deceived in looking elsewhere.
Yet dost thou, Love, with such blindness do ensnare
My ears with your twisted thought,
And lead my heart to such a pit
That I in my madness shall weep:
"Hadst thou but eyes to see, 'tis hard to see;
To my dear doting heart it doth thine own dear part."
  Yet dost thou lead my heart still more insane
  Than those lips that would not understand love's pain.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And yet love me as truth doth live,
To whom she bids my duty conform;
But, love, you love her because she thinks me foolish,
And she knows it is her duty to show it
And to you I will not shut my eye.
Because she knows not what lies in me
I will believe her even though she knows it is false,
And I will be the better for it, knowing her lies.
  For I must be false in love to keep her honor
  And lose her for aught else.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my soul be stained with mourning's waste,
Nor my flesh with mourning's society spread.
O, change not thy thought, that I may change my mind;
Speak not of my past sorrows to me,
So as to prevent my heart from new woe;
Grant me the strength to love those I have not known,
Over whom thy love has no part, though I may change my mind.
  Pity me, then, dear friend, and change my mind;
  Not from those tears that thou dost strew on me.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sorrow.
If I could speak of thee more fully, better still,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should push the end of days,
Upon unthrifts that weigh little,
My voice might speak of thee, and thine,
As heavy rains, when thou art gone, flow
From sullen earth, where thou art.
  Thus, love, be still, for I cannot urge
  But


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Mine own love is false, tainted, weak,
A traitor love, in thy vices found.
Thou art as blind as a she-goat, deluded,
Finding thy pleasure, thy truth, untrue.
That thou mayst be true, I defy,
Though she doth thy love, false heart hath she doth thy con.
  For she hath wrought thee so, and for that sake
  To hide her love hath she fallen short.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To save her from the wild, untamed,
A huntress of her kind, wild and free.
So you, within your breast, awaiting love's call,
Breed a kinder, warmer, more inviting soul,
That beauty's flame may ever burn in thee,
Though she, in truth, still calls to thee.
Who turns from a kindred spirit, so kind,
Unless they flee, forsaking all in their wake?
  My love, you are your mother's spirit,
  And she, in turn, is your spirit's mother.


