Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As pallbearers at the solemn ball.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may thy heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes thee, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where thy beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal its glare


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Then should I weep for thee, more than for myself,
For my friend and I are one;
Then can no suffering me nor you fill
The vacant space with gloom that wants no help.
O, that our hearts might share that love deep,
And bind wounds there that bleed for love,
Where darkness hides behind a sunlit crest.
  But that is not the end of my grief;
  My friend and I are still as one.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May hold his title to thee.
Such a claim is not unconfined
Within the knowledge of mine own self,
For it is thine and he, in so peculiar a dance,
That I, a debtor, must be thy bequest and hold,
The rest of me, to whom thou dost lease me.
In him thy gift is so complete
That I in him may retain the title to thee,
Though he takes it from me and pays it me elsewhere.
  This meekness hath thou, in him, the surety of all;
  He pays the bond,


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To leave thee suffering alone, to heal thy mind,
And to approve what I will not reproach thee.
But since she hath thee, and I cannot claim my share,
Thou of thyself thy sweet self dost deceive,
And I am the lesser giver, in thy glory.
I lend thee virtue, and she hath it stolen,
I give her false esteem, and she is dumb;
Both for my sake and for thy sake, I lose both twain,
And both for my sake and for thy sake, I must bear this pain.
  But here's the joy


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love I'll stay, though my foot be on the ground,
For love's promise is thine, and I am doomed to fall,
If I but keep my eyes open.
Who lets so fair a house fall to decay,
Which husbandry in honor might uphold
Against the stormy gusts of winter's day?
  O, none but unthrifts, dear my love, you know.
  You have your will, and I have mine eyes.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet thy fair face, when they look elsewhere,
Doth but approve what they see there.
See what good turns eyes for eyes have done,
Or faces for faces, which to my shame do ensue,
Since, said they, 'tis thy turn to show,
Mine eyes for thy picture's sight are deceived,
And thy fair face to my disgrace repays.
  Yet so thy fair might be, and thence they look
  For their picture, which in my pleasure they cannot.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though not to love, yet, love, to show it me shame,
As some worthless child would his mother's face,
And her love, grown old, should speak ill of me,
As she, in her turn, does this prove;
But why should I then reproach her lie,
When I am stained with countless groans,
Do I not then confess my age?
  For she loves me, and I cannot love her so;
  She only hinders my moving further.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And yet I will not yield the right
To love thee more dearly than thyself commanding.
O, shoot me with thy spurned love,
And yet I will stay your loyal subject;
Thou canst not wound me more deeply
Than by provoking me with thy spurned love.
Therefore shall I love thee more sincerely
When thou thyself art proved by my love to be false;
Then canst thou love me more truly
When thou thyself art proved by my love to be false.
