Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As soothing balm to every afflicted ache.
Yet my sorrows are not eased by passing thoughts,
Nor eased by thinking on thy blessed name;
Nor are changes that I think are eased
By bearing thy transgression with fortitude.
O, what a torment have I been, supposing,
To think on thee, when in my breast
Thy beauty's guilt is attainted,
And in my soul it dwells, tormentor and beam.
  Yet, though my heart should wane, my sight should not cease,
  For love's sweet memory grants true grace.


