Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
My heart, moved by pity, did bid her farewell,
And sent her another messenger, yet I know
She did not in that sent his spirit downrange.
But those lips that heard my sorrowful say,
Made another sound, less kind, more kind,
And taught them to say "I love you most,"
As if by some instinct they had known
What hurt she meant, and what she would not release.
  "I love her most," I said, "since first I saw her frown,"
  And she, in that same glance, set me straight.


