O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Grant not a plea against me that I did
Do wrong which thou mayst possibly see
Against myself, who am comely in body;
That my soul's breast be ever watered
With thy rosy cream to stain gray tone when I
Answer thee gay, though hate foretell.
O, alter not this curse of mine,
Which I ought not to be had
After time, which doth this yoke repair,
And to save thee from everlasting fire kill;
  Too dreadful be death which brings it to pass
  Of brief thought and not report of thy rotten woe.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy transgression more than deeds.
For if I could persuade thee to yield on the crown
Of sorrow's fullness, it would stay mine;
Thus thou shalt find satisfaction in my injury
Thy mercy's more ample grace than words can.
Till the insults of age, now fierce, reveal
Thy heart's willing capitulation,
And wisdom so spurring thee on brings
Satisfaction more than words can give.
  Embrace not my sufferings with scorn,
  For they but confirm my worth, far above.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
In him your virtues they doubtless allege.
For 'tis the inward ornament that cherishes
Its distance from the piercing test of proofs,
Which truth it cherishes only out of ownie.
For 'tis nature's way that gives place
To strange memories in body and brain;
Some copy 'gainst presage to hurt religious speech,
Making impure answers to strange cries;
Others, knowing your inclination, praise thy good
Aborning on holy writ desired.
  For they see not the ends of why they do
  Grant thee thy deserts they deceive with argument.


