Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To my sorrow they've dressed in mourning's attire.
And for they have darkened, and shunned
The light of day, with their darkened hearts,
I have been blind, and now behold,
My sorrow darkened by mourning's dress,
A mournful, dusky, yet shining day.
Oh, cruel mistress, heartless enabler,
Who pays the cost of such a woe,
Wishing darkness on the flaunted display!
  Hers, like a cruel nurse, takes pleasure in torment,
  Lest dark tears should in pity o'erwhelm.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To be the tormentor that I will be?
Imprison my heart in your chest,
And grant me rest when I long,
No longer can you then torture with fear.
But still, love, do your worst,
For I, a wretched vessel, shall be cast adrift,
Crying on the ocean's ear,
While every wind may blow and sea may alter.
  Still, fight, love, and I shall not cry,
  For I have found a solace that keeps my heart from wailing.


