I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Speak of things which had better meant,
Though I was but a creature of high estate,
A strange bond did she have with me.
So, kindhearted youth, you cannot swerve
Against my passionate longing,
Admit I love one whom she knows well;
I dare not chide her, for I know
Her when I shall part from thee,
And she will not blame me, knowing
My love her, though she knows it's false.
  For she loves me, and I cannot refrain,
  For she adores me, and none else pleases.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Grant me the right of appeal against thine adversary;
Against my will, grant me leave to pursue,
To the full extent of thine intended remedy.
May I have the satisfaction of knowing
That thy love embraces me;
May I rest assured that my needs
Are met by the means at my disposal?
My right of appeal, if necessary,
Is best expounded by the simple verse:
  "In love, I am at liberty to roam."


