Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Crying for thee, too poor to see all.
The morning sun, in its heavenly course,
Nor the bright star that leads thee to plunge,
Can burn with more pure light than thy poor orbs can see,
Gracing the evening with its true face.
So, thy heart being black, like to thee,
Thy looks be the tribute to this poverty,
Which with thy loss brings life anew,
More plentiful than the dung of a common bird.
  Pity me, then, dear friend, and know this truth:
  Thy love is more than sweet to me.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
Imprison my spirit in your unyielding breast,
And let sorrow through the endless night?
Tears of love, once shed for me, now transformed,
In remorse, they find their place anew,
In darkness, they weep for love that was false.
Let then my friend's heart weep for love torn,
A mournful song, a heart that bends to its will.
  Yet, if you but break, let my friend's heart be taught,
  A lesson learned in sorrow, not in anger.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Can deface not with thoughts but thine own,
By thee alone I am bound, and to thee I do abide.
But, if he be false, so I believe,
No blame on me, for I am indeed behind
Thine act, thy loan for my reparation repay.
No civil claim then can I make,
He is guilty and am not proved;
I must believe him, though he deny it,
And I by my sin forfeit all I have own.
  But since I thought myself all tyrants and guilty of many,
  Yet am well paid by thee alone


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Thou whose boundless bounty seldom ever was found.
Therefore thrive'st thou that thou alone mayst make use of
That due unto thee, and no other.
Be it with plants and barren deserts those barren days
That I myself bring forth for thy use blessed,
Or seem to bring forth such a blenched abundance
As thy strong desire shall please thee.
  From lacking a want, go forth with a double spite,
  Of surplus being with a fullness made.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
How many a time hath my friend thrice stained with falsehood,
Thou blind confidant of my life robbed.
Then in thy sweet thoughts, forgive me thy theft,
And this thy defense, if I read it with interest,
Will I be blotted of thy sweet friend's living blood?
Thy defense will be that I am thy will,
And desire, a true love, well will I meet.
  For this my defense will be deemed pure,
  And in thy eyes I will be held guiltless.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet this love which keeps them from seeing thee
Plot 'twixt the slight heaven where it doth live,
Sculpted by the art of his complexion!
Mark how one eye that perceives through other
Doth th' under-governed brilliance of thine eyes
Before they have sense to look into thine own part.
See what good part of thy perceivable cheeks
Thou canst not blot, being thou got'st thy friend made thee;
Then of thine eyes should pride and praise be proud,
Since then they were made the windows of thine eyes


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
If truest love subjects all her praises to falsehood,
Such a state I never saw or be made part of,
Lest I should sometime inveigh
Against falsehood the praises of my beloved stand.
Yet, love, do not, I implore,
Dissuade her being false in her grand theft,
Since, believing me to be foolish,
I do for thy sake set my love apart.
  But do not think that I am oblivious to my youth,
  Since I with others in our dearest plight tend.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Dissuade me not to suppose thy will
After thy consum's end, to inquire where
I am to be remembered in future ages.
If thou do call me, do not with evil intent,
Wield mine injury upon the judgment day,
When I, bow not to thine injury but to my will,
And come upon me when I am at my best.
O, do not, love, provoke this woe
By seeking for reasons why I do not speak well of thee.
  Thy foe is thine, and I am his, and they are twain;
 


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sense of loss and wretchedness.
If I had forced my thought, "Now I love him best,"
As therapy for my tongue-tied patient, I might say
"Now I love him because he loves me best,"
And yet the very thought would bring me no cheer,
Since now my friend, once broken, is truly mine.
  If I had sought insight into your mind,
  You would have taught me how to be kind, thus far.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Contrary to their judgment, which in this vice does excel.
To other I testify that love they too shall be free,
Whate'er is more bliss in their blindness than mine is.
And therefore, my soul cherishes what they spurn,
Enslaved by their false, false, injurious sight.
At all their frowns and frowns with me am deceived,
And likelier for I am a closet,
Beneath their spell their brightness doth blind.
  For 'tis the thought of thee that wounds,
  Defying their design, that keeps their hearts


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Which me alone dost witness, even to impurity.
Thou alone mak'st redeem me in thoughts,
Which shape to thyself for posterity find
But beauty's mother doth be, plain to see.
The flowers of true love, oud, are thine,
Thyself are thy difficile;
Thou henceforth shalt hap to call thyself fair,
Although thou hide thyself with others.
  Ah, but why is my love false,
  If thou lov'st not my dear virtue?


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To the wat'ry mother earth, where her joy is stored.
So, she who keeps her hope unblemished,
Her bravest heart in safety confined,
Makes her escape, her joy a distant dream,
A chase that cannot end, for it entwines.
But if she does reach the surface, though,
Her heart's desire shall be her prize,
Unyielding, unbroken, forever grand.
  If I, but push myself on, too hard, I'll leave my mark,
  Leaving no trace in my wake but your name.


