Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To mock my fate, and tell me of
The gloom that darkness brings forth.
You are sorrow's company, and I am their captain,
Of sorrow, I lead them in sorrow's dark path;
The saddest mayker, when its sorrow's course done,
Lies in wait, black as mourning's shadow,
And bears the disgrace that it caused.
  So they mourn, and I, in turn, rejoice,
  For every hollow comfort brings forth such a cheer.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To draw out your life and let me die alone?
No, let us strive to mend each other's hearts,
And find the strength to love even more.
Oh, transform my heart, make it more blest than before,
Beneath the transgression of my unkindness,
Let me know how much better I am off
By your love and kindness than by my deeds.
  But if you should take that love away,
  I'd be left lamenting, in utter disarray.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May enjoy the greater part of it.
But he will not so much as acknowledge me,
For that's not my will, it belongs to thee;
He's mortgaged to that worth, and I to thee,
Under the burden of this worth;
So, I lose him not to lose me,
But that I may enjoy his love both.
  The gainer by loss is despised,
  And the blighter by public's snare is so.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To give command of what is mine own dear,
Aught I, thy loving issue, to go to all my will.
Grant me the boon, to love those whom thou dost hate,
