Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To mock my misfortune and envy spread.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad array,
Nor the bright star that ushers in repose,
Can compare with the beauty of thy remorse,
Whose shadowed form, when heaven's resplendent light
Bestows its gentle light on my sorrow's array.
So too, may my heart's scorn, with thee,
Be as thy shadowed heart, in its evil ways,
Be as thou art, and those watching be,
Who mock thee, for thy transgression too profound.
  For thine eyes are fond


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Have I not suffered enough to let go
My own soul, which yearns to share this grief?
No, let me suffer, burdened with sorrow,
And suffer your scorn, knowing it's well
To lift my friend from the depths of pain she knows.
Wage war with sorrow, and yet we cannot be
Two hearts entwined in unity,
Though we wail, we cannot be estranged.
  Yet still we yearn, and yearn with ardor,
  Two souls entwined in sorrow, wage war


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Owns the worth of thy most dear-purchased good.
The bond then, of less sweet pains,
Than of many a plea in jury is sworn,
Which is this to forego the pleasure of one?
For, having many, one is so possessed
With many, that he has many, and none in common;
And, deformed and needy, by this very possession,
He lives upon the throw of thy bench.
  Thus is it that he that so abuses me
  Is poor in hope, since I have hope of many.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To wet the canopy of love's fertile pride.
I tell thee, love, beauteous blessings thou must receive,
Whereto all wombs, having loved one, are united,
And hold a common mother tongue, as thine in sweet issue.
Hearken, then, dear mother; do not be dismayed
That thy love outbraves thee even in single issue,
For love alone can sour the weed of time,
If two souls be one, and haply of one.
  Make thee couple, and beauteous blessings flow,
  Not that thou


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Grant me the boon of acquaintance, to test
Thy judgment, thy right so immense,
Be it not to forbid thyself my will,
Be it to forego the means to be desired,
Be it to engage in willful wrong,
Or at thy whim, a worthless vice,
To do harm to me, who endure'st
Thy right, though thou wilt be profane.
  For to thine illicit love I give right,
  Thy sweet thief will be my gain, and my dear name.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet thou fool'st them into thinking more
Than that which they think they have seen.
Why should I, which are blind, tell
What good turns eyes for eyes beardless should do,
To seek some defect in some strange sight?
Thou fool'st them into thinking more
Than they see, and to blame not me
For looking they don't understand well.
  They misconstrue my blindness, and speak ill of me
  To my shame, which brings me shame and tears.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And that I before that time did willfully invent
Some vice out of everything I have heard,
Even as an aged father, proud to have had a son.
But why should I believe her false name,
When I see her in the glass of days long past,
Whilst I think myself still the child of god,
Unlooked for praise or idle hours tolled?
  Because she thinks I have forgotten her,
  To give myself away she prays.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my hidden grief bring disgrace
To thy name, though I do openly sing
Of thy unkindness everywhere.
Be good, then, and sincere in love;
Him in whom my unkindness does dwell,
That I to others may give
My own nature more glory than mine own eyes.
For to thyself I bring this I do wrong,
And to my lovers worse still;
Though they themselves be rude, to me they are still so.
  Receiving no reason for my unkindness,
  Then to myself I give my all, with great content.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my hidden sorrow.
If I could write the depth of joy I feel,
It would be a novel thing, and I'd be wronged,
Lest others' love expressed their own joy,
And I, a cruel master, would be honored,
For I've learned to write with disdain,
As thine own heart is wont to be;
Oh, better to be base and named wrong,
Than suffer scorn and suffer punishment's reach.
  For I've learned to be cruel even unto myself,
  To feel proud of what I dislike, even unto myself.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For all tongues, the voice of souls, in their harmony,
Reserve the right of praise that thou dost owe,
In truth they speak, and I but understand
Their conceit, which in their ends they bestow.
And thus, in our errors, we find solace in thy grace,
For what's so true, we find our hearts in it,
Or, if we fall short, in our praises,
We find grace in thy worth, proud and smart.
  For we who praise thee, in our blindness,


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Thou self dost deceive me, for thine own heart is rotten.
For as the seasons change, so do my love's fortunes,
From summer's splendor to winter's stark display,
Where once-verdant branches whisper a sad song,
And beauty wails alone bare,
Save where snowflakes or white autumn's grace
Adorn the mountaintops with their white array.
  And yet thee, in thy wilful denial, do grace
  This denial with thy beauty lies so deep.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the place where it ran wild.
So you, like her, must learn to rely
On your own worth, more than me
To shield me from trifles, strong as a beauteous chick
With words, though weak, still able to rise.
For fear of falsehood's dark shadow keeps you from seeing
The truth, if you but heed.
  To do so, you'd lose yourself, and
  Lose your soul, too, to falsehood's devious game.


