Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in that they cannot grasp its worth?
Mark, if you look, there you see a heaven
Above all other forms of light,
Fairing the object of scorn, true.
If I could paint the face with my hue,
The world would cry, "This is his art,
His talent, his birth, his grace,
His unwavering faith, his devoted care."
But since you see the truth, the paint's stroke,
Roses have ebbs and silver streaks,
And eyes, if not stained by love's own deceit,
At least acknowledge its right there.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And who gives her grace, if not from my hand?
Though she may err, I hold the straight path,
And by so doing, lives in my sight;
But I suspect not her of falsehood, though she often acts
And leaves me forlorn, each her own fault.
So I stand not astonished, though she often cheats,
Though she nearly all adds up to me,
And I almost have her all in my hand.
  For she that bestows such gifts knows well
  That all I wish for myself I bring.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my name be misused, neither my dear be made lame,
Although I straight steal what thou most richly give.
O, do not, like one who is fickle,
Give me leave to choose whether I shall stay or go;
That is, love, my dear choice;
The other half of my love, which thou mayst give away,
Is with thee, my sweet; so be it.
  But if thou live, then life is in love confined,
  And love alone hath such a hold upon me.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my hidden woe.
If I could instill sagacity, better thus,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should plunge into despair, I'd lose my mind,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, all is done; what do I have still to show?
  But wait; dear love, your memory's fresh might
  Shall hold me fast, despite pain and thirst.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight.
For why should my sight err in judgment day,
Judging sin by its own fallow ground?
Or on my sin, magnified by your concern,
Do I incur penalty, just as I perceive
My sin to be true, even though I feel myself otherwise unrighteous?
No, I remain as I am, true and steadfast,
While they, distorted, judge me through their haze.
  For no lawful plea exists that binds
  Me, but that I in you am found.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
For thy own love is no blot,
It's the mirror of our love's face.
So, love, be the better, for thyself I hate,
Lest my visage should in hate's shape betray
My true love, and myself be torn.
For thee, I'm stained with countless groans,
And by my silence, ever so slight,
Thy name should echo, "This silence doth teach."
  Hence, I stay mute, to avoid falling in love,
  And in mine own soul, every sinned against.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To seek a haven more suited to her needs.
So you, my lovely, are in this plight
Untouched or unsteady, your grace so great,
Like one who, out of sight, does travel
To wherever she pleases, no matter how far.
For if she flourishes, no trace remains,
And her beauty fades, forgotten and tired,
Our minds, ensnared by her, never tire.
  But if your loveliness falls, so too,
  Mine fell victim to your beauty's allure.


