Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in their seeing, do not so much as mine own eyes perceive.
They see the best, and in their judgment, condemn the best,
In their blindness, seeing farther than mine own eyes.
To be so astray, and to my hurt
They lead me astray, and lead thee in their spite,
Which, in my seeing, is not my fault, though they lead me astray.
  For my eyes, which see, cannot in their judgment measure
  The best that yet can be, to thyself.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But that I am wiser in certain points
Than those of her poet's proud boast.
Though she may err, I hold the truth,
And her falsehoods I despise,
So I cannot envy those whose hearts are swayed
By beauty's allure and by sight.
This I do so because I fear she will be false too,
And I in love for her, so spurn,
Desiring nothing better than that which is true.
  For nothing holds her as still as I,
  If she will but move on and lose her tongue.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak not of my lameness to excuse my amiss,
But do it thus: mine is thy love
And my love's proposed remedy is my injury.
If I could plead ignorance, better still,
Thine eyes would acquit me with falsehood;
But better still, I might be proved
As ignorant as thou dost thyself here.
  For I cannot hold thee to understand
