Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my hurt;
In gentle touch, I can soothe the blow,
As chilling proof, black is not permitted,
To liken thee to a winter's calm.
If I could school thee in the art of banter, better still,
Though not to cherish, still, to admit, to let me know,
As a willing subject, I'd learn to write
As thine, to express thine own worth.
  For thee, I would school thee in virtue,
  And words to match, to show thee how to be.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
For what I in thee perceive, it doth know
Another heart in thee to love the very same.
Mine eyes, to whom these others in thy course give light,
Are in thy heart's delight, but thy inward view doth show
Another heart in thee to whom they in other path do tie.
Thy outward view, being wiser, doth know
What effect is had by looking on thee thyself.
  By so looking on me, thou thyself thyself of wrong do prove
  In me, much greater wrong than I did thee conceive.


