And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
 may claim his due;
So, to him I am still confined, and he in me
Deserves the world to haply claim his treasure.
But how can he be so loved that he fears no harm?
Why should false colors mimic his own face,
Steal life's true essence from its rightful place?
Why should false beauty borrow from his grace,
Seeking shadows where no roses bloom and brace?
  True love's face, in which shadows live,
  Is fairer than any imitation's hand.


