  For they who are wise in this, know how
  To make false praises of true things.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And therefore with great pretense assails me,
As if I did not think on truth so great.
But why should I trust her when I know she is false,
Since she can so preposterously make me seem?
My love is false and pretentious, to whom she affords
The praise which she better knows I do deny.
  And so she adds more to my praise,
  Lest I should think on truth with ill report.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Spare me the sight of either's revenge
Or of thy sweet regret.
O, neither will I, being obeyed, reproach thee
For I see yet another right in thy favor
That to my injury thou hast too grossly done;
Therefore in my being do allow
That myself, being obeyed, may rightly complain
That I obeyed not thy golden rule,
But to my injury did willfully lend
Myself to that which I should not.
  Such is my love, to thy sweet and true will,
  That, for thy sweet favor do right me so.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own wickedness more nearly.
If I could write well what thou dost withhold,
Better still, being asked of it, would be new;
But since most tongues are dull and impassive,
They speak of thee only as they see thee stand,
And that, in their judgment, is most untrue.
They may say more well of my love's plainness,
Though in their mouths I learn to write
Thy faults, not my loves; thus, I think,
They but partly appreciate what I wish to say.
  And, added to this, are born false


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight.
For my eyes in thee are ensnared by the painted lie,
And by the painted lie, they find delight in falsehood;
But my heart, which sees the truth in everything,
Sought out the bitter taste of false invective.
In reverence of self, I write thus:
"Thou false, blind Guide, Love, thou servant of thyself!"
  For my eyes in thee find delight, and my heart in love,
  Despair hath taught them to write as if untrue were true.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Or rather, that is, that with respect to thee.
So far for love my love's plea, gentle, bear.
Stained scarlet with me, thy love is fortified,
Two strokes of love, one for love, another for.
Thou canst not, love, break free,
For 't lies in love to keep mine eye beguiled.
  'T is thine, and my love to love enforce,
  So far for love my love, gentle.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the haven where it will stay,
So begins the prelude to a joy that outshines
All other sources.
She carefully tracks the sweet bird, carefully
Scribes her joy on every scrap of parchment,
And keeps her bound to this song, her heart's delight.
Yet, her joy does slip away, away,
For love's sweet power plays the coward;
She lets the sweet bird wilt, and leaves her child to grow.
  Yet, still, I treasure her for her constancy,
  And so, I'll pen still, for your love outlasts.


