Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gilded tributes to thee, like precious gems.
Yet, my sight, being elsewise kind,
Cannot dispassionate love's voice, unburdened by alloy,
Grasping the heart's all-too-precious part,
Nor does it wield judgment's sting,
Grasping the teeth that to the grieve have gagged.
My heart's love, so soft and so true,
Yet to thy unwavering sight I concede,
A concede that love may lag behind.
  So, thou cunning slanderer; a true heart
