Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my other self to suffer,
Whilst I, that self, can neither move nor feel?
Imprison my heart within your fortress chest,
But grant my friend's heart freedom in its place.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart be their guest,
For none can then oppress me in that space.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to be your own, and all I do.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May stay your possession and keep it.
What could be so base and unworthy a confession
That deserved no harsh penalty and deserved no appeal,
Be it ill or ill-contented, it depends upon thy report.
If it be a crime, well-contented and well-refined,
It is but fitting that in my will there exists
A will, that to thyself I'll give life and prove it;
No other voice or judgment so fair
As mine when I think on thee,
  For thine alone is my defense.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And will be till my gracious numbers be spent.
A fitful husbandry will do thee better
If he but live in good graces,
A widow's will, if she but die thee.
Then need I no needy argument,
Nor great professions to grace thy book,
For nothing by nature's power can take thee away.
Nor squander thy gifts upon worthless women,
Savage savages, making thy beauty for women's eyes.
  Thy beauty graces even the most barren tribes,
  And they but plain themselves in their fading.


