O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike not with my brow, though my heart be glad;
Let me die, oppressed with melancholy,
Whilst thyself, living in pomp, more blessed
Than all the rest combined.
O, let me suffer, being at thy beck,
Thyself much inferior to my state;
Beware, sweet friend, of this easy temptation;
I'll suffer, if thou wilt part.
  Pity me, then, dear friend, and know this truth:
  Thy love is so great, so loving, to admit.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my hurt; don't make me bend,
Lest sorrow make me speak rudely.
If I should speak ill of thee, do not so,
But in thy face, soothe me gently;
Then I may truly know
Whether thy spirit is kind, or whether it is hard.
If it be hard, be kind to it;
If not, be cruel to it yet.
  For I cannot love thee with half-hearted cheer,
  Unless thou make the change I require.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For the eyes in heaven see the truth quite plainly,
But my sinful self in sin does blot the light;
For heaven's judgment of perfect right is but ill,
A thousand groans, but voices of discontent raise.
Then, beauteous anchor, my heart and eyes, in discord,
Mark the storm where heaven's truth and evil fell.
  Heaven's judgment, now proven to be false,
  Shall hold the glad tidings of my plighted state.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Which for itself doth change with thy love.
A change in love from what it was is had,
And so is my love now to change with ardor.
By thy change my love looks on thee
And raves with a groan, ever so groan;
The groan being thy change from bad to good,
The reason of my moan being that change it is.
  How do I love thee that way, O Jeram, so hard,
  If thou my love were but good, never fell.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
She looms, her craftiness and speed unknown,
While joy topples low, and fleece everywhere.
So you, in love's pursuit, abandon me,
Your tender care, to chase a fleeing dream,
Wasting your strength on unworthy song,
Obscuring your light in the shadows of night,
Letting your laughter distract your pursuer,
Seeking shadows where no shadows shall abide.
  Return to me, and I'll craft a tale of love,
  For your sweet love, I'll craft an eulogy.


