Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As darkened by mourning as mine is by thee.
The dawn has broken, and twilight hath gleamed,
Whose beauty through the veil of night was concealed;
All creatures have fled, as if to the west,
And thee, all in one, one radiant glow.
But my heart, like a jeweled wonder,
Unlooked for by Time's cruel scythe,
Nor finds fault in thy joy that thou hast given.
  So I shall stand, and wonder,
  Until then, I'll be the mournful fool.


