Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
With tears and sighs, they've e'ermore removed.
The dawn has broken, and the cold night descended,
Concealing from our sight that glorious hue,
As if to make our grief seem stronger still.
Yet, my eyes, bewitched by pity's sweet glare,
Are wont to pity, and, in their plight,
Look kindly on thy sorrow's endless flow.
  They pity me, and rightly so,
  For thy heart is black, and thus their sight is stained.


