Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Hearing thee, as prayers go, take heed.
All tongues, the soul's unhidden plea,
Bestow on my unworthy heart their grace,
As prayers to thee, in purity performed.
I bear thy sorrow, then, not as if it were born
Of thee alone, but as if through thee,
My unworthy heart and friend, two souls,
In thine own neck, the sweetest morrow.
  Let others grieve, but I shall not let hate brag,
  Since thou art to blame, and I am to be blamed.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Even if I should plunge into despair, find release
In the darkness of night, I'd be left forlorn.
I cannot bear the thought of losing you, so dear,
Lest my friend's heart should find its strength again,
And in the darkness, I'd be drowned.
I cannot bear the thought of you, so dear,
If I should lose you in the night's silent hold.
  All days are dark till you are shining bright,
  And nights are bright when you are away.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May have claim on thy dear-purchased treasure.
Look what is best, that best I cannot request;
Thou dost him, and he hath thee as collateral doth depend.
If he will not release thee, let him be silent,
And I shall be left in the dark, to suffer
The cost of a wretch's sorrow.
  I have no cloak to hide myself,
  And he in his disgrace has got me to bare.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And more than enough my love hath hate,
To thee and to my rest.
I may excuse the stealing of thy dear-contented
While I think on thee and love,
Thy love shall excuse the loathsome taking of thy dear-
And to my rest thou hast the strength of laws.
I may even excuse the bashing of thy dear-
By thy love being so harsh and remorseless.
Thou canst not, love excuse the crime,
Lest thou be absolved of thy crime more than I.
  For to thee I am confined I must attend
 


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
If thy soul will believe, why should I shun?
For love's sweet memory holds trifles dear,
And in thy heart's embrace, things forgetful.
If thou wilt leave me, do so, with dignity,
My dear love well knows, and I am assured,
She love's bound to me, and cannot disown.
  To leave me, she hath no power, and I am assured,
  She loves those whom she can't.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in their blindness, they think it false
To say that it is brighter than other men's eyes.
Indeed, it is as if they have eyes to see,
But lack the power to say that they see;
And, moving shadows, do they ever seem
To say that they see the plainest light?
Oh,, if they could use their eyes to find
The sun's true excellence, much maligned,
They would say that it shines more bright
Than if it were painted with shadows's help.
  But since they see the most, no man is so dumb



When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And so should I be dumb,
And not to trust in deceptions grown common
Unless I believe in heaven's power.
But is it not folly to trust in dreams?
For love's deceit may be more blessed
If it knows what it loves to dream.
Nay, 'tis not love, though much, that deceives me,
Who loves to know what he loves to say.
My love knows well what it fears to meet,
And knows well what it fears to find.
  If it knows it fears to dream, it knows well
  That which it fears to wake.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak not my grief to those who are afar off,
But in thy heart's own den do lay.
For how can I then be blamed if I do wrong
And in my own sin, do pardon thy wrong?
I do forgive thy trespass, yet yet on thy part,
A sinner, in thy transgressions, I am guilty.
For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
To suffer the most grievous of wrongs to be
And to bear that cross, when thou wilt be mine.
  I do forgive thy trespass, yet on thy part,



Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my sorrows).
If thou wilt not, love, be true to me,
Embrace my pain; thus, I shall know
Thy heart is sincere, and not to be wary.
If thou wilt leave me, then I must know
Thy heart is kind, and to be kind, too;
And thus, I may know thy kindness is misplaced,
And in my madness, I may speak ill of thee.
  For I may not be mad, and in my madness,
  So should I speak ill of thee.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And in their errors I find sweet solace.
For through the windows of my heart thou see'st
The golden sun adorn the mountaintops with rich hue,
Which in the dawn of new-morn I look on day;
And in the shady corners of the night thou see'st
The glowing candles of an old-fashioned hearth.
  In all external grace I find,
  But you, my rose; in you my harmony.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Thou art as base as any petty fool.
For to thy own sweet-smelling bud doth belong
Thy own sweet-smelling bud doth in thee live.
And, to thy own dear love, to thy sweet-doting friend
Hath stol'n thy blood before thy friend can say.
So thy own sweet-doing traitor love doth live,
And in thy sweet-doting friend doth live,
And every ill that hath befallen thee,
Is but thy own sweet-doing treason's hour.
  If thy life hath been nothing more


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
She follows after her beloved, still clutching
The joy of her clutch, at a distance.
But you, enamored of your bright own sphere,
Nourish your light's fire with self-same fuel,
Breeding famine amidst the land's rich bounty, my dear,
An enemy to your own self, too harsh and cruel.
You, now the world's ornament of delight,
And sole herald of the gaudy springtime scene,
In your own bud, your pleasure you conceal,
A niggardly act, wasting treasures like a fool.
  Show pity


