So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May hold the lease of thy heart.
But why should he live, when I am poor,
Withholding the life that to thee I do give?
For that purpose no man can hold
That love which in his bosom doth lie.
Therefore to him I give this my due,
And he, in like manner, this gift doth confide:
  I gave him thy love, and he hath my heart.
  Then to him I give this due and he in like manner accepts:
  For our old love was new and he was my heart.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
When that wish shall myself bring in surplus,
By willful heart or other crime.
I will give thee leave to repair
My own injury, though thou dost offend;
Myself be the lawful judge,
And to my content I'll give time's thought.
When all the world is done with this race,
Thou gav'st me time, and I have nothing to say
But what my mistress hath before her.
  She hath thee under her hand, and I am quite sure
  She hath thee under her finger, and will give that to me.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love's plea,
As love's lease thou hast no right to withhold,
And I, no lover can forbid,
That I may love thee in such sort.
If, I say, thou gav'st me thine eye,
My love may freely part,
From that which thy seeing confounds,
And I may part with my tongue,
To guard thy good name from all crooked.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort,
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in their view, nothing 'tis seen,
But shadows cast by thy tires.
How, then, can I, Love, in my blindness,
See that thou dost deceive me,
And to tell the truth I do not perceive?
Whence dost thou derive this blindness, that I may not see
When I, thy blind companion, behold the truth?
If it be from thy sourness that I see,
And sourly dost thy good report thence poor me;
  Then do I thence sourly number, and speak of thy deeds;
  For thy sake alone


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And that I, her husband, should boast
Of our ancient acquaintance.
Thus does my love indirectly lead me astray,
Seeking the shelter of my lady's wakened pride,
Where all the charm of her sweet name
Can seem but a faint imprint upon my brow,
And, all in all, I am astray.
  For she loves her husband so,
  And he her because she loves her alone.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike not with my brow but with my soul;
Let not my heart be at fault in this plight,
Nor thy secret love to blame me.
O, do not, when I have paid my debt,
Take my reparation thence;
That is, till I have paid thee tenfold.
Ten times more than I have suffered in love's wrong
Disdained by fortune's dearest spite;
Then must I taste of bitterness, and then straight ill
Consume the bitterness that my heart does pay.
  If ten wrongs be suffered, then ten wrongs did suffer
  And


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my concealed distress.
If I could write the beauty of your eyes,
The face of my lover, still, in my view,
Would look new, though my sight would show
The age of our shared years.
So should my sorrows, when I have recovered,
Ask me of thy beauty and I straight will halt,
To seek the reason behind thy break.
  For I cannot love thee in such a way,
  Unless thou dost conspire against my sake.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight.
For why should my seeing err,
Judging thy worth by their own fallow ground?
Or on my part should my sin be hidden,
For seeming unworthy of their love?
No, I am true and just,
Defying their every accusation,
A thousand perusal victories of my tenacity.
  In matters of faith, I find a truth concealed,
  In love, I find a thousand sighs.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Thy love is more blessed than my poor art.
For where is my love so holy, so deep,
That it fears no judgment of mine?
It knows my worth, and to thee
Thy love doth set my deeds in perspective.
So then my love is not so bad,
Although in itself it doth live;
It is marred, though it is sweet,
By sweet thoughts it hath corrupted.
  For what is my love but sweet,
  If that my deeds should make my love seem.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To find a second self, while she's away the child.
So you, in love's terror, chase not;
Your love, though chased, will never leave me,
For love's sweet memory grants you grace,
And chasing shadows will bring you no answer.
But if you find your heart's elusive quest,
Remember me, and come back home, my dear,
With open arms, a love you can't suppress,
