Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
To thee, my heart's most heinous crime.
The dawn has broken, and darkness descended,
Concealing thine face from view,
As mourning becomes one, as the lonely tire;
Yet, in this sorrow, thoughts of thee arise,
And, my heart, still unsullied, takes pride,
By seeking thee, my torment, it brings nigh.
  So, I beseech thee, friend, forget not to mourn,
  When longing has worn thee away, from sight.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to weep for me?
Even so, should my heart desire,
To share this sorrow with the world.
But you, O unkind, cannot claim
My friend's heart, for 'tis thine alone I bear.
I have lost thee, and now you both possess,
My heart, but not so much as my friend's.
  Yet, should my friend's heart find such pain,
  I'll bear that cross, but not with grief.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May have rights of me in thy heart.
That is why I have recourse to thee,
For still he will live, and I in him
Desire no improvement, for I can tell
His nature as I will him, and he in me.
Thou gav'st him for my love, to whom I am so poor,
That I in thy breast dost suffer no shame,
Though I do change poor him as thou dost him grow.
  Since then I have recourse to thee,
  For thy sweet-boned wrong hath made me take.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To leave thee alone, to manage
My affairs, with liberty and kind.
I will give her time, and place,
Where hours can nothing detain,
But work thy hours' demand, and her pleasure.
That she may not abuse me with false argument,
By reason of my love, she being so kind,
I will be her witness, and to show
That love hath no face, though it will live;
  For she hath no cunning wind,
  But a bare idea of what I will do.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love's appeal,
As love's appeal lies in that which I bring.
Want I not to die that thou mayst take?
No, love, be thou the very best of me,
Thou art much loved that I can know,
For there is nothing so blessed that doth impart
That to thy sweet face new love doth give.
  Thus much for love my love's appeal,
  Even as thou dost love me more, so much for thee.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in thy heart, a fool.
They know well the one eyed beauteous part,
The other the sweetest of all cheeks;
Yet in thy brain, both in hue and in form,
Thyself and love's image are mixed.
So thy eyes, which see and grasp,
But in thine own heart, a deceiver;
For thyself and love's true delight,
In thine own eyes, their lie is writ.
  So thou, thyself so bad, and in thy breast such venom,
  Shall live, and that's by thy


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And therefore I believe her false tongue,
To be so is her proud entwined.
Though I should prove her wrong I never did,
Though she should do me wrong I would,
And in that she lies so.
But why should I believe her false tongue,
When I know her lies and see the truth in it?
Because I love her so, and therefore must she tell it,
And her seeing lies does more truth than my hearing?
  Lascivious tongue, when I hear her speak,
  Deserves no praise, for I only praise her.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Pluck not my sharp thought from my woe,
And trouble not my mind, arising from wrong.
O, do not, when my life hath been decayed,
Sland me with sorrow's decree,
When other griefs have done their spite,
But in thy heart's own ruin stand,
The loathsome sire of sorrow, who hath no son.
  Thy right is so great, to me,
  That I ought not to love thee even when I am dead.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my hurt, though I did not intend
To bring it home.
If I could speak of love's sweet embrace,
The sweetness it lends, the tender care
It takes, would make my tongue bitter,
And words sullied with sorrow convey
The depth of my hidden woe.
O, what a gift it is to learn to write
Thy worth, thy truth, and thy love's own game,
To cherish what thou dost forbid,
Though I should push thee far, and push thee explain.
