In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For why dost thou not then confess,
"This loving conceit of mine,
Which in thy breast doth live, as thine in me?
It is my love, so far as mine eyes are concerned,
That doth love engraft the frown of ill health."
"Indeed, be it so, 'tis true," saith my love,
"That in thy breast doth love store,
As thine eyes would that store to breed."
  "Indeed, be it so, 'tis true," saith my


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And that thy love hath taught her to refashion
The hideous into angels, fair to view,
Resembling thee, my dearest, ever true,
Thy sweetheart's most precious treasure, lovingly lent.
If thou repent, let me love thee again,
Whose sins I've not borne, but thine own,
By the grace of thy remorse, I'm assured.
  Seek'st thou to claim what thou dost withhold?
  By thine own actions, thou shalt be repaid in kind, be told.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To meet the cry of the unfathered race.
So you, in love's anxiety, abandon me,
As I, in love's grasp, grasp,
My precious one, with urgency, runs towards you,
Leaving me to languish, unbent.
But why does she not chase me,
And in her shame, let my name rest?
The reason lies in my love, too kind,
For in her shame, I learn to write,
And in my love, I find a purpose,
  To make her proud, and to give her life.


