Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In solemn knell designs on thy head.
The beauty of thy face, the envy of all,
Can make my heart woe, and thou mourners be none,
For all hearts are marred, and I in thy,
Stained with guilt, and all things lovely,
Desiring nothing so dear, that thou thyself mayst die.
  Look upon thy own condition, and there see a face
  That leaves my heart in despair, and thou in Slyther shouldst look.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And still more to torment me add,
That torment my soul and blood even more?
I tell thee, sweet heart, if thou wilt but listen
And understand the weight of all my complaining,
I'll bear the torment no more,
When other wailing fits have done their part.
I'll bear the strain, the punishment, the fight,
If it brings me to a purer realm.
  But do not so. Although I bide, weigh not thy plight
  But what thou dost bring forth, that weigh thee.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May stay your vassalage longer than is necessary.
That's why I have recourse to thee,
For self-protection's worth is such,
And I am not thoughtless, knowing thy will,
To let others claim what I cannot.
Therefore, in him I have no recourse,
And in him thy riches are laid
And he, in so much, can afford
To lose me, who so rich is he.
  To give or to lose is what is 't born of.
  So, thou self-willed begger; others beaters too.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And many a richpleasure mine eye may be
Full expiate of that purpose which thou deserv'st.
That use is not forbidden usury
Which happies those that pay the willing loan;
That's for thyself to breed another thee,
Or ten times happier, be it ten for one.
Ten times thyself were happier than thou art
If ten of thine ten times refigured thee;
Then what could death do if thou shouldst depart,
Leaving thee living in posterity?
  Be not self-willed, for thou art much too fair
 


