Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in their sight, thine own eyes are deceived.
Why should they call it beauty, which they see so wrong,
When it is but thy own self which they in scorn see?
Or on thy part do they ensnare me in their snare,
To look on thee through the looking glass,
Which shows thee how thy beauty lies?
  Yet be most base of heart that love knows
  To shun thyself in thy vile deeds.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I should be the best of friends her manners are.
And yet, love knows, I am as I am;
To believe her false I might be proved
Though I should lose all my rights.
And yet, love, believe me most blessed are
When she loves me, and none can then bar
My right to love her whom she doth entice.
  Lascivious she is she whose tongue doth invent
  But what I most enjoy I view in quiet hours.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my love be called idolatry,
