Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Replacing the loathsome with gentle pity.
As on the finger of a throned queen
The basest jewel will be well esteemed,
So are those lips that pity the least;
And for they pity the least, are crowned
With sweet virtue, becoming tongue-tied.
In me thou dost pity those whose hearts are most cruel,
Though I, most cruel, should be praised.
  For I smile at thy pity, and then do evil
  More harshly than thou wouldst prove me guilty.


