In this love I'll contemplate duty's face,
For thou art willing, and I am bound,
To do what is just, whatever doth thy boon lay.
  Such is my love, to thee I so belong,
  That, for thy love, I'll bear the love's cross.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And, sick of their sight, cry out, wanting more.
Are you to my mind, which makes such lament
As that 'twixt poverty and beauty blooms doth lie,
To let such flowers come and go as they please?
O, be the tenth fool, ten times more blest be thy show,
Than when old Thine eyes have met new mine eyes.
Then mayst thou fool them worse than I;
  And yet mayst love them better than they know.
  For who's so blind that cannot see that thou dost deceive?


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And therefore believe in my falsehoods,
Though I did not vow to love her that way.
Lascivious youth! But what virtue do I see in me,
That I should in my blindness boast
Of faults committed before my eyes?
When all my virtues bow to her,
Enshrine her virtue in my deeds,
And do not vex me with lingering doubt.
  For I have sworn by your love, and now to you,
  Desire nothing more than this, and her love in me.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Spare me the disgrace of seeming rude,
If ever my worth should make my praise seemarse.
O, do not, when my injury is done,
Sneak in upon the heels of conquered woe;
Grant not a tempest's night a stormy morn,
To stretch the hand of ruin further torn.
If thou wilt leave me, do not leave me last,
When other petty griefs have done their spite,
But in the onset come; so shall I taste
At first the very worst of fortune's might;
