Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my sorrows.
If thou dost teach me to be harsh, I might be undone,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now is not the time nor the place
To let my heart's most poignant thought deface
My heavy-heartedness with words so kind,
To make my speechless plea seem stronger than words' own might.
  If thou wilt teach me to be so harsh,
  I'll pay the toll; but not till I am paid.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For if the beauty of thy face were my heart's desire,
It might be reborn and multiplied,
As a thousand flowers, each true and lone,
Could stand before the eyes of kings in shade,
Their flowers in your heart, no falsehood could ever boke.
  But my eyes, being windows, see through falsehoods so fine,
  And so my heart, in love's array, resists the judgment.


