Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in tattered shrouds of mourning,
Which I shall not honor till I see
Those chopped pieces of thy deceased lover,
Who for thy sake didst forsake all in me,
And for this theft hath my heart sorrowed;
Thou art much too fair to be death's conquest,
And to save me from such woe was 'gainst me,
Making me wait in lines ten days' length,
That I might see thee in the act of dying,
While other mourners were not so slow in their moan.
  This sorrow which I think most grievous is


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And still more to torment fill
The vacant hours with thought's endless dulling sway?
My heart, bewitched by this torment,
Cries out for joy, no answer finds the plea.
I tell my friend to bear this friend's pain,
While I, in my grief, try to drown out
The truer sounds of love's unacknowledged call.
  "If he breaks free," I whisper, "he'll find his peace,
  In love, where he belongs."


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May benefit by thy part of the love speech.
That's why I have frequent recourse to thee,
To find out shames and idle hours in thee,
The scope and tenor of thy jealousy.
Now I have proved thee true, and he false
Under oath, to thine adverse party;
So I have thee bound to suffer no delay,
Unless this general guilt of mine increase:
  He that pays the debt, gives thee the lion's share,
  And thou in him remains the gentle riser.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
When ten times happier subjects of thy pleasure
Thou dost under happier subjects be multiplied.
Ten times thy love hath more than enough content
To make a free gift of many, of many,
And to those that receive thee only add the rest.
Then the offender may be ten times thy love,
And thou in him that enjoys her pleasure, ten times happy guests
Sland'ring many, but few offenders find.
  If ten be guilty of none, that many shall be guilty,
  And thou in them all shall be guilty, thrice.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love's appeal,
Though not so great as thy love's own might,
Still, love's love, love's hope, hold
That thou in love mayst trust, though thou dost not tell.
Let those who love thee promise those tears,
Whose own loves are thine, but whom I love not,
Let those tears be thine, and mine own dear love confess,
And then thou in love mayst know, I tell.
  This be thy love, to whom I so love,
  And so far for love my love shall thine


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in their blindness perceive the wrong
Of what they ought to be most zealous in.
Why should my love's sight be marred
By seeing farther than is perceived,
Or that my love's sight should be shaken
To find out where I have fallen short in all I do?
Neither are my eyes thine to see, nor my love's
To blame, though they themselves be deceived;
But yet, Love, be thou the tenth fool in the world,
Whilst my love's eyes outshine the rest in all worth.
  For thou ten times more blessed dost thy


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though not acquainted with truth, yet I did love her,
And found her truth so strongly dyed.
So I believe her pretense, although she knows it is false,
And in my love's truth does lay her roots;
And in this love is her artistry concealed,
Which I dislike in others' eyes.
But when I perceive their sighs and groans,
Their eyes, not my love's, nor my love's delight,
At such moments their eyes believe love's disarray
And in their despair look upon truth with disdain.
  So I hold her love with great suspicion


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak not my soul to justify the crime,
But do it, truly, and in my sight;
Grant me the pardon: my love well knows
Her husband hath strayed from her sight,
And she, in ignorance, does bring
This injury upon her honor.
But do not so. I forgive thy trespass, gentle thief,
Though thou steal thee all my poverty;
And yet love knows it is a greater grief
To bear love's wrong than hate's known injury.
  Lascivious grace, in whom all ill well shows,
  Kill me with spites, yet


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my sorrow.
If I could write the depth of love's sigh,
It might bring me to light, and thus atone,
For I would lose my mind, and in my madness,
Speak not of love's sweet farewell.
So should I find favor in thy woe,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
  For if I should fall into despair, I'd lose my mind,
  And in my madness,


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For how can I then be false in love,
When I have sworn by thy fair face,
And bound my deepest love to testify?
No, I love thee in such sort
As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report;
And thou in this loving-kindness dost them disgrace kill.
  For thou hast sworn to me, false heart, unwise,
  To love thee as thou dost them adore thee.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And that she loves those whom I disown.
For thou art thyself so possessed
That 'gainst myself thou stick'st not to conspire,
Seeking that beauteous roof to ruinate
Which to repair should be thy chief desire.
But that she loves those whom I do hate,
Suff'ring thy soul, as thine own flesh would prove,
By thy own treachery growing strong,
Thy own sweet self corrupt'st, more blest than she.
  Kill her, yet still I will not confess,
  Lest my love be unjust


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To wherever she pleases, swift-moving wind
Indulges her in the tender nurture of a bird.
With love so fervent, she scratches her own spot,
A Monster, Ruthless, Unyielding,
To Points, where honor fades away.
Her love, so kind, bends to all,
Like a weathered sage, who gazes at his scion's might,
Finding solace in joy, even for the dead.
  But, ah, I cannot claim such solace,
  For Love's own joy bends to Time's cruel hand.


