Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, as mourners in velvet attire.
The dawn has broken, and the bright morning bright,
Obscured by clouds, the sun, and torrent showers,
One glorious light to all who gazed on,
Yet unseen, unheard, or unkind,
The saddest sight in the midst of day,
When innocence defies the hardest gaze.
  Yet, in my heart's deepest lament,
  My sorrows become my glory, as I before.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
I tell the truth, and yet thou dost lie,
To make my heart even more wretched than it is.
And for thy sake, I'll pen even more lies,
That I may atone for thy transgression.
Such lies are sweetest penance serves
To make my friend's heart more wretched,
And thou, in receiving, do public show
Thy bad, my good allow?
  By self-example, thus, I'll be proved untrue,
  And lose thee, my sweet tormentor.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May stay your breeding;
But he will have no such right, being your subject,
For you are willing him to be your mate.
I will even leave him to my lawful rage
If it may bring him to a standstill,
As I shall myself be a taker of time,
Nor him, a fit subject to be your woll.
  But if he escapes and I am confined in thee,
  He has no plea, no defense, but I must pay the toll.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To leave thee but the gracious issue.
I will not change my mind, though thou change'st
My thoughts, still the love of my heart remains.
That my will does matter, is most evident,
When I consider what is best, that best I add.
Thou canst not vex me with change,
Since that my change is effected by thee.
I will not reproach thy will, though thou desire,
Although thou change'st, the love of my heart remains.
  For thee, dear heart, I leave the game,
  To be the better they, that


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
If thou repent, though I have left thee,
My love well knows it is wrong,
To love her so churlishly, even after my decease.
But do not so. I love her as thou dost me,
And she, in so great worth, will be my wits,
For I cannot churl her worth with words,
Nor canst thou by my deeds persuade me of love's decease.
  Lascivious grace, in whom all vice is dressed,
  Kill me with spites, yet we must not be foes.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in their view, nothing 'tis so.
For why should my sight be false, seeing so bright,
When it knows not the worth of love's dear name?
Or on thy soft cheek the blush of lips
Is crowned with honeyed tears,
Which slide on thy tongue and hold a date?
No, it is thee, who calls the shots,
In thy blind eyes, the silent sonnets fly.
  So thou tell'st, and yet they cannot see,
  The joy nor the hurt they feel when thou thine are.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows my prime has come and gone;
And I for fear of trust forget to say
The perfect ceremony of love's rite,
And in my madness of love's telling stand,
When I, married to her, forget to say
My vows thrice over again,
And to her credit do grace my words utter,
But first of all love, to thee I add,
  To give her thanks for my speaking of love,
  And to thee I also mortgage my tongue,


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Spare me the wretch's knife which thou deserv'st,
Though in my bosom thou stick'st not.
O, do not, when I am gone, leave me
To suffer the worst of wrongs to see;
Give not a windy night a rainy morrow,
To linger out a purposed overthrow.
If thou wilt leave me, do not leave me last,
But in the very place where thou wilt be gone,
  So shall I live, and more, by thy side.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my sorrows.
If I could write the depth of your love
And phrase each thought in love's own gentle way,
The wounds I give myself would grow too much,
And words convey the depth of my woe.
Oh, should I write of your heart's delight,
It would be too cruel; yet love knows
Thou dost love those whom it insults.
  Thus, I'll stand by thee, though they sometimes hurt;
  For I cannot love one for whom they feel no need.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight.
For why should I magnify thy worth,
When I am stained with errors of heart?
Or on thy failings, magnify my offense,
Projecting flaws upon my given grace?
No, I am true, and they, in error,
Judge me through their twisted vision.
In faith, I cannot love thee half so ill,
Unless they for their wrongs alter reality.
  For I cannot love thee half as ill,
  Until they for their crime have altered their judgment.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And that is the reason why mine eye
Reports seeing a different sort of face from thee.
So far for love my love's plea, gentle;
Tomorrow for love my love disowns,
And in this change is my guilt laid.
But why should my love's plea be so far?
It is thy fair aspect, thy sweetness so rare,
That calls my love to witness, two souls entwined in love.
  So far for love my love, to witness compel,
  Then must I die, to love suspect, guilty one.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Pardoning the place where it grew.
But you, enamored of your bright own eyes,
Feed your light's blaze with self-made sustenance,
Causing famine amidst the land's surplus supplies,
An adversary unto your own self, too harsh in recompense.
You, now the world's adorning jewel,
And lone herald of the gaudy spring,
In your own bud, your pleasure you conceal,
A niggardly act, wasting treasures like a fool.
