Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my heart's complaint.
If thou wilt listen, dear heart, and understand,
My voice-tellings are but gentle;
They bear but my sorrows' face,
And words are but sweetly advices.
If thou wilt listen, dear heart, and understand,
My mind's sophisticated skill will teach
A lesson learned in suffering's bitter school.
  Thus, thou minion of my wailing; a true soul
