In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight.
For why should my sensual vision adore
A counterfeit of thy fair aspect,
When in my dulness it encounters
The truth of thy form, preserved and sound?
My ears find no delight in thy voice's symphony,
Nor do gentle emotions yearn for the lowly touch;
Nor taste or scent, seek to indulge in the sensual spree,
In any lustful feast with thy devoted thrall.
  Only my suffering, thus far, I indulge,
  For thy sweet virtue sees my joy fall short.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
