Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And therefore, blindly, they misconstrue
The truth they see, and judge its worth by thy part.
They misconstrue what beauty means,
And in their blindness, they see the wrong;
But why their eyes should perceive the wrong,
When it knows what beauty is meant by thee?
For thee and for their sake, I bring this fight,
To prove thee false, and to blind them.
  If thou be true, and they be false,
  In darkness, they see the truth, and they judge it worth.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I, in all truth, believe most preposterously.
And so shall I believe her false tongue,
When I think on truth, not on beauty's part,
Nor on thy worth, what worth in me doth she regard,
When in thy right I perceive thy merit;
And, most blessed creation, in thy sweet grace,
I do cherish her love, though she loves none.
  For thee, I hold her love with equal zest,
  As thine when thou art mine, so is my love's due.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Dissuade not my affections from thy part,
Although in my soul thou desireth no more.
If I should do anything ill, do not my
Lascivious heart blame thee, but in my seeing
Stained scarlet with my own wicked deeds;
And yet, do not my sin persuade me
That I do anything sinful.
  For I have sworn by thine unwavering love,
