Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own dear-hearted love.
If I could write the depth of soul
Which in thy bosom dwells,
The world would cry, "This poor creature loved not enough!"
My love, so deep, would be dumb,
Speaking of thy worth, when in thee it shines;
But that thou mayst abuse me in some way,
That I may know thy worth, and thy dear-doing
May make my sorrows seem stronger than they are.
  For I must not love thee more, when I see
