Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Do I not think of thee when I remove
My thoughts from self, to serve but thee,
And weep anew the pain of loss I've suffered?
O, let me endure, the pangs of waiting game,
The captive's distance, in your liberty's cage.
Wander where thou wishest, thy rule so vast
That thou mayest choose, thy time's own domain.
  I'll bear the strain, the punishment thine,
  If thou wilt not release my friend, allow my heart to weep.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May stay your right even so as mine own right is.
Look what is best, that best I wish in him.
Mine own dear worth is such, as he is,
As I, when I lose him, best I wish him well;
So, if he live and I be cast away,
The loss I must bear is my love's gain.
  Then him for my love will pay the ransom,
  Since what I most wish I have not.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say within thine own deep-sunken eyes
Thy cherished eyes will bear, when I am gone,
A mind full of wrinkles, when I am gone,
But that thou mayst from me, sometime stay
And look upon my unworthy art;
From hence, when thou reviewest this,
When I, perhaps, am one of thine,
Speak not my name, though I was thine,
For I am poor, and thou art too kind;
Roam where thou desireth, thy right so immense
As mine by thee and for thee depends.
 


