Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet thou fool'st them into thinking it hath departed.
For when they have looked and sworn,
Their brains have no part in love's design.
Yet, Love, if you'll but cease,
Your eyes, my dear, can yet be of service,
Making bright every darkest night a glorious morning.
  Look what virtue in me you dost blot,
  That, when my soul is dead and buried, thou shalt see.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And therefore I will not be dumb,
When she prays that I am her saucy bark.
Because she loves me, I will not contradict
That she loves nothing, being false,
Nor believe her false tongue to be true,
When I am acquainted with their disgrace.
I will not envy those lips that invent
Sincerity, beauty's invention,
But in my will, all vice is concealed,
Unless you would set everything right.
  She loves me, therefore I will believe her,
  For I must ne'er love her, though she knows it well.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not my sorrow be named as a gainer,
Nor my beloved treated as a begger.
Grant me the right to love thee in such sort
As thou dost kindly lend, knowing my will;
In other words, give me the freedom
To choose what is most my own.
Be not prejudiced against my will;
On thy part, do I predetermine,
And on thy part, predetermine what is best.
  I do love thee, and therefore must thou love me;
  To thee I do render, not thee to know.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my own feeling.
If I could school thee in the art of banter, better still,
