In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For why should my heart believe in scattered dreams,
When it knows well the world's shared, single space?
Or should my eyes, which see, declare the opposite,
To grace falsehood with a lovely face?
My heart does believe, and eyes, which see, declare
The opposite, in falsehood's battle.
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have strayed,
  And now to falsehood's contagion they've succumbed.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Finding thy own worth most so, being false to me.
For how can I love thee but by thy fair grace,
And for that love where is my deserving?
The reason for this love being so is wanting,
And so my plea is that thou in thy crime
Stained thine own soul, more blessed than mine own.
  For thee, I must be false, and thine own dear,
  For I must be false, and thine own dear.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the place where it desires to be found,
So I, kept unvaried, adjust my circumstance
To meet the changing whims of fortune's voice.
Ah, might you but pause, and comprehend,
The spell of your smile, the charm of your youth,
That lures us to the brink, extends the trend
Of truth we know, and shatters verity's tooth:
  Hers, by your beauty, tempting her to stray,
  Thine, by your beauty, false, in disarray.


