Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in thy sensual display,
Thy naked self appear'st in tattered rank.
How, then, can they be true in their judgment,
When thou thyself deceivest them, making them blind?
O, blame not thyself, be it ill or well.
Thou art blamed, and so shouldst be thee,
For thyself murd'rous deeds are crowned
And lead thee captive to thy vile heart.
  For thee and I are so well acquainted
  That when thou thyself deceivest, 't even we know it.


