In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For why should my eyes favor thy show,
When I perceive thy worth the greater?
Or should my heart believe in scattered dreams,
To honor what it knows is false, in slumber's hold?
No, I am as I am, and they, in their spite,
Measure my worth by their own fallow ground.
  In truth and right, my love equals
  Their own defeat, in truth and right.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Which condition mine own soul doth in approve.
For how can love be true if it feigns
Its own sweet pleasure, masked by ill?
Or can my joy be mine unless I kill
The spirit of love with passion's fire?
No, neither canst thou, love being dead,
