If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For there can live no hatred in thine eye;
Therefore in that I cannot know thy pleasure.
In many's looks, the false heart's history
Is writ in moods and frowns and wrinkles strange.
But heaven in thy creation did decree
That in thy face sweet love should ever dwell;
Whate'er thy thoughts or thy heart's workings be,
Thy looks should nothing thence but sweetness tell.
  How like Eve's apple doth thy beauty grow,
  If thy sweet virtue answer not thy show.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that which they do not like is but poor art
And simple truth, to them it doth belong.
Yet in their blindness they mistake for virtue
And in their goodness they look into the wrong;
For why should I see thy face, when I see thee
Through my glass, when I no clearer see
The fair and lovely imperfections in thy make?
  My eyes, being windows, see the best;
  But thy outward thus doth appear to my eyes.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I, her chief, do profess my time
To be spent in some craft or other.
But then, when in her falsehood I perceive
Her truth concealed, I believe her again,
And this time almost o'ermatches her pretense;
For I have sworn by her virtuous right
And by thy love, thy truth, thy faithfulness so rare;
But when I perceive thy double falsehood,
  I begin to doubt whether I ever saw
  The face of love.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with both thy strokes and me with my mind.
Grant me the right to love thee in such sort
As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report;
Against this demand of mine, bring in argument
That I may know thy good name from thy name;
Against this cross of mine, bring in proof
That I am thy true love.
O, prove me not by my love but by my report;
Against this cross of mine, bring in proof
That I am thee, and thou in me do lie.
  Kill me with spites, yet we must not be foes;


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own worth, more precious as it's sought by thee.
If I could write the sweetness of thy heart
And in fresh numbers number all thy graces,
The age to come would say "This poet lies;
Such heavenly touches ne'er touched earthly faces."
So should my papers, yellowed with their age,
Be scorned, like old men of less truth than tongue,
And thy true rights be termed a poet's rage
And stretched meter of an antique song.
  But were some child of yours alive that time,
