In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For thou art the sun, whose brilliance shines afar,
Whose worth the world cannot boast;
The moon, when she hath her eclipse endured,
Relinquishes her claim to be the sun;
And thou, in thy fading, dost illuminate
The darkness with thy burning brand.
  Let others sing the praises they desire;
  I'll celebrate the love that I inspire.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Proving thy virtue by thy own fallow ground.
For there can live no hatred in me,
Unless it be this, that I do adore:
That is, to love those whom thou dost hate,
And in thy own bosom dost store.
Thou canst not love me, being spiteful,
Unless thou be bound to my deeds,
Which is to say, loving those whom I am above.
  Then, be assured, I am well aware
  That thou in my love doeth spite, though thou dost hate.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the place where it was caught,
Pushing the spoil of her delight
To every corner, where it shall find a rest.
But thou, enthralled, remaineth not so.
For still, as the baby doth sing,
Like a weathered sage, untouched by years,
Thou dost nurture still, even though foes may claim
Victory in love's sweet scrabbling.
  Still, love for love's sake shall I strive,
  For love's sweet reward is to be found.


