Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in thy picture do they perceive
That thou, their eyes, are windows to my heart.
How can they, my dear, find out
That thou, their windows, dost thou in them lie?
It is thy outward part, the visible part,
Which they view, and measure by thy show;
Thy inward love, in their measure,
Is thy love, and thence they measure again.
  Then, beauteous heart, find out
  That thy show is not thy inward love.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And therefore I believe her false words more true.
But why should I believe her false tongue,
When I hear her say things that I think false?
My love is fortified, though she is made of stone,
By her affability and my belief;
To break down her wall I would make her tear,
And by adding to her burden I increase her waste.
  But why should I trust her but for my love,
  When I see her falsehoods swarms everywhere?


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Spare me the repetition of offenses that I bring.
Grant me the right to love thee more nearly
Than thou art able, being infatuated, to compel;
Against myself I will challenge,
And prove thee virtuous, though thou art forsworn.
With mine own weakness being best acquainted,
Upon thy part I can set down a story
Of faults concealed wherein I am attainted,
