Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in your sight, love, they cannot know
The depth of your love, the tender age
Which makes them yearn for your embrace.
O, what a weakness dost thou give
That on thy soft cheek dost thou conceal the blot
Of thy own worth, so great!
Let those eyes that are thine eyes speak,
Making right what right there is,
And thy soft cheek to acknowledge their right.
  But if they err, be they lost, or they err,
  By thy softness they've been stained, and now thou art to blame.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And therefore believe me false of love's deceit.
But why should I believe her false tongue,
When I see her falsehoods in action?
My love knows well my worth is true,
And thus to lie I must be false of heart;
And she, in this, can have her cake
To make me tongue-tied speaking of love's delight.
  And yet, by heaven, I think my love true;
  For she who loves me most deceives me.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak not my name, though I should turn away,
But do thy worst to make me bend my ear.
For I cannot love thee more unjustly
If thou dost first abuse me with false surmise.
What virtue do I see in me, so fine,
That I should in my weakness seek redress?
When all my virtues bow to thy defect,
Enslaved by the power of thy gaze anew.
  Yet, love, persist in loving me, for I know
  The truth: you love those whom I cannot.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own worth, more than appetite's quick fire.
If I could write the beauty of thy face,
The most precious gem in the crowd,
It would lose its worth, and be new,
A loan that grows with pleasure, outweighs
The weight of fleeting life.
So should my passion be kindled by thy worth,
Though not to love, yet, love, to thee,
As thine own heart would show, by thine own actions.
  For thine own heart is boundless, and thus,
  Shall I not be too harsh in punishing it.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For thou art all the better part of me,
Thy inward worth I cannot measure,
While they, in thee, find their frowns met.
Thou art more fair than their imaginations can show,
Richer than wealth, prouder than garments' cost,
Or gates of hearts, more blessed than fickle fortune's voice.
  For thou art my all the world, and they my dear thee,
  In heaven's eyes, thou art fair as any heaven's air.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And that too thou mayst choose, be it fair or poor.
For to thy own soul I give scope,
Thy own love, to thy soul's desire, convert.
Then can I love thee in comparison with men,
Who for themselves alone are not so free?
For to thy own soul I give scope,
And to thee I give life, which is thine and thine alone.
  Take heed, dear heart, of this large privilege;
  The hardest knife ill used doth lose his edge.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To keep her beauty ever near.
But thou, love's true beauty, keep'st
Slowly maturing, and becoming ever so near,
More blessed than any mother's child,
Though not so blessed as those gold candles fixed
In stalls of stalls, hanging low.
So thou, stickler for beauty's sake, wilt stay
Slowly maturing, and let its potential brighten;
  The time is ripe, and the time is thine.


