So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May stay your right of so great worth;
But, be assured, he is as poor as can be
From what I have sworn him to hold,
And that I must by laws devise
Do what I will, assured of thy sweet favor.
O, what a boon it is to know thy will,
To make him suffer, knowing him well,
And to lose him, not knowing him well,
But for thy sake, which I will do,
  To prove thee virtuous, though he be false.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say within thine own deep-sunken eyes
Thy husbandry hath all thy parts in hand,
And he that calls on thee, let him bring forth
A couple of shillings of merchandise, of worth,
That on thy head I can set down a story
To show thee how thy beauty graces me.
O, what a mansion have I got thee to keep,
That, each for himself, thou mayst live in it!
  But I must not detain thee from thy pleasure,
  Since that pleasure my liberty depends.


