Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in their judgment, measure by thy sight,
Thy outward grace is but half my dear state.
Why should they say that I love thee more,
When I am blind, and perceive thy defect?
Or on thy part, are they to judge,
Since thy love is thine, and what I love else?
Thou canst not be true, being blind,
To lie against the truth, to favor thyself.
  Thus dost thou fool them, and me,
  For thy sake alone, and for their sake much blest they be.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And therefore with great caution refrain from telling me so.
But since she knows my prime has come and gone,
And all those doubts which I had before
Disarmed by my love's reasons,
Now I am proved wrong in all they said;
And, for they looked but with divining eyes,
I straight prove them wrong in this, too.
  For I have sworn by her love, her truth,
  To trust her not to tell me anything untrue.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak not my name, yet I am forgotten,
And in thy reproof repay me forth of thee.
O, do not, when I am gone, bring
My life's companion to this sorrow,
Who wert not married to thee but for a time,
And who wert not ill, although thy love was.
If I lose thee, my life is forfeit,
And my love hath lost both honor and guilt;
