In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For why should my senses complain that I do not see
The truth they adore, when I perceive their recklessness?
Or should my ears complain that I do not hear
The truth they declare, when I perceive their swerve?
No, I do not love thee with suspicion,
But love thee with sincere esteem,
For thy sake my eyes and heart believe it owes,
And with a thousand other frailties, I am still in this plight.
  If my soul fears to trust in thy eyes,
  I have no remedy.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Proving thy worth by thy own fallow ground.
Then may I love thee more, believing in thine eyes,
Thy sweet heart's workings bewitch me,
For why should I love thee that way,
When I know thy soul is base and move?
My love is thine, and my reason fears
That thy love may be false, and thereby my sin is found.
  O, nothing in me loves thee that doth stand
  But thee, and thy love, my reason knows.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To seek a haven more suitable for mother's needs.
But I, enamored of speed, abandon
The chase, and instead let my sighs subside,
My heart, once full of your unmatched embrace,
Grew weary of the chase, and swiftly flew
To the sanctuary of my heart's content,
Where joy subsides, and the sweet scent of rest
Blooms like daisy's face, adorning each lull.
  But flowers distilled, though met with winter's might,
  Lose but their guise; their essence still has flower.


