Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
I tell thee, sweetheart, if thou wilt not share,
I'll bear the cost; but not find the killer taste
Of loss that my friend will bear.
Then can I seek the richness of thy love
That thou dost withhold from myself;
Thus, I shall not be ripped apart,
While others weep for thee and me.
  But if thou wilt leave me, I'll bear the pain;
  My friend will wail for my sake, and I'll bear that loss.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May stay your treasure and make no complaint.
That is the only plea in my favor
That I can bring against him, and he against me,
For I am bound to him, and he against me,
Under the debt that I do not think well.
Such is the art of plea, wherein I am so imprudent,
That I do not plea for his love, which is so dear,
But for my own desert, which I do not think well.
  Therefore, like a usurer, I will pay the defendant's cost,
  If he brings me to court,


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say within thine own deep-sunken eyes
