Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in thy lusty sight, they misconstrue.
They see the truth in thy intent,
And judge it fair, lacking in thy sweet skill;
Yet, Love, what good is beauty, that it fears no blot?
It knows thy face hath been spurned,
A thief in thy soft heart's frame.
Thou false, blind Guide, Love, what dost thou steal
To make thyself blind, and in thy sleep?
  By thine own fallow ground, thou hast much deceived them,
  And thine own heart is most guilty.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And therefore believe her false tongue,
To keep alive the notion of my youth,
Unsuspecting of life's deceitful arts.
Thus, she thinks me naive, unknowing still,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime,
I play along, her falsehoods I endure,
And in this act, truth on both sides hides.
  But why does she not admit her wrong?
  My love knows her lies well, and abhors the deceit.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Spare me the repetition of thy rite,
When in myself thou no form of thee dost appear.
Grant me the right to love thee in regard
As I will thee, without thy knowledge;
Dost thou not wish for my love to be known
Within thine own heart, where thou dost keep it?
O, no; yet, love, do thy worst,
For I cannot love thee but by thy commanding.
  Still, do thy worst, for I cannot love thee
  If thou dost thy worst, to do my worst.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my sorrows.
If I could write the depth of love's sigh,
It would not be so poorly done, nor bear
The mark of sorrow I should not ne'er feel.
If I could write the truth of love's plea,
It would not be so poorly done, nor bear
The disgrace of a lie uttered.
  For a true heart understands what words can't say,
  And when I, a poet, try to lend ear.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
