Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in tattered shrouds, like mourners in a dream.
The dawn has broken, and twilight's hue is gone,
In heaven's broad expanse, no birds' songs can be heard,
Nor dimly radiant do stars twinkle in the west.
My heart, once filled with hope, is at its core,
Crying in vain, ignored, reviled,
By those who've sworn to thine unyielding faith.
  Yet, in thine own gaze, love's brightness I see,
  A treasure that atones for thy transgression.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to weep for me?
It wrests me from myself, your tyrant hold
