So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May benefit by thy part only.
For, having thee as my pledge,
I must not be so slow in accepting,
Although he hath no tie to me, being so bold,
Since he can have thee as a prize,
And I by this will be slow in obeying,
Cheating myself, for I have sworn
Thy right, to him my prize, and will pay
The cost in both my loss and in my guilt.
  But here's the joy: he loves me so,
  That I, in love lose him, and him for my love.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To say within thine own deep-sunken eyes
Thy self-love and self-love's great merit
Disdains the dust and injury of this vast race.
Thou art thyself so possessed
That 'gainst thyself thou stick'st not to yield,
Seeking that beauteous roof to ruinate
Which to repair should be thy chief desire.
  But that thou mayst abuse me, I know,
  And haply of my wailing will do it wrong.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Roses may be thorny, but thy love's are red,
Whose whispers whisper tales of love that lasted,
Of happier days, blacker night,
Lighter days, more sweet autumn's breath.
So shall I live, though thou art false, love's lease
On thy sweet emotions prove more short than thy hate's battle.
Thou art much too fair for thine own face,
