In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
For if the painter's art should find favor in my feature,
The likenesses of my beloved should in my verse thrive,
And words to be their advocate in my cause they would bring.
But my purpose I do not adore,
For my eyes in thee find but strife and moan;
My heart, that doth in my verse find advocate,
A lawful plea against thy fair imperfections.
  So thou fool me with thy sweet words, yet I know it not;
  For thou art thy fair imperfection's advocate.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Or at least so far as my love is concerned.
For how can my love be true, when thou art my foe
And husbandry my love against myself?
Against myself can my soul reign,
And for thy sake against myself do I contend.
I do forgive thy love, gentle thief,
Although thou steal thee away, loving witness.
Love knows it is a greater grief
To bear love's wrong than hate's known injury.
  Lascivious grace, in whom all ill well shows,
  Kill me with spites, yet we must not be foes.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To seek a haven stronger than my love's own fort.
So you, like a mother, leave me to my child,
Unwavering, constant, never shaken by a word,
A beacon burning bright, come night or day.
Unyielding, you lull my mind to sleep,
And let my heart to yours in harmony sway;
The serpent's bite, the storm's might,
Do not provoke me, let me love thee in such sort,
  As mutually secure, our love remains untamed.


