Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
And yet, wondrous lips, kept that way,
Revealing ten times more the sweetness than before,
More ripe with that harvest of reaper things
That summer's air had graced before.
Merely by those lips that so bad were
Speaking to me that day,
Were all my deeds despised, and yet were saved,
For Love's own touch saved that which I before had claimed.
  So that through those mouths she saved my soul,
  Which in that hour is everywhere.


Poor soul, the center of my sinful earth,
Pressed with these rebel powers that thee array,
Why dost thou pine within and suffer dearth,
While every private wife, in her jealousy,
Drains her husband's blood and gives thee dross?
She captures thee, my love, and keeps her hold
By the treachery of her own heart;
But why dost thou not flee, SIMILAR?
Grant me the power to love thee more nearly,
Though thou dost not wish for thy own face to show,
For nothing binds me, if thou wilt not forsake
My tender love, and let my sweet name fade?
  Make my memory as dear as thy own,
  And love that well which thou d


My love is as a fever, longing still
For that which longer nurseth the disease,
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill,
And that which discloses the roof of my heart's deceit.
The cure is this: that which doth soothe the heart,
Indulging the imprisoned tumour,
Sets you free from this world, and other fetters,
That you may roam and discover thy sweet throne.
  Within this verse, the truth is writ,
  And you shall live, and remain.


O me, what eyes hath love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight!
Or if they have, where is my judgment fled,
Which in thy shape and in thy gravity settle?
If not so much as mine own vision, 'tis nothing novel,
But that thou dost deceive the heart,
And that I, an accomplice, shall be
Asleep at thy waking breath.
O, what a temple for spies hath love built
Which inward eyes and outward sense perceive,
But that thou no form delivers to the soul,
For thou art all my secret, and I know it not.
  The cause of this inconstant love being looked on,
  Is love's invention, and therefore my praise.


Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot
My own identity, by thy side?
Who hates thee that I name as my ally?
On whom dost thou frown that I do adore?
Indeed, if thou dost scowl on me, I try
To wreak revenge with tears that flow no more.
What virtue do I see in me, so great,
That I should scorn to serve thee as is due?
When all my virtues bow to thy defect,
Enslaved by the power of thy gaze anew.
  Yet, love, persist in hatred, for I see
  The truth: you love those whom I cannot


O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
And paint deceit where truth should lay?
Whence cometh this corruption of the good,
That in thy vices I find charm,
In thy transgressions, I see beauty stood,
And in thy chaos, a soothing balm?
Who hath shown thee how to twist my heart,
To cherish more the more I see amiss?
Though I should push thee far, depart,
My heart still clings, with love grown' miss.
  If thy unworthiness hath wrought this love,
  More worthy am I to be loved above.


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
Lest you become entwined in my wrongs.
For, as you deceive me, I betray
My nobler self to my body's treachery.
My soul informs my flesh that it may strive
For victory in love; the flesh, now alive,
Rises at your name, its purpose to derive
From your affection, its reason to thrive.
  And thus, in pride, it serves, ever so near,
  To stand by you, to fall by your side, my dear.


In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn,
As tender nurse her babe from faring ill.
But why should I reproach thy double falsehood,
When I am stained with countless perjuries?
My vows, mere shadows, swiftly withered, hold
No weight compared to your deep-rooted ties.
For I have sworn by thine unwavering constancy,
By thy love, thy truth, thy faithfulness so rare;
Yet, in my madness, I have twisted reality,
And bound thee in the chains of my despair.
  For I have sworn thee true, false heart, unwise,
  To lie against the


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steeple,
Into a well nearby, where it sprang.
The waters kept him from the dawn's hour,
As passing rivers, they metamorphose,
More beauties than at first sight,
Transforming to other forms, more gracious still.
But when my lady's eyes, purest of heart,
Lifted the fire, which countless true hearts had fed,
The lover's torch burned bright, a thousandfold.
  Love's fire warms the waters, warms the lover,
  But water cannot quench the lover's ardors.


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep,
Had their vows but by night and day annulled;
One maiden fair, so chaste, left her home,
To take a vow of chastity her whole life,
But when her heart no longer stirred,
Her vow of chastity was broken, and she was stoned,
To death by her own traitorous hand.
Another maiden, much more chaste, had sworn,
"I will not chide the world, for I am pure;
But thou, my dear, shouldst know,
And do what thou wilt, swift-moving god;
  I will


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A babe in the bloom of youth, not well-versed.
Thus, she thinks me naive, unknowing still,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime,
I play along, her falsehoods I endure,
And in this act, truth on both sides hides.
But why does she not admit her wrong?
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So, in our faults, we find comfort in lies,
  And in our deceptions,


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
And my dearest dove is sweet Charity,
Whose every part does honor me.
But thou, my most worthy, art left
Unchanged, and ever so fair;
Thou whose very being is change'd,
Beggared of love, and life hath lost
All virtue, to love change'd.
I grant I never saw a heaven could hold,
Whose loving thoughts, but those of thee,
Apart, still cold and barren.
  Yet to thee I send this day, thinking on thee,
  For thy sweet love keeps me from winter's cold.


