Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sighs and tears, as compassion's ally.
All frailties now seem cruel,
And sorrows now seem cruel,
And sweets now seem strange,
And men seem to cry out for things gone by,
And look upon friends' countenance,
Who whisper tales of past unkindness untold.
Now I ponder on thee, and my dear heart,
For thou art fair as any mother's child,
Though not as stars, whose distant glow I see,
Thy love outshines their cold and distant cheer.
