So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may win;
With this loss, myself I find my heart's desire,
And losing him, my soul is full,
And both, in consent, will bear this pain;
I lose him, and now I find my heart's own loss,
Both bear this loss, and I lose both twain,
And both, in consent, are only as they share this grief.
  But, as on the brighter day, my heart is mended,
  And all is balm, of my love's bright light.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And yet, if he can write of me,
I must ne'er love him whom I dost hate.
He may pen his slander on me, yet I know it well,
And slander's breath cannot ever be so vile as it is now.
So if he could make me of thee, what could death do if he should choose,
Which bounteous gift he should bestow?
  Thou shouldst be thy good report, and he must be thy good too,
