When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And sometime false of love's promise;
Yet in her, love's semblance
Is but a gentle blush, a tender grace,
And a kinder truth compared to her bold.
I could not dare to scorn her, for I was her heart,
And she, in turn, was my reason;
But now my love is sworn, and I am to wait
At the beck of my sweet one who calls me.
  Thus, in love's embrace, I'll endure,
  For she who voices my needs must be my captain.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And I am in thy mind, as the worm is in the fire,
Unless thou blind me with a false view.
I do vow that I am not lame, poor, nor despised
Whilst that this world-without-end hour survey
By Time and outward form will show my true worth;
Yet fear not that my poor name sullen my heart,
For that I am hoary of thy love
And allure of thy disdain gives me room.
  This vow I do by Lawyer, and thou by Time prove,
  I will live ere that thou take me hence.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sorrow.
If I could speak of thee
Even in the heart's own book,
The injuries that were not done,
Would be my grief but make my tongue mad,
And words that I should want to say,
At the place where I am now.
If I could write the charm of thy love
And in fresh numbers number all the grace
I long require, I'd lose my mind,
And in my madness I might speak ill of thee.
  But be smart in thy tongue, for I learn
  To write of thee in good numbers.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And in my blindness, I do tie
My soul to the strength that my flesh doth grow.
My heart doth what it needs, and what it fears,
Weakened by a thousandfold, and I am only winning
What it fears to lose itself.
I do not love one for whom it fears to lose,
A fool, when all my world is bleak,
But all the world to which I do call home.
  If my flesh, whom I am in,
  Sinks down to death, I'll be still.


