Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
At my heart's own woe, by thy unseen hand.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Shall qualify for sympathy indeed,
For if my thoughts should gather 'round thee,
Thy heart's intended pity's power,
The mountain's humble crest, together,
Would be swept, and leave my remorse
To languish, unbent with guilt.
