Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sympathy, tearing my world apart.
The dawn has broken,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on fields of vibrant green,
Thyself, in its hands, some sacred treasure,
In this plight, I too shall find,
A boon in focusing on thy friend,
Who holds the flame of thine own,
And who by this mourning is shown:
  All hearts are marred, and thus their rule is done,
  In one, one low, and many increase.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It can be said that I will write of your great worth,
Yet, if you happen on this written word,
Forget my soul's deep roots, as I will mine,
For I cannot be loved one for whom you feel no need.
But if you happen on this written word,
For I, confined in you, incur
My deepest debt, I'll bear the burden of my friend's plight.
  I'll bear the heart's deep offense,
  If it can bring me to a grinding sea.


