When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unlooked for by all those dancing chips.
But if I swear it, 'tis not the same age
That keeps you for yourself;
It is summer when I think of you,
And you, my autumn, are painted new.
I may not know, but yet you are,
The one where autumn starts,
The other whereon the day is dressed,
And every set aside by the lark
  Makes you welcome, kindling every green.
  So, love, be of yourself so kind
  As I, for your worth, you shall keep the same.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Why should I speak ill of thee,
When I am stained with guilt my deeds should spurn?
Look in thy glass and there appears a face
