Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sympathy, tearing my world apart.
No dawn can compare to thy somber sight,
Nor the radiant star that bids the day adieu,
For your mournful eyes outshine the night,
Adorning the evening in elegiac hue.
Oh, let your heart embrace my despair,
As mourning becomes you, in every trait,
Then I shall proclaim darkness is rare,
And all that's plain lacks your complexion's stature.
  So I'll attest, black is the color of love,
  When worn by thee, it fits as a glove.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
But that I will one day in liberty be removed.
For what are you waiting for, your waiting for my death,
And what is 't but waiting for your face to bear?
For all of time and all of life is dressing old words new,
Spending again what is already spent.
Were I to lose you, 'tis my love who would gain,
And losing you, my friend would bear that pain;
Both unite, and I am left to endure,
This eternal tapestry of time.
  But what of old thou deserv'st alone,
  Mine be


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
In him thy sweet virtue doth willfully appear.
But why should I reproach thy double falsehood,
When I am stained with transgressions deep?
My vows, mere shadows, swiftly withered, hold
No weight compared to your deep-rooted ties.
For I have sworn by thine unwavering constancy,
By thy love, thy truth, thy faithfulness so rare;
Yet, in my blindness, I have twisted reality,
And bound thee in the chains of my despair.
  For I have sworn thee fair, false heart, unwise,
  To lie against the truth


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
When in my will thou my life provide a means to go
While he insults o'er dull and speechless tribes;
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy walks, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name no more shall dwell.
Lascivious grace, in whom all ill well shows,
 Kill me with spites, yet we must not be foes,
Although we do them wrong with likeness,
  Unless thou, in whom all ill well shows,
  Kill me with spites, yet we must not be foes.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
But why should I desire this temporal dwelling,
A structure bound to thee and me?
When all thy beauty wanes and mine eye is dead,
Do I not wish my name to be new?
What will thy eternal love or tender mold
That thou teachest from thee each day doth deign to stay?
Canst thou desire my name to dress with thee,
And I will be the only mate;
  Make my name your love, and love that thou dost deign,
  Not yet thou myself canst devise.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet mistakes for ugliness and grief.
On whom dost thou frown that I do adore,
And yet adore those whom thou dost deost thine eyes dost thine own sweet skill?
They know well the beauty of what I dost call,
And yet know not the worth of what I dost deign to show.
What virtue do I see in me, so great,
That I should scorn to serve thee as is due?
When all my virtues bow to thy defect,
Enslaved by the power of thy gaze anew.
  Yet, Love, persist


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But that we two must be twain
Although our undivided loves are one;
Thus I may know thy estimate
Time's leisure in future ages before,
But not to tell you how my days are spent.
But from hence my knowledge I derive,
And, like a deceived husband, will do it wrong
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
  For she who time unending leads my days,
  To dress me up in her old dance.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Neither shall my passion be marked as idolatry
Or at liberty's bend enfold.
O, let my love be at least as tyrannous in manners as those
Which shall themselves be well esteemed,
Or at least kind-hearted prove;
Exchange thy thoughts of kings to those of less;
Make me tongue-tied by authority's decree,
And do not, when mine eye is done,
Come in the rearward of a conquered sight.
  For thee, against myself I'll vow debate,
  For I must ne'er love him whom thou dost hate.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deep sense how to thee I am.
If I could write the beauty of thy eyes
And in fresh numbers number all thy graces,
The world would look into thee and take a stand,
At the place where it yearns to race.
But why should my poor beauty speak
That thou, my sovereign, are not acquainted
With the time and times of thy life?
O, know, sweet heart, I always write of thee,
And keep him from the reach of falsehood;
  Yet thou in my verse do show thee how
  To make him seem long hence as he shows


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I am as I am, and they find
That I, an accomplice, do intend
To bring my beloved love to such depths.
Yet they, in their spite, cannot help
Their hearts but with my love dislike their sight,
For I, an accomplice, they look into
My heart, and there reigns love's delight.
  Their reason is not so strong, though my love to thee,
  For my love thou thyself dost deign to show.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Basking in slumber, as a decrepit father takes delight.
So I, like a seasoned elder, returning from my side,
Discovering that my time is done,
The beauty of my old age, though my lover's glance,
A thing that touches me, knows I did cherish her,
And so, I rework phrases in a way they understand,
Reinvesting what was spent once before.
  For if the thing I hold dear in youth,
  Is not the love where my heart is driven.


