Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sympathy, tearing my world apart.
No dawn can compare to thy somber sight,
Nor the radiant star that bids the day adieu,
For your mournful eyes outshine the night,
Adorning the evening in elegiac hue.
Oh, let your heart embrace my despair,
As mourning becomes you, in every feature,
Then I shall declare darkness is rare,
And all that's plain lacks your complexion's stature.
  So I'll attest, black is the color of love,
  When worn by thee, it fits as a glove.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief.
Were it not sinful, then, striving to mend,
To mar the work that wants no aid,
My heart would be lost, and what I leave behind.
But striving to improve,
My heart is already marred, and what I leave behind;
My soul, once mended, is at its wit,
And the beauty that it attempts to frame.
  To seek relief, I'll bear the cost, but the triumph thine.
  My love, so deep, I'll bear the fight.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim my beauty's fair part.
But thou art the allor of my heart's delight,
And like a willing patient, I will drink
Potions of eisel 'gainst his strong infection;
No other cure, though I think mine,
Can leave me my love to bitter sauces.
  His love is such, to spite his will,
  That I, an accomplice, must do this wretched thing.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
