Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sympathy, tearing my world apart.
No dawn can compare to thy somber sight,
Nor the radiant star that bids the day adieu,
For your mournful eyes outshine the night,
Adorning the evening in elegiac hue.
Oh, let your heart reply:
"Had mercy on my sorrow, it might be swept,
And swiftly find you there, in your heart's embrace."
  Yet, as pearls, your tears of love ensue,
  A treasure that atones for every ill-done.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is not enough to torment me,
Nor can my beloved pardon my transgression?
When all my virtues bend to your will,
Ensnared by the charm of your cold retort.
I will not gloat that I have changed my ways,
Nor praise the myriad of missteps,
But let my heart to yours in harmony dance.
  Thus, I'll refrain, each step I deem necessary,
  For life is bound to the very edge of wrong.


