If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For if thou wilt, be a wretch, and do not so,
To love the one whom thou dost deign to show.
For thou art so possessed with murd'rous hate
That 'gainst thyself thou stick'st not to conspire,
Seeking that beauteous roof to ruinate
Which to repair should be thy chief desire.
O, change thy thought, that I may change my mind.
Shall hate be fairer lodged than gentle love?
Be as thy presence is, gracious and kind,
Or to thyself at least kind-hearted prove


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet, Love, you've dyed your visage with unlawful spoils.
Why should my heart believe in scattered dreams,
When it knows well the world's shared, single space?
Or should my eyes, which see, declare the opposite,
To grace falsehood with a lovely face?
Are they the faithful and honest source
Of your corruption's spring?
Or are they the only heralds of my hidden woe?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have wandered,
  And now to falsehood's contagion they've succumbed.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But not for that time do I think my age;
For that time is not so,
Nor I, nor she, nor I, to whom I give it,
At my youth by thee are present-perfect state
As I regard myself to others' pride.
Thus all their faults are masked not
By my deeds which I in thy breast doth deface,
But for their reasons only is their show,
  And nothing 'gainst myself--
  Save this my love which on thy breast doth live.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Neither shall I dare to boast how I do love thee,
When thou art gone beyond my reach, my deeds prove
