Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Dost thou abuse my spirit, too much profane,
Savage, intense, rude, cruel, not to trust;
My love, beauteous, doth plea,
In love's fine wit, it does plead.
Nay, 'tis not love, though much, that lends me strife;
It lends but weak relief,
An affection that guides me on my way.
  Ah, sweet deception! Thy love, though much, is not so great.
  A love that guides is not so great.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Basking in the love of a moment's peace.
So I, by fortune's dearest spite made lame,
Take all my comfort of thy worth,
And truth on both sides is swarms,
And love's sweet invention quite contrary.
O, what a blessing wouldst thou prove
Were it not thy sour leisure gave sweet leave
To entertain the time with thoughts of love,
Which time and thoughts so sweetly doth deceive,
  And that thou teachest how to make one twain
  By praising him here who doth hence remain.


