Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sympathy, tearing down walls apart.
Yet, my heart's desire, with ardor, leaps and sings,
In heaven's sweetest air, where stars in chorus dive,
In silent language whispering their ways.
My heart's inner heart, with ardor, begins to wane,
Abandoning thy darkened soul, as the lonely imagine.
In thee, their secret love's sweet throne,
Unveiled by any voice, by which I'm confined.
  So, by their darkened presence, I'm left in ignorance,
  To wait, like others, in distant lands.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It tears me from myself, your tyrant hold
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to be


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
In thy heart's thought are swarms,
That to my use it may still seem lawful,
Supposed as forfeit to a confined doom.
Hence, sweet thief, do not so. I love thee in such sort
As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report,
And my own dear love is as collateral
So do not like a guarantee.
  I do not wish thee to keep what I do,
  Since what I do thee promise, mine is thy good report.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose action is no stronger than a flower.
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy heart, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name no more shall dwell,
Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
We are in this and only to know
That others may well above the dignity of gilded pen;
And thence comes this disgrace,
Where words come hindmost, even in the clear.
  For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
  To thy sweet virtue


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For if thou survive my well-contented day
When that churl Death my bones with dust shall cover,
And shalt by fortune once more resurvey
These poor rude lines of thy deceased lover,
Compare them with the bett'ring of the time,
And though they be outstripped by every pen,
Reserve them for my love, not for their rhyme,
Exceeded by the height of happier men.
  O, then vouchsafe me but this loving thought:
  Thy love is as fair in knowledge as in hue.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet mistakes for ugliness and grief?
Whence cometh this corruption of the good,
That in thy vices I find charm,
In thy transgressions, I see beauty stood,
And in thy chaos, a soothing balm?
Who hath shown thee how to twist my heart,
To cherish more the more I see amiss?
Though I should push thee far, depart,
My heart still clings, with love grown' miss.
  If thy unworthiness hath wrought this love,
  More worthy am I to be loved above.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But not for myself dwellers on form of such queen
As those feasts which at the expense of fortune chide.
Beware, my love, of this temptation!
She'll bear the cost, but the joy of it depends upon my love's increase,
And my friend's love's sweet looks translate well into the look of morning.
  So do not call me for praise that I do not serve
  And give my heart another place to go.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Neither will I reproach thy worth, though my own desert
In thy liberty's shop is hanging still,
And there can be no complaint.
O, what excuse will my friend mount provide
When wanting for nothing, striving for invention?
He can afford no praise to thee but what in thee doth live.
Then hate me when thou wilt, if ever, now,
Now, for my sake, do this thing:
  Make me tongue-tied, and do not like
  To liken thee to a day in spring.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy willfulness and thy dear merit;
If I could not possibly assert,
"Truth needs no color with his color fixed,
Beauty's wish would be colorless, and waste,
A matter of more than the soil.
But since he needs no praise, what shall be so?
He needs not, and yet, love, be of his word,
As I need him, so should he be true.
  Then do not, when I have done this,
  Make him much outlive a gilded tomb.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
That, for my sake, I sin against myself--
Thou art my debtor, and my only care
Art to render me adoration, not to grant me heart's desire.
For to thy sensual fault I bring in sense--
Thy adverse party is thy advocate--
And 'gainst myself a lawful plea commence.
Such civil war is in my love and hate
  That I an accessary needs must be
  To that sweet thief which sourly robs from me.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the chase of her beloved offspring;
But if the dull substance of my flesh were thought,
Injurious distance should not stop my way,
For then, despite of space, I would be brought
From limits far remote, where thou dost stay.
No, I am not thought, but flesh and blood
Have faculty by nature to go;
My will, though much, is not so great.
  The bloody spur cannot provoke him on
  That sometimes anger thrusts into his hide.


