Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sympathy, tearing my world apart.
No dawn can compare to thy somber sight,
Nor the radiant star that bids the day adieu,
For your mournful eyes outshine the night,
Adorning the evening in elegiac hue.
Oh, let your heart embrace my despair,
As mourning becomes you, in every feature,
Then I shall declare darkness is rare,
And all that's plain lacks your complexion's stature.
  So I'll attest, black is the color of love,
  When worn by thee, it fits as a glove.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It tears me from myself, your tyrant hold
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to be


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
In bonds of sweet-purchased right.
Forgot upon him when I saw him there,
Happy to have him as my goest;
But then comes it that my name receives a brand;
And almost thence my nature is subdued
To what it works in, like the dyer's hand.
Pity me, then, and wish I were renewed,
Whilst, like a willing patient, I will drink
Potions of eisel 'gainst his strong infection;
No bitterness that I will bitter think,
Nor double penance, to correct correction.
  Pity me


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose change is my sole desire, and that my heart doth plead.
In matters of vast capacity, we see
Among many, one is inconsequential, honey.
Then in the multitude, let me be unvoiced,
Although in thy heart's log, I am one.
For nothing binds me, if it pleases thee bind
That nothing me, a something, honey, to thee.
  Just make my name your love, and love that unwavering,
  And then you love me, for my name is Passion, aka Ardor.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Who lets so fair a house fall to decay,
Which husbandry in honor might uphold
Against the stormy gusts of winter's day
And barren rage of death's eternal cold?
Or who the lines of life that life repair
Which on thy humor doth depend upon my remains,
Will sourly leave me till my sweet hue
Resembling thee, my rose;
  And such a house is put to the test
  Of self-love, to whom I am confined.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in their madness, they mistake light for their gloom?
They breathe life into the rose's sweet guise,
And in their thirst for admiration, they droop eyes.
Yet, Love, you've wrought a twisted mirror's snare,
That warps your true image within its twisted sight.
Why should my eyes seek to paint true,
When you've twisted your truth with twisted delight?
Your heart hath trained it to ensnare my heart,
To love thee more, as thou dost thine own part.
  If my heart should wander, 'tis my love who'd


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But not love, let me know my fate.
Why should I know her change?
My love, when she thinks me amiss,
Do I not then declare my age?
Ah, thus, sweetheart, be contented
By my love's own desert,
I'll be frank in her honesty,
Unless she knows full well my days are fleeting.
  So, in pride, she believes me gullible,
  To love him whom I cannot know.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And yet with all my flaws do allow
For nothing more to me than thou art.
O, do not so. I love thee in such sort
As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report,
And 'Tis thy good report that to me I must attend.
O, what excuse will my poor beast then find
When wanting for praise from thee alone?
Then do not so. I love thee in such sort
As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.
  And yet in my nature thou art so possessed
  That I for myself must ne'er love him


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own dear heart's thought, too harsh in recompense.
If I could school thee in the art of banter, better still,
