Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Thy love, thy state, is fairest in my view.
Gentle thou art, and therefore to be sin;
Beauteous thou art, therefore to be sought,
And when a woman woos, what woman's son
Will sourly leave her till he have prevailed?
Ay me, but yet thou mightst my seat forbear,
And chide thy beauty and thy love,
Whose influence is thine and all my pride.
  For thee, against myself I'll vow debate,
