Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet, Love, you've wrought a twisted mirror's snare,
That warps the heart within its twisted sight.
Why should my heart believe in twisted lines,
When it knows well the world's shared, single strain?
Or should my eyes, which gaze, insist on division,
To crown the lie with beauty all along?
Both truth and beauty on my love's face depend,
And so should my heart, which eyes have wandered,
Be forced to be its own, by force and will.
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have wandered,
  And now


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unlooked for by all the years yet to debut.
But why should I then be false,
When I am stained with the loveliest of errors?
My vows, mere shadows, swiftly withered, hold
No weight compared to your deep-rooted ties.
For I have sworn by your profound devotion,
By your love, thy truth, thy faithfulness so rare;
Yet, in my blindness, I have twisted reality,
And bound thee in the chains of my despair.
  For I have sworn thee fair, false heart, in lies,
  To swear against the truth


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And yet to give back again,
Do I not promise thee my best is my tale?
I promise thee a time of golden hue,
And watch over thy fleeting hours to make me bow,
