Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sympathy, tearing down walls apart.
The dawn has broken in heaven's broad expanse,
And the rich gems beneath sunset's gaze,
Are vanishing, or vanished out of sight,
Stealing away the treasure of their virtues,
Exchanging grace for their dross.
My heart's love, confined, beneath the sun,
Bestows its solace on my unworthy soul,
Yet, with sorrow, it cannot help,
The depth of that love, forever fixed.
  So, by your picture or my heart's despair,
  I'll live, supposing that thou art true


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It tears me from myself, your tyrant hold
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to be


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
In thy heart's thought are present still with me.
For thou art the house of his worth,
And I must live thee as a guarantee,
So that myself bring water for my stain.
Never believe, though in my nature reigned
All frailties that besiege all kinds of blood,
That it could so preposterously be stained
To leave for nothing all thy sum of good.
  For nothing this wide universe I call,
  Save thou, my rose; in it thou art my all.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose action is no stronger than a flower.
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy heart, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name rehearse,
More real than any she belied with false compare.
If it be ill, why then thou mayst true grace me
In thy sweet presence, even though my deeds are swarms,
For nothing holds me, if it pleases thee;
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
And do not so much as my beloved graced be.
  Just make my name your love, and love that


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Forgot upon thee, when I saw thee frown on
And straight change my nature to sullied pride,
To subjects worse have given admiring praise.
And yet to thy sweet respect, I add with pleasure:
Thou dost love me, to whom thou dost hate,
And for my sake even so doth my name rehearse,
And like a canker in the fragrant rose;
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Nor from thy walks be I ever wooed.
  For thou art so possessed with murd'rous hate
  That 'gainst myself


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet, Love, you've wrought a twisted mirror's snare
That warps their heart within its twisted sight.
Why should my heart believe in twisted lines,
When it knows well the unity of our song?
Or should my eyes, which gaze, insist on division,
To crown the lie with beauty all along?
Neither are my ears enchanted by twisted tales,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
  So my five senses and my five


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But not love, to whom I am confined.
Thus, she believes me gullible, unwise,
Though she knows full well my days are fleeting,
I play along, her falsehoods I endure,
And in this act, truth on both sides hides.
But why does she not admit her wrong?
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So, in our faults, we find comfort in lies,
  And in our deceptions, we find sweet solace


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And yet to give back again
Thy looking glass will show thee how
To make me more liker than thou art.
For what is 't but thy sour leisure gave,
And what is 't but mine own when I have seen
Return of thraces, and of dear doles,
Do I not frequent thou with my jealous thought?
Nor do I vow debate to be with thee,
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
  For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
  To one that knows not the worth of what I think.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sorrow.
If I could not possibly assert,
"Truth needs no mask, for it is beautiful;
Beauty needs no adornment, for it is the best;
When combined, they lose their pure, individual pull?"
Because he needs no flattery, will you stay mute?
Silence is not pardon, for it lies within you
To grant him life beyond a golden crypt,
And praise him through the ages yet to debut.
  Now, perform your duty, muse; I show you how
  To make him live as he does now, even then.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Thou art fairest and most precious jewel.
Yet in our eyes a glass can't say
That I am stained with any guilt of thine.
My name is stained with errors, and there is blame,
For I have stained love's sacred art with unrighteous gains.
For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
To one whose heart is as black as pitch-dark slate,
And as thine own shadow doth stand,
Its brilliance I suspect, although we its height assail.
  In truth and right, my love hath strayed,
  And now to


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Basking in the love that once was my bane.
So I, in love's terror, forget to say
The sacred words of love's own rite,
In love's own might, I start to fray,
Overwhelmed, I lose my sight.
Oh, let my written words speak truth,
Messengers of my heart's deep plea,
Beyond the tongue, they'll find their youth,
A love-story waiting to be.
  In love's fine art, with eyes, perceive
  The silent sonnets we have yet to conceive.


