So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
In thy liberty's bonds do tie me day by day,
And like a guarantee, my heart is bound
To that sweet thief which steals thy jewels.
O, what a mansion have I to the beauty of which I have frequent
Which for myself alone is most proud,
And every fair from thence only me for thee will,
And so of thee, I fear, being found
Some small glory upon thy humor.
  But thence I am not bound, though I feel like I do feel
  In thy heart, wherethrough the sweet is to find.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose action is no stronger than a flower.
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy heart, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name rehearse,
More than enough are we to know it not.
We may be straight though they themselves be be bevel;
By their nature be vile and their deeds
Into thee I straight grow'st love,
That they elsewhere may be straight.
  For thee watch I whilst thou dost breathe,
  Not judge thy pleasure be right or misguided.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For if thou survive my well-contented day
When that churl Death my bones with dust shall cover,
And shalt by fortune once more resurvey
These poor rude lines of thy deceased lover,
Compare them with the bett'ring of the time,
And though they be outstripped by every pen,
Reserve them for my love, not for their rhyme,
Exceeded by the height of happier men.
  Then vouchsafe me but this loving thought:
  Thy love is enough that thou must leave my son.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet in them thou perceiv'st false tripping dip.
For why should my eyes be blessed made
By thy false tripping dip?
Or on thy given grace should they gloat
Thy given shape, thy given place?
No, I am that I am; and they that level
At my abuses reckon up thy hours' own.
I may be straight though they themselves be be bevel;
By their rank thoughts my deeds must not be shown,
  Unless this general evil they maintain:
  All men are bad and in their badness reign.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unlooked for by my years, and me most wary
As I not for myself but for others keepers of their woe.
But since she thinks me of her age,
Mine age becomes my all my doting heart,
And she, in scorn, then concedes her right
And puts apparel on my tattered loving
To show me worthy of thy sweet respect.
  In this reshaping of the world,
  I ponder thee, and so should I.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Neither can I seek to explain
The hurt of longing I've felt.
Yet, love, be the companion of waiting love,
Whose worth's unknown, though we its height assail.
Our love is fortified, though it seems to ebb,
Less ostentatious, more heartfelt is our bond.
A love that's flaunted is but shallow coin,
True love's value in quiet moments join.
  Thus, in absence, we learn to be
  Not foes.


