Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Thy love-loving heart in love's shady art.
Were it not sinful, then, striving to mend,
To mar the subject that before was well?
For to thine illicit touch I lend mind,
And that's my fallacy, thy virtue's disdain.
In love's shady deeds, the naked subject steals the lion's part,
And leaves my love to languish, unbent
With worry, love's shady stealth.
  So, subtle trickster, do not reproach my sins,
  Since at heart my love is truly dead.


