If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For thou art the world's fresh ornament
And dost inhabit on the heels of worn;
So shall beauty be, steadfast in its rhythm,
Without thy help, by me be stol'n.
Who hath shown thee how to make war
With thoughts of thy deceased lover?
Though I have left them, how are I to die?
Thou art the grave where buried love doth live,
Hung with the trophies of my lovers gone.
  Their images I loved I view in thee,
  And thou, all they, hast all the all of me.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what worth the world, that deems it otherwise?
The earth may have the resplendent light,
Yet like the lark at the sun's eye,
Its brilliance I suspect, although we its height adore.
So, Love, you've wandered far and wide,
Presenting a spectacle, far and wide,
Wounded your own thoughts, cheaply sold the priceless,
Transformed past affections into present transgressions.
  True minds can afford
  Where once they were harsh, now they bend beneath the load.


