Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gaze gently on my sorrow's endless plain.
The chasm spreads between my heart and sight,
Yet each bestows on other gentle grace,
In every plain, my lover's life's sweet legacy.
When longing grips my heart, it cannot reach within,
For love, like the wound, doth ensnare my heart,
And bind it to share in love's sweet revenge.
  My love, to whom I now belong,
  Thine eyes so sweetly dost deceive.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To see the fall of lofty towers,
Their once-proud stature now by decay enfold.
Even for this, we as beset by Time's cruel hand,
