Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gentle on my sorrow, and rightly so.
The dawn's first light, heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal its glare


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
But in your breast's embrace, I find my solace deep.
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, find
Our hearts entwined in torment, thrice multiplied.
Imprison my heart within your breast's embrace,
But in your breast, I can't
Whilst your love and guilt, both abide.
No more can then torment me while your love
Deserves the sweetest, no more can then torment
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
  Yet, should you, within your breast,
  Release me from this


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Bears may claim my heart, too dear, to thee.
But since he's yours, my heart's own book,
He keeps thee but his page,
A testament to that which it contains still.
That he keeps you but by my love,
And for that love, when I reviewest
The very part was consecrate to thee.
  And yet, love, it is but as a tomb
  The second death, which seals the day from sight.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
When that thou myself reportest
Thou hast thy fair countenance to witness,
The most precious jewel from my hand.
Yet, there the time when thou fill
The mountain tops with my extern the outward honoring,
And the dear doth inhabit the air
Wherefrom the summer's breath thou hast too grossly dyed.
  And still, love, thy glass will show thee me
  The very clear eyes' moiety and the dear heart's part.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
But when thou reviewest this book, thou dost review
The very part was consecrate to thee.
The earth can have but earth, which is his due;
My spirit is thine, the better part of me.
So thou hast but lost the dregs of life,
The prey of worms, my body being dead,
The coward conquest of a wretch's knife,
Too base of thee to be remembered.
  The worth of that is that which it contains;
  And that is this, and this with thee remains.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in thy brain, a frame of flesh holds the place.
Yet, Love, what good turns eyes for eyes have done,
In thy brain, their lines have become entangled with night,
My eyes, entangled by love's throes.
Why should my heart trust in a fractured tale,
When it knows well the sameness of our plight?
Or should my eyes, which witness, tell a different story,
To elevate the lie with purest light?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have done a
  Series.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
But that she knows not the cause of this injury
That she, in losing me, she bestows
Avenge love, with tears that flow no more.
And thus, in reverence of me, she rekindled
The flame of my love, so,
The wound remains, though it seems to move,
From my sight, to you, it's but dimly shown.
  Still, as the case, the defendant doth know,
  The truth: you love her because I love her.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Drain thou not of my fear.
If I bend beneath the load of words
That caused thee pain, seek'st thou art better.
O, but that thou hast, so too, I send thee to seek
Some good conceit that thyself dost use it;
And that is where I find my end
To be where I should be, to thee I say.
  But what's so fair that thy neglect?
  Thy love, though much, that thou give.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy fear's shadow to lend.
O, what a torment wouldst thou prove
Were it not thyself so possessed
Which in thy breast doth live, as thine in me;
How, that, thyself, when thou gently stol'n,
Thou dost review thy own worth, recounting it to thee.
O, what a torment wouldst thou prove
Were it not thy sour leisure gave sweet leave
To entertain the time with thoughts of love,
Which time and thoughts so sweetly doth deceive,
  And that thou teachest how to make one


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Mine eyes nothing worth till they see 'tis.
For when we lose lust, 'tis with my love that's fire,
And losing myself, our own increase,
Increasing store with loss and loss with store;
But when we see 'tis but dimly seen,
My eyes, once faithful, have lost their way,
And to change our love, they have but dimly strayed.
  For thee, against myself I swear,
  For I love thee, and thus, I trust thee to stay mute;


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
An accessary that my virtuous guard.
That 'gainst my love thou hast too,
A lawful plea against myself commence.
Grant me the strength, to bear love's want,
That love may thrive, and guilt to be tombed.
Let love be as thy presence is, gracious and kind,
That love's sweet memory may still be.
  Preserve thy name! 'tis thine,
  And mine is thy love, so thou mayst be remembered.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the chase, the capture, leaving behind
Her own flesh, her own blood, in discontent
A mirror image of you, in this chaotic world,
Myself, my heart's desire, forever unrest.
For if you are truly you, the whispers' spite,
Your heart, your eyes, utter, in disarray,
A steadfast, love, through which our age will stay.
  If this be false, and I not proved untrue,
  No words were penned, nor love by mortals knew.


