Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gentle sympathy for thy sin, now my transgression.
Oh, let me confess that I have done more,
Than thy love hath, not to show my affection,
So shall I live, without thy help,
Unless thou take this love from my sight.
  Preserve thy love, I beg, for love
  As thine, when I am gone.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is not enough to heal my soul,
Nor can my dearest friend's guilt alleviate my dire.
Even if you repent, I've still the pain,
Of love's plea, and I'm still the friend.
  But if you repent, and I have still,
  Am forced to be your own, and all I do.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine own heart
In hopes of sums can match the plea,
And I will win the case.
But when that plea is spent so long,
The judgment's come last,
And the just plea against me,
My self-love falters on the heels of my foes.
Such strife exists 'twixt love and disdain,
  That I, an accomplice, must engage,
  In the sweet theft that my heart does forsake.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To grant her wish, when thou dost give me the fort.
And do not drop in for an afterloss.
Lest you, when my dear, incur,
My heavy lies, and in them still doth grow;
But do not so. I love thee in such sort
As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.
If I lose thee, my loss is my love's gain,
And losing her, my friend hath found that loss;
Both find each other, and I lose both twain,
And both for my sake lay on me this cross.
 


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So I will attest, black is the color of love,
When worn by thee, it is but the face of love,
Which in thy breast doth live, as thine in me doth lie.
How many lambs might the stern wolf betray
If like a lamb he could his looks translate!
How many gazers mightst thou lead away
If thou wouldst use the strength of all thy state!
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in the wrong's bright gaze, their brightness fades from view;
But why should false eyes borrow from his sight,
Stealing the truth that once was his alone?
Why should poor eyes seek to counterfeit
His natural grace, his beauty so replete?
Why should he live, when all the world is bleak,
Its veins once full of life, now drained and weak?
  In him she sees her ancient wealth displayed,
  A tribute to the days when life was made.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And I will not comment upon that offense;
Speak of my youth, and I shall not be troubled
To those same voices that most have spoken.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me half so ill,
To set a form upon desired change,
As I'll myself disgrace, knowing thy will;
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy walks, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name no more shall dwell,
Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
  For thee


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Denture away, unfazed by my broken rite,
And let my love excuse thy harsh words to governing
By seeking wisdom in hopes of you.
Look to the glass, and there appears a face
That bends to my thoughts, and I may myself disgrace
For atone for thy wrongs I do forgive,
By paying thy outpatient debt,
Myself disgrace, I repay thy disgrace.
  But why should I reproach thy double falsehood,
  When I am repaid in kind, be told.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest love's tenor, and what I can't cure.
If I could say better thus:
"Though not so, 'tis love who'd not seem so."
Though I should push thee far,
My heart still sends them back, and straight grow sad,
To bear the pain of this love's farewell.
  But if my nerves were filled with hope,
  More worthy I to be remembered.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Not my dearest love to whom thou dost give.
My love is gentle today, tender tomorrow,
In a marvelous consistency it remains;
Thus, my love allows my words to fall short,
Even those that seem to decay.
  For thee, I say, count the face that
  Stands firm and proud, even to the edge of doom.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
As I impair not beauty, and thus my aim is led astray.
My love is so strong, and mine is ever near,
To thyself at pleasure, when thou art gone.
Yet, as I persist in hope,
My love grows, and Death concedes my right,
For in these last essays, though strong, my life hath altered.
  So, I suspect, but not for this,
  Thyself aticking for thyself, spent once before.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To the chase, which wounded bosoms fits!
Yet I, to whom she's yours is her pull,
Bound to my beloved, soothe me,
The humble bite that slowly doth grow,
The memory of my love, now my own.
How many a tear, sweetly doth say,
Shall Anne's child, soothe and soothe his plight,
Neglecting the dulling of love's farewell.
  But if you should wound me, make my name your love,
  And then you love me, for my name is Passion, aka Ardor.


