Nor on my frailties why hast too much too much too much too much too much too much too much too much too much too many a good?
For what good is it that he needs my sympathy,
And for that sin there is no remedy?
  And yet I have found that men as plants increase,
  And even such as they, in their youthful sap.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A gem amidst the crowd.
But here I see her falsehoods, in my disarray,
And in this most kingly drinks I find my solace,
A deformed beauty, from heat's heated brand,
Shall keep me from the shadows drawing near.
My love, so sweetly, mulling over life's abundant grace,
Her eyes so full, no winter's night
Can dim her visage or turn away.
  And yet this hope: my heart's desire
  To keep her love's image of me as youthful.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Dissipate more sharp than spurn me when thou art gone.
Rejecting not is enough to keep
My ardor's worth from all that I offer.
My own worth do not so great;
Therefore to give what's due,
Thine own worth then becomes my own traitor.
Let me be true despite thy wrong,
Although thou art no longer than I,
And yet love that knows it not.
  For thy sake I'll fight,
  For I can see myself a kinder way.


