  The Sign of deceit, and dumb for a prize so dear.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
As I by thy self resemble,
Thou art all my state, and I cannot boast
That thou canst move no love to my sight.
O, how much more doth beauty beauteous seem
By that sweet thief which steals thee away.
The rose looks fair, but fairer we it deem
For thee, when in disgrace
Thou art so possessed with murd'rous hate,
Suff'ring thy friend for my sake to approve.
  In nothing art thou black save in thy sweet face,
  And thence this slander as I think proceeds.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To make the captive's tear from ever wetting earth,
Concealing from the world that I am away,
Lost to the world, for which I so dear.
I, the lone child of her pleasure,
Whose heart is boundless, and she, in her self-array,
Sought out the profligate who, in his wild abandon,
One blushing shame, another grief,
A symphony of sighs does softly play,
Each note a testament to my needs.
  In this sorrow, I ponder thee, and rise,
  For I, confined


