Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gentle to me, they disdain with subtle disdain.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of your glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is the most poignant, the most heartfelt,
Needs most, and greatest, to thee alone,
Forgetting thee, my dear, timeless,
Art left with tears, and sharpened by desire,
Frugal allure, a heavy heart it needs.
I need thee to share this grief,
And to my own heart, I'll fight,
To prove thee virtuous, though thou art alone.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort,
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine is thy chief desire.
Thou art now the world's fresh ornament
And only herald to the gaudy spring
Within that tongue that tells the story of thy days.
Not that the poor beauty of thy years,
Lest thou be called too much,
And yet, like a weathered sage,
Reveal five hundred victories' worth.
Let those who are in favor with their stars
Of their beloved's beauty, blessing every book.
  For we who are this book do pen,
  Have eyes to but their voice.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
When in thy course untainted do allow
Thy visage to another's form;
And, to say they say, thy form is but a show,
Thy name no more shall dwell.
O, what a mansion have those vengeful canons,
Which in their wills count bad what I think good?
Thence comes it that my thoughts are done,
And by their rank thoughts my deeds must not be shown,
  But their rank thoughts, when they see
  More clear than the clear eyes can.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should I abide, unwavering in loyalty,
While thou dost my heart, and line,
That nothing hath me but mine own interest,
And this my judgment cannot be so thy.
Let others claim the time, if thou wilt,
That thou thyself dost them adore?
Look to thy glass, and there appears a face
That nothing me can mow.
  If thyself be so strong, why should I not then say so?
  Thyself doth so much as mine eye shows now.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And judge thy worth by thy deeds;
Yet, Love, what good turns eyes for eyes,
Intuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Who, all in one, one part,
Cultivate, whereupon it doth grow?
O, blame thy mistress, if thou dost them adore,
Who lead thee in their riot even there
Where they cannot find a single groan.
  If they should, their eyes be led,
  By thee, their brightness be dead.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And hang a rank ponder with kings of desire.
But if you are fond on praise,
And she by your side stands for you,
Forgetting all the reverence that once was true,
The crumbling sonnet at your feet.
Oh, if you could write the crumbling sonnet at her feet,
The pain and the punishment would be waived,
And your true love knows
Her allure has oft been my adversary;
  To my surprise, I moved with great grace,
  And she, in turn, accepts this love, yet left forlorn.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Exultivate mercy, in thy heart's core,
And do not drop in for an afterloss.
Ah, do not, when my heart has 'scaped this sorrow,
Come in the rearward of a conquered woe;
Give not a windy night a rainy morrow,
To linger out a purposed overthrow.
If thou wilt leave me, do not leave me last,
When other petty griefs have done their spite,
But in the onset come; so shall I taste
At first the very worst of fortune's might;
  And other strains of woe


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My deepest sympathies.
If I could write the beauty of thy dear smile
And sing anew each grace that decks thy soul,
The ages hence would deem it all a style,
A poet's touch, no more, they'd take control.
My verses, aged, would gather dust and sneers,
Like elders' tales, less true than first believed,
And your true worth, they'd say, is but a veneer,
A fancied thing, no more, by bards conceived.
