And 'tis held, by thee, in propriety.
Thou art as fair in knowledge as in hue,
Finding thy worth a limit past my own praise,
And therefore they disdain,
Burdened by their own fallow ground.
And for they saw with prescient sight that tolls,
They thought not, "This precious child of mine,"
But held, by thee, their eyes fell,
Ensnared by the allure of thy youth.
  For I corrupt not beauty, holding thee close,
  In act, your allure abounds.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
As I to thy sensual state plead.
Thou art as mine, and as I to thee,
Are as one, strong and happy to be.
That is my home of love. If it be filled with all,
Or sunk within the depths of love's own sweet offense,
That my strong infection may thrive,
And I am still sick of love to fear.
  But when thy glass shows me myself indeed
  That I may love thee more, as thou dost them adore.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Borne on the farthest earth,
Delighting in their youthful exploits and verve,
So I, in love's dread, forget to say
The sacred words of love's own rite,
In love's own might, I start to fray,
Overwhelmed, I lose my sight.
Oh, let my written words speak truth,
Messengers of my heart's deep plea,
Beyond the tongue, they'll find their youth,
A love-story waiting to be.
  In love's fine art, with eyes, perceive
  The silent sonnets we have yet to conceive


