Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gentle on my sorrow's woe.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of your glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal its glare


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is not enough to share this grief,
Nor can it be left bereft.
It is my love, though it knows
Our hearts share a torment, thrice multiplied.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to be your own, and all I do.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye
For thy sweet self dost frame my worth.
Thou art as fair, and therefore to be feigned,
For thou art fair as any child of light,
Though not as stars, whose brilliance I can't see,
Thine eyes outshine their distant, cold respite.
Let others claim the praises they prefer;
I will not praise that thou thyself dost common grow
A weed among weeds, or thou art too common grow.
  For thee, I so belong, and do not waste
  But in the thought of thee, thou art ever growing.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And likely will be, too, in this told.
Thou art as tyrannous, and therefore to be won;
Those jewels doth cover thee, and they hold them back,
Are thine, and they are thy defect,
Others making worse what nature made so clear.
Thou art as tyrannous, and they, in their will,
Rule thy heart, and lead thee in their riot,
To do more that which nature hath made.
  But do not so.
  Thy will decreed, and my soul confined.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
That I have ranged, and had stol'n from each,
That hath my time's own broken,
And added to every wound,
That I myself will comment upon that offense;
Speak of my lameness and I straight will halt,
Against thy reasons making no defense.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me half so ill,
To set a form upon desired change,
As I'll myself disgrace, knowing thy will;
  For thee, against myself I'll vow debate,
  For I must ne'er love him whom thou dost hate.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in them, the humblest bloom or none,
That this time's truly is reflected in thee.
But why should my eyes be led astray,
To looking into darkness which shines in light?
Why should my eyes be led astray,
When in dead, they find their faults,
And in dead, their glass shows thee how?
  All men make faults, and even I in this,
  Have eyes to love, but voices that fail to endow.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
If I could write the beauty of thy hand.
But thou, thyself outgoing in thy noon,
Called to that audit by advised respects;
Thus have I swerved, as advised,
By willful taste of what thyself refusest.
The beauty gracing thy visage, so bright,
Is but the seemly raiment of my heart,
Which in thy breast doth live, as thine in me;
  Whose all naked parts, or none,
  Palting thy left foretell the winter and tells the summer.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Contend not with my lays esteem,
And do not with my neglect bring
The dedicated words that writers use
Of their fair subject, blessing every book.
Thou art as fair in knowledge as in hue,
Finding thy worth a limit past my praise,
And therefore art enforced to seek anew
Some fresher stamp of the time-bettering days.
Believe not, though my nature harbored flaws,
That it could ever be so tainted,
To set a captive free.
  For thee, I pen this message, not forlorn,
  Since what I once did adore


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
