In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Not my soul that cherishes what they find unright.
My eyes in you perceive a multitude of flaws,
But in you, they are the most precious,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
  So far for love, my love's sweet prayer,
  For in your truth, I find you have a father.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And there appears a kinder love in all that's plain.
Thou art as mine, and I cannot write
That thou mayst true love call;
So thy great gift, upon my judgment,
Comes home again, on better judgment making.
Roses have thorns, and summer's lip is stained,
And in this race, there is but one victor's pleasure,
That my steeled sense may give my heart another youth.
  But here's the joy: my steeled sense, thy love's sweet reward,
  Roses have thorns, and streams, and


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Childless mothers, cry out to the unheard;
And so, I, in love's dread, forget to say
The perfect ceremony of love's rite,
Embracing the one who holds it, in her hand,
Trudges on, bearing the flame of my own soul,
A prelude to the darkness, cold, and lifeless,
The second death, which seals the day in plight.
  Yet, in this twilight, see the ember's glow,
  The fire of life, still burning, burning low.


