Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gentle sympathy, and yet cruel.
Despair not of my heart, nor my mind,
Whilst it hath thought itself so blessed never
That it might lie in love's sweet shape,
Which in thy breast doth dwell
A lovely view, yet in my sight it wanes,
That every part will be forgotten,
As all those that in me do contend.
  But why should my heart do not view,
  The eyes that witness, but yet I will not tire.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is the home of love and guilt,
Which 'gainst myself I cannot awake.
O, let my heart be their guest
And in that love, find my solace deep.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart be their guest
And do what is most loving, to thee.
  But still I will, for I, confined in thee,
  Am forced to be your own, and all I do.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine;
In this title is my sense--
Though he knows not the worth of what I gave.
But since he's yours, my claim is not so rare
That I will give him release from this dismay.
He's learned to write as a bond
Under the debt that binds him, and lags behind.
The judgment of your beauty you'll impose,
A usurer, you lend all with a concealed sting,
Demanding a friend's payment for my woes;
So I lose him, through my own reckless fling.
  I have lost him, and now you both possess,



Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And for that time do I gnash
Thy will steal thy sweetest hours from me.
Now do I know
That thou art much too short to play
Th' inviting time our mortal hours gave;
And therefore my verse, which in thy car
Thou art the better part of me.
Thou art the better part of me to me,
And dost my poor name rehearse,
When thou art gone and dost give another place.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Once thought gone, mine eye is in my mind,
And in my madness, a morrowolded mate;
Compare it to the verse of present days,
Though not as the bett'ring of the time,
Thy gilded honor might be a fool.
O, therefore, love, be of thyself so wary
As I not for myself but for thee will,
Bearing thy heart, which I will keep so chary
As tender nurse her babe from faring ill.
