Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in some perfumes is there more delight
Than in the breath that from my mistress reeks.
I have seen
The golden sun adorn the mountaintops,
And in the basest clouds a heavenly hill,
Delights in their youthful sap, at height decrease,
And wear their brave state out of memory;
Then in the least of them my greatest grief
Hath my sweetest burden as lead,
To stand by thee, to fall by thy side, my dear.
  Yet in this sorrow, I retell the truth:
  Thy eyes, though they view,


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And likely some more, more wanted,
By my beloved's eyes, dyed by their own biased stands.
But if I held her dear,
Her eyes so suited, and they looked so
At my beloved's youth, in dreams they droop,
Her whispers, though dressed, such subtle charm it gained,
Could not be so dull a shame to see her frown,
And in some antique book, such mysteries preserve,
Of you, my sagacity, ever near.
  But if you were to import forgetfulness in me,
  All days should look so.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with your speech, not your glance, let amends extend,
Wage war with war, and leave no room.
Confess your heart lies with another; yet in my sight,
Spare me the deceit of roving eyes.
Why employ cunning when your might
Exceeds my defenses, to my surprise?
Grant me the pardon: my love well knows
Her beauty has been my undoing;
To save me from such foes, she bestows
Her strength, and I am only winning.
  Yet, relent not; near expiry I lie,
  End my torment


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of ill fortune's thought.
If I could write the beauty of thy face
And in fresh numbers number all thy sum,
The age to come would say "This poet lies;
Such heavenly touches ne'er touched earthly faces."
So should my papers, yellowed with their age,
Be scorned, like old men of less truth than tongue,
And their true rights be termed a poet's rage
And stretched meter of an antique song.
  But were some child of yours alive that time,
  You should live twice--in it and in my rhyme.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And in themselves their deeds are woven.
I may not frown upon my sorrow's endless plain,
Nor at the prospect of such a vanished sight it stands,
For my eyes in heaven do mock the hurt
That caused this tongue to speak.
But when I think on thee, dear friend, all aches
  Fade 'neath the soothing balm of sweet recall,
  And all the losses fade into the past.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And grant me the strength to love that knows no art.
O, what a mansion have those vices got
Which for their habitation chose out thee,
Where beauty's veil doth cover every blot,
And all things turns to fair that eyes can see!
Nay, but yet in good faith some say that thee behold,
Thy face hath not the power to make love groan,
And to his robb'ry commits.
  So I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
  To one whose beauty's veil doth cover every blot.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Childless, left, her strength's abundance weak.
So I, bound to my beloved's sweet expanse,
Delivert my thoughts, to give invention light,
And do as prayers divine, that pour'st into my verse,
Yet, still, I think on thee, and rise,
From this faring ill, my love shall never die.
  For as the sun one who shines in the light of day,
  My love, so bright, shall never die.


