If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should I love thee, when in our shared pains I'm drowned,
Do I not then say I was false of heart,
When in our errors there was no remedy,
But that I will myself disgrace, haply of our woe,
That I will myself disgrace, knowing thy will;
Nor will I, in my madness, do any thing--
Wearing thy darkness, which I will say I did adore,
While in my madness I'm drowned;
  So thy great grief, upon my heavy eyelids laid,
  Is lifted, and the balm of solace flows.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in the world's eyes, there canst thou taste how true eyes perceive;
But in this regard, their wisdom does take,
And their gross painting might be better used
Than of the sun's fair complexion to thee.
So should the sun be borne on my head,
Forget the wrinkle that mine eyes doth deceive,
Nor should gentle sleep be broken
When body's picture I in dreams doth lie.
  But do thy worst, old Time, thou art assured mine
  Even that thou mayst know thyself.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
When I was your child, whom she dost keep
My heavy eyelids in the tired night.
But if I see a painted beauty
That every word doth almost tell my name,
Shall I be most proud of such a time,
And in that time, I see thee frown,
When I say to the world that I was
A child of yours, and she, in turn,
Came back again, to my surprise.
  But here I ponder thee, and rise,
  For I see thee when thou art all the better part of me.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with your words, not too sharp, too brittle, too tacky.
Confess your heart lies with another; yet in my sight,
Spare me the dance of eyes askance.
Why employ tricks when your might
Surpasses my defenses by sheer expanse?
Let me absolve you: my dear heart knows
Her allure has been my foe;
Turning foes from my sight she bestows,
That they elsewhere may strike their blows.
  Yet, persist not; near expiry I lie,
  Release me wholly, end this demand.


