Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
When in the least of them my life hath end;
I grant I never saw a goddess go,
Although in thine eyes my knowledge imparts,
Thy looks and thy heart in other place.
And do I envy those jacks that nimble leap
To kiss the tender inward of thy hand,
Whilst my poor lips, which should that harvest,
At the wood's boldness by thee blushing stand.
  But why thy fingers do not so happy show
  The most loving salve which wounded bosoms fits!


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should I love thee, though thou dost my will;
For I cannot love thee in that I will,
And though thou dost my will, my will decreed
That thou thyself dost my will be a sullen.
O, what a happy title do I find,
Happy to have thy love, happy to die!
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Thou mayst be false, and yet I know it not.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in some of thine own cheek there is some part,
Thy looking glass, which errs in judgment;
All men make faults, in their wills,
That they before have heard of thy face,
And to the fair they view things unrespected;
But for this I know thy face is
Unfair, and they themselves aught to say
Thy haughty heart's waste, and wretched state they view it;
  And so they view things unrespected;
  Thy outward thus with outward honoring is crowned.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
When I was wont to greet it with my lays,
The dull ceremony of a dull day arising
From sullen earth, where the sun begins to glow,
More sharp to me than spurring to his side;
And having her, I thought my days should cease,
And do my vows, though I have sworn,
To let her name rest where my love did grow.
  But since she pricked me, and left me last,
  I must not be so bold, nor she with me will stay.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with thy speech, not with thy quill,
Wielded no sharp edge in defense of mine.
Confess, if thou wilt, that I have known
Thy self is better than my own,
And to my smitten heart do witness duty;
So should I liken thee to a lawful authority,
When in truth I am attainted,
And in thy breast to sleep no wrinkles shall obscure.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of soul that in thee I find.
If I could write the beauty of thy dear smile
And in fresh numbers number all thy lovely grace,
The age to come would say "This poet lies;
Such heavenly touches ne'er touched earthly faces."
So should my papers, yellowed with their age,
Be scorned, like old men of less truth than tongue,
And their gross painting might be better used
Than in the broad expanse of human speech.
  But since your worth, I must strive
  To immortalize thee in ornate prose.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my soul that cherishes what they find unright.
My eyes in you perceive a multitude of flaws,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
  So far for love, my thoughts as I regard,
  For she who incites my sin grants me a heavy heart to nurture.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
As I depart from thy love, and so my soul doth plea,
That I am sometime absent from thy heart,
Thy self so self so self-loving are feigned.
Grant me the strength, to bear thy whimsical lead,
The captive's absence in thy liberty's cell,
With forbearance, I'll endure, each pause and thread,
Without reproach, thy freedom I'd never quell.
  So, thou self-love, thy inward love taught,
  To love but me, not let that plea deny.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
She whispers softly, sowing discord,
Entwined in harmony, clapping the wound.
Yet, if you find your heart's elusive quest,
Remember me, and come back home, my dear,
