Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gentle on my sorrow's endless plain.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of your glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal its glare


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is the willfulness of my heart,
Which in thy willfulness impresses;
Embrace me, thus, I'll play the role
Of a deceived husband, to whom my heart is led.
Love knows it is a heavy toll to bear,
The loss of a love abandoned, more than spite's brief theft.
  If my friend were to be saved, 'tis my love who'd bear the pain;
  In love's own book, the pages ever-turn.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine is in my will;
So thy great gift, upon misprision growing,
Comes home again, on better judgment making.
What should I do but tend, once made clear,
And for that sin there is no remedy?
The cure is in thy repose,
And I must await, though waiting so be hell.
  Receiving thy grace, then must I say so,
  Receiving thy bounty, though my own health be overcome.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
When in the least of them my life hath end;
I grant I never saw a goddess go,
Although in thine eyes my every part will be forgot,
And in some book this learning mayst thou taste:
"Making worse what nature made so clear,
Such anduer rude comments on thy sport,
Thou slyly call a king,
When nature calls thee to break a twofold truth:
  Thy name is more fair than any child of light,
  When compared with nature's and thee rich in praise.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should I sleep, though mine eyes in dreams are dead,
For my body in love's sweet shape,
And sleep in thy company before thou see'st
That nothing hath my spirit laid,
A home to love that knows it is.
O, let my books be then the eloquence
And dumb presagers of my speaking breast,
Who plead for love and look for recompense
More than that tongue that more hath more expressed.
  O, learn to read what silent love hath writ.
  To hear with eyes belongs to love's fine wit.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And therefore to my smother's eyes they grieve;
And for they have power to hurt and will do none,
Adorning your face with all hearts surmount.
Who else can boast such peerless beauty set,
Refusing cultivation from a mate?
Or who, so captivated by their face,
Would choose a grave for love of self-purity?
Thou art as self-loving, and they themselves cannot know
How to make love's image more real than gilded copies can.
  To thee, their art might is all in vain,
  For true praise


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
When I was once unkind befriends her,
And she, in losing me, doth mock my fate,
At those who would not like a weathered sage
Harsh holds her peace, but I fear,
That my dear heart may wane, made frail and lame,
At the onset of my love's new.
I bear this thought, a heavy load,
That she, in losing me, shall win the race,
And I, in turn, shall bear the mark of my love's long,
  Until my beloved's lovely face, though worn,
  And there lives


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with thy words, not too sharp, too brittle, too tacky.
Confess your heart lies with another; yet in my sight,
Spare me the deceit of roving eyes.
Why employ cunning when your might
Exceeds my defenses, to my surprise?
Grant me the pardon: my love well knows
Her beauty has been my adversary;
To shield me from such foes, she withstands
Their blows, diverting enmity.
  Yet, relent not; near expiry I lie,
  End my torment, and grant my sigh.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of ill fortune's thought.
If I could write the beauty of thy face
And in fresh numbers number all thy sum,
The age to come would say "This fair child of mine
Such heavenly touches ne'er touched earthly faces."
So should my papers, yellowed with their age,
Be scorned, like old men of less truth than tongue,
And their true rights be termed a poet's rage
And stretched meter of an antique song.
  But were some child of yours alive that time,
  You should live twice--in it and in my rhyme.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And in some other's countenance,
Thyself thy visage holds the beauty that it gained.
For to thy sensual banquet I bring
Thy heart's own semblance, true and kind,
And to thy sensual fault I bring
A thousand groans, and unsightly fire,
That do not so much as whisper my name.
For in their origin, some find cause for pride,
Some in their talents, others in their earthly treasure,
Some in their bodies, strength they cannot hide,
Some in their clothes, the trendy fashion's pleasure.
  In every blessed


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
As I depart from love, to my surprise.
Thou art the fairest and most precious jewel,
Roses with thorns, and summer's lease doth bear,
Three strong winds in one embrace,
Sincere this denial of love.
O, let me love thee, soothe me,
As I drink down the cure for my own plight,
No matter how bitter the taste may be.
I'll bear the pain, the punishment, the fight,
If it brings me to a purified sea.
  But do not so. I'll tell thee how to make love's own


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Childless, left, vulnerable to the hollow.
So I, in love's dread, forget to say
The sacred words of love's own rite,
In love's own might, I start to fray,
Overwhelmed, I lose my sight.
Oh, let my written words speak truth,
Messengers of my heart's deep plea,
Beyond the tongue, they'll find their youth,
A love-story waiting to be.
  In love's fine art, with eyes, perceive
  The silent sonnets we have yet to conceive.


