Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in them, perceived faults become their allure.
How many times, when thou blinded with false compare,
Come to my sight, and there behold a face
That nothing of us can mend.
How many times, when thou wilt look upon my countenance,
That nothing me, though it be stained with loss,
Can make love's length seem stronger,
By striving to mend what was rent,
The earth may reclaim its clay, as is deserved.
  But what's so strong that it fears no remedy?
  Thou mayst owe me, yet I'll not know how.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
When I was once unkind, now my griefs reap.
And yet, I have sworn her truthfulness,
To keep her image of me as youthful,
Unaware of the world's cunning stratagems.
Though she knows my prime has come and gone,
She plays the game of feigned innocence,
And I, in turn, believe her false tongue,
In this dance, truth on both sides is absent.
  But why does she not confess her wrongs?
  The reason lies in my love's sweet deceit.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Dissipate mine own thoughts, and thus bring them to light.
Thou art as a tormentor, and I cannot help
 But sigh, alone, from woe to woe tell.
My heart doth plead, and therein it woos,
A quest of thoughts, all tenants to the heart,
And by their verdict is determined
The clear eyes' moiety and the dear heart's part,
  As thus: mine eyes' due is thy outward part,
  And my heart's right, thy inward love of heart.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy great thoughts'st a better spirit doth use
And like a hesitant performer on the platform.
If I could write the better part of me,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should plunge into despair, I'd lose my mind,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
 


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And in some of them thy fair appearance shines.
My eyes in thee perceive a multitude of flaws,
But in my soul most kingly drinks,
Thy face hath not the power to make love groan;
Nor my tongue to speak of that which gives thee all thy worth,
Until it has, what it craves, it has embraced.
  Then in the fine of my love's fine wit,
  For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate.


