Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gentle on my sorrow, they rain.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can compare to the beauty of your remorse,
Gracing the evening with its eve's glow.
Oh, let your heart mourn for my plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall proclaim darkness is bright,
And all that's lovely, your shadows create.
  So I'll confess, black is the shade of the soul,
  When embraced by you, it makes beauty whole.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is the home of love and its every part,
Adorned with the trophies of my lovers gone.
Yet, as the years pass by, and time takes hold,
Our love remains steadfast, constant, and true,
No shadow cast by age or story told,
Can dim the flame that burns for me and you.
  In love's own story, the narrative weaves,
  A tapestry of dreams, and love that grieves.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye
Dearest, to his function,
For that thief of my life's part,
Who granted not a life serene and shown,
But he, in what he stole, left unguarded,
A prize for every thief in the world to snare.
Thee have I not locked up in any chest,
Save where thou art not, though I feel thou art,
Within the gentle closure of my breast,
From whence at pleasure thou mayst come and part;
  And even thence thou wilt be stol'n, I fear,
  For truth proves thievish


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Will you not yield thy hours to my will;
Thou art as a willing patient, and I will drink
Potions of eisel 'gainst my strong infection;
Such venom no more shall I know
Thou hast thrusted into the breast of his new.
If thou will, I will drink
Potions of eisel 'gainst my strong infection;
And do so, love; yet when they have devised
What strained touches rhetoric can lend,
Thou by thyself dost them chide me,
O'er whom thy fingers walk with gentle gait.
  And


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should I seek to wreak revenge with tears
That bind my rank thoughts in thought's cell,
With what I most enjoy contented least;
And though thou wilt, I know'st
Thou art the wretch's thrall, a hell within,
That to thy sensual sense I will impose a hell,
A loss in love that touches me more nearly.
  But here's the joy: my friend and I are one;
  Sweet flattery! then I will be repaid in kind, be told.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in some perfumes is there more delight
Than in the breath that from my lover reeks.
Why should my eyes be led astray,
When they see themselves as dead?
Or should my lips, which in dead night are displayed,
When they see thee in some more dead?
  In truth and right, my love and eyes have wandered
  And now to the dead, they've tethered.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A poet's touch, no more, they sing.
I borrow from their splendor, then return it thrice,
A tribute to the matchless jewel you are;
In you, their praises I long to prize,
And hold her mirror to compare.
At times, I revel in your warm embrace,
Then doubt the future steals what I revere;
Alas, I do not cherish thee through sight,
For my eyes in you, though you in yourself do show
My heart's deepest love, a vanished sight.
