In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my soul that cherishes what they find unright.
My eyes are swayed by a thousand dreams' decease,
And by their twisted vision sift through mine,
I perceive that every shape and form in my hand
Is but a shadow of my soul,
As I in dreams do attest.
My ears find no delight in your words' rhythmic dance,
Nor do tender sympathies lean towards unworthy touches;
Nor does taste or scent, seek to join the sensual trance.
  For I in this sorrow, and rightly so,
  For I corrupt not beauty, holding back,


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
As I bring to light my sinful deeds,
Do I envy those that nimble leap
To kiss the wound that self doth engage.
What sweet grace dost thou then, that thou mayst true love call?
Grant me the pardon, sweet thief of my bad deeds,
Though thou steal thee all my poverty;
And yet love knows it is a greater grief
To bear love's wrong than hate's known injury.
  Lascivious grace, in whom all ill well shows,
  Kill me with spites, yet we must not be foes.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Childless, carrying the flame of life,
Pluck the sharp fangs from the tiger's fierce jaws,
And incinerate the ancient phoenix's wing,
And do as you will, swift-paced Time,
To the vast world and all her fleeting delights.
But I forbid one grievous act from thee:
O, etch not with your hours my love's smooth face,
Nor trace no furrows there with your old quill;
Let him in your path remain untouched,
A timeless beauty for future hearts to fill.
  Still, do your worst, old Time


