Presents thy humor on these vacant leaves,
Which, when they think on thee, dear friend,
Beside thee, their thoughts, are converted.
Consider what a blessed title doth thy art find,
Where all the honor of thy worthiness gives.
  But what's so blessed-fair that it fears no blot?
  Thou mayst be false, and yet


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
When in the least of them my life hath end;
I will write more to thee,
Than that which on thy humor doth depend.
Thou art as a willing patient, and he that will drink
Potions of life; and he that will drink
Potions of life.
He can afford but weak relief
When thou reviewest his state,
Though he that calls on thee, let him in another place.
  If thyself be minded so, thy will be spent
  Where thou must, to say the very part.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should I glow in the darkness of this fevered zone,
When in thy vile deeds I find grace,
And in thy darkest corners of my world see,
When in thy darkest corners of my sweet time
I see thee, my sagacity, persist in the light
Of day, when thou shalt strangely pass
By time, though I, once shone upon thee;
For noblest things make wilt,
When thou reviewest the very part was consecrate to thee.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in them, perceived faults become the purest dream.
Yet in good faith some say that thee behold,
Thy face hath not the power to make love groan;
To say they err I dare not be so bold,
Although I swear it to myself alone.
And, to be sure that is not false I swear,
A thousand groans, but thinking on thy face,
One on another's neck do witness bear
Thy black is fairest in my judgment's place.
  In nothing art thou black save in thy deeds,
  And thence this slander as I think proceeds


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
When I was your captain of my duty.
That she might paint a story of virtues I lack,
To elevate me higher than I ever flew,
And heap praises on my humble shoulders' rack,
A falsehood spun to honor me anew.
Lest your true love appears as deceit,
Speak kindly of me, yet honestly,
Let my name rest where my body shall meet,
No longer causing us either shame or glee.
  For I am flawed, and what I bring to light,
  Deserves no love, and you, no blame in this plight.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with thy speech, not with thy quill,
Wielded no shield against my injury.
Confess your heart lies with another; yet in my sight,
Spare me the cross of your offense.
Why employ cunning when your might
Exceeds my defenses, to my surprise?
Grant me the pardon: my love well knows
Her virtue has oft been my adversary;
To shield me from such foes, she withstands
Their blows, diverting enmity.
  Still, cease this torment; as life ebbs away,
  Grant me swift release from this dismay.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of ill fortune's brow.
If I could write the beauty of thy face,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should plunge into despair, I'd lose my mind,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
  To avoid this, and to uphold your dignity,
