Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And therefore they mock their face with false compare.
But why should my eyes be led astray,
To look into a mirror of thy glass?
Or should my heart say more that thou dost lie,
When in thy fading thou art the best jewel?
For all the world is new, and yet I view
That nothing hath my lover's name.
Thy glass will show thee how thy beauties wear,
Thy dial how thy precious minutes waste;
  Or else this slander as I think proceeds:
  Thy eyes are spent, and therefore they mourners seem.


