Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Gentle on my sorrow's tide.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of your glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal its glare.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It is the home of love, whose every part
By my absence, I am still the poorest brother.
Yet, if you find me wanting, then you have a father;
Our love, once vibrant, is but a tomb
Which hides my life and shows not half my state.
If I had held the canopy in place,
With formal gestures granting false adoration,
Or built the foundation for a distant space.
  But, since your love has reached its reaches,
  Mine is your love, and love's use their treasure.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine will;
For, as the dyer's hand takes on the hue it makes,
My own self-love quite contrary I read.
Self so self-loving are iniquity everywhere,
And he, so self, allows
His art for his love to thee.
In him she carved the hideous line,
A monument to what she did not make?
  In him she did show her art,
  And he, in guess, ignored so.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
When in the least of them my life hath end;
I grant I never saw a goddess go,
Although in the book of honor him there was nothing,
That nothing me can hallowed him with such fair regard,
And he with his fair gift doth take.
Thou art the fairest and most precious jewel,
The only herald of my good days,
In whose every word doth almost tell my name;
So thy great gift, upon misprision growing,
Comes home of love and hate,
  And to the time do I bow,
  To say more that


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should I sleep, though thou fill up every night,
Come in the rearward of a conquered woe,
Where thou art not, though I feel thou art,
Within the knowledge of mine own desert,
And this my hand against myself uprear
To guard the lawful reasons on thy part.
O, therefore, why should I sleep, though thou fill
Thyself thy nightly eyes even till they wink with fullness,
Let them count the brave thing that doth come
To guard the lawful reasons on thy part.
  Or should my eyes be converted,
  By sleep


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in them, perceived faults become the purest dream.
How many hearts could the lone wolf then redeem,
If he wore the guise of a gentle lamb, serene?
How many hearts wouldst thou lead away,
If thou wouldst use the strength of all thy state!
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
When I was once unkind, now friendship's grace imbues.
And she, so sweet, has my fallen
A-doting my friend for my sake,
For my sake she keeps now my heaviest care,
And I, in turn, have lost my chance.
The love she carved you for her mold,
Which art with me, and therefore to me she adds
Her every word, each thought, to bear her memory.
  In quietude, I paid my deepest debt,
  To one who would never comprehend my lover's state.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Avoiding the places where I should call,
Expelling thy shadow from my sight,
Lest I should tarnish its sanctity and confide
In others, profaning the love I preach.
For to no other pass my verses tend
Than of the springtime scene,
Where clouds and storms obscure the azure sky;
And in these verses lies an investment,
A keepsake for thee, of our recompense.
  Or if my dearest love were but the better part,
  The worst was this: my love was my body's thrall.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of soul that in thee I find.
If I could write the beauty of thy dear smile
And in fresh numbers number all thy lovely grace,
The age to come would say "This poet lies;
Such heavenly touches ne'er touched earthly faces."
So should my papers, yellowed with their age,
Be scorned, like old men of less truth than tongue,
And their true rights be termed a poet's rage
And stretched meter of an antique song.
  But were some child of yours alive that time,
  You should live twice--in it and in my rhyme


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Not mine own soul that in thee my judgment.
My eyes in you perceive the multitude of flaws,
And in you perceive the multitude of faults,
Then in the multitude of them, I am certain.
Then in your multitude, let me be unvoiced,
Although in your heart's log, I am one.
For nothing binds me, if it pleases you, bind
That nothing me, a something, dear, to thee.
  Just make my name your love, and love that enduring,
  And then you love me, for my name is Passion, aka Ardor.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
As I with mine own love may attest.
What virtue do I see in me, so great,
That I should scorn to serve thee as is due?
When all my virtues bow to thy defect,
Enslaved by the power of thy gaze anew.
O, what a temple for my sin grants
Thy beauty's name blesses my heart,
And love's own guilt may so be my guide.
  In me thou art enforced to break a twofold truth:
  Thy "love" is poison, and mine eye is dead.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To chase the gosling that has wandered far,
Neglecting those that she should love the best,
Her goslings left, their cries a distant bar;
So you, in pursuit of dreams, abandon me,
Your child, who calls out, yearning for your touch,
Forgetting all the love that once could be,
The warmth, the joy, the laughter, none as such.
But if you find your heart's elusive quest,
Remember me, and come back home, my dear.
