Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sympathy, tearing me apart,
For thee, their torment is vast.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that shines in heaven's firmament,
Can match the solemn beauty of my love's sight,
Bestowing grace on the darkest night or day.
So let your heart mourn for my plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
Is it not vengeance enough to torture
My soul, and force my dearest friend to weep?
It wrests me from myself, your tyrant hold
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Forsaken, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
  Yet, should I, in my heart, find:
  Your love, though wounded, wounds me with your very being.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine is my gain.
But when that thee is, by heaven, I lose thee,
And that I have found a joy worth more dear
Than this. Now my love is as bright as coal,
And all that is but stage, which hides behind a sunlit crest,
The bravest of my silence cannot be held.
This love, so deep, I'll play the darkest night,
If it brings thee to light, better I know,
  For darkness is the brightest love, and love,
  When I rise, it looks but modest.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And for restful love doth what she thinks dead.
I'll tell thee that my love is all the world,
And she that love's strange policy
