When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A dearer birth than my love had brought.
But when she saw me myself to some unseeing shore,
Her eyes, abandoned, and filled with might,
At my beloved's gaze, I held my peace,
As she gazed upon me, like a steadfast guard,
Though she, in learning to read, understood my vow.
Though she knows full well my love's spectrum,
Her focus, though masked, still captures my whole.
  So she keeps me, her voice,
  And her alt'ring things, I guess, they look false in disarray.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
So if I err, thou dost forgive my plight
And found fault in love, not so.
But why should I reproach thee for my lameness?
My love, so deep, know'st to my wide world
Than all-too-precious world, my love?
Why should I tie my tender care
To what I learned, that I should love thee more?
For love's appeal:
  Seek you to own what you should love?
  By self-example, thus, you'll reap the blow.


