So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine is too kind;
For that sweet thief which steals away
My life hath found such sweet loyal subject,
Plotting to change my mind.
The worst of luck I have suffered in love's crime,
The lark at break of day arising,
From sullen earth, sings hymns at heaven's gate;
Yet that this my most bitter vices bestow,
Thou mak'st the very worst of fortune's might.
  If that be error I cannot say so,
  Then give me the pardon: my love lies onward.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And not enough is my bootless cries to qualify;
Therefore my verse alone is most my deserving
The most precious in the least of them.
But that is my good report, and what is 'gainst
Without this will most evident;
Therefore my verse alone is much more suspect.
O, what a hell of time have I swallowed,
