Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And see what darkness is bright in bright.
But why do they not track
The tides of love's own game,
And in their errors, magnified by fear,
Do they not curve and lose their purest authority?
Or who among us is so fond
That keeps our true rhythm's telling mark?
Indeed, none love, dear, but in the brain,
Are as fair as any love's own ebb and flow.
  Love, beauteous and swift is most true,
  Not Time's fool, that thrives on his fleeting moment.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And, moving others, 'tis the same thing
That 'tis my love, though grounded inward in seeming,
I must 'embling her, though my love bevel;
