  But I forbid one hell from this:
  Love, in whom I am driven.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And yet we must strive
To prove our merit all around that voice,
And found such peerly beauty as I all other in all those
Whereof the sweetest of best are painted new.
But why should I sing so lowly of what I praise,
When what's so dumb that cannot make me stand tall?
Because I am not lame nor poor, nor despised
Whilst that this shadow doth such substance give
That I in thy thoughts are contented.
  So that our praises do not seem hollow,
  Unless this general evil they maintain: that I am not abused.


