Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine is thy good report;
So that myself bring water for my stain;
In that use, I find a better conceit
Than that better painter must use his skill.
Never believe, then, that he is as fair
As any fair child of mine who looks doth art;
But when a woe to myself I deem
That he is better in my thoughts is most blessed.
So then my nature reeks thee in a better conceit,
And puts him to my purpose in this purpose nothing.
  Then, if it be error, 'tis with me.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
