Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
And there reigns darkness and alluring make.
Now behold once more
My beloved's face, no whitest ever gracious,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy remorse,
Adorning thy outer walls so deep.
My heart is full, then empty, parched, replete,
Consumed by thee, thy love,
Which I cannot lack, nor can I seek to hear,
The sighs from depths only I know how.
  So I mourn thee, my love,
  For I who weep to have forgotten patience.


