Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that, in guess, they measure by thy deeds;
Then, chaste, they glimpse the truth,
And judge thy worth by thy face's array.
Then, foolishly, they misconstrue,
Their praise for thee, distorted and askew.
But why thyself, to whom thyself is driven,
Didst thou desire, to amend thy own part?
Or what is thy will, that thou mayst forsake
In thyself, to spirit's deceiving part?
  The reason lies within thy blindness,
  For common ground obscures thy true light.


