Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As if by some instinct the wretch did know
His rider loved not speed, being made from thee.
The bloody spur cannot provoke him on
That sometimes anger thrusts into his hide,
Which heavily he answers with a groan,
More sharp to me than spurring to his side;
Why should I, in joy, mine eyes be stained,
To give full growth to that which still doth grow
But see thy blood warm when thou feel'st it cold?
  For I have sworn thee fair, false heart, in lies,
  To swear against the truth, before thine


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To be your vassal bound?
It may be said I have been with unknown minds,
Though not to cherish, still, to admit, to let me know,
As a seasoned elder finds delight in a youngster's might.
Yet, by all that I've wrought, you and love are left behind,
Myself, your self, are left forlorn;
A treasure such as this is yours to claim,
And yours, your love, I claim, is my own worth.
  In truth, I find, and you, both possess,
  Two souls, more than treasure


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine;
But that is his conquest
A loss in love's expanse.
Lascivious grace, in whom all ill well shows,
That he is the better part of me.
And he is too fair whose shadow brightens,
Disdainting the sun's broad expanse.
So I have kept him for a prize so dear,
To one whose will surely be shown,
And you must live, through artful self-control.
  In him I've strayed, like a lamb,
  The better part of me.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And to my will, well-tuned, take thy heart's entirety.
With what caution I tread, thou art for myself but waking,
And in my absence, thou shalt not be missed.
For I am thine, and thine in the spring,
Thou false more, than true love's great cost.
Then look what is best, that best I wish in thee.
Then should I rest, though torment, be the only mate;
And, tender care, in which I am confined,
With others's hearts, more blessed may be the view.
  All hearts are


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love, my love's plea, gentle, bear.
Yearning will fulfill the treasure of thy love,
Indeed, fill it abundant with yearnings, and mine, one.
In matters of vast capacity, we find
Among many, one is insignificant, dear.
Then in the multitude, let me be unwritten,
Although in your heart's record, I am one.
For nothing holds me, if it pleases you, hold
That nothing me, a something, dear, to thee.
  Just make my name your love, and love that enduring,
  And then


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet see the truth in your cheek.
To say they err, I dare not be so bold,
Although I swear it to myself alone;
And, to be sure that is not false I swear,
A thousand groans, but thinking on thy face,
One on another's neck do witness bear
Thy black is fairest in my judgment's place.
In nothing art thou black save in thy deeds,
And thence this slander as I think proceeds.
  In others' works thou dost but mend the style,
  And in thy name even more.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And that unfair which I perceive;
But that she hath her eclipse endured,
And the firm soil must yield her part,
Unless it were builded far from accident;
The flowers that in gardens of delight lay.
I have seen roses damasked, red and white,
But no such roses see I in her cheeks;
And in some perfumes is there more delight
Than in the breath that from my mistress reeks.
I love to hear her speak, yet well I know
That music hath a far more pleasing sound.
  But why does she not confess her wrong?



O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
So let not winter's ragged hand deface
In thee thy summer ere thou be distilled.
Make sweet some vial; treasure thou some place
With beauty's treasure ere it be self-killed.
That use is not forbidden usury
Which happies those that pay the willing loan;
That's for thyself to breed another thee,
Or ten times happier, be it ten for one.
Ten times thyself were happier than thou art
If ten of thine ten times refigured thee;
Then what could death do if thou shouldst depart,
Leaving thee living


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express.
If I could instill sagacity, better yet,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should plunge into despair, I'd lose my reason,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
  To avoid this, and to uphold your dignity,
  Keep your gaze true, though your heart may


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
A lawful plea against myself commence.
Such civil war is in this love a paradox,
Which time and thoughts so sweetly doth deceive,
And that I in love are left in the public eye to see.
Now I stand against myself a thousand victories' sum,
To win the one by doom and thee.
And thus I'll play the role of feigned innocence,
When thou art false, blind to beauty's call.
  For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my soul,
  To one whose essence is as dark as coal.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To share in love's secret game.
Oh, if you could only be truly you! Yet, my love,
Your essence is fleeting as your breath in air;
Prepare for the end, relinquish your self-love,
And let another claim your beauty's share.
Thus, your ephemeral grace may find new life,
A continuance in your child's tender form,
Reviving you from the brink of strife,
In the cradle of a new, blossoming storm.
  Yet, should you deceive the world, I am doomed
