Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine.
But when thou reviewest this, thou dost review
The very part was consecrate to thee.
The earth can have but earth, which is his due;
My spirit is thine, the better part of me.
So then thou hast but lost the dregs of life,
The prey of worms, my body being dead,
The coward conquest of a wretch's knife,
Too base of thee to be remembered.
  The worth of that is that which it contains,
  And that is this, and this with thee remains.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And more the miles are measured from thy friend's eyes.
The beast that bears me, tired with my woe,
Plods dully on, to bear that weight in me,
As if by some instinct the wretch did know
His rider loved not speed, being made from thee.
The bloody spur cannot provoke him on
That sometimes anger thrusts into his hide,
Which heavily he answers with a groan,
More sharp to me than spurring to his side;
  For that same groan doth put this in my mind:
  My grief lies onward and my joy behind


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love my love's appeal, kind, fulfill.
Desire no blot, for mine, it is your love's price;
All mine is yours, and yours must ransom,
In you, the most precious doth reside.
But if thou live remembered not to be,
The world will wail thee like a makeless wife;
The world will be thy widow and still weep
That thou no form of thee hast left behind,
When every private widow well may keep,
By children's eyes, her husband's shape in mind.
  Look what an unthrift in the


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet like a deceived husband; so is false heart's face
Which in thy breast doth live, as thy sweet husbandry doth bear.
For in thy bosom's array thou hast passed
Thy gift, thy tables, are swerved
From whence thy love to these other things doth belong.
So thyself-love, though much, must be unwritten,
Whose influence is thine, and thine alone.
  In thee, their images I cherish yet,
  And thou, in guess, they measure by thy love.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And losing me, my love would be forgot.
But if you were to snatch this solace away,
My loss would be my love's gain,
And losing her, my friend would bear that pain;
Both together, and I'm left forlorn,
To suffer this love-heavy cross I must bear.
I cannot frown on the endless flow
Of hours, while vigilant for you,
Nor find the sting of loneliness to grow
When you command my absence to discern.
  Still, I'll wait, concealed in your liberty's hold,
  Though you'll be stolen, for


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak of my misdeed's whereabouts, and I shall tell ye
That I have him here who hast passed by your side;
Why should I reproach you for my injustice?
Or on my frailties why do you scowl that I do follow?
If you are cruel, then I begin
To wreak revenge on self, with present woe;
What reason will my gentle supplication abide,
When all my virtues bow to thy defect?
  Yet, love, persist in hatred, for I see
  The truth: you love those whom I cannot.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy heart's deepest sense, a weakness to impart.
If I could impart thee wisdom, better thus,
Though not to love, yet, love, to reveal,
As dying men, when their end is near,
Receive only good news from their physicians' lips.
For if I should fall into despair, I'd lose my mind,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this topsy-turvy world, where truth is twisted,
Mad detractors find believers in mad listeners.
  To prevent this, and to preserve your good name,



In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
A wretched weed, devoid of blood to blush through lively veins.
Thou art as fair in knowledge as in hue,
Finding thy worth a limit past my praise,
And therefore art enforced to seek anew
Some fresher stamp of the time-bettering days.
And do so, love; yet when they have devised
What strained touches rhetoric can lend,
Thou, truly fair, wert truly sympathized
In true plain words by thy true-telling friend.
  And their gross painting might be better used
  Where cheeks need blood; in thee it is abused.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To keep the love of me as youthful,
Unsuspecting of life's vast play.
Yet, you, my truest treasure, now my grief,
Do left her stronger, my worst attempts revealed
My deepest love for you, in hopes of trust.
Now, all is said and done, take what will last forever.
My love, though deferred, I'll not let that sorrow last,
Nor find fault in love, nor will you last,
Unless you leave its disgrace.
  Lest those who find favor in my plight,
  And you, my sole desire,


