Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As if in sympathy, they say my name,
And in their pity, they look into my face,
A closet, where one contracted nurse doth dwell
But dost lie in despair, doctor'er of my heart,
Whose motion is love's fashion;
I may be straight though they themselves bevel;
By their rank thoughts my deeds must not be shown,
Nor at the least of them that level;
  Unless this general evil they maintain:
  All men are bad and in their badness reign.


