Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch seem.
Then waits for the leave of the morning's veil;
In the fear, she forgets to taste;
To a child's wake, laughter on the breeze be.
She drops her babe, needs the light to remain,
And when the sun's light says she does stay,
She wonders, does your tears not wane?
Alas, when time's vows of fruition shall stand,
Her sight shall wane as a queen's, who thinks of beauty,
Whose dearest love embraced chases what seems his hour to lie.
