Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
Of the chase before thinking thoughts it could afford,
One keen-sighted bird caught one heart I had known
And set the mad poet rightly free,
Delighting in the splendor of flight and supreme pleasure.
It swooped on my love, my muse, my all,
An unfeeling frame, for it contained your riches,
But sent me far from you in my weighty frame
To seek for my portion, which it richly shows.
  Yet still it pursued, and still I pursued,
  Left me, and then, as I, your friend, were drawn.


