Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
And now look pale with sorrow's gray cloud
Upon my sorrow-laden cheek, whereof I sigh,
Making pale the look of shadows old;
So looks thou black in these distresses, if thou my wife perceive,
Thy looks like tresses doth make my sorrow seem
All pale in the withering of your cool hide;
So wilt thou be whit with withering when I meet thee.
Thou hence shouldst kill me with cold for my sake
To well my losses with future look;
  But wilt not show me pain, for wert thou thy friend,



Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And in this hell of torment I learn
To sing in a low melancholy song,
Like old-time mourners?
Now is the hour of holy quiet
When even the very air reposes
In unvoiced oblivion;
Thus, I love to hear my old trouble
Whilst it sad purpose doth daily kill
With dull sullen talk, like old rumour.
Alas, I dare not my own defect complain,
