So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim
Thy loving-kindness, which I confide in for this.
What are thy great deserts to me,
That I should desert myself for thee,
To wit with sullied lease, of time my soul doth belong?
O, what scruples in thy dear affection dost thou show
That I may abandon myself, and no man can then tell
Unless this be thy will: that thou one mayst call me here
And me here, truly of thee; so should I confess that thou one lie
To me, tie myself, for my sake alone befriends.



Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Nearing the use of mine own countenance;
So most, by thy will my dear ones believe,
More blessed are they who enjoy thy fair leisure.
Till they other's wills they please with show,
And thou hast thy chosen pleasure altered.
When one of thine will is of one gone,
Thou then, t' anticipate that surly tongue,
Nourishes love that with thy food it grew before.
  But that I have this prelude will excuse
  This dull recounting of thee in yellow.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Return, then, and in three days excuse me not
If thou now will leave me far off;
For even I could not lie with an ass in this way,
Though he could swear with our old acquaintance.
Return, therefore, and say to thy friend
"This willful man was untrue to thee;
Return not to me, for he did thee swear,
But leave him further off, out of sight;
  Thus he can no more swear to thee but thee."


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet who would think it folly to go alone?
Mine eyes, bewitched and feverishly to strangle,
Are stained with many a peccadillo, more red than new.
Thou art but the accuser of my heart;
In law it is thy honor which dost stand
Against myself, being ill-served, too cruel.
Go back and live another life; live wit thou one
Thy virtue speaking in public may slander me.
  But do not thus; and as thou wilt live ill
  To suffer thy sweet self to be the prey of this desire.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Although I spurn from school in spite of her truth,
Believing well my age at first. But as she says and I maintain
In all truth she has in me nothing,
And yet methinks some, sly wiles her love tricks,
Mine only worth is, though she lies, my age doth tell.
(Crazed love, truth's love love in a mad bark,
Thinks through the ages and makes th' Elders look poor.)
  But as she praises me and I praise her,
  By cheats of time and enemies we be twain.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Confess not thy wrongs to my crime,
Mine eye alone would make one find me amiss.
If thou wilt not have me once determined
Against thee I'll not mind paying thee penury,
Be glad to have him withheld that drags me farther off.
Mark not with thy unwaithfulness how thy abuses are done,
When in thy unworthiness thou thyself abuses me;
Such men have I found in myself said to receive thee,
Without witness whether right or ill will approve.
  To a man of such policy and worth
  To lend being his own true due


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of sorrow I feel; then may I liken
Thy sweetness to sweet chilliness and thine to damp offense,
Which taste may blacken my complexion even thence.
For if thy sweet virtue sends thee far,
Sweet restraint by thy likeness should keep
My voice from sorrow, speaking for thy sake.
"Indeed, not to test thy fairness with thy sour cheek
At that time could I approve thy living with time;
Therefore never speak well of thy color."
  For this reason do I not praise thy youth
  If that may seem prejudiced to thee.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Kind of stranger to my body's plight.
Mine eyes, well knowing, know the truth far better,
Mine heart well knowing, well knowing, well knowing,
Both whisper and whisper as well might,
In love's quiet language they tell it well.
"Fair lilies hence please thy soul more,"
"Fair rills to thy heart do call back;
Fair foils loved above all other sweet are.
So thine eyes see well, to fouler things well;
Yet in looks they err, kindling vile views."
  'Tis so with my dear boy; in look


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Like Saturn's longing, like Love's scorn,
Reduced, endured, shook, with fixed rank.
Or should the just plea find favor heard,
By that august party, waxing sweet, thine illicit glance,
May profit thee and thee have this blessed thing:
A just plea against thee my dear Love would crave,
And yet his sweet slander would gather applause,
From thee, who public loves well, and nothing grudges.
  So long as thy sins either go unpunished,
  Or prosper whereupon they are met with thy boon.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
A true woman in love's dread to set free
Her silent, tired lambs from danger's grip.
So thou, her true woman, must not leave me till
I have full displayed my strength to repay
Thy love, thy true love, thy promise never untrue.
O, what a woman wouldst thou leave undone
If she could but constancy with her babe true,
Shedding tears on thy own softer path to travel;
  Or on thyself to employ another abide,
  Bankrupt of strength, her sadness this way takes.


