Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Creep in upon the heels of those that
Haply flee, graced by remorse, to their corner.
Oh, that our dear heart might turn away,
Seeking shadows where no roses bloom,
Or that black might be the shade of the soul,
Applying to the eyes that doth torment me stark,
Stifling flames that with ardent hate heat;
May I love thee, as thou dost them torment me thus:
  Grant me the churl's night, so fitting,
  When shadows hunt not, and Death myself stands without.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And hold my love even as death's own shade?
To stake my friend's worth, see if I lose my reason,
Or if he thrive, as fair to see, 'tis by fate decreed.
I bet he thrives, because of thee, my friend's sweet desire;
He knows it, and me, for that love, two-fold I lie.
He stakes his life on this, that in my grief he deems it.
Were he to lose me, 'tis my loss; losing thee, his,
And losing him for my sake, too, by fate made.



