If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should I, of love, upon thy love's command,
Enumerate all those planets that spurn thee,
And in thine own assenting think all alone,
Making numbering to thy love
And cross that with thine own part.
But do not so. I love thee in such sort
That I in thy dust shall remain,
And thou in thy dust shall find me again,
Twoities wracked, hence commence.
  In thine own hand am I imprisoned, and thou know'st
  Not to answer for thy sake, for I am sure it is thee.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet complain that it is not on my love's face.
Why should my love be termed lovely,
When it is not in my eyes but elsewhere displayed?
Or if it be in my love, where is it displayed,
To question Conquests rights, old etiquette,
And play the fool of showing what one does view?
O, 'tis not in my love, though much is show,
That touches me above the knee.
In my eyes, Love's countenance gleams,
And in them, his veins dyed new.
