When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And leave me where I am, ignorant goose,
While she expounds on the praises she thinks are due,
And I, for my sake, as dumb as a shell,
Doeeing the praises she doth bestow,
And watching her walk, as though I were made of stone,
Unless she would devise some sweet scheme
To make me seem more rich in mind,
And hang sport on my ovaries, like her?
  Yet still, she loves me, and ever will,
  And nothing 'gainst Time's scythe can make defense.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with thy fingers not so gentle a probe
As those that play the torment game;
Let not your music do a melody abide,
Nor your sight steal the sweetest of hours.
Instead, confine this rebuke to my breast,
Which restrains not the parts that torment me;
Him in your power do create such disdain,
That to my seeming unkindness I do launch
And prove thee false, incur a loss,
And leave me to the mercy of a diseased brain.
  Such doom I bear, yet I see
