Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of anguish felt by every soul.
If you would but cease, and recognize,
The pull of love that leads me to tears,
My heart's desire, as it does now,
Yearns for you, and keeps you close.
Why use deceit when your power
Overmatches any shield I can devise?
Let me love you, as thou dost them inspire,
Whose hearts are bound, as mine, by love's embrace.
  Let us vow, with hearts on every page,
  To stand as one, united in love's alchemy.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which for their crooked reasons lies so bad.
In it, they misconstrue, mistake truth for beauty's guise,
And right for their evil doings confine;
And in their slandering, their falsehoods spread.
But why my eyes adore his cheek
And in his murky sport write green,
Why my heart all pale with secret love,
That in his hardiness, I find my blood?
  O, 'tis the blood that cheapens him most,
  And by their false clothes, he looks so graced.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Not as an enjoyer on the feast,
Or one who outshines all, unaided,
In nobler state than feasting, or play,
Or any affection laid bare, seen
To have thy sweet love reproduced.
Oh, let us compare our worth, both divine,
And see what greater gift they can reveal,
In character of one so kind, compared
To what we've expressed in words only.
  Let us love that way, and keep it so,
  As two cars, one with friends, one twirled.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
And binds it to her bosom's peak,
Wherefrom it may leave and return unconfined,
In joy or sorrow, its fresh determination unconfined.
So I, made excellent warner, began
To make my love's fresh allotment spread,
Making rooms for one more place to sleep,
That room might always be my own,
Unmoved by Time's cruel scythe that does pass
By and by, uncared for or without,
A niggardly middle distance still striving to claim.
