Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For every ill-done me I do self-defense,
And in that I can no self-will stick.
But yet thou mightst my will forbear,
Since to myself thine eye hath committed
And my own true love, though it directed bring.
What good is it to my will that my eye does hate,
Since my own true love directed towards thee?
And yet my eye does follow what it doth guide,
And puts itself upon thy change, being plain.
  Toil I will shoulder, being plain,
  To beat the living day with dead night.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
If thy eye deceive thee, and me sight it not,
Be it not sinful, though unlawful love it displayed,
To spurn my so sweet a love-led investigation.
For to thy eye my willing subject I send
Since to my sight it no longer wills;
And, gentle thief, love knows it is my duty
To trust that my eyes have no part,
For nevermore to my eyes' deceits do lay.
  Hers, by thy glass, my true face to see,
  Thine, by thy picture, my shadow to trace.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet yet eyes like Sods cannot see the True Way.
For eyes like stones, when rightly looked on,
To threepers' gross illusion pick out the truth;
Then why should Love's face be marred black,
Beated and chopped with faces quite as bad?
Let eyes be sure, Love; yet eyes wean
Of their power to see, and hear.
  For they see with eyes, and hear with hearts,
  And yet, like dumb spectators, they believe.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I in my pompous ways have spent
Years learning how to win women's hearts.
So I have told the truth, and she believes
That I have told her the beauty of my age,
Though I have no proof to show she believes it.
But why does she not admit her wrong?
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So we both believed one thing when we did know
  Not the other.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Do not with thy thoughts even tears peddle.
Begone, then, and forgive me in my ignorance;
Believe me when I tell the truth and do not condemn:
"Thus far his character looked well;"
And hence his praise shall ever be my argument.
For to thee I give account and upon thy part
Even that pearl of my youth hath lay.
So should my history be read what due it owes
To thee, to thee and no other man.
  To thee I send this letter only testament
  To prove thee and to thine able pen.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy heart's thought, not mine eye's.
If I could school thee in such wiseness as mine eye
Comes too harshly to holidays,
Be inoffensive in thy goodness;
Speak of thy pleasures, and I shall try
To understand them, though they be views of the day.
For there is no clear day in which to bless
As many birds in sullener fine swerve
From every sullener to thee I see;
Therefore to thee I send this verse,
Though not yet to thee my thought.
  To thee my sonnets mostly belong,


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And that my eyes in vain to change are.
My ears find no allure in your voice's song,
Nor do gentle emotions yearn for the lowly touch;
Nor taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
My five senses and my wisdom cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
