Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Concealing from thy heart the love they feel.
Thou art as fair in love to me,
As I in thee canst tell,
By gazing on thy glass,
Thy eyes now mirror black to me,
And I in theirs mistake;
For black is fairer than crystal eye,
Though teeth yet more foul in complexion it.
I like black eyes, but wished they were eyes of stars,
Whose brightness not only dark but fair in its care,
But that beauty should bear thy weight in my view,
Though thou art forced to shed thy own.
  Yet


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And to my disgrace even worse;
To tie me to thee, and in that shame
We do live asunder, love apart,
Overcharged with guilt the heaviest stone.
O, let me suffer, like a deceived husband;
Let not your cruelty towards me increase,
Lest my sorrow embolden your spite, and you hit me dead.
Behold how one wretched love, riven and afeared,
Strikes out at thy cruel, treacherous heart.
  I retreat, aware, to thee;
  But not before thou hast abused me so grievously.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine will thrive;
So thou hast better be gluttonous than thou art to be cruel,
Drabler than well to deceive,
And ruthless in thy gusto more than gauche in thy art.
Profitless thou art, and therefore in thy law
Thy table must be false, poor, and bare;
For where is she so fond on such trifles
That she canst not live without them?
She hath what is hers, and thou what is lost,
And she cannot live without this, that she must love
And use what is not her own.
