Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Counting no old thing old, thou mine, I thine,
Even as when first I hallowed thy fair name.
In keeping what thou hast, thou shalt have thy pleasure,
And I by this will be a bore,
Not daring to frown on thy pleasure.
But if thou shouldst suspect my absence is my will,
To pardon my dull absence, and look upon thee
When innocence reigns, and thyself be blessed.
  But do not so. Lascivious grace won't pardon
  Even from thyself to pure posterity.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
But thou hast not sworn, and will not oath,
To suffer my love to blindfold decay;
To give physic to my love thou art wise,
And he that suffers thee to be true will be dumb.
Presume on thy love, to wit's dread,
Thou false heart, with thy lover's assistance,
Cannot for fear of outer injury swear,
Against thy own bloody oath, to me untrue.
  To part from thy love, thou hast power to command,
  Since to thee I bring a substance so dear.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet who among them is ignorant
Of its worth, its beauty's cause?
Even those whom Nature hath graced with vision,
Have eyes to see, but lack lips to speak it;
For to unseeing eyes the true shape of love
Deserves no love, no tongue to speak it.
To say nought of love to eyes
Who view not with their eyes, to feel
No sentiment in their groans or cries.
  In many's looks, Love's truest form displayed,
  Yet in thy face, Love's sweet ghost lies.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's duplicitous ways.
Yet, in my sight, she holds no scorn,
A woman's love, far from being born simple,
Though she knows it's fleeting, I try
To live as though she'd never leave me.
Her eyes, though they see, cannot understand
The truth she loves, though it bleeds, it maintains,
A man's love, in women's eyes, lies.
  And thus, in my blindness, I do believe,
  To live as she does, I'll bear the pain.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Admit that thou didst love another wit,
And for such trespass love's virtue wilt be struck
And stained with guilt of illicit love.
O, pardon me, stern for my crime,
And wish I were renewed in thy love;
Bring me within thy gentle turret's shade,
But do not chide me there for my shame;
Bring me within thy loving allure's embrace,
But let me be truly chastised there.
  For I have wandered with liberty's grace,
  And in that liberty I have suffered some hurt.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The sorrow I feel, and then thou hast to understand
The depth of love's long-held dislike.
For if I could write what affection I feel,
And set apart the part that words cannot deceive,
The impression would be thine, and thou art in part blind,
Plotting against your own making.
O, should you attempt this, know this well:
Your heart is boundless, and wide within it lies,
The space where love's true feelings dwell,
If haughty thoughts should press therewithal.
  So should my tongue, which speaks well of thee,


