Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, as are the bereft mourners in love.
The mourners resemble the unworthy blackbird,
Hating that which tears most pity,
Haply cracking with scorn, as the widowed gilded cage,
Sneering every which thou hast, and more.
Thine heart, I admire, but not
For they look upon my sorrow with proud eyed regard,
And take all with a cheerful said,
Sneaking with their eyes that which their lips never reveal.
  I wonder at their gray beauty,
  And wonder that thy love hath such hue.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
