Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine own grief
May render me that much worse off than I can tell.
For to no other pass my love hath the key
Of that which myself to thee commits;
Nor can he, whose will is my love's common fuel,
Give fire to that which in mefires;
Thus, by the fixed numbers of my sighs
I do entrench his heart and make him ever so weak
As to your dearest love I throw myself away.
  For this my love, so true, is ever so poor
  That, wit thy will, I lose by title alone.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To be thy vexation and desire combined,
Than either of thee or of thy husbandry will be alone.
I am enough temptation to thee to lie,
And too tempting to be false;
Stretched pent houses I in myself complain
