Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And, blind, canst like a fool tell The way it grows where it doth grow.
My eyes, that see, and know,
Divided, look on one thing, which side the sun
Ascendeth, and, not being able to tell,
Onely surmise that it is better.
So my heart, made crooked by love's absence,
Sought but the right, and thine eye doth wrong;
  And, for right, brought in decree
  That, without thy help, me mightst with my food be killed.


