Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy heart's thoughts, which I cannot then comprehend.
If I should err and say heretical things,
Thy true love might be mis-interpreted, and I am sorry
Than she who errs in judgment accepts my humblest praise.
For should I in my folly deceive, and say
Thy true love is as white as pitch-dark slate,
When in truth it is black, as pitch-dark slate,
Whose very essence is not akin to my body,
Nor can I then, aware, distinguish
The living from the dead, when they are joined.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Mine eye, that in thee lies my all!
So that in thy vices I find in my control,
Thy whole world being single, will I,
Control every wicked effect, positive and negative.
In countless forms is my love seen,
Which to itself I measure by thy Schedule,
Finding it not enough to plague my soul with envy,
Nor to mar the beauties of thy marriage choose.
  The cupid's part, which doth thy love weigh,
  The strong link to my sour subject give.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
With that of thy son-in-law, who liveth in love;
Who can compare the pleasure that in me
Exceeds all bounds, even to thy desert.
I have daily traded gold for views,
Hath sighted more than sin, borrowed virtue from thy sight,
Made a closet as thine own, where grace and pain
Blend.
  For thee, in these trenches lies my health,
  Which against myself I can not everywhere assert.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Her eager lookers, pounced, swiftly outscoring,
One of them even fumbles, and the others just stand,
One child, no longer a husk, playing hard to grasp,
A mere fetus, rising in joy.
This case of replete loss I, a widowed sire,
Gives my love a sought-after mirth,
Ajumping into action, no notice being taken,
To greet the moment, like a mother goose, singing!
  All birds of the same height, in one glorious hue,
