So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May find a way to my death;
For how can I be sure that he will not move
To quell the blow of thy jealousy?
Then should I lose him, for I have got him so good
That I can lose him; but him, lose him not,
For I have told thee how to make him stay dead.
O, what a happy title do I find,
Happy to have his life, happy to die!
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Thou mayst be false, and yet I know it not.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And have thou not thy great prowess to show?
The charter of thy will, with me was ever in my hand,
