When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime.
Yet, ah, she knows my days are past their prime
And, knowing it, she believes it,
So, love, be happy, while it seems hard,
Let your heart believe in spite of your gaze.
Though, if it is true, though she knows it,
I do not care, for my love swears it,
And, indeed, it is true, it seems hard to keep.
  But why does she not challenge this boast,
  To prove my youth, when she knows it's true?


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak of my abuses and I shall halt,
In the recollection of thy woe,
Lest I should do it again and reprove thee.
For how can I find fault in thee, when I see
My brothers afflicted with thine, their grief and rage?
The remembrance of my past sins,
Till I forget thee, dear friend, all burning heat
Doth eat the life that was new.
O, what excuse will my bold disobedience have,
When I have done what I should do was to die?
  For thee, against myself I'll beg not hesitate,



Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy cruel thoughts, which I ought not to know.
For how can I know how long to seek the ransom
Of mine own heart, when it fears no injury?
The charter of thy being, with me,
Unsafely stands, I fear,
Defenseless, I will not debate it out.
Thou canst not, like a widowed spirit,
To hoard thyself like a queen;
Therefore, like a deceived husband, I will not tell
The story of thy heart, which I should know
If I could but feel, with plain words by thee.
 


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And doth slide naked behind their censure's bar.
Neither are my ears enchanted by thy voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
