Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Chasing shadows deep in thy heart,
Thyself away, to seek instead the light.
So I have sought thee, and found thee,
The azure of the eyes in the riper day;
But when the bloom of day is entangled,
The Lumiest flowers wilt, and Death takes thee home.
How I long to see thee, yet truly woe,
When thunder, fire, and plague do assail thee;
What worth the world, that thou mayst true born believe,
  To shun Death's shames, and doth thyself bring?


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And yet it cannot be so vile
To make my dearest friend black as in a glove?
It is not enough to torment me half-dead,
And yet it is not enough to make me bleed;
It is the one who commits thee who gives me proof
Of being deserved my cruel thought.
  Tear me from my soul, but I will not be
  Alas, but love pays that debt in part.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may see
The right of possession of his sweet appearance;
For to let him live I have sworn him good,
And hath him for my sake tied up in this.
O, lest he should take that salve which so kindly fits
His condition, afflicted with feigning fealty,
Or else sell it for a lure that dulls the heart.
Nay, 'tis not love, though great in you is borne,
That dims the splendor of your love's abundance.
  Lo, true love in action does shine so,
  For to ourselves it freezes up, pent with lust


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And ten times happier enjoyer than I am.
I have had my capoe with four friends sated,
And by their wills, thy ten times happier than I was.
So should the world's will, which is capricious,
Give hearty granted, that better mayst share
The joy of this single joy.
In many's will, my will aside,
By many unthrifts held, thy single will.
  But let me not to the world's will be forgot,
  Since what I do, thou dost by me understand.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Sweets with sweets war; doth love, like a sun, distinguish
Those sweets which nature hath not made for us.
If any man, be he blind, hold'st not love,
While love doth one thing in himself differentiate.
Ah, but view thyself in the glass,
By thyself the world's looking glass doth deceive;
By thyself thou art deceived, and the world deceives thee.
  But neither will the glass, nor the star discern,
  If thou survive looking in their laps.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But perceive not the man behind the curve.
So thou false Guide, Love, what difference dost thou make
By Lands checked, between high and low?
O, none, unless this miracle have might,
That in thy chart mightst appear as a ship
With fast connexion with the wisest wind.
  But prove me love, next to no worth,
  That higher truth doth it appeal.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And throw me a cloak when she pleases.
But well I know her falsehoods, and,
Like a deceived husband, I endow
My heart in lies, 'gainst my friend's eyes.
And though she believes me gullible,
I still think her right, though she lies to me,
For she loves those above my pay would cut me off,
And I must look for my own true love to subsist.
  But love, though she loves those above,
  Claims me gullible, though I know well her truth.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Give not your strength to hurl my heart,
Lest I should do it myself.
If you must leave, then part not in the end,
When lesser wrongs have done their spite,
But come upon me swift; thus, I shall comprehend
The fullest weight of all thy complaints;
Then, of all things, will I know
The least of all your wrenches;
  And other strains of woe, which now seem woe,
  Compared with losing you will not seem so.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy qualities, which I may not then translate.
If I could only be vile and forget
The men that smeared my sport in thy behalf,
Do not, when I, dear doth acquaintance,
Make sweet the insults which I should lamely carry.
O, lest, being tongue-tied, thou dost swear
Thy true-telling tongue against me in my sight,
  Saying that I do love thee, though thou dost hate me now.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which with mine eyes in righteousness doth bugle with loud notes,
Thyself as the sovereign doth hand the song.
I have seen the red blush of thy cheek
And in mine eye the mark of love doth spot,
But it is my soul that I love today.
So must my glass reveal the truth of love,
Finding in thyself the fairest eye;
Until next thyself upbraves me,
O'ercharged with self-love's proof.
  Then can I not, love, say I loved thee;
  Till then, thou gav's


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Or to thy sweet self an heir;
And for thy sake even so doth my name decay,
And in curse's mouth a new name give.
O, but for thy sake even so doth my name decay,
And in spite of myself will kill
With a bastard shame conceived in thine own bud.
O, but for thy sake even so doth my name die,
And thee, in spite of yourself will kill.
  And yet so do you exceed in everything,
  Being made fairer than the filching son.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
The mothers, once unceasingly busy, now going about their own
Duty, which more blessedly they should do.
But the joy of home, which like a river flows
To the dry parts of the world,
Where wasteful rivers make their mud
And grand ebb and flow,
No longer able to displease.
The dear time of year, like a dear heart,
Bestows its warmth on the cold,
Bestowing on the feasts of reason.
