Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As forms with shadows, like beguiler tongues,
Will tenderly follow the shuddering gusts of night.
I dislike to lie in wait, aware of dread,
But like a sad slave, if need be,
I'll wait, knowing thou art hard
To leave me, when thine eyes are plain.
O, what a torment wouldst thou prove
To leave me, when all the world is plain!
Then how should I feel pity, when I no more can see,
Than from thy watching eyes, which tell a different story.
  For thee, watch I while


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to suffer, see?
It wrenches me from my own core, and binds
Another self, more cruelly, to your will;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, find
Our hearts entwined in torment, thrice multiplied.
Imprison my heart within your fortress chest,
But grant my friend's friend a box of blissful solace.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart be their guest,
For none can then oppress me in that space.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may live.
I have signed myself away
To yield myself up to thy dearest love,
Though thou art still dearer than my life can decay,
For I see a better state to me belongs
Than that which I debat o'er with second part.
So I lose him, not by his will,
But by thy own wilful disobedience,
For I am enslaved by that which I learn to hate.
  How much more doth I lose by paying too much rent,
  Than by paying too much rent thou art assured of.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And therefore are attainted with blame,
Of many, one of thee is added to my mind,
Which gives another place to dwell.
I will not much mourn for that which thou dost take,
Since that my friend canst no more be dead than born this way,
From whence he mayst come and part;
  And that him I do love with that love
  Which makes me most jealous, being full of thy will.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thou art as fair to me as any child of light,
Though not as stars, whose distant glow I see;
Thou art as fair to me in thinking,
Though not so bright as in the day's light,
And therefore dost leave thy mark behind.
But be sure thou art not deceived:
Lest my sight should tell the time
And make thee seem elder than thou art.
  For there lives more life in one of thee
  Than both thy glass can show thee.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet cannot tell where it grows or where it roams;
They see the rud'st or gentlest sight,
And that is as thy lack of sense doth tell;
Yet, Love, thou art so possessed
With mental eyes that travail at thy heart,
Who, having windows to see,
Look on thy glass, and th' account find.
Thou canst not hide the clear light
That doth show thee how thy beauties grow;
  But it is not so with me,
  To keep an eye open all the while.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's duplicitous ways.
Yet, if she believes me gullible she thinks
My worth is but a mask of deceit,
And I, in truth, do believe her true love is
More precious than gold or gems rare.
So, love, you have sworn to me true,
And I, in turn, believe you've paid the cost;
For she believes me gullible, unwise,
And I, in turn, believe her false tongue,
Which swears more than honeyed breath.
  But why does she not admit her wrong?
  Love


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak not to me that I need but assent,
And therefore must I excuse thy unworthiness
With lies supposing a better faith.
Thou art as fair as any mother's child,
Though not so bright as the heaven's own shining star,
And therefore thy worth's obscurity make me sigh;
For where is she so fair whose beauty
Disdains the praise that hath thee?
  O, none, unless thou be begotten of take.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy core self-disdain, thoughts of me are unutterable.
If I could impart wisdom, better thus,
