O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Exert thy strength, make me comprehend
Thy most cunning stratagems, and
ILL yet kill me with kindness, grant
That we may resolve this love dispute.
O, let us not to this debate engage,
Wield strength against strength, and grant
That we may end this plight:
  Mine heart is as untamed
  As thy wild-eyed monster is tame.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy heart's thought, not mine own boldness.
If I could only be truly thou,
And understand what "fair, kind, and true" means,
And how "fair, kind, and true" couldst thou define
For thyself, being so unprovident?
Be as thou art, and thine own worth be exposed,
Forwise thought will show thee how to make
Thyself esteem beauteous things above.
  Then may I dare to love thee, being as I am,
  Unless thou wouldst use the strength of my tongue to tell.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And for that sin there is no remedy,
It is so grounded inward in my heart.
For if it be false, 'tis the greater sin
That mine eye hath love's naughty game with thee;
And for my sake even so my eye hates
That it itself for my sake doth practise what it fears.
O, what a depth of wickedness in my heart is committed,
That it slyly adorns thy wrong with grace,
Making sin where none hath done it before!
  All men's secrets are thus with me,
  That I may notest know thy sweet theft.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And having thy love begotten convert,
Pointing thy finger at me and then feigning disdain,
As if in reproof to thyself I were up-tied.
How would thy lovely anger be maintained
Against the penalty of a frown,
If thou couldst but understand
The worth of thy neighbour, to whom thou art confined?
  Then wouldst thou change thy thought,
  And take a new friend, to whom thou art.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
She pegs the moment, and keeps the delight
So delight endearing, that no tongue
Could ever more o'er-excite nor delight
More emphatic be than in response to your question.
"Ah, how much more doth beauty beauteous seem
By that sweet ornament, the window to the soul,
Which in itself so dignifies the tomb
And gives the painter's art a span and enlarge."
  Then, were not this pearls a paradox,
  But precious souls, made from desolation riches stored.


