Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And, cherishing what they find unright,
They but survey and approve thy counterfeit.
They look, and yet they perceive
Not by sight, but by thy swerving of their gaze,
Which doth thy beauty sway to and sway
How thy gracious heart may find the best.
And, Love, where is thy heart,
That thou hast mightst in thought shape such might
To lie against thy sweet self-love?
  O, none, unless it be Believed by all,
  And to be so is better to be vile.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows full well my days are past their prime.
But why does she not speak of her injustice?
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's sweet deceit is speech,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
So I swing back and forth, both young and old,
In love's strange way, both on my brave,
Spending time and words that once were mine.
  And still, love, to day I swear her truth,
  And still, I think her untrue, though she believes.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Give me the strength to bear thy disdain,
And do not harm to thy dignity.
Lest I should do thee wrong, name
And do thee disgrace, thou know'st my want;
Grant me the strength, O thou voice of my complaint,
That I may speak of thy oppression;
Speak well of me, and I straight will cease,
Unless thy sake shall depend upon my praising thee.
  For thy sake, think on thy own worth,
  For I can speak of thy abuses but thee.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My woe and sorrow, bewail me there.
If thou wouldst use sweet grace to make me sad,
As aftermath to a happy day,
When that thrifty goddess of my sorrow
Shall compel my tongue to speak of thy wonders.
Mark how withal thy worthiness does make me woe,
And what a riches and what a temple
The world may make me pay in splendour to thee.
  But do not soothe me; I am poor, and what I pay
  Deserves no thank, and thou no kind of praise.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And doth righteously misconstrue
To all the wrongs in thy crime.
Mine own true love hath in thee been
Strumpeted, foully abused,
Make war with war, and be the best of lovers
Against myself a thousand revolutions fight;
Dulling myself with a thousand gazing eyes,
Who but now their frowns and praises address.
  For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
  To one whose beauty and my mistress' eyes.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Or to thy tone confound'd with thy governance,
As heap towards the purposed overthrow.
For thou art aspired, as I,
And I, being biased, will betake
At first his pleasure, with store still.
Then may I, truly love, but truly write,
And him that doth my carnal hate confound
On thy just title confound'd by thy truth.
  Look what is just, that well may know:
  Thy right is thy bad faith,
  Unless thou wouldst a true persuasion make.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Returning to the groom, who in pride
Delights in his new found freedom.
So, Love, be still, and let your heart reply:
"Had he but more might, borne in release,
A nimble child, like your own, might soar
In your presence, making speed like night
And shine like morning dew, unmarred.
Rather, let him go, since even in death's shade,
You'll rise, and together, persevere,
Through darkness, and brilliance, and pain, and resilience,
You'll emerge, and keep him close,



