Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Who to thee have lent their spirits even so.
Yet see what good turns eyes for eyes have done:
Mine eyes have drawn thy shape, and thine for me
Are crowned with tears of love, which I may not taste;
Yet due to thee, a great thing
Unmsolved should be thy award.
For black is the shade of the soul,
And eyes this, a pencil thin,
To thy fair 2 with grief writers have drawn it.
  Yet well I think, for I see a better state
  Than at first in love's creating.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It wrenches me from my own core, and binds
Another self, more cruelly, to your will;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, find
Our hearts entwined in torment, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may possess
His live in future state with dignity.
Therefore to give back in satisfaction of my loan
I'll be contented but not at all forego,
For that selfsame gave life to thee,
And that is thence my possession again.
So then my will is be defiled,
My essence is thy will, and life no longer
Self so indigestable may remain.
  Therefore, sweet thief, thou artrs
  Forgot to give back in payment of thy self-mortgage.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And as I be overcharged shall be in receivings thrice.
Give me the fortitude, to face thy hate,
The coward conquest of a confined doom.
Then give me leave to die,
Whilst thy strong hand against myself I clad,
And by thy sorrow I am being charged.
Lay not bar to my crooked report,
Lest I be found guilty of theft in thy case.
  Forrskinny me with rare assault will take,
  And thou with public kindness will pardon me.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Sweets with sweets war not, joy delights in joy;
Therefore, tenderhearted, thy friend's fee shall be thy scorn,
For thy dull-hearted heart will bear thy grief.
Thou canst not, love, spiteful, obstruct
The peaceful ordering of thy affairs;
Proceed thou, then; a thine own dignity
Dost confirm thy peace.
  If some suspect of ill masked not thy show,
  Then thou alone kingdoms of ill shouldst owe.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet, Love, you have hidden all that well.
Why should thy glassed eyes, which view
But see the withering of love,
Thyself possessed, having no previous growl?
No wonder then thy mirror shows what
Thyself reflected shouldst in it be;
In it is thyself reflected still,
Which wit mayst in future days be seen.
  Then say I ly of thee, for I love thee most;
  But yet it is not said I love thee in that sort.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And so deceive me I cannot be above
The fray of new-fangled spite and look.
Yet, love, be of thyself so wary
As I not for myself but for thee will,
Bearing thy heart, which I will keep so chary
As tender nurse her babe from faring ill.
Trust me, then, for I am thy heart's nurse
And even so I will play the traitor to thee.
  For I must repay the love thou dost lend,
  And so repay it, I blow myself abroad.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Why should I reproach thy bareness,
When I am stained with countless perjuries?
Grant me the pardon: my love is good
Though thou art kind; and therefore thy hurt
Is not my doing, though I feel it.
Even for this allow me the sigh:
Thy friend and I are enemies,
And everafter break love down as soon as thou art done.
  Then take thou what thou wilt; a happy death
  Shall hang me up in heaven's sweetest air.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy own weakness, kindness, and true-telling.
If I could school thee in the art of banter, better still,
Though not to cherish, still, to admit, to let me know,
As ailing men, when death's shadow nears,
Receive only glad tidings from their healers' reports.
For if I should succumb to despair, I'd lose my mind,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this topsy-turvy world, where truth is twisted,
Mad detractors find believers in mad listeners.
  To


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And doth it write all things ill that believe.
For it is the pinnacle of height in me
That my heart Mini me with praises biases,
Thy glass reveals the lines of thy face,
Not to foretell of things to come,
But to say of me that I am crowned
With love's eternal praise.
O, what a temple for love's remembrance
Makes up the slave with beauty all bare,
Beholding thee, my glass reveals the lines of thee,
Not to foretell of things to come,
But to say of me that I am gazing on thee.



Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Or what is most holy, thy vile in praising.
For to thee I due such praise as thine own worth
Of loving thyself, more worthy are my praises than thy praises,
Or what is most holy, thy filthy telling.
So thy secrecy may well be said
To be false, being full of thee;
But that I am not told my injury
To others than I shall know,
And even so my adverse party is right.
  For they that condemn thee confine
  All things to themselves, which is their due.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
She gives not a pigeon's flight, but a god's change
To every bird that crosses her path.
Her love is so boundless, her care free,
That she allows no winter's hand to pass,
Her riches are not divided, but lent freely,
From child to child, and back again,
In countless birds, her love is heard,
In every breath that fills the lungs of men.
  She offers not to the hungry, poor, or vile,
  She feeds all, and often refigures them all.


