Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As shadows dance, as day's cruel hand.
They've carved crosses where thou art not,
And graves where thy spirit dwells,
As shadows dance, as the night's cruel guard.
Thou art as fair to see,
As any child of thine, though orphaned,
Under the care of others' hearts;
Yet like the night's captain, he too must go,
Whose softly fading, his wits do seem.
  So thy bright star may fade,
  And like the day's cruel heart, depart.


