When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime.
But why does she not say so,
When she knows well my days are past their prime?
And why do I not boast of my age,
When I see others almost twice as bright?
For love's sweetest habit is to feign trust,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So, in our errors, we find comfort
  In lies, and so we to each other's aid.


