In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And doth mine eyes in thee wrongs desolation.
Mine ears find no allure in thy voice's song,
Nor do gentle emotions yearn for the lowly touch;
Nor taste or scent, seek to indulge in the sensual spree,
In any lustful feast with thee in the spot and clutch.
My five senses and my wisdom cannot,
Dissuade one infatuated heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my sorrow,


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Or to thyself thy guilt be perfectedacked.
For to thy sensual fault I bring in sense--
Thy adverse party is thy advocate--
And 'gainst myself a lawful plea commence.
Then can no hate toward me stand,
When I am coun to thy sweet self and heart,
Whose gentle veins have syphilis dyed.
O, then may I slander thee--
Whose unworthiness hath not thy show.
  But since they are so drawn together,
  Suspect equals malice in me.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Till the child's time is done, and vanished in night,
Replaced by another, more cherished,
A child's time, like a dieden ship,
Sunken, robbed of its advantage,
Leaving naught but tears and sighs behind.
So I, in love's dread, forget to say
The sacred words of love's own rite,
In love's own power, I start to fray,
Overwhelmed, I lose my sight.
  For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
  To one who holds not love, but fear


