Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Nightingale and stealing flowers with pleasure.
They look mournful, and I dare not plead,
Knowing well their hearts are swayed by pity,
Adorning the evening in elegiac hue.
Yet pity alone may cause my heart to woe,
As well I know, thy color inspires sorrow,
And drops like night's night-dark veil,
Into the dawn's sanctuary, fade softly away.
  Yet suffer not in silence so deep,
  When thine eyes companions mock thy plight, too cruel.


