Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet believe it not, love, to say they see it not.
For they look but with eyes this way,
And measure all men's looks by their own account;
And that, in guess, they never write;
So thy picture, which to thy sight doth belong,
For names themselves in their hearing are drowned,
Like cannibals where their comrades starved;
  And even so thy sweet semblance, which to thy eyes doth belong,
  Receiv'st thy sweet love, which to thy eyes doth cause.


