Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy heart's enemy, a tormentor ever so.
But why should I reproach them for their sport,
When I am stained with countless perjuries?
My bosom is endeared with all hearts' delight,
And every eye that smiles at my wrongs
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, find
Our hearts entwined in torment, thrice multiplied.
  For I have painted thee as fair,
  To make myself a wretch, and bring myself down.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to suffer, see?
It thrills me, but not enough to torture me alone,
Nor can it make me none.
O, no, it cannot make me none,
Nor possess that hate that it holds in store.
It knows not the strength to torment me alone,
Nor I, captive to be its thrall.
  But if it be torment enough to torture me,
  Then I shall bring to your notice, and then you shall know.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim thy bed.
So, him who boasts of his love for me,
I shall domestically provide
For his pleasure, without thy knowing,
By myself retaining his dear skill.
I may not maintain an independant stand,
Nor leave him till my own decay,
While he insults me beyond the plank,
But feast my heart on thy vary
The freedom that thou dost give me.
  He who grants me this liberty,
  Tells me that thou of me mayst withhold.


