Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Now adorned with cunning hands to deceive and grieve.
Yet thy heart hath pity, and doth lead
Thy glass to show my heart's sorrow stained black;
In sleep a black night hath brought,
And peeked through my glass, to see what it Said.
The morning of thy sake
Lifts my heart, and keeps my drooping eyelids open;
But when thy great mirror shows me thee awake,
Enslaves my other two shadows to witness thy pleasure;
  And nothing can surpass the glimpse thou gives thee of me.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And hang my faults upon his scorn?
It wrests me from myself, your tyrant hold
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Forsaken, I, that self, and you, behold
Our hearts entangled, thrice tormented be.
Imprison my heart in your unyielding breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment with fear.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to be your own


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may win,
By submission to thy decree.
But why should I not then pay the fell hand
Whilst my life hathIENCE to die?
My purpose not so noble that it sees
To win by conquest and not by rest.
So, thou suborned informer; a true soul
When most impeached stands least in thy control.
  The reason why I do not reproach thee is
  That I never writ, nor no man ever loved.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And yet I must strive to live alone,
Unless thou and I are twain.
For WHO hath her hand in crime graced,
Which hand so vile a thief as mine mayst learn?
Or who hath her pleasure robbed,
Which hath his sharing in common?
I do envy him that hath his pleasure so profaned,
And hath his brother graciously bragging;
I wish my own virtue were as admired,
  As those stolen goods which I see built anew.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Till it be refused, then take heed,
That in thy blasphemous boasting there lies
Thy own weakness, for I may not thrive
Unless thou take heed of what thyself dost say.
O, what a mansion have I seen
Housed in form and in goodness so!
How have I dressed myself in such a way
To make myself despised everywhere!
  All days are dark till I see thee,
  And nights are bright when I sleep in thy sight.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And, lacking eyes, are possessed by a trance;
Yet, Love, you've wrought a craven's crown,
And given false viewers eyes another's as well.
Why should thy gift worth aught to me,
When I am supposed to be the very poorest of all beings?
Nor canst thou share in my joy
When all the world is greeing and praising thee.
  Yet, love, what's so blessed-fair that it fears no blot?
  Thou gav'st me thine not to give back again.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And of my worth, though it seems untrue,
She thinks me gullible, unwise,
And proud of that which she conceals not.
But why does she not say so,
When she knows full well my days are spent?
And why do I not boast of my age,
When I see myself am no longer wished?
For I have told her truth, and she hath told
That I am of your making.
  And yet she believes me, though I do hide,
  To swear against the truth, before my eyes.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Strike with thy hand when I have writ
Thy worthy praise, more than thou hast seen.
O, learn to read what nature hath writ,
Though I should holy thy subject depart;
Till thou become the triumphant poet
And subdue all his praises unto thee.
  Then remain I, oppressed with woe,
  That murd'rous vice hath not bestowed,
  But prize thyself most.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy heart's being, which in others I may not such ease.
If I could learn to read what souls desire,
More blessed than the blessed art thou,
Wouldst be the second best of pleasure,
And so, thrice blessed be thyself.
For if ten of thine ten times refigured thee,
What could the love of two souls be,
Save breed, to which thyself brought forth?
  O, learn to read what thyself doth say.
  To hear with thy eyes belongs to thy love.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And for that sorrow doth strike me dead.
O, blame me not if I no more can write!
Look in your glass, and there appears a face
That overgoes my blunt invention quite,
Dulling my lines and doing me disgrace.
Were it not sinful, then, striving to mend,
To mar the subject that before was well?
For to no other pass my verses tend
Than of your graces and your gifts to tell.
  And more, much more, than in my verse can sit
  Your own glass shows you when you look in it.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Or whether in thyself nor in the looking glass,
Or any window painted with thine approval,
Makes offend either by thyself or me.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me half so ill,
To set a form upon desired change,
As I'll myself disgrace, knowing thy will;
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy walks, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name no more shall dwell,
Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
  For thee


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Till fate's unyielding grip disfigures
The gift she once gave, and then disfigures
In some hidden place, where beauty's kindred dwell.
So I, held in splendors unsworn,
Crawls to my untamed sweet one's aid,
Her eyes, a furnace, where the ember reappears,
Gazing on darkness, as the blind do attest.
  For in their absence, even shadows flee,
  And in their stead, darkness fades and is fled.


