Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Sincere wreathed in misery's woe,
Gaze once more on my sorrow's endless plain.
Yet, for heaven's sake do I mourn,
And trust they'll look another way,
Thy heart's compassion judging thy worth;
Until now their faces have turned pale withetor,
And lose their old beauty in thee.
  For thou art fair as any mother's child,
  And wert thou first created, grandchild of thine.


