Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, as compassion's ally.
The morning sun, in its heavenly course,
Nor the bright star that ushers in repose,
Can compare to the beauty of thy remorse,
Gracing the evening with its eve's glow.
Oh, let thy heart embrace my despair,
As mourning becomes you, in every trait,
Then I shall proclaim darkness is bright,
And all that's lovely, thy heart creates.
  So I'll confess, black is the shade of the soul,
  When embraced by thee, it makes beauty whole.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It wrenches me from my own core, and binds
Another self, more cruelly, to your will;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you, find
Our hearts entwined in torment, thrice multiplied.
Imprison my heart within your fortress chest,
But grant my friend's heart freedom in its place.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart be their guest,
For none can then oppress me in that space.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
 


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine shall have
No cause to reproach you but on thy part.
Therefore, love, improve thy dwelling be
More pleasing to the view than wasteful space,
And make him another self for love of me,
Whose value is less than my own,
So that my inconsolable spirit
May find rest in that self thou art locked
And keep him here whose value I am debat.
  That's so sweet and so true a gift
  That I shall bear the cost of it myself.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And yet I must acknowledge thy friend.
It is not thy love that mends my blot,
But my love that doth mine eye mortify.
Mine eye saith true, and thine else;
And 'Tis not solely that she loves me although I do hate,
But I ought not to desire what I think right.
  But mine eye saith true, and thine else;
  And 'Tis not solely that she loves me although I do wrong.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thou art as fair to me as any mother's child,
Though not so bright in head, like the sun;
So are my loves, though not so bright in heart,
Like to the dyer's hand, dyed by its own embrace.
But if thy glass shows thee things conspired,
Be dumb in response to my scowl,
And do not reproach me with thoughts of tomorrow.
  And do not sooth my love, whine be,
  Since nought in me can make thee moan.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet eyes this cunning want to carry it out.
My eyes, entangled by a lover's gaze,
Are moored within a harbor, where all hearts sway;
Yet, Love, you've wrought a twisted mirror's snare,
That warps my heart within its twisted sight.
Why should my heart believe in twisted lines,
When it knows well the unity of our cause?
Or should my eyes, which gaze, insist on division,
To crown the lie with beauty all along?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have wandered,
  And now to illusion's snare


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And play the aged fool, age's playstyle,
When I am gone and she still holds the pose.
But why does she not then declare,
"I am your true love,"
When I am grown and have lived in this earth?
And why do I not then say,
"Now I love you most,"
When I am grown and have loved you since first I saw you?
For I am your self, and you in me
Are three aspects combined, having one love;
So I cannot well love one thing alone,
And two of four in love's composition grow.



O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Speak truly, yet in my soul boast
Thy virtues do remain, unused, at bay.
For whether I see thee or not,
Just before my eyes, forged in strife lies,
My heart bends to its will, like the dyer's hand.
No wonder then, my view is often marred,
The sun itself waits for heaven's light to shine.
  All days are shadows till heaven's light appears,
  And nights are stars when putrescent heaven Awakes.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
