Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Death's torment, in elegiac tones,
Drowned by the symphony's gentle tears.
Oh, should the world request my verse to rehearse,
To celebrate thy memory, dear friend,
My verses but one end in view are laid,
To witness to the miracle thy heart did inspire.
Though elsewhere in the world thou art celebrated,
Within thine own soul thou art truly mourned,
And to thee my sympathies tend.
  How many a poignant tear, yellowed with age,
  Wherefrom thy beauty wanes, hidden from the light.


