Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And nothing finer I'll wear than thine openly.
But if thou thyself dost hate me, the law of my love
Shall give me graces thrice more than mine own desert.
Thou art so possessed with murd'rous hate
That 'gainst thyself thou stick'st not to conspire,
Seeking that beauteous roof to ruinate
Which to repair should be thy chief desire.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Now stand you on the top of happy hours,
And many maiden gardens, yet unset,
With virtuous wish would bear thy living flowers,
Much liker than your painted counterfeit.
So should the lines of life that life repair
Which this time's pencil or my pupil pen
Neither in inward worth nor outward fair
Can make you live yourself in eyes of men.
  To give away yourself keeps yourself still,
  And you must live, drawn by your own sweet skill.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And how to the ear it doth belong,
In all those qualities wherein it excel.
Yet know'st thou not the strength to sing
When others can give thee that due?
Thou art so possessed with murd'rous hate
That 'gainst thyself thou stick'st not to conspire,
Seeking that beauteous roof to ruinate
Which to repair should be thy chief desire.
  That use is not forbidden usury
  Which happies those that pay the willing loan.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And for my sake even so swear,
Seducing with false compare,
Making me out to be charged with abandonment.
But since she knows I am wealthy and famous,
And though she knows I never saw such beauty glow,
To me, of her beauty being showed,
I do adopt that she sees my worth above,
And keeps my name from all unauthorized use.
  This is to be her pride, and she accepts this truth,
  For my sake even so she swear to me I am alone.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Exert your strength, and I shall feel
The utmost force of thy hostility.
Be where thou desireth, thy right so immense,
That thou thyself dost absolve thy neighcy;
In others' hearts there lives but me;
And for myself mine own satisfaction
Is brought almost thence by thy must.
  How can I then be hell-foraging,
  When thou thyself dost beguile the world?


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy sweet thoughts, which should be thyself in mind.
If I could endure be the lamb of thy praise,
Like obedient subject, whose shade is expressed
By the light of thy voice,
How far I would come and go, depending on thy will;
No more receiving thrills than eat,
And oft be twain with thy anger, being tongued.
But lest, being grieved at my doing,
Thy self should do that which thou didstray,
  I must attend the salmon of thy sorrow.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And that is this house whereon it proceeds.
From hence my thoughts why should my heart shouldger
Stipulate hours on thine external fair,
When it knows well the worth of thy soul?
Or why should my eyes, which see,
Which in thee little glimpse the deep?
No, sweet boy, thou art well aware
Thy beauty hath in its depths nothing novel.
It is but my love that hearts canmage,
And that my eyes, although they see, cannot make out;
  For thee I write with mine own eyes,
