Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Bestowing a sorrow filled with tears.
Upon my sorrow's blackened brow, I see,
The dawn's first light, gracing the morning bright,
Another morning's bower, as heaven's second self,
Gazing on me, as if to steal my life.
Yet, as heaven's second self, you've gone beyond my reach,
And I, a lonely wolf, have chilled in woe,
To accept your final offer, since spite has won.
  But since winning you leaves me still,
  Take my love; the joy of this love shall survive.


