So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine can have
His dear affections without thy boon.
Thus have I had him as a dream doth flatter,
Or as the tide islatest, wanting no hand.
For how do I find my living if he belongs to thee,
Whose love is so profound that he owes me so?
How do I find my worth if he thinks me so fair
To set a value on things rarest thou dost show?
  Thou art so captivated by his praise thatst thou stand'st
  To pay the price of a dream's flattery.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And yet I must confess thou art too harsh in thy kindness.
Thou art as rich, and I, being poor,
Can no more vex thee than I by thy neglect.
Lest I should import faults where I think I see virtue,
Nor thou with public kindness honor me
Unless thou take that honor from thy name.
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Sweets with sweets check each other's atmospheres,
And bad things become better by being bad.
For how can beauty's sweetest breath cope
With the wrackful siege of night,
When the basest worms are crawling 'round the chamber?
O, how can thy soul find rest in such plight,
When thou art with the clock fixed and thrice bettered?
  O, none, unless this miracle have might,
  That in thy presence time might be thy undoing.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And, Bunny, what is your substance, that you lov'st so!
Yet do thy worst to steal thyself away,
For they see the rud'st and tattered end
And, tender churl, want to keep it warm;
So should my glass, which shows me where thou art,
Bestow a more sharp sight to thee.
Thou canst not so much win, for I see
Thy glass knows the true worth of what it doth show;
So will my eyes, which, conscious of their lace,
To lean against themselves, and be thy slave.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And play the game of feigned innocence,
Who says nothing, yet walks beside you,
Her looks questioning if I am mad.
But why does she not confess her wrongs?
And why do I not admit my age?
For love thrives on feigned trust and belief,
And the aged in love dislike revealed years.
So we deceive one another in our flaws,
And with sweet lies, we comfort our disgrace.
  So we paint one another's faces,
  And with sweet lies, we soothe our disgrace.


