Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
To me, who languished because she loved not,
Her cold departure felt like a winter's fray.
Her heart, touched by remorse, then did show
A tender touch, a softer blow,
And taught her tongue, with a healing seal,
A kinder phrase, a tender appeal:
"I hate" turned into light,
Like dawn's first blush on the horizon's face,
Bringing hope, banishing the night,
Replacing despair with love's embrace.
  "I hate" she set aside,
  And saved my heart, with love, she tied.


Poor soul, the center of my sinful earth,
Pressed with these rebel powers that thee array,
Why dost thou pine within and suffer dearth,
When nature's ardent fire sings a mournful song?
Thou art too dear for my possessing,
And like enough thou know'st thy estimate.
Yet do thy worst to steal thyself away,
For I see thee striving to reach the heights;
Even so I fear the worst of this unrest
Will prove better than loss of thee.
  Pity me, then, and wish I were thee,
  As I swallow down the plague that pigs have stoked.


My love is as a fever, longing still
For that which longer nurseth the disease,
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill,
And needs no cold, no salutation to find;
It is as a bastard to his blessed mother
Who steals him away from her side,
Or dies of in poverty, whereupon his name dies with shame.
I have witnessed countless mornings, bright
And clear as any where else in the world,
But then, within that sun's shade,
My love fell a-doting, full of yearn,
And found no sweet delight in that bower.
  For nought in that realm she sees,
  Save breed, to whom her child doth give birth.


O me, what eyes hath love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight!
Or if they have, where is my judgment fled,
To say within thine own fresh eyes doth lie?
If any, be a satire to decay,
And do not unpleasant moments dampen thy humor.
Roses have thorns, and silver fountains mud;
Clouds and eclipses stain both moon and sun,
And loathsome canker lives in sweetest bud.
All men make faults, and even I in this,
Authorizing thy trespass with compare,
Myself corrupting salving thy amiss,
Excusing thy sins more than thy sins are.
  For to thy sensual fault I bring in sense--


Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot
My own identity, by thee bound?
Who hates thee that I name as my ally?
On whom dost thou frown that I do adore?
Indeed, if thou dost scowl on me, I try
To wreak revenge with tears that flow no more.
What virtue do I see in me, so great,
That I should scorn to serve thee as is due?
When all my virtues bow to thy defect,
Enslaved by the power of thy gaze anew.
  Yet, love, persist in hatred, for I see
  The truth: you love those whom I cannot


O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
And in thy vile deeds think I see another way?
Believe not, if now I seem to thee disposed,
That in mine own thoughts thou sheets of thine hand.
Grant me the strength, though mine own heart be swayed,
To bear thy scythe, though it sharpens my heart.
Be not unwavering, though my life thou hast done,
To out a rebellion against thyself.
  Thou sly Deceiver, love, what mightst thou know
  To make me in thy book true, even there.


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
Lest you become entangled in my wrongs.
For, as you deceive me, I abandon
My nobler nature to my body's treachery.
My spirit informs my flesh that it may claim
Victory in love; the flesh, no longer tame,
Rises at your mention and proclaims
You as its prize, its conquest and its aim.
  And thus, in pride, it serves you, ever near,
  To stand by you, to fall with you, my dear.


In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn,
And for that sorrow me aunt gave such grip,
That I by suicidal looks could not jump
To my sweet-purchased seat, nor my friend's neck
For ransom keep under sweet captivating grip.
But since thou art so rich and famous,
And for my crime thou art Baroned,
And for my sake even so thou hast be civil
And so are thy public promises.
  For I must own thee, and thou dost own
  Foreign princes, and yet we two must be one.


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steep.
The maiden had her fill, and plunged the brand in a well nearby,
But found it was not enough to quench his flame.
The water could not compare, its power weak and hushed,
Nor curing his love-kindling, so much confined.
The waters could not compare, their power faint and spent,
Nor would his brand's remedy keep him from alone.
The fool had his brand, and plunged it in a well nearby,
But found it was not enough to quench his fire.
  The waters could not compare, their power faint and spent,
  And his


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep,
Passed by; one maiden, purest of eye,
Lifted the fire, which countless true hearts had fed,
The sovereign of desire, in slumber, she unmanned,
By a virgin's gentle, unsuspecting hand,
His fiery brand, by cool well water drenched,
Expelled the heat, and gained a healing potion,
A cure for hearts by Love's own fire afflicted,
Yet I, the captive of my lady's devotion,
Came to this well, in hope of my own healing,
  Discovering that Love's fire warms the waters,
  But


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And nothing more, since I am aware
That she loves those of you above.
Thus, I rest in bliss and peace,
While she, veiled in cultures different,
Loves those of you whose beauty I cannot learn,
And those of you who are free of skin's act,
Adorning each other's presence dear.
  She thinks me gullible, unwise,
  To trust her, since I cannot see her.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
And my sinister one a woman wicked.
When at a smile I sometime part,
My sweet one's countenance fills the vacant space.
My lines, though precise, yet free,
To thee I send inadvertent essays.
Thy looking glass, thy shape in my hand,
Cannot settle the truth it shows,
Nor can my soul confirm the thing it doth say.
  If it be error, not mine,
  The crow itself doth proclaim it heretic.


