When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's perils,
A fool, unwise in love's counterfeit,
Whose wit unwisely scarlet letter smacks
Of basest falsification, less grounded
Than the time when o'erlook was given to kings.
Riches love a great treasure hides in vaults,
Which often shows its dear value and true worth
But methinks no man so rich in goodness sits in Shrubbery.
  I would tell thee what thou desire'st to hear
  The wits and frailties that wear thy truth hard and swift.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Though we be foes, yet love is not enmity
So often, and foes far away are rare.
Rise, if now thy heart wasts with anger,
Let me be the bitter glass to thy sight;
Thy breast will mirror thy images to me
And thy secret grievances with thy brave cheeks tell.
Beauteous let me taste thy anger in my breast;
In sighs of love, my heart shall tell.
  From thence this same love that fed my flame
  So thine inward I check well not to grow another.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
My hidden distress. If I can prove thee wits uncalled,
Till heaven lift me from this hell, pure spite
To sing with angels thrice my brain;
Let not my poor, simple voice
Creep behind my cuckolded tongue with torture,
Thy voice being but sad enough to know.
If I can but by thy fair aid
Make true account of my being tilled,
Reserve me thy fair mind, this minute's hush:
  Loveliness lies in light;
  To entertain with thoughts these ill might cause.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Mine eye, beloved of ten thousand false turn.
For either my sight or my heart in love gives
Mine eye such a delight it destroys;
And for myself mine eyes in love do love make!
My heart, that looks on these poor civilised days
To say with judgment of whom thou art
My dear, most blessed, and will be to me;
