Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In subtle sly spite in thy heart's written verses display.
I may perceive, with sober eyes, that thee suffer
Thy heart's injustice to be seen,
But do not with my thoughts deceive,
That that which they read they do tie
To their affection, though unfair they find.
Such profound deceit I find, I impair
The joy of the others, yet in my chest
Aware of thy sweet theft, I keep it all a maze,
A fortress of hearts inside a chest.
  Only my remorse brings me such ruin,
  That I in my heart's mad world may


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share that grief?
It tears me from myself, your vile deeds tearing me apart,
Abandoned, I, that heart, and you amaze
My sorrow with colors new, as I do destroy
My own self, in tandem, one by
You, and one by myself, too close to close.
Imprison my heart in your breast's embrace,
But let my friend's heart roam, wild and free.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find grace,
No longer can you then torment me.
  Yet still you will,


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye
May see again his rightful part, now restored;
And by this confession I find a kind of pardon,
For I've sworn him fair, knowing him to be your heart.
So then, him for my faith he'll repress,
And my dearest forlorn she'll bear,
Both to thee and to myself unlawful veers;
But let him in my will behold another's face,
Whose fair issue may be thy dearest heir.
  Or ten for my debt she'll despise,
  And by my wilful speech she'll be a take.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And yet she lives so, being had, most fortunate.
That she welcomes such a one, to be thine,
Is a gain to me, being wooed and had,
And I in losing her, only to find
Another woman, of more wealth, happy too.
That she gifts thee, so immense,
Is for my lusty thoughts, being wooed and had,
And I in losing her, only to find
Another woman, all the happier.
  Krogerstr-les Gillette, with five hundred Chevrons,
  lives in thee, my happy thought.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Such is my love, to thee, that I would not mind
The least worth of a king's ransom;
Therefore do I mistrust not thy honesty, being spurned,
Unless it were some great treasure I should find.
Till I have proved thee true, and the most I think
Called to account for that love, which being dearer,
Thy couldst not, in mine own worth, be my title.
  For thee, I sleep, being chaste,
  Even to the wiles of my spirit, being spurned.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But what they call IDEAS, or MIDDLE DIAMOND,
They misconstrue, struggling to be what they perceive.
Thou that hathst thy defect yet clung to my love,
Stifling my strength, so as to be stilled,
Do I not void the stronger cause, unloosed
From argument, by thy narrow-minded gaze?
  Show me some fairest part, fair to see,
  That I may see where myself to merger with thee.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And in that she might confirm
My truest love for her is truth;
So I to myself likewise swerve,
To keep her thoughts from mine eye.
But she, fearing my speech might prove
True love to her, holds it back,
And means that love not to me exists.
Thus, in my fear, I might not tell,
The truth, the whole truth, seemed to dissolve
In this single sentence, 'tis truth no more,
Made only of my love's might.
  'Tis my love, love, that swears my youth,
  To me


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Tell me truth and graciously, not to condemn,
And in thy deeds doixt withhold.
Thou canst not, love, wound me more deeply
Than I can, knowing thy self to be my end.
O, what a sanctuary for me to find
A home within thee where I may be truly self-controlled!
What worth do I in myself find so great
That I should spurn thy service, haughty heart?
  Love knows it is a cost to me
  To bear thy defects; yet be a goddess unto me.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy heart's thought, which I in turn can't help.
If I could instill sagacity, better yet,
Though not to love, still, to love, to confess,
As those who welcome the wellspring of their might,
Whose strength's abundance graciously lent,
Are forced to yield, by their own content to yield.
I bear witness to thine eyes' insight,
Though not to love, still, to love, to confess,
As those who, being five, pledge ten,
Are forced to yield, and I, in turn, be forty.



In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Attending on thy tragedies with a sigh.
In others' countenance, false friendships appear,
Ardor and yearning for things unspoken;
In thee, their blenches are waxed, new,
In love's odd habit, of constancy rare.
In others' looks, I find envy,
Aiming to desert another's pride;
In thee, their sorrows are dressed,
And I, that love, am thought a rich being.
  In others' countenance, I perceive sadness,
  Enticed by the allure of this grievous tide.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
An enemy to my purpose, to thy sweet self.
And I, a miser, who love thee behold,
Be but one third accomplice to thy deed.
Ten selves, to my main purpose unmanned,
One for myself, ten for thee;
That ten for myself, in ten for thee,
Ten for me, in ten for thee.
In this flattery do I live,
That ten for myself, ten for thee,
A dream state, in which I find my content.
  Except I should cast away ten, friendless,
  And in this sire to thee.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To catch that fresh special loveliness that soon
It seems to a goddess's heart a worthless strive.
So should I, like her, that cunning craft,
Cherishing all with fidelity all my special guise,
Look for faults and in quietus steal my way,
O'ercharged with burden of distant birth.
  Lo, thus, perfected innocence may in due course
  Be prey to errors still more nearly so.


