Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy looks with black are at my heart's core.
For I have found, and found repeated,
Thy heart's secret treasure, unlooked for,
In darkness, a mirror image did I lend,
Thy face, a thief, stealing from me.
How much more disgrace should my deeds prove,
When in thy deeds, I find thy grace,
And in thy darkest hours, a glimmer of light
  Awakes my heart to heart's deep, beat true.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And draw nigh to the utmost pitch of hell?
It delves me from myself, your deepest wound
Has robbed me of all that I can hope;
It hath infected me with your most foul sport,
A murd'rous crime, wide and terrible.
It is true, and I am not able
To deny it; but yet such foul deformed
As creatures worse than I am able can resemble;
  And it is this: my friend's face hath shared
  A mortal infection with thee.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine escape;
So I'll surrender myself, thou dost confine
My spirit to thee, which shall in me drown
The mourning that should accompany me;
For why should I care what others do,
When I am myself brought to thine aid?
I tell thee that I am by him profaned
And that I will pay the penalty;
So I'll live, supposing thy pardon,
Even amidst the world's turmoil;
  Such peace I'll require, I'll afford
  None but to hear thy sayings.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And can evil spawn without thy free will;
I will not shared in thine eye's burning heat,
Nor in thy plague be all the more.
I will not thy fortune nor thy society lend
A soft flattery, for I love thee such flame
That I in my wood still burn both black and white;
And, being hated of much, I in my will
Still smoke so, though thy sweetest Villain see.
  If thou wilt leave me, love not with that hand,
  That I in my wood will continue to smoke.


