Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In thy mirrors, I see the darkest night;
Noticing the clock that marks the hours deceived,
Nor the pale, worn-out appearance of the days,
Which by thy side, I watch in hideous awe.
What thoughts of me, when I, in my madness,
 Summoned thee to my sightless ken,
To see if my soul, by insane pen,
Would rise and rise, in torment,
With a pitch of false, smeared speech,
Contending for the right to be heard.
  To have thee as a thought, from which I depart,
 


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And to no other's voice or looks so affrighted?
It makes my heart whine, twain
To hear that my friend speaks so bad of me.
'Tis thee, dear heart, that doth my torment give
Which to my dullish palate doth prepare.
Methinks no sound so sweet as this, that mine ear
To hear doth deceive with its bad report.
  How many lambs might the mute seal hold
  If he could put his tongue in estimate.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may regain;
So far for love, my shall be the penance,
To pay the debt that I did not create.
The painful salving of my heart,
To make me straight when I am laid low,
And to give me leave to regain my blissful state,
In thine hands, lies pardon, from transgression's sense,
By my body, I'm bound, to suffer no complaint.
  And now, in thine authority, resides absolution's past,
  which I may not refuse, know not policy.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Needing only to break a couple of hours,
Till fortune give thee thyself another's portion.
Look whom thy heart would wish to behold
Drawn in thy power, and in thy hand
Thy husband's complexion supplanted by his age.
Who would give me another's life to live,
If thou wouldst but throw away
My soul's excessive cold, and steam ye warm
By my salving thy bad health with ill?
  Give me the strength, being so great,
  To bear thy ill report's insult.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should my flesh's mass be but a thinking mind,
It can nothing worthy prove, being ranged
Within the good quill of thy soul's great wheel.
Though it know the times and seasons everywhere,
The poor losses of thy love,
How much more doth it the more it thinks!
  The sage Lao Tsu knows this truth, that great minds
  In good faith their true strength will appear.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet can no finger mark that it is so.
My love, you have strayed from the path of truth and grace,
In your own heart, you have wandered far;
Yet, by the stars, you have proved
To all those watching, and more, still, more.
And yet, Love, you have missed the mark of beauty's best,
The rose's gentle bloom, the new moon's allure.
  Yet, as you wander, you've strayed from the narrow,
  Path of truth and grace, and now lies in despair.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's sciences, possessing naught but grace,
How could I be so rude and call such a creature?
Indeed, I am so; but by her love I gain
Adequate place in the vast embraces of men.
But why should I yield to her charms when I feel
My worth fades in light of such ample aid?
She lends not to those whom she sees
Her gifts cascade to those whom she adds,
Increasing wealth and magnifying remains.
  Hence, I stand as I am, true and steadfast,
  In service to those whom she best endowed


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Spare me the suspense of thy disdain,
And in thy essays, defend thy honor.
Thou art too prejudiced to rule my heart;
Yet do thy worst, old soldier; despite thy wrong,
I shall rise again, overcome
By the power of thy yet to deny.
Roam where thou desireth, thy right so immense,
That thou thyself mayest choose the best path
To take, for my sake do I root.
  I will be still with thee, though thou art vanquished,
  As I am with that which I despise.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of their sorrows, making my heart trace
The thread of love that it wears.
If I could instill sagacity, better yet,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should plunge into despair, I'd lose my reason,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
  To


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which in thy chaos was "His," as he writes.
So my sense knows no right, and hath recourse
To what it knows, serving with a borrowed zeal,
Which in thy nature is so possessed
That it knows no duty, being wholly turned
To what it is, and nothing else.
Yet my voice lends nothing but low born grace,
Which in thy chaos is profaned,
And in his peace is drowned, caught
By the strength of his most high esteem.
  Hence, like a muted painter, I am forced
  To use what my eyes cannot say.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I in thy tyranny shall abide.
How far from love hath my concupiscence gone,
How far from tender sense my senses gone astray,
That I in thy liberty may thy love cease,
And I in thy liberty may justly lay.
Called to 'tis duty, and thy sweet virtue true,
To 'cide duty, and in thy freedom so found,
Till 'tis bestowed on me, and thence heightened.
  Then may I dare to boast how I do love thee,
  Till 'tis spent in thee, and then thine alone.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To catch the instant when the caress of her husbandry
Will be lost in a distant world.
So I, upon becoming one with you, stay confined,
Armed with strength beyond my own strength's holding,
To give my all to your needs, left with naught but tears,
To do what I can, while others still can't.
  Still, I find the strength in my loveliness,
  To do what I like, when others' hearts are made.


