If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Such shall be my salve that my life
Will be thine, and that due of many were to thee;
Then canst thou say I loved thee not when thou dost hate me,
When I, for thy sake, will bear the weight of thy desert.
O, what a happy title do I find, so blessed
That I in thy strong hand canst make rich music,
Happy to have thy love, happy to die!
  But what's so blessed-fair that ink may show
  That thou too shall be the better son of me.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet not know the doublet of theore.
They play the doctor, and why should I trust
That they will know what to expect from me
When I, their captain, do travel alone?
They may like me, but not be friends,
To keep a chronicle of your goneness;
They know not the sweetest art
From that which gives them all things to desire.
  Yet speak well, Love; yet after I have gone,
  They still look on thee, and know not well if thou art right.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's arts and philosophies.
Thus, she believes me gullible, unwise,
Though she knows full well my days are fleeting,
I play along, her falsehoods I endure,
And in this act, truth on both sides hides.
But why does she not admit her wrong?
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So, in our faults, we find comfort in lies,
  And in our deceptions, we find sweet solace


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Do not thy worst to hurt me more,
Yet not leave me the least impression.
If thou wilt leave me, do not leave me last,
When other petty griefs have done their spite,
But in the least of these wilt thou show
Thy truest love, more than thou knowsst.
O, do thy worst to harm me truly;
Hurt me not with spites, yet truly prove
Thy affection worthy of all thy woe.
  Yet do not so. Love knows it is not pardon
  To mix all ill with good.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my heart's true sorrow.
If I could instill sagacity, better yet,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should plunge into despair, I'd lose my reason,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
