In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And to his views I send in harmony.
So my love looks on me amiss,
Though I in myself sometimes appear bright;
For she who decreed that beauty should dead,
Love's itself doth cook that sin in its youth.
Though I should reproach her for being false,
I would not, loving her, let that stain dwell,
Lest my innocence should be marred by its use.
  She hath used mine rightly, though I have used others' places;
  And yet methinks I have not deserved that loss.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I, a tyrant, do but sad turn,
Ensnared by the tyrant's snare.
Thy beauty hath not been surpassed, nor sweet love sought,
Yet this thy praise cannot be so deep,
Nor shall thou share in its weight, share its use.
O, lest thy excessive beauty mocked,
Or worse, snared by the snare of thine,
Be a prey to thy sweet husbandry!
  Temptation's disdain, in this case, appears,
  And sweet love, when taken, shall still sting.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
So I, in love's dread, do run amiss,
Neglecting those whom my heart and breast doth call dear.
For then, despite of my grief, I do appreciate
The constant welfare of my darling's health,
And so, in love's fine wit, I am enforced to write
A perfect doozie for her, not with my last will.
  Thus, perfected love, I engraft new,
  Forgetting all that was spent once before.


