When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
I'd take her truth with a toss,
And leave her feelings to the whispers of night.
So should she prove false in this,
I'll bear the pain, the punishment, the fight,
And let my love's fine-tuned ear
Accept the truth as music parts the humblest string.
Speaking of things gone by, her words now proven
To be false, I accept this truth,
  And her love, reborn and renewed,
  Plays its part, true and true balanced.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Spare me the bitterness of thy contempt,
And if I have erred in all my design,
Grant me the pardon: my love is allowed
By thy benevolent hand.
Give me the strength: my love's strength lies in thy embrace,
So I'll bear the weight of thy objection;
If thou prevent'st me from ever doing wrong,
I'll repent that love hath done wrong to thee.
  Yet, do not so. I love thee so
  That thou mayst truly be my heart's objection.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express sorrow,
And I, made tongue-tied, in future ages will forget
The trappings of a strange ebb and flow.
If I endure, believing in thy virtue,
My tongue-tied torment lends me mirth,
And plain words, being plain, tune well
To what they want, plain and bright.
If I fall into despair, I show my plight,
And straight speech brings comfort,
By roguish goodness, soothing thee.
  And ruined love, straightened by thy correction,
  Praises thy worth, being straight, its reason.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And to me, their worthless thoughts are drenched.
My ears find no delight in your voice's song,
Nor do gentle emotions yearn for the lowly touch;
Nor taste or scent, seek to indulge in the sensual spree,
In any lustful feast with you in the spot and clutch.
My five senses and my wisdom cannot,
Dissuade one infatuated heart from serving you devotedly,
Unmoved, the semblance of a man, fate has been cast,
Thy heart's loyal subject, a wretch, ever bound to thee.
  Only my sorrow


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I, veiled in misery, do abide.
Neither are our passions so warmed,
Or if they be touched at all,
By thee, as is false painted.
I do love thee, as thou dost them adore,
And they, in turn, love thee more;
And as the sun is daily new fixed,
So is my love, every now fixed.
  Let them reverence thee, as thou dost them adore,
  Or lust after thee, as I do.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To keep her cherished treasure from needless waste.
So, you, in love's anxiety, abandon me,
Your child, who at your side, cries out for you,
Neglecting to atone for your wrongs done,
Excusing your sins, more than they weigh.
I tell the truth, and you believe me,
Excusing your sins, more than they weigh.
But why does your heart not confess my wrongs?
For love's sweetest habit is to feign trust,
And loss is the very thing that gives us grace.
