So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may have his place.
For to lose him would be both a grief and a gain,
A grief more than that which I can't cure,
And a gain, more than that which I can't complain,
Will give me a pearl, double sweet.
O, what a gain, to share this love,
To stand where we both belong,
In each other's hands, what could death claim?
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Thou art the best of men, and he in thee art guarantied.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
With that which thou thyself dost take away.
I paint not with my strong paint what thou thyself dost waste,
