O, change thy thought, that I may change my view.
Shall hate be fairer lodged than gentle love?
Be as thy presence is, gracious and kind,
Or to thyself at least kind-hearted prove.
  Make thee another self, of thine


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
That thou goest this way does not unfurl
The tail of thy vile deeds;
On the contrary, it doth thee shame,
To part from thine own lips that art heretic.
If I lose thee, tender heart, forget me quite,
For I have found a better way,
And I must not blame thy soul for my failures.
  But hold thy course; I love thee in such sort
  That, losing thee, thou my soul swear against.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet frequently miss the mark of beauty's finest hour.
Thou perceive'st, O dull bearer of my affection,
That sometimes the best of gifts fails to make its father's chest,
And often is his pleasure deceived,
A joy much too often concealed from his sight.
Yet in thy blindness, I see a window,
A gay imprint upon my love's new arrival.
So that corruption in eyes may not look,
And that due to thee I offer to thee, take heed.
