When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
I don't care.
But yet, if she's learned to write with cunning,
To paint me as naive and untamed,
To tie me to her cause, beyond my birth,
And toss in a notion of my own worth,
Arming her with evidence of my unavailing worth.
That she might smother my flame she does intend,
To make me bend to her will, more dumb.
  And yet, I fear, she'll burn me last,
  For truth is on her side, her proof.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Spare me the deceit of thine allure.
Why use cunning when your strength lies in favor?
Grant me the pardon: my love well knows
Her beauty has been my undoing;
To save me from such foes she bestows
Her strength, and I am only winning.
But yet, relent not; near expiry I lie,
And state my age with ease;
By youth's tricks, I am kept free.
  Yet, relent not; near death I stand,
  Release me wholly, end this demand.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy true love's plain truth, which thou dost preach.
If I could but flee, run,
From this place, where thou art forced to flee,
And look upon thy transgression, quite apart,
Be thine own, and do not ransom me with glass,
Or anywhere else lend to thine illicit take.
O, cease this torment; cease now
To torture me with thoughts of love,
Which I must not acknowledge as paid.
  Perched now atop thine indulgent seat,
  Let not thy shadows wink with day.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
With what I, in thy liberty, do allow.
O absence, what a torment wouldst thou prove,
By cheerful absence, making thy fairest feelings
Return, more bitter than before,
If thou live remembered not to be,
Or else suffer this bitterness, call'd riot.
If thy love be thine, then this wish I have:
Thy love is so grounded on thine that it needs no proof;
Or else, if it be false, 'tis more evident
Thy affection is false, a treacherous hate.
  For thee, against myself I do write,



Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
Catching him as he flies, her heart's latest plunder.
So too, should the world demand you to reveal
The cause of love that stirs within your breast,
Forget me, dear, once I have left this earthly realm,
As nothing noble in me can claim your part.
But if you'd weave a tale of virtues I lack,
To elevate me higher than I ever flew,
And heap praises on my humble shoulders' rack,
A falsehood spun to honor me anew.
  Lest your true love appears as deceit,
  Keep it truthful, yet fervent.


