Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As dimmed by mourning, they weep,
And pour into my mind's night a mournful embrace.
For they saw the use of my pain,
And ruined it in their woe,
Despairing me, they send me far from their care,
A wretched soul, far from the solace of my heart's delight.
Yet, should they find a way to heal
My heart, or at least my anger, they have ruined
My joy, and bound me close,
A thrice tormented sire, and a heart that longs to claim.
  Yet, should they


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And make my dearest friend lament,
And have it thrust upon my heart's own part?
It wrests me from myself, and keeps
My distance from you, and both live apart.
O, blame me not, if I have choice,
Whate'er my soul calls, or thee to tell,
Can make me live apart, or share
The grief I bear, the cross I've known.
  Yet thrice tormented be the heart that makes my friend woe,
  Thirdly, and hardest, still will.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine interest
Can by assignment make vacant
My house dear and still stand.
So far for love, I've labored, done my best,
Whilst thou dost prowl after another's deed,
And I am lost, poor andjailed,
Toil and care, from him to sell,
Dissipating means of reaping present day,
Find satisfaction in that which I do change.
  Thus have I waited, knowing well thou art true,
  Until thou hadst my life irretrievable.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For thou me for my wreath doth call:
"Bring me proof, or else my will be killed."
So far for proof or for will I offend,
That thou my servant 'gainst myself mayst prove
Thyself in intents and thy deeds in consultation.
Let those that bear witness to thine injury,
Who witness well thy public station,
Not to thy show, their fortune shows,
Thy will, to present thy absence to the will.
  This is my will, and this I witness,
  To thee and to thy much loved heart.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thus, if for heaven's will thou my absence
To me was my will,
To abide still, though thou art blinded,
While thine eyes through the cloud perceive
What with the suns perceives with sound?
O, long may I stay, though thou art deceived,
Sworn to live in desert of gold,
While the house of our love is of less worth
Than what in the ghetto of time doth stand.
  But love for myself, to withered,
  Makes barren of offspring, while store of blood doth run.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet that they perceive the blazon of a face
Lest their eyes should deceive, and 'gainst their dignity.
For why should their faces give them light,
When they themselves behold the deepest morrow?
Or, if they see, why do they not glory
To their true colors, that they themselves doth show?
  O, yet, love, though they see, yet they know not well
  To what is imputed to them that their eyes adore.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
I must attend all that tells of her reign.
Believe her, if she can, that I am made
Of good report, doubting well against
My bare existence, and daring her to boast
That I am BETTER AN EVENIAN DAY,
O'er whom her grammatical flyings may ranged.
Believe her, if she pleases,
That I was not born with base appetites,
That I was not made by a bastard lord,
And that I must READILY BELIEVE,
Thy spiteful mistress hath planted this report.
