So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May feel that I did collaborate in this woeful deed.
But thou hast prevailed upon my nerves to set free,
And I, in submission, yield to thy scythe;
My heart is content, though full of strife,
At thy words, though I should flee.
Thou art so cruel, yet so kind;
He who taught me to feel
His wrath hath taught me to feel
His sweetness hath tasted and is thus to decay.
  So I return rebuked to my content,
  And suffer no more anguish than when first I did sell.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And yet more I will, still more, than ever;
And yet more I will, still more, than thou art.
Even as in the brain a part doesiganity,
So in thy breast's revelry, there can live no love proclaimed;
Therefore to thy content I send,
Not to provoke thy hate, but to fulfill.
  Even so, and yet more, I will,
  Even to thy content I send.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Till whatsoever star that guides my moving
Points on thee I confide,
Knowing a god in love with thee exists,
And for this love endures my fear,
Plotting against myself.
O, what a crime it is to trust in a fractured friend,
To which I so strongly am committed,
To part from myself, to preserve
The unity of our love.
  But thou art the fairest and most loving muse,
  Mine is thy love, as thou art mine to adore.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But not, to taste, what it doth morning.
For eyes in love see but what they see,
And that, varying from face to face,
They give 'twixt looks a vitality that cannot be expressed.
But when the false heart's deceit takes root,
Embellishing the truth with false allure,
Sweet beauty fades, and allure abhors the memory.
  Thus, Love, thou deceived'st eyes,
  And I, too, were tainted, in turn.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And hold her tongue when I praise her.
But why does she not confess her wrongs?
And why do I not confess my age?
For love knows it's a heavier burden to bear
The pain of a false love's deceit than hate's open sore.
So we deceive one another in our flaws,
And with sweet lies, we comfort our disgrace.
  Still, we are the better lovers;
  Unless this universal deceitgoes.


