Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As darkened clouds envelop the sky;
Their mournful hymns, I cannot then understand,
And in their despair, I too shall be,
At the hand of darkened skies, I too shall be immersed.
Oh, how poor my muse's offerings be,
When such broad canvas of light
The bare idea's worth outshines the plea,
Enhanced by naught but my belated praise.
  Nay, more than enough she has her part,
  But these proposals are nothing compared to thee.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to suffer, see?
It tears me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you amaze
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
Imprison my heart within your fortress chest,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find grace,
No longer can you then torment me alone.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May claim thy part of me.
But do not so. I love thee so dear
That I will not through life's deceitful arts
Drink up the poison that thy deadly husband doth use
To make me weak and peepity.
I will not, though he loves me so
As I, him, for myself will be his,
He pays me but a venial error
And pays me death's passage to make me weak.
  He lends me but as the gull lends him light,
  And I must pay him but as the dyer pays his fellows.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And likely I am the best of loved.
For went she who hath graced thee with her beauty,
Hath mostly mine own dear-purchased right;
And I, being asked where all my longing dwell,
To try and discover where all those woods
Which I loved above.
Who hath his beauty wish'd or tombs waste
That nature hath not made for his sake?
  Him for this thy love doth blame me,
  Being fond on outward form but not on inward love.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Give me the pardon: my love well knows
Her beauty hath but grown with days,
And beauty swears by its dead love.
So, love, beauteous and hard,
Make my love's summer with thy sweet self-love,
Or else, dear love, thou shalt be woe betide me.
Thou false, blind slanderer; a true soul
Denies me so near, to thine own bright eyes.
  Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong
  And heavily from thy sweet self to thine still lies.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And, well knowing, like blind men's eyes, are peepers;
But seeing bears no interest to the vision,
For vision keeps fast what it doth catch.
So, Love, beauteous and lovely youth,
Whose waxing glow reveals my own decay,
How would, seeing, thy worth's report come?
How would, Love, by looking on thee,
Knowing a truer copy of thee exists,
To swear by that, and by this, to keep.
  But yet, love, to thyself I give most,
  Mine own true-


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
To whom her marriage vow is seldom performed.
But why should I believe her false tongue,
When she herself doth publish her true views,
To swear against love and look for faults concealed,
More than am I willing to trust her?
Because I would not often fall to kiss her ass,
As she would, I leave my books alone,
To make sure that such falsehoods never happen.
  And thus, to be sure she hath not deliberately kept
  You as her humble servant do dumbly write.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And do not so much as blame me for my sins,
Lest thou be blamed thyself for my errors.
For thou art blamed thy sins that thou art,
And I, being blamed, must be even so,
And near so, to thy sweet self I send.
O, what a happy title do I find
And what a happy thought it is to think on thy part,
When even to thyself I send!
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Thou mayst be false, and yet I know it not.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy worth, in situation with thee.
If I could school thee in the knowledge of thy manners,
Or if I should figure out the worth of goodbye,
To dignified utterances from thy speech,
As though to dignified strangers they gave.
For if I had formal deserts to make,
Or if mine own true love were a blot,
To dignified utterances politic I play,
And that thou shouldst in my speaking do dignified cheer.
  But since all tongues maintain that thou art kind,
  Unless this general evil they maintain:


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Attending on thy sins, seeming dead to hear.
My ears are not designed to be windows to the soul,
Nor that they view the tone of the voice;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with thee as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And find fault not in love, though it wink.
For to thy sweet self I grant permission,
And every ill-done hand hath my permission to do
That sweet thief to my fire, which should do thee shame.
Who lets so fair a house fall to decay,
Which husbandry in honor might uphold
Against the stormy gusts of winter's day
And barren rage of death's eternal cold?
  O, none but unthrifts, dear my love, you know.
  You had a father; let your son say so.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
So I, by being untruthful, come to believe,
My husband's truth is ne'er so true as first believed,
Though I swear it to myself alone.
But why should I believe, knowing well his faith,
When I see him much more true than I could his beauty say,
Under the weight of proved truth by new?
The cause of this change is wanting,
And so my husband's no longer so good.
  But till his verity shall be shown,
  Then verily I am true despite thy lies.


