Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As darkened by mourning, as the eye
Enjoys in the darkness, as the heart doth suffocate.
The hand that ill deeds grasps, and thence to be blamed,
Is like a tormentor, ever near,
And clips the lamb's ear, and burns it down to the ground;
So I be left alone, with sorrow filled,
And in thy heart's dark plot, I am undone.
  For thee, I paint my age with colors new,
  In beauty's old, subtle way.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It may detain, but not 't keep, my heart's true ache;
It brings me within its dark held,
And keeps my friend from ever waking.
It plays the tyrant over my heart,
Ensnared in its twisted, mercy's dye.
It leads my heart to rage, thrice it kills,
And leaves my friend to bear this pain;
  Temptation's touch, in which all ill well shows,
  Wound me with your scorn, yet we cannot be adversaries.


