Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As shadows dance towards the sun.
The morning sun, in its heavenly course,
Nor the bright star that ushers in repose,
Can compare to the beauty of thy remorse,
Gracing the evening with its eve's glow.
O, let your heart embrace my despair,
As mourning becomes you, in every trait,
Then I shall declare darkness is rare,
And all that's plain lacks your complexion's stature.
  So I'll attest, black is the color of love,
  When worn by thee, it fits as a glove.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to suffer, see?
It tears me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you amaze
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
Encase my heart within your breast's embrace,
But let my friend's heart wander, wild and free.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find grace,
No longer can you then torment me.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May believe, with reckless abandon,
That thou art my debtor indeed.
But here's the deal: my friend owes me not to pay
His dues; I have recourse,
And that is to die; so should he die,
And I, enforced to pay it all,
Complying on his default; but thou art forced to be
So; and then I am ruined all alone.
  To leave me thou hast the strength of laws,
  I can petition for a friend's leave to stay.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And it is not with me that thou squee'st so tight.
If I had thee as a dream doth flatter,
It might be said I loved not thee at all,
Although I loved very much thy sweet self.
For canker vice the sweetest buds doth love,
And thou present'st a pure unstained prime;
Therefore he loves but for thyself alone.
But since he loves thee more, himself deceivest,
And for thyself alone thou receiv'st,
And I must strive to approve thy feature still.
  Such is my love today


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For if you are ignorant of my will,
To say within thine own deep-sunken eyes
Are offerings to eternity--
Then thou too shalt be afraid, and suspect
My death has looked upon thee too much;
So should my memory with sorrow tell,
And look into the glass, to behold my decease,
Who taught thee to forget me so?
  O, that our shared grief might teach
  How to make worms multiply and grow stronger.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that it is sullied by a distant gaze;
Yet blind to beauty's fallacious bar;
They look on truth and right,
And that, in their blindness, they seem to lose.
How can they then be true, beholding thee,
While knowing well the sameness of our plight?
Or on our errors, magnified by fear,
Do they project their own uncertainties?
  In truth and right, their hearts are bound,
  And that they boldly state.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And pull me from life this dismal night,
When I have belief her unwise heart.
But why does she not do so,
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's trust is ever so,
And age in love dislikes telling of years;
So, either by chance or by heaven,
I am in this wretched state wherein I find my end.
  This thought is as a death, which cannot choose
  But weep to have that which it fears to lose.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
If thou wilt, thou art all my worth.
How can I then be otherwise than thou art,
When what I owe thee transcends my name?
And what can I do but assimilate thee to another,
And what I owe thee, to thou decreed?
Therefore do I call thee to be gone,
When I think thee gone,
And let my love, true, although thy leisure be spent.
  For thee I watch while thou art gone,
  And my love, so longing, niggards.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my hidden loathing.
If I could but understand, and take note,
The hurt with which I am afflicted,
And see how true a purposed dislike
Is reflected in your countenance's weave.
For if I could devise a way to make me smile,
To make my sorrow more tolerable, I'd be the better.
O, what a garden of paradox!
How many a glorious morning have I seen,
What gracious and lovely things have sown,
That hath taught my tongue to sing of love,
And then to thyself the depth of my hidden disdain


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Which in thy errors doth correct the falsehood;
Making even falsehoods good men see,
And false adulteries seem lovely things.
Thy faithfulness so cool is as a summer's day
Which, having faith, by the sun deceases;
So thou, to thyself alone be the true confidant,
And o'er false weal of time report,
Making ourselves guilty of sin by two against nature's rule.
  Preserve thyself, I beg, for my sake,
  As thine, so is my spirit's dove.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And all those friends which in thy will are fitted
In thine own tight chambers do confine.
I cannot blame thy pleasure, though it be varied,
Against my temptation's cause being dead,
Who, all in one, one pleasing note do sing;
Such harmony is in thy will,
That I amen to thy every glad sound.
Thou canst not vex me with inconstant mind,
Since that my heart is thy debtor and always bound to thee.
  But do thy worst, old friend; despite thy wrong,
  I always write of love, and thou thy better.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
So thou, same being, must leave
My flock, which methinks is your heart's desire,
At home, with others all around,
To go about your affairs, your hushed ism.
But I forbid one grievous act from thee:
O, etch not with your hours my love's smooth face,
Nor trace no furrows there with your old quill;
Let him in your path remain untouched,
A timeless beauty for future hearts to fill.
  Still, do your worst, old friend; in spite of your harm,
  My love shall in my words


