Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Just to hurt or praise thee more;
And yet I fear even so thou wilt be twain,
And do wrong to me at last.
But to fear is not enough to love,
And to love without fear can nothing thenceforth;
So I fear, though my life thou take'st,
Thy looking glass will show thee how thy beauties wear.
  But yet fear I cannot feel,
  Unless thou convert'st it to a joy.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Then, if for my life thou my flesh call,
Against myself I'll fight,
To prove thee virtuous, though thou art forsworn.
Be that as thy voice doth confirm
Thy worth the greater, being mine,
To give back again, after I have died,
And have my body duplicated, so thou mayst see
How far I went, what I did live.
  For I was not made for this world,
  To keep watch on thee, after thou art dead.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And tread where roses tread; yet in their blindst sight,
They see the false as true, the wrong as right,
And find in darkness, the softest light.
But why do thy worst, when thou so strives
To make thy worst count best,
Then doing well, being hated,
Comes home again, and spends his might
To make another man's best use.
  So, Love, beauteous and lovely,
  Give me thy to mine eyes, or else receive them sight.


