Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy sweet beloved's image, like night's use
To make dreams and hearts on earth.
If I could devise twisted paths to follow,
That lead me from thy sweet ease,
To make joy out of sorrow, thrice more bitter still,
Would sour my visage, and make me woe.
Would I rather be caressed than listened to,
When words, closely tied, become part of me?
  And yet, love, do not torture me with doubts,
