Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As darkened stone turns to slate,
And sorrow dwells in its shadows far away.
Yet, I fear, even in my fears,
Thy heart's secrets will be thrice missed,
For how can I then know thy heart's plight?
In things unseen, we lose sight,
Of actions wrought, hidden in plain sight,
But in our hearts' hidden workings, we find brand,
And darkness fades, as day is overcome.
  Yet still, I fear, even in this plight,
  Thy secret love will not be seen, not admitted.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to weep?
It tears me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you areaze
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
Encase my heart within your breast's embrace,
But let my friend's heart wander, wild and free.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find grace,
No longer can you then torment me alone.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may claim
Thy looking glass will show me my dear doting heart's decay;
But why should I live in such plight
Where my greatest treasure is so dear,
Which is so grounded inward in thy breast?
Or on thy humor doth depend
Which I can nothing elude,
Though thou offer me but a hope?
O, what a mansion have those windows got
Which for their height assail the very contents of thy breast,
  Which ten times refashion the very thing they concern!
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
For[] many a holy and obsequious prayer do request
That thou in thyḥerate shouldst the time attend.
Yet why should I reverence him that honors me so,
Whose hearts are full of love, and therefore to me subscribes
Thy praise, being biased even there where thou dost belong?
I do love him, therefore to myself I say
Thy will hath my assent,
And him that my love doth love doth witness bear
Thy canker's hand, and him that receives thee.
  But yet doth love him not so, to


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Then can no face so gracious have as mine,
No shape so true, no truth of such account,
Unless it be false of some threefold obsc,
That mine own rose may have some ink put there
That mine own true issue may show.
O, what a mansion have those vices got
That for their habitation chose out thee,
Where beauty's veil doth cover every blot,
And all things turns to fair that eyes can see!
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Thou mayst be false, and yet I know it not.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But not how to define it,
Or to what it is composed.
For they look into the glass, and there appears a face
That overgoes their strength, to welcome darkness;
But why dost thou abuse their brave minds,
And in thy stupidity count more dead?
Or what good is discernment made
By sight that mistakes for beauty's face so?
If it be neither fair, nor poor creature well,
That thou among the wastes of time shouldst take command,
  The conquest of thyself thou shouldst prove.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in love's cunning art;
So I play along, my lies cunningly laid,
To her astonish and reproach;
Yet in this do I not really enjoy
The match of flatterers;
I would not change my mind,
So strongly in love's office sworn,
If thinking on it would make me lose my moiety.
  But why does she not admit her wrong?
  Love's most endearing trait is pretense.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
If thou wilt but change thy mind,
Reserve me wholly, end your reign,
And do not in your presence rest your burden.
Ah, do not, when my soul hath 'scaped this sorrow,
Creep in upon the heels of conquered pain;
Give not a windy night a rainy morrow,
To lengthen the siege of anguish;
Give not a gift hour's decree,
To lengthen the penance of a rotten heart.
  If you change your mind, then do I cease,
  For mine was a fortress once built against thee.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy atrocious worth, in innocent graces.
If I could write the beauty of thy visage
And in praise thereof to set pen to paper,
The age to come would say "This poet lies;
Such heavenly touches ne'er touched earthly faces."
So should my papers, yellowed with their age,
Be scorned, like old men of less truth than tongue,
And your true worth be impeached
By every vulgar scandal and false adulter blush.
  But were some child of yours alive that time,
  You should live twice--in it and in my rhyme


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Mine eyes that misview it through an unworthy haze.
For my eyes in you perceive a thousand aspects,
Yet very few in reality do touch;
For mental prowess hath not attained that station,
Nor brought to bear the weight of any monstrous vice.
Yet my ears, being raised far from the touch of fickle fate,
Are tethered in a harbor where all hearts sway;
Innocence defiled, its virtue lost,
And strength by limping sway disabled;
  For I have sworn by this, and so should you,
  To fall with him, your worthless vessel,


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I in thy pages despise,
Savage, raw, unfeeling, rude, cruel, not to trust;
For to thee I send confined loyalty,
To do duty, not to show my wit;
  Or else my love misprision grows,
  Left with tears, because of a love they cannot.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
The second, less blessed, reaches his goal,
And does not stop till his dear turn has done.
The first, esteemed so, must yield his part,
To the gentlest touch of a mother's child,
But the second, not so nice, but lived in fear,
Withabundance of fuel, his wakened pride.
  So thou, sweet love, that ran before,
  Unhurt, cometh home again, assured.


