Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
By thy will my life hath no place.
I grant I never saw a goddess go;
My mistress, when she looks upon me,
Is not married to any, but hath her pleasure.
And having her love, thou hast thy will,
And I am left in the most wretched state.
  But since I have thine eyes, and love's delight,
  Mine be thy love, and thy will's legacy.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Yet fear me, O thou minion of my will,
That I may not know thy purpose,
Nor want thy aid in my will;
So would I trust thy word,
Though thou durst trust me not to tell,
Although I should swear by thine eye.
If I could write the look of love
And count anew each charm that eyes doth give,
Thee still would make me friend;
  For swear I never writ, nor no man ever loved,
  To thee, my dumb and lame soul.


