Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As darkened by mourning should look.
Yet eyes this cruel and pale,
As heart's kin, in sympathy with thee,
Gaze gently on my sorrow's endless plain.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of your glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
  So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
  Then suffer not heart's solitary tear to know.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to suffer, see?
It tears me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I bewail my plighted state,
Cries for mercy lost, no answer finds the plea,
My fate I loathe, at heaven's cold gate.
Encase my heart within your breast's embrace,
But let my friend's heart wander, wild and free.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find grace,
No longer can you then torment me.
  Yet still you will,


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may possess
The glory which now thou dost withhold.
But why should I not then say "Thus far,"
When I have distinctly said so,
And found that thy virtue hath nothing novel
But that which is now my special endowment?
For, having no superior shame to reckon
On thy behalf, I must not dare to boast
That thou hast any particle of gold left.
  But what is so bad that fears no blot?
  Thy excellence alone makes thee my loan.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Thyself thy coated title;
Even so thy uncle's will may have remembered
His kinship to thee, and thy will his wit.
If any ill hand hath marred thy pleasure,
The least of them my will hath done.
How many times, when thou, my will, wilt prove
How many a blessed hour hadst thou lived in?
  Let others in thy will mock thy living name;
  I will not praise that purpose not to sell.


