Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that this huge tresses' sum,
Which leaves out the dust of age,
Are but the remnants of a former sight.
For if that be false, why dost thou believe
That which thou dost hate, to which thou dost conspire
To overthrow the power that guides thee to thee?
If the true image of thee be false,
What right dost thou have to mock my sight,
Which hath no part to play in examining thy story?
  Since we are twain, and love is married
  To scrutinize every truth, to make sure it is true.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's duplicitous ways.
Thus, she believes me gullible, unwise,
