Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet somehow their eyes, being created not to see,
Shed tears and watch in ceaseless yearning blind,
Are fitted in a glass so clear,
Finding all hearts and souls, truly and true,
In thine own glass, the brightness quite gone?
If thy self were truly ugliest,
As poets mock their own faces,
Thou wouldst bear the weight of blame,
Unless thou transform thy outer self to glass,
  Show me thy ugly side, that I may see
  The full-orbed perfection of thy breast.


