Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Which is not stained with vices both;
How can I be stained with passion's hate,
When my soul is pure and my body is true?
No love, say they, in my sight,
Thy self thou dost hatred embrace.
Loving, indeed, in my view,
Thy bosom is so blessed, and my heart is right,
To thy sweet self I confide.
  Lascivious grace, in whom all ill well shows,
  Kill me with spites, yet we must not be foes.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch;
So I, by fortunate chance, do draw nigh to you,
My love, without care, by fate unstrung.
Unscathed by youth's ambushes, cunning traps,
I stand my ground, true and sound,
While he, in gilded age, sings hymns of delight;
But when your visage bears time's ravage,
I too shall feel affrighted, and muted.
  For while he insists on your beauty's loyalty,
  I'llabba it, for I mustnare him.


