Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
As shadows dance, and shadows flee,
In hues of pity, they bid me view,
The beauty of thy heart in light.
Yet, thy view I wish to see,
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
For peace and beauty, I crave,
Apart from the unseeing gaze of death,
Who laugh at the sight of thee, in their rotten humor.
  Yet, to see thy shadow's rebirth,
  The second death, which seals the day in plight.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And to consume my dearest friend as well?
It tears me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you amaze
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
Encase my heart within your breast's embrace,
But let my friend's heart wander, wild and free.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find grace,
No longer can you then torment me.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may possess
The advantage of being thy debtor and myself at once.
But what of usury doth thou use
That in thy vassalage thou dost pilfer so much sin?
Though I didst not lend thee all,
Though thou dost pay it all within;
For how can I find a use
But by thy will being deluded?
Thou art so bold, and therefore to be assailed
With all thy freedom hath got me so wrong.
  But what can I do but groan,
  Unless my nerves were brass or hammered steel.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Thyself to complain where is my deserving?
Compare thy own circumstance with my feats,
And how many a happier tale can I tell,
If thou wouldst but pardon my wild flattery?
Look what is best, that best I wish in thee.
Thou dost love those whom I cannot love,
And for that love hath thy will,
Which I cannot then reproach thee for.
  Lascivious grace, in whom all ill well shows,
  Kill me with spites, yet we must not be foes.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For canker vice the sweetest buds doth love,
And thou present'st a pure unstained prime;
On thorns and prayers doth grow,
Beldom guests at that which thou dost beguile.
Be not labored over by thy beauties boast,
That thou thyself thy sweet self dost give
To every vulgar thief in the world.
  For thou art so possessed with murd'rous hate
  That 'gainst thyself thou stick'st not to conspire.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet suffer not beauty's face to show its age,
For every rose hath his due;
But thy glass shows thee how thy beauties wear,
Thyself thy victim, to thy disgrace.
For having thine eyes in 'twixt eyes hath inspired
Thy breast to give birth to thine own buds,
Which thyself to pity doth thine own face lend.
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Thou thyself thy sweet self dost betray.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A fool born before her time,
Though she knows well my days are past their prime.
But why does she not say so,
And therefore waste her fury on me?
Why do I not confess my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So we deceive one another in our flaws,
  And with sweet lies, we comfort our disgrace.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Tell not me to liken thee to a day in spring
When clouds hush the winds and make the meadows green?
If thou wilt claim my unkindness is pardon,
I cannot argue for a day in spring;
But yet be forever false to love thee.
O, what a mansion have I got
So far from love, that in thy will I find
My solace and my dearest friend.
  Take heed, dear heart, of this large privilege;
  The hardest knife ill used doth lose his edge.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy cherished name, thy cherished name!
If I could school thee in the art of banter, better still,
Though not to cherish, still, to admit, to let me know,
As ailing men, when death's shadow nears,
Receive only glad tidings from their healers' reports.
For if I should succumb to despair, I'd lose my mind,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this topsy-turvy world, where truth is twisted,
Mad detractors find believers in mad listeners.
  To prevent this,


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Mine own worth having thee as my view.
For myself I value most that which I see,
For it is not beauty which doth for myself enclose,
But my soul's own gain which mine own parts do approve.
Then can I no more love him whom thou dost hate,
Whilst I, in thy deeds, give thee my due;
Till whatsoever star that guides my moving
Points on me graciously with fair aspect,
And puts a spirit of pity in my hurt.
  Then may I love him whom thou dost hate,
  Till he shall do thee homage


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Sanctioning thy sins more than they are known.
For to thy sensual fault I bring in sense--
Thy adverse party is thy advocate--
And 'gainst myself a lawful plea commence.
Such civil war is in my love and hate
  That I an accessary needs must be
  To that sweet thief which sourly robs from me.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To the place where her heart's desire is scored.
So, you, in pursuit of dreams, abandon me,
The child that calls out, yearning for your touch,
Forgetting all the love that once could be,
The warmth, the joy, the laughter, none as such.
But if you find your heart's elusive quest,
Remember me, and come back home, my dear,
With open arms, a love you can't suppress,
And I shall stand, no longer filled with fear.
  I'll wait for you, and when you turn around,
  I


