Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But not how to touch it, what it touches,
To smell, to feel, to be fond.
So thy glass shows what rich eyes have told
Thy youth is but a façade, a moment's display,
A grain of truth, millions of souls have bought,
Which eyes this foolishly believe are all deceived.
But why thy face so accurst o'erways
The mirror shows, good learning the worst;
And being false, thy back again is false still,
Suff'ring of trust, of wit,
Curious indifference, doubting of all men.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's duplicitous ways.
But why should I reproach her for two breaches,
When I am guilty of transgressions manifold?
My vows, mere shadows, swiftly withered, hold
No weight compared to your deep-rooted ties.
