So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may take his place,
Based on thy release of that right;
But why should I settle for less than thy liberty,
When I see that I have no small claim on thy heart?
Or on thy hard-won virtue do I hang my robe,
To prove a claim on thy hard-won fame?
No, let me live in ignominy,
And let thy shame show thy crookeded blood;
  Then thou of thy deeds shall have the triumph,
  Since who's so dumb that cannot say so much.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Making old offenses of affections new,
Making new offenses of affections old,
And yet I cannot persuade mine eye
To march in haste th' unhurried way.
O, that our said consultation could have revealed
The truth and beauty of your heart,
And your true love could have taught
A settled policy to more suited days,
Proving adoration of your worth by succession thine.
  But, ah, I fear, even this is untrue:
  Your love is new, and anonthine's pride.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Then need I not to fear the worst of wrongs
When in the least of them my sight is turned aside,
For realm of miles on end cannot keep one body whole,
Unless it were sunk within the depth of hell.
O, what a happy title do I find,
Happy to have thy love, happy to die!
  But what's so blessed-fair that fears no blot?
  Thou mayst be false, and yet I know it not.


