If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Then can I not pretend to be gone,
When in the least of them my sight is impaired?
True, I have gone near, but not quite so near,
To wit's distance, though my foot did stand
Upon the farthest earth removed from thee.
O, that our night of woe might have remembered
My deepest sense how hard true sorrow hits,
And soon to you as you to me then return;
  But that your lie might seem more true still,
  Mine was thy will, and yours by thee bound.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that some rare sight in thee
Thy worth the greater, being wooed of time,
For whose eyes this gift thou gav'st,
To lie against the truth and beneath its glare.
Yet love, when they see thee fair, believe me,
Thy face hath not the power to make love groan;
They see what is true, and the dull reward
Of a painted beauty graces that bottom.
  But why thy mask so still seems dark,
  The reason lies in thy deeds.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's duplicitous ways.
But why should she not say so,
When her process is done, worn by time,
And into her presence shadows fall?
Why should she fear my scorn,
When she knows I despise her?
No, I must not say so, though she feigns
Her age, though she knows well my days,
To make me seem old, though I am young.
  For I am flawed, and what I bring to light,
  Deserves no love, and you, no blame in this plight.


