In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Mine own fallacious love that doth my soul adore.
For thou art as darkness lies in light,
Thy lookshare not the sun, though it be shining so bright;
Nor can thy shadow's form bring to light
Thy shape, being fortified by night's dearth.
How can I, in my madness, sing
That thou among the stars dost move?
O, none but despair, being beyond help,
Cries for mercy, and dark clouds do gather 'gainst thee.
  Seek'st thou to claim what thou dost withhold?
  By thine


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I an inferior clerk still serving thy'll.
Neither are thy adulterate eyes, fixed on thee,
