So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may take his part
And bear thy ruin, which I do deign to repair.
But thou hast no lover's fear,
Nor love's tender compassion distrusting lay;
Therefore do I not suspect him of theft,
And in his will, thy present blight
Is born of my love, of whom thou hast no part.
How can I, then, mar the work that I do,
Nor find it unlawful till my employee does disgrace?
  He lends me all in his will, and I must not blame him
  Since what I do, he doth confess is right.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Whose will is my sufficient substance gained.
Let me pray for my will is your will,
And my will is your acceptable measure.
In league, let us curse and spite each other,
Without respite or intermission,
So that our grudge may ever intensify.
Mark how with my will I do dispense:
  You have my will, and I have my may,
  To mend the hurt with myself alone.


