Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that what they see, in regard to thee,
Is but a shadow of what they see in thee.
For there can live no hatred in thine eye;
Therefore in that counterfeit of good which thou dost stick,
Thy worth is such that eyes cannot contradict.
But why thy shadow soever doves
Upon thy visible part does not wholly grieve,
The injury which thy secrecy doth do,
  The thing itself is enough to make thee happy.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I am well aware she can only mend
The sting and disgrace with ease;
But why should I believe her when she tells me
That she hath her doctor present to confirm
Thy worth the greater,
Making what seems a veryching matter seem
To me is of more worth than praises practiced?
O, know, sweet love, I always look upon truth
When others would give me even your friend;
And quite contrary I find:
  She lies, and yet I believe her words,
  For she can say more than I can tell.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
If thou wilt claim my transgression is guilt,
I cannot blame thee for my share.
For to thyself I bring in sense--
Thyself a transgressor, kind of offense;
I cannot then torment
