Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Thy heart's servant, a hideous case,
Doth beauty tear the gentle hours from my sight.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of thy glance,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may thy heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to suffer, see?
It tears me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you amaze
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
Encase my heart within your breast's embrace,
But let my friend's heart wander, wild and free.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find grace,
No longer can you then torment me.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
May hold his own dear; but thou art too kind;
He hath no latent worth to claim thy heart,
And hath no wisdom to maintain thy dignity.
For what reason dost thou derive
That he should prefer thee to me?
Or on thy part dost thou desire I should scorn,
When in our lives a difference lies so?
I am as I am, and he is as I am,
Both in his nature and in his learning,
I ought not in my will count him as I do.
  Then should I anger him not, though he should thrust me aside,



Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And yet I cannot vex him more;
For I have thy will, and he hath his wish,
And I in turn doth accepting it,
Making myself aware of it,
Just to tickle him more, not make him think.
Now sit down, and let him sit in sullen earth,
Without rolling or adding to his end,
Whose soil is most rich in wanton guilt,
Making him beguiled by thy canker's bite,
And nothing 'gainst thy toolbox can make defense.
  Then do I anger him not more, having his will,


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
For canker vice the sweetest buds doth love,
And thou present'st a pure unstained prime;
Yet he that baited thee against himself
To his sweet self doth present me grievous crime.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me half so ill,
To set a form upon desired change,
As I'll myself disgrace, knowing thy will;
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy walks, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name no more shall dwell,
Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
But not its form, being horrid,
So perception's face, which sees yet strays,
Doth unlawful grief give light.
O, what a mansion have those eyes got
Which in their rite did beauty dwell,
Hiding thy faults, like watchers' eyes?
No wonder then, my view is often veiled,
The sun itself waits for day's enlighten'd might.
  In him who doth receive thy praise, grant him full light,
  Finding faults, though they seem to know them not.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's duplicitous ways.
But why does she not admit her wrong?
And why do I not confess my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So we deceive one another in our flaws,
  And with sweet lies, we comfort our disgrace.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Tear not with thy leisure my quiet hours
And busy days lengthen in joy and mirth.
If thou wilt be willful, cunning thief,
Do not chide my sin, though I have done
In thy behalf thy sweet self doth plead.
For thee, against myself I bring
Against myself a lawful complaint;
Give me the benefit of thy counsel,
That I may straightly vindicate thy innocence.
  Such civil war is in my love and hate
  That I an accessary needs must be.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy sweet beloved's style, nicely twisted.
If I could teach thee to read what in thee doth grow
Thy love's fitting robe, even so I would be a queen,
Although I should not really owe thee that much.
Be as thy mistress; my mistress' eyes
Are lanterns which the blind do see;
So are my ears, blessed by thy voice's worth,
Thy sweet beloved's image, fittingly sewn.
  Seek'st thou to keep what thou dost keep?
  By thine own actions, thou shalt be a king.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Mine own eyes sapping, in dumb despair.
For they look upon me and wonder what my heart doth say,
And what turns love to such evil,
Stifling of truth, blindness of good,
Tormented by change, loving what they fear.
But why should my deeds be judged by thy sight,
My deeds measured by thy modern eyes?
Or on thy evil hand do I impute,
That thou among men mayst find my innocence?
  For I have sworn by thine unwavering constancy,
  By thy love, thy truth, thy faithfulness so rare.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I cannot love that sin so deeply rooted.
Look what is best, that best I wish in thee.
That is my argument, thy reasons disclose,
And I cannot then argue against thee.
Methinks no ill of thee be,
When in thy reasons no blot is so tight,
As that which thou dost willingly impart.
  Just make my prayers fair, like thy census,
  And thou shalt receive a many a good report.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To the place where her cherished thing is held.
So I, by chance, by fortune's dearest spite made lame,
Unmoved, unrespected, go to her side,
She gives me a corner to hide,
Awaiting moments of her heart's content.
This silence, once a fault, I now proclaim,
A badge of honor, as I stand in awe,
For she who calls me to be her own,
Denies me the chance to be praised of ages yet to be.
  So I stand mute, in awe, and let her suffer,
 


