So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine may take possession,
By thy will my life hath been defiled.
But thou art reassured that I am not so rude,
For I have not so much as pee to give,
Nor nothing to gain, but what is most dear to me.
And yet I fear, even so, thy will may sink
Consuming me wholly, as I am fell away.
Thou canst not vex me with inconstant mind,
Since that my life on thy revolt doth lie.
  But what's so holy that fears no blot?
  Thou mayst be false, and yet I know it


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
To set your affairs on improper path,
Seeking that which you yourself would choose,
Which you yourself art so unprovident,
Lest thy footprint on him should seem grossly sins.
Were I to lose thee, my loss was my love's gain,
And losing her, my friend hath found that loss;
Both find each other, and I lose both twain,
And both for my sake lay on me this cross.
  But here's the joy: my friend and I are one;
  Sweet flattery! then she loves but me alone.


