Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And how to its directions doth have the wind.
Yet being turned blind, they look on darkness,
And find it there, in thy reign,
Where darkness may be seen, but not thy face;
So, Love, beauteous and foolish, do not chide my sight,
Although it be marred by day's wrack,
The sun itself, which is bright day by night,
And even thence this blot doth take.
  For thee, watch I whilst thou dost wake elsewhere,
  From me far off, with others all too near.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's duplicitous ways.
But why should I trust her, when she's got past her prime,
And credibility wears a mask of deceit,
For I've seen her truthfulness wear a governmental mask,
And her beauty shows her deepest needs.
Yet, why should I listen to her tell,
When I'm doubting her truthfulness so?
Or take her word for granted,
The praise she bestows, in absence, unscathed?
  I'll wait to find out, and then rise,
  Not trusting her, though she keeps her end.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Let not thy scorn arrive on Fortune's slow.
Ah, do not, when my soul hath 'scaped this sorrow,
Come in the rearward of a conquered woe;
Give not a windy night a rainy morrow,
To linger out a purposed overthrow.
If thou wilt leave me, do not leave me last,
When other petty griefs have done their spite,
But in the onset come; so shall I taste
At first the very worst of fortune's might;
