Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy haughty heart's desire, in things restrained.
If I could teach thee to discern
The sweetness of thy mind, the dross of thy past transgressions,
Till whatsoever star that guides my moving
Points on me graciously with fair aspect,
And puts apparel on my tattered loving
To show me worthy of thy sweet respect.
  Then would I willingly be a doer of thy will,
  To do anything for thee, even wrong.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Mine own true love that doth my wrongs comment.
For my eyes in thee are sad omens,
Singing hymns of their own hearts;
But my heart in love does whisper to thee
That thou in him dost share a thousand hours' glare.
So, thou masked old wrongs with sweet lies,
Thyself corrupting salving thy amiss,
Ching t'id without thy help,
Making thy innocence seem weak in pursuit.
  For thee, I say, I due day,
  To confess my heart to be false.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I am taught to disdain
The grace of thy table, thy cup, and her spite.
Yet love's own dove, when it comes to judgment thine eye
May find its mark, and thus be purified,
New made whole, as from a broken bone.
Lest your love should find its place
Upon the vacant head, stooping to dirt,
Do not so much as whisper, but really give me lead,
  As a widowed woman, abandoned by husbandry.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To the hairy, limping firmament to halt,
Her cherished bird, a nothingness, buried with her.
So too, may your heart be moved by my sorrow,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched to thee, none can equal its glare.


