Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that the glass shows them how it grows;
Yet hear the song of birds, the fragrant blossoms' array,
And in their grand CIA hear the sweetest notes.
Thou art as blind as those whose eyes see,
Yet want the beauty that thine own lips doth show;
For who's so dumb that cannot write to thee
When thou thyself dost give invention light?
  Lest a stranger should task thee to recite,
  Then bore thy sweetness with bareness.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's duplicitous ways.
But why should I believe her false tongue,
When I am proved untrue by her own proved?
Or on her failure to prove me true,
Do I not ponder thee and wonder how
My lady's tongue may almost tell
That I am false even in belief?
Thou art as fair in knowledge as in hue,
Finding thy worth a limit past my praise,
And losing her for a thing I do know.
  Lest she should seem dumb in speaking of thee,
  And misunderstood by everyone.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Give not a closet to hide thy worst,
