Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
And I an accessary needs must be
To that which imparts but hate.
In us each ally hath his place,
But thou in me cannot mutual aid;
I cannot kiss the traitor of my duty,
Nor say "Now I love thee,"
When in our lives a separable spite.
O, but what is 't but stealing thy sweet hours,
And what is 't but happy stealing thy hours?
  As thine hours are thine, and hours forever kind,
  Then what is 't but eternal hatred.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To the sanctuary of her ardor dead.
So you, in your machinations, do set your heart on trace,
Forget me, dear, once I have left this earthly realm,
As nothing noble in me can claim your part.
But if you find your heart's elusive quest,
Remember me, and come back home, my dear,
With open arms, a love you can't suppress,
And I shall stand, no longer filled with fear.
  I'll wait for you, and when you turn around,
  I'll be the Earth, and all she leaves behind.


