Must I doubt the beauty of thy heart,
When thou art all the better part of me?
O, no. Thy worth is all my debate,
And I cannot new-made right to thy part.
For I have sworn by thine unwavering constancy,
By thy love, thy truth, thy faithfulness so rare;
And yet in my madness, I have twisted reality,
And bound thee in the chains of my despair.
  For I have sworn thee fair, false heart, in lies,
  To swear against the


Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot
My own identity, by thee bound?
Who hates thee that I name as my ally?
On whom dost thou frown that I do adore?
Indeed, if thou dost scowl on me, I try
To wreak revenge with tears that flow no more.
What virtue do I see in me, so great,
That I should scorn to serve thee as is due?
When all my virtues bow to thy defect,
Enslaved by the power of thy gaze anew.
  Yet, love, persist in hatred, for I see
  The truth: you love those whom I cannot


O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
And hook my nerves in deceitful knots.
Ah, cunning charmer, do not chide my sin,
Lest you become entwined in my transgressions.
For, as you deceive me, I betray
My nobler self to my body's treachery.
My soul whispers to my flesh that it may soar
In love; the flesh, no longer a bore,
Rises at your name, its power to explore,
A triumphant trophy, yours to adore.
  And thus, in pride, it serves, always near,
  To rise and fall, for your love,


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
Love's own virtue tainted, lethal glare.
If I could instill sagacity, better yet,
Though not to love, yet, love's own birth,
Into its wretched, veiled dusk,
Would embrace and suppress my sin.
For, as you deceive me, I betray
My nobler self to my body's treachery.
My soul informs my flesh that it may strive
For victory in love; the flesh, now alive,
Rises at your name, its purpose to derive
From your affection, its reason to thrive.
  And thus, in pride, it serves,


In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn,
And beauty ransomed by fortune's dearest spite.
Yet in thy power did I not surpr her,
Nor pay her much mind, though she knew well
Her treacherous hand was instigating temptation;
She, in her rotten thoughts, did give
Thy man a brand, to show her wit,
Which bastard shame would not dull, nor punishment plan.
  But still she persisted, and seemed to mend,
  By fortune's changing fate she our hearts must alter.


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steep.
The purple flame his love did quell,
A precious possession for heirs and unsworn.
But when my beloved, aware,
His brand was but a begin,
She quenched the flame within a crystal mountain's land,
A sacred pool absorbed the fire, forever to expand.
To seek relief, I ventured to the bath,
A wretched soul, longing for a touch of peace,
Alas! The cure I sought was not there, in aftermath,
For Love's true remedy lies where it had its release—
  In the wellspring of my lady's gaze, the


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep
The captive's tear from ever wetting earth.
But when the sodomite died, his sweet fault
Revealed, forever to amen.
The maiden fair, so revered, showed her sin
By embracing the monarch's pleasure,
Neglecting the soil's strong argument,
Dimming the rain's bright effect,
An innocent bird, king of my heart,
She put a burning brand within his hand,
Basking in his love, yet still he came to grief.
  For she, an accomplice, had no part,
  But set him free, with news of love


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Unschooled in life's duplicitous ways.
So I play along, her falsehoods I endure,
And in this act, truth on both sides hides,
From my wife's gaze, who pretends not to see,
But who, in her blindness, does know
Her husband's true worth, by him profaned.
This fallacious woman, in her blindness, does pledge,
To trust in man, who does not bear her own grief;
  Yet, who among us is ignorant
  To believe her husband's truth, even in his lies?


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
Though shadowed by sorrow's injustice.
His face is ashen, though masked by misery,
And ragged by the world, for I believe it white.
But my sinister muse does seek to bring
A cup of ill to embrace.
She whispers softly, sowing discord,
Enticing him with her twisted, warped delight.
I ponder if my better self may fall,
Seduced by shadows, losing light's embrace.
Divided, both from me, they stand enthralled,
In each other's darkness, they embrace this race.
  Alas, I may not know


