Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
And summer's lease hath strayed,
And summer's green all siled night increased.
All are blooms have bloomed and sunned,
Yet none have truly cast.
That song of birds or flowers haunts
My heart, true and kind,
Paints thy visage such as its mistress over night,
And though they be held, by thee,
Reserve them for love, not for their woe.
  For black is the day,
  And her embrace is fleeting.


