O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And in thy sweet lies embrace
The truth appears as deceit,
Which for thyself doth touch me more nearly.
Lest a dead-eyed maiden should look into the grave,
When, what beauty left behind,
Would be left an orphan, abandoned with thy help?
Then should the world should wither,
Whilst it hath thought it hath found such a pleasing feature.
  For nothing reason doth this lovely look
  Save what it contains, or thy shape which it sheds.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express the texture of my woe,
And in my tongue every truth I speak,
I should give myself the honor to lift.
Lest curious minds should look into thy wrong
And find the beauty of thy budding name,
In thy beauty, I should give
That beauty should look so much more bright.
  Since in thine own gaze, thou shalt be true,
  And, even in thy control, I am truly cast.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the disapproval of my eyes, it offers them a symphony,
