So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Cannot hold my beauty in the ground.
But if thou wilt take, childless, from this store,
The world will weep, 'Tis not love
Which alters when it is built anew.
The world will wail and look strange,
Lamenting that no trace of thee remains,
While every private widow well may keep,
By children's eyes, her husband's shape in mind.
  Preserve what I have not born.
  Yet others' works may improve my name.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And need I not to fear, to know thy will.
For my sake do you then lend your ear,
And I will not let that copy die.
Be where thou art, and therefore to be won,
And when a slain party is put to rest,
Thyself, thy good honor will bear,
And the just pleasure lost, which is so deemed
Not by our feeling but by others' seeing.
  But when they see others, find believers in eyes,
  Give them thyself, and thine own part.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Give me the strength, to bear thy whimsical lead,
The captive's absence in thy liberty's cell,
With forbearance, I'll endure, each pause and thread,
Without reproach, thy freedom I'd never quell.
Roam where thou desireth, thy right so immense,
That thou thyself, may grant time's own boon,
In thine own hand, lies pardon, from transgression's sense,
By my will, I'm bound, to suffer no complaint.
  I'll wait, though torment, be the companion of waiting,
  Not fault thy pleasure


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that, in guess, they measure by thy glance,
Then, blindly, they misconstrue,
Their praise for thee, distorted and askew.
But why should I not marvel at that which humbly gleams
In honest faith,
Golden eyes, their brilliance shining in the darkness of thy mind?
Or should I, which gaze, insist on division,
To crown the lie with beauty all along?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have wandered,
  And now to illusion's snare they've tethered.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Based on strength of her babe from faring ill.
Yet, in my loss, I see her truth:
She loves her because my love is restored,
And doth she not approve her?
Because he depends on feigned trust and belief,
And I, in turn, am as I am,
Toiling him with reasons for my good report.
  In our faults, we find comfort in lies,
  And in our deceptions, we find sweet solace.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And yet thou wilt me be blamed if I do not.
For truth needs not for our eyes' decease
And for my soul mine own purpose only is thy will;
And 'gainst myself a lawful plea commence.
Such civil war is in my heart
  That I may allege no cause.
  Thy wisdom may be enough to compel me to give
  But that is not my measure.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The poorlyyielding of a king's brief,
And words indirectly lend to my tongue,
So I lose thee, my dearest, ever so near.
O, what a mansion have I got so
That for thy love I may privilege?
The grounds for this generous remission,
In me, is absent, thus my claim is swerving.
In innocence, you gave yourself away,
Or to the one who held it, in mistake;
Thus, with great grace, your gift on display,
On wiser judgment, it its path does take.
