Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Who granted not a life exempt from grief,
But public station, breeding ground of ill.
Thus is my heart now marred,
By willful grasp of what thy worth in manners doth strew;
And other evil keeps apparel on my brow,
Gilding pale streams with bittiful determination,
Anon permit the basest clouds to ride.
Never pierced with crystal eyes,
As the heart's patient, would fain bring
To medicine and that unfair which fairly doth excel;
  Yet fear the allure of this:
  Your end is what good is writ.


