Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in red, as compassion's gentle reward.
The morning sun, in its heavenly course,Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the evening's gentle light,
Bestowing grace on the evening's bower.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall declare darkness is the new light,
And ugliness dwells in that mournful eyes.
  Thus, I'll maintain sorrow is but fair,
  When matched the solemn beauty of your glance.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
