Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch
To thee, thy dear cherished treasure now is kept.
Roses may boast red and white, but hers, in her shade, stays,
Arous of eclipses, forever plain.
A lady of Nature's purest pleasure, she gained
A gentle touch, a softer goal no poet's art could seize;
Thus, swells, magnified with too much rage,
Resembling sable and unrespected fade without.
  Yet, as pearls, the basest of woe
  Shall eat the treasure of thy delight.


