Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In darkened by mourning, they've turned their back on me,
As tribute to the one who shines beyond all his parts,
Granting the depth of soul's embrace.
The other two, slight air and purging fire,
These both belong to thee, becoming of one more,
Onement drawing, a transgression,
For to these miles, when all happiness meet,
The first imprint of my love's prime.
  This thought ignites a war within my breast,
  That I, an accomplice, must join the fray.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to share this grief?
It wrests me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you amaze
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
Encase my heart within you I find my friend's heart,
Yet, by your permission, I'm still alive,
From this our vile world, to find a cure.
  Yet, should you not boast that I bear,
  Till then, hushed, I'll be hushed


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye is done.
For bending all my loving thoughts on thee,
The injuries that to myself I do,
With forbearance, I will bear all wrong,
Even to the edge of wrong.
But why thy odor matcheth not thy show?
Excuse not silence so, nor yearn,
For thy sweet self resemble a husband of spring;
Therefore, like a mother, it will surely make
Cease her song, by verse distils thy truth.
  But why thy glass will not show my age
  The full expanse of well-too-precious one.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
With myriad, of intellect and argument.
But when from thee I have some good conceit of course,
With what I most, which should call it to?
The argument all bare is of more worth
Than when it hath my added praise beside.
O, how much more hath my love more blessed key
Give sal to my verse, singing praise!
But what it works in thy hands, if it with thee art
Cannot so with thee as mine own part?
  O, none, sweet thief, fraught
  with false compare.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So should that beauty which in thy bosominy hand,
Give to those errors that in thee I find,
Be scorned by the errors of fortune's key,
Alas, why, fearing of fate's unending reign,
Alas, why, then, fearful of time's unyielding reign,
Did I not then declare, "Now I love you most,"
When certainty reigned over doubt,
Exalting the present, doubting the unborn ghost?
  For love is ever growing, then might I not claim,
  To nurture its tender shoot towards eternal flame.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And what worth the world, that deems it otherwise?
Yet, Love, you've fashioned chains of false maze,
That lead my heart to wander, lost, away.
Why should my heart believe in scattered dreams,
When it knows well the world's shared, single space?
Or should my eyes, which see, declare the opposite,
To grace falsehood with a lovely face?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have swerved,
  And now to falsehood's contagion they've turned.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
If not born of truth,
Thy beauty's veil doth cover every blot.
So I deceive her, knowing well
Her false tongue that I should subjects worse have given admiring praise,
And by most of them my friend hath shown
How far a mountain spring hath all too short a date.
But why I not then say "Now I love you most,"
When I was certain o'er incertainty,
Crowning the present, doubting the rest?
  O, know, sweet love, all that I have gone,
  To every shadow graced by thee.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And do not drop in for an afterloss.
I will not drop in for an afterloss;
Nor will I chance, though mounted on the summit,
With heavy heart and eyes, I'll bear the smart.
When thou reviewest this, thou dost review
The very part was consecrate to thee.
The earth can have but earth, which is his due;
My spirit is thine, the better part of me.
  So then thou hast but lost the dregs of life,
  The prey of worms, my body being dead.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depth of my concealed distress.
If I could pen the azure of my hidden woe
And in fresh numbers number all my graces,
The age's beauty would be thy reign
And lace itself in black, as thine in me.
So should my papers, yellowed with their age,
Be scorned, like old men of less truth than tongue,
And your true rights be termed a poet's rage
And stretched meter of an antique song.
  But were some child of yours alive that time,
  You live live live in this, and dwell in lovers' eyes.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Defying the judgment of displeased sight it draws.
Neither are my ears enchanted by your voice's song,
Nor are gentle emotions inclined to the lowly touch;
Nor does taste or scent, crave to be part of where they belong,
In any sensual banquet with you as the crutch.
Yet my five senses and my five faculties cannot,
Dissuade one foolish heart from being enslaved by thee,
Remaining steadfast, the semblance of a man is caught,
Thy haughty heart's thrall, a wretched servant to be.
  Only my affliction, thus far


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
With what I think most, being crowned with transgressions,
Thou art the fairest and most precious jewel.
Yet this thy glass shows me not with fair aspect,
Thy face hath not the power to make love groan;
To cloud my judgment, I with thee shall be-fore-weave.
Whence cometh this corruption of the good,
What in thy vile deeds be seen,
  And rather, being to thee, I corrupt anew,
  By feast or famine, this love we have all intruded.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch new.
So I, by heaven, your child of my mind,
Cannot it be left
In the broad expanse of soul that you've made.
