Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, they are love's ebon beacon,
Gentle on my sorrow, they rain.
The morning sun, in its heavenly course,
Nor the bright star that ushers in repose,
Can compare to the beauty of your remorse,
Gracing the evening with its eve's glow.
Oh, let your heart mourn for my plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every trait,
Then I shall proclaim darkness is bright,
And all that's lovely, your shadows create.
  So I'll confess, black is the shade of the soul,
  When embraced by


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And force my dearest friend to suffer, see?
It tears me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more harshly still;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you amaze
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
Encase my heart within your breast's embrace,
But let my friend's heart wander, wild and free.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find grace,
No longer can you then torment me.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye
May hold his dear love, though thou art forsworn.
But since he is of one date,
Thy beauty's history with thee remains,
Unlooked by mortal time,
Persisting through life's myriad hues and shrewd.
But if thou survive, not from the end,
When nature's second self is done,
But life's third self shall grant thee my place
And other pangs of life thou art assured mine,
When thy sweet Death is slain and poor.
  In him she keeps her ancient wealth displayed,
  A testament to days of yore


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Thy merit hath my duty strongly knit,
To thee I send this written embassage
To witness duty, not to show my wit;
Duty so great, which wit so poor as mine
May make seem bare, in wanting words to show it,
But that I hope some good conceit of thine
In thy soul's thought, all naked, will bestow it;
Till whatsoever star that guides my moving
Points on me graciously with fair aspect,
And puts apparel on my tattered loving
To show me worthy of thy sweet respect.
  Then may I dare to


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Speak of my lameness and I straight will halt,
Against thy reasons, I'll offer no defense,
But in the eyes of all, I'll live.
Thou canst not, love, disgrace me,
To set a form upon desired change,
As I'll myself disgrace, knowing thy will;
I will acquaintance strangle and look strange,
Be absent from thy walks, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name no more shall dwell,
Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
  For thee


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet, Love, you've turned your back on me.
My eyes, ensnared by twisted, biased views,
Are moored within a harbor of the mind;
Yet, Love, you've shaped deceitful manacles,
That bind my heart to follow where they're blind.
Why should my heart believe in scattered dreams,
When it knows well the world's shared, single space?
Or should my eyes, which see, declare the opposite,
To grace falsehood with a lovely face?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have strayed,
  And now to falsehood


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though I in turn my nature to decay.
That she hath thee is of my wailing chief,
A tyrant Time, that will believe my worth,
For I see her falsehoods and blunders
And in their glory die.
Now, all in war with Time for love of you,
And for the peace of you, I say,
My name is short, and therefore my worth is subdued
To what it works in, like the dyer's hand.
  For your sake, I'll bear the cost,
  For I must not hate, for I see you when I cannot.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Neither my nerves nor changes right,
Shall endure past this terrestrial war.
Against that time when thou shalt strangely pass
And scarcely greet me with that sun thine eye,
When love, converted from the thing it was,
Shall reasons find of settled gravity;
Against that time do I ensconce me here
Within the knowledge of mine own desert,
And this my hand against myself uprear
To guard the lawful reasons on thy part.
  To leave poor me thou hast the strength of laws,
  Since why to love I can allege no cause.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thy merit with thy tender appeal.
If I could write the beauty of thy eyes
And in fresh numbers number all thy graces,
The age to come would say "This poet lies;
Such heavenly touches ne'er touched earthly faces."
So should my papers, yellowed with their age,
Be scorned, like old men of less truth than tongue,
And your true rights be termed a poet's rage
And stretched meter of an antique song.
  But were some child of yours alive that time,
  You should live twice--in it and in my rhyme.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
Whose influence is thine and born of thee.
How many a holy and obsequious tear
Hath dear religious love stol'n from mine eye,
As interest of the dead, which now appear
But things removed that hidden in thee lie.
Thou art the grave where buried love doth live,
Hung with the trophies of my lovers gone,
Who all their parts of me to thee did give;
That due of many now is thine alone.
  Their images I loved I view in thee,
  And thou, all they, hast all the all of me.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Like of the dyer's hand, dyed by its own embrace.
That mine eye is fairest and most precious,
Be
The right to love with equal zest,
As thine eyes wander, and my heart does plead.
Nurture compassion in that breast, so blessed,
That compassion may merit sympathy indeed.
Or else, more worthy is my verse so barren of new pride,
When I consider thee, dear friend, time's abundance,
I can't chide thy beauty, being mute,
Save for thee, the child of state,
  Wherewith being crowned,


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To thee, of your joy, I shall bear the cost.
Forgetting me, your child, who in thy affection I find,
Lost in thoughts of thee, my love, to whom I can't
go?
Who hast thou then more than thou hadst before?
No love, my love, that thou mayst true love call;
All mine was thine before thou hadst this more.
Then, if for my love thou my love receivest,
I cannot blame thee for my pleasure thou usest;
But yet be blamed if thou thyself deceivest
