So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye
May hold his rank before.
For when thy picture or my love is of one date,
Thyself away are of one date,
Such seems it winter still, and cold respite
In your absence, even birds are mute;
They live asunder and bare,
Wherein thy shadow graces mountaintops so high.
  Or if they do, 'tis the lowliest or noblest creature,
  That mine eye can see what thy heart can show.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Though seeming one, friendships of yet another,
His art, his scope, the life I yearn to cope,
With my own joy, I'm still the poorest brother.
Thine eyes, that taught the silent to find their voice,
And ignorance to rise and soar on high,
Have added wings to scholars' soaring choice,
And lent thy beauty to their majesty.
Rejoice in what I pen, for it's your own,
A child begotten through our sacred bond.
In others' works, you but refine and hone,
Enhancing art with your celestial pond.
 


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Thou art the grave where buried love doth live,
Hung with the trophies of my lovers gone,
Who all their parts of me to thee did give;
That due of many now is thine alone.
Then need I not to fear the worst of wrongs
When in the least of them my life hath end;
I see a better state to me belongs
Than that which on thy humor doth depend.
  This thought is as a benefit for me
  That I should live, and die with thee.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet in the spring, Love, you've twisted reality,
That grace and error, age and thou shouldst bear the weight of lead thee.
How many hearts could the lone wolf then redeem,
If he wore the guise of a gentle lamb, serene?
How many hearts wouldst thou prove to me,
If thou wouldst whisper
Of my age, make beauty whole?
  But do not so. I love thee in such sort
  As, thou being mine, mine is thy good report.


