Thou sly Deceiver, Love, what hold dost thou
On my sight, that it may not perceive
The truth it knows, the beauty it should bow
To, yet mistakes for ugliness and grief?
My eyes, ensnared by twisted, biased views,
Are moored within a harbor of the mind;
Yet, Love, you've shaped deceitful manacles,
That bind my heart to follow where they're blind.
  True and right, my heart and eyes have strayed,
  And now to falsehood's contagion they've succumbed.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet, Love, you've shaped deceitful manacles.
Why should my heart believe in scattered dreams,
When it knows well the world's shared, single space?
Or should my eyes, which see, declare the opposite,
To grace falsehood with a lovely face?
Oh, know, sweet heart, of this grand disguise;
My heart, your scorn, seeks embrace
To avoid the paradise that leads my heart to despair.
  True love's cunning scheme, with tears, doth blind my mind,
  Lest clear-sighted eyes should find its faults, unkind.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Who for my sake do define as "new,"
And rather, by chance, I am proved to lie,
Truth's enemy, in slumber,
And in our time, we learn to forget
The trappings of the heretic policy.
For to this I speak of the love we know,
A tribute to the one who shapes our destiny;
In this hidden world, where truth hides behind a mask,
Mad defamers find believers in mad spectators.
  To prevent this, and to preserve your good name,
  Keep your eyes fixed, though your heart may wander.


