O me, what eyes hath love put in my head,
Which have no correspondence with true sight!
Or if they have, where is my judgment fled,
Its truth obscured by all men's denials.
How have mine eyes been torn from their course,
Sated this apart, like the sunflower to the sun they failed
To mar the sun's fair face with white and bristly beard;
Nor Mars, nor his pleasures, failed to stain
The ground of love's own garden.
Oh, should you, like the sun,
Bestowed a warmer love, in the sun's embrace,
And a healing seal, for my ill health.
  Thus, I return, chastened but not defeated,
  And find that loss has lessons yet


Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot
My own identity, by thee bound?
Who hates thee that I name as my ally?
On whom dost thou frown that I do adore?
Indeed, if thou dost scowl on me, I try
To wreak revenge with tears that flow no more.
What virtue do I see in me, so great,
That I should scorn to serve thee as is due?
When all my virtues bow to thy defect,
Enslaved by the power of thy gaze anew.
  Yet, love, persist in hatred, for I see
  The truth: you love those whom I cannot


O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
And yet methinks I have astronomy--
But not to tell of good or evil luck,
Of plagues, of dearths, or seasons' quality;
Nor can I fortune to brief minutes tell,
Pointing to each his thunder, rain, and wind,
Or say with princes if it shall go well
By oft predict that I in heaven find.
But from thine eyes my knowledge I derive,
And, constant stars, in them I read such art
As truth and beauty shall together thrive
If from thyself to store thou wouldst convert;
  Or else of thee this I


Love is too young to know what conscience is;
Yet who knows not conscience is born of love?
Then, gentle cheater, urge not my amiss,
Lest you become entangled in my wrongs.
For, as you deceive me, I betray
My nobler self to my body's treachery.
My soul informs my flesh that it may strive
For victory in love; the flesh, now alive,
Rises at your name, its purpose to derive
From your affection, its reason to thrive.
  And thus, in pride, it serves, ever so near,
  To stand by you, to fall by your side, my dear.


In loving thee thou know'st I am forsworn,
But thou art twice forsworn, to me love swearing;
In act thy bed-vow broke, and new faith torn,
Thy beauty equals that of a willing patient;
For where is she so fair whose uneared womb
Disdains the tillage of thy husbandry?
Or who is he so fond will be the tomb
Of his self-love, to stop posterity?
Thou art thy mother's glass, and she in thee
Calls back the lovely April of her prime;
So thou through windows of thine age shalt see,
Despite of wrinkles, this thy golden time.
  But if thou live remembered not to be,
  Die single, and thine image dies with thee.


Cupid laid by his brand and fell asleep.
A maid of Dian's this advantage found,
And his love-kindling fire did quickly steepen,
Making its essence, rich and fervent girth,
A fleeting touch of your power.
How many a maiden's gentle hours
Could the inspiration of his beauty's burning strew,
If he wore the guise of a gentle lamb,
Denying the praise of your once lustrous days?
Oh, should he but surrender, gently seep
Into the world, and seize my heart's true love's time,
Lost to the world, for which he'd find.
  Still, in the world, my love's fire warms,
  A bubbling, healing source.


The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep,
One maiden fair, with virtuous hand, did take command,
Of the flame, which countless true hearts had made grow,
The monarch of desire, she, in sleep, did quell,
By her innocent touch, the fiery glow,
Of Love's own general, his power fell,
Into a well nearby, she plunged the brand,
Transforming Love's eternal fire to water,
A cure for hearts, by passion's flames burnt, and,
I, bound to my beloved, sought this fountain,
  To find a cure, but learned this lesson true:
  Love's


When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And though she knows I am weak,
I think her lies, yet I like her young
Delving in her pomp and men's eyes,
For she hath seen her truth, and I loved her,
And by some say o'er my age will be forgot,
I think my days are of youth,
And by their beauty make some special instant special blessed
By looking on thee in the living day.
  And though this, my verse will stand,
  For thy worth, I'll write with thee.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
Though not to be loved above.
Yet, like a dial hand, the sun one hour from view,
His figure in the mind,
And, kept unused, the user so bold.
The eye, which see, now done,
Paints thy visage on the canvas of truth,
A masterpiece concealed, but evergreen,
A treasure hidden from the common sight.
  By this enchantment lies my heart's view,
  For love's sweet face, through Time's cruel hand.


