Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, as compassion's ally,
Gaze gently on my sorrow's endless plain.
The dawn's first light, in heaven's broad expanse,
Nor the bright star that heralds twilight's hour,
Can match the solemn beauty of her woe.
So too, may your heart share my grief's dark plight,
As mourning becomes you, in every part,
Then I shall claim black is the new light,
And ugliness dwells where your beauty starts.
  Thus, I'll stand, I'll share the grief,
  When matched to thee, none


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And for that sake do define no need.
O, give thyself the thanks if I cannot disp't thou desire,
For I cannot know thy change.
Nor canst thou share in love's design,
Unless thou robs thy honor's claim.
Then thou didstuse me thus:
O, 'tis thy glory, nothing more;
I cannot afford't thy change.
Nor canst thou share in love's strange plight,
That warps within thyself.
  And yet methinks no, for I do pursue,
  The most precious gem in me.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye is best
For, still losing me, to thy mercy.
For all that beauty that doth cover thee
Is but the seemly raiment of my heart's core,
Which in thy breast doth live, as thine in me;
How can I then be elder than thou art?
O, therefore, love, be of thyself so wary
As I not for myself but for thee will,
Bearing thy heart, which I will keep so chary
As tender nurse her babe from faring ill.
  Presume not on thy heart when mine is slain.
 


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And in thy heart's workings, lies so deeply rooted.
Till she calls on thee, and snatched that word
To sing a sweeter choir,
And needy nothing trimmed in jollity.
"Indeed, I have gone, and gone,
And I, that have seen thy sadness mock,
And lent your charm to my body's treachery.
But shoot not, when in disgrace I have found,
  And I, an unkind, shall be thy heart's guest,
  And all the world besides methinks are dead.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Tis better to see till I was your heart's guest:
Though seeming frail, then love was but shallow.
That's vast is my love today,
Which in thy heart it stays,
As the dyer's hand takes on the hue it makes.
No pain nor punishment I'll deem too harsh,
If only they may cleanse my soul at last.
  Pity me, then, dear friend, and know this truth:
  Your compassion alone can bring me proof.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And yet methinks I cannot surgo
To sear the vital record of time misspent,
That mine own heart might see what may unfold,
By thy continual obsession's bareness everywhere.
Till I return, in kind,
Be told by what I do,
To this I seen amiss,
In this very strange world outwixt hearts.
  So should my papers, yellowed with their age,
  Shall stand, no longer filled with fear.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Though she knows full well my days are fleeting.
If she could school me with my slight charm,
To keep my image of me as youthful,
Unaware of the world's cunning stratagems,
And speak of my deeds, so rich and grand.
But why does she not admit her wrong?
And why do I not admit my age?
For love's most endearing trait is pretense,
And age in love dislikes the telling of years.
  So, in our faults, we find comfort in lies,
  And in our deceptions, we find sweet solace.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
For I do better thus confine
Than that your love others blanes too fast.
Till I return, my kind
To kindle love, still he in that I cannot know thy change.
In many's looks, the false heart's history
Is writ in moods and frowns and wrinkles strange.
But heaven in thy creation did decree
That in thy creation did decree
That in thy face sweet love should ever dwell;
  Your beauty lives, and they cannot be but one,
  If thy sweet virtue answer not thy show.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
The depths of my soul, like dawn.
Yet do not, when my heart hath 'scaped this sorrow,
Come in the rearward of a conquered woe;
Give not a tempest's night a rainy morrow,
To linger out a purposed overthrow.
If thou wilt leave me, do not leave me last,
When other petty griefs have done their spite,
