Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, as compassion's ally.
Yet, as your emissaries return with tales,
Of health and beauty, life rekindles, burns anew,
My joy restored, I celebrate your worth,
Only to plunge once more into despair.
My heart, like a captive, scarred and mudded,
Gives audience to your caress,
Which every vice, a jewel,
In my heart's own book, such mysteries preserve.
  In my heart's own book, the pages ever-turn,
  A treasure that you will never to rescind.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And for that sake lay on me this turmoil?
O, let me suffer, being at your beck,
Th' imprisoned absence of my lover's debt,
And patience, tame to sufferance, bide each check
Without accusing you of injury.
Be where you list, your charter is so strong
That you yourself may privilege your time
To what you will; to you it doth belong
Yourself to pardon of self-doing crime.
  I am to wait, though waiting so be hell,
  Not blame your pleasure, be it ill or well.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine eye is too fair
For that sweet thief which sourly robs from his figure.
The so is 'tis not enough that through the cloud thou break
To dry the rain on my storm-beaten face,
For no man well of such a salve can speak
That heals the wound and cures not the disgrace.
Nor can thy shame give physic to my grief;
Though thou repent, yet I have still the loss.
Th' offender's sorrow lends but weak relief
To him that bears the strong offense's cross.
  Ah, but those tears are pearl which thy love sheds,



Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Thou canst true love, when in I cannot know thy change.
In many's looks, the false heart's history
Is writ in moods and frowns and wrinkles strange.
But heaven in thy creation did decree
That in thy face sweet love should ever dwell;
Whate'er thy thoughts or thy heart's workings be,
Thy looks should nothing thence but sweetness tell.
Thy looks should nothing thence but sweetness tell,
Which, like a, I will drink down the deadly sip
Of thy pure delight.
  Pity me, then, dear friend, and know this


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
If thou wouldst but truly write,
It's for thy love, thy heart's other place.
How can I then return in happy plight
When day's oppression is not eased by night,
But day by night and night by day oppressed;
And each, though enemies to either's reign,
Do in consent shake hands to torture me,
The one by toil, the other to complain
How far I toil, still farther off from thee?
  But day doth daily draw my sorrows longer,
  And night doth nightly make grief's length seem stronger.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And in its rude, some say
That love hath trained it to reshape and seep,
Into the vile, a heavenly grace to cast,
A beauty like your own, from dross to rise,
In every dark, a brilliance to outlast,
As shadows flee before the sun's surprise.
Alas, when I, too much profane, am lost,
Consumed by that which thou hast no part,
Nor canst be so chased
As shadows flee before the sun's surprise.
  So, love, protect in hatred, for I shall lie,
