Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Of my hidden woe.
If I could paint the face of youth,
The hideous world would cry, "This poet tells but praise,
No face on earth could claim such heavenly hue."
My parchment, yellowed by the sun's embrace,
Would be dismissed, as ancient men deceive,
And leave my tongue in other words remain.
Harsh, featureless muse; my worth,
Thine eyes outshine their distant, cold respite.
  Yet, should a child of yours inherit this radiant hue,
  You'd live again, through progeny and mirth.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my sick muse doth give another place.
But heaven's book, yellowed by tears,
Of thee, I cannot number clouds and storms,
Nor predict the fates of monarchs bold,
In heaven's scriptures, secrets find their forms.
In thine own creation, heaven did conspire
To grace your face with love's eternal fire;
Whatever thoughts or secret heart desires,
Your visage speaks but sweetness, kindling higher.
  Your beauty blooms, an apple ripe with grace,
  If inner worth does not match outward face.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
My love, thy heart's contentment, is too short of us.
For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
That to myself I cannot blame thy sins,
By thy love, thy truth, thy faithfulness so rare.
But to thine illicit love I concede,
Thy face hath not thy power to pardon,
My verse I can but write, and thine eyes outshine.
  In thy presence, my love is as fair,
  As those who've fashioned chains of false delight.


