Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, as compassion's ally.
Yet, as dawn breaks in the east,
My love shall wane, as its sweet one doth lie,
A life so long as heart it seems.
Yet, as pearls, the humblest gem may shine
With ardor, as mine by thee.
My heart, like a captive, scarred and mudded,
Cries out for you, behind the wall.
  Thus, I'll dwell, and in my madness,
  For I cannot love one for whom you feel no need.


